(14,1,1,13,25,25,'1\nSECTION 1: LOGICAL REASONING & VARIATIONS\nSTRAND: NUMBERS FOR EVERYDAY LIFE\nSub-Strand: Proportional Reasoning\nWeeks 1, 2 & 3\nLearning Outcome: Establish the validity of logical arguments and use them to make \nrelevant decisions in solving problems.\nContent Standard: Demonstrate conceptual understanding of logical reasoning to solve real-\nlife problems.\nWeeks 4 & 5\nLearning Outcome: Analyse the impact of variations and conduct simple investigations in \nsolving day-to-day problems.\nContent Standard: Demonstrate conceptual understanding of proportionality in relation to \nvariation and use it to solve real-life problems.\nINTRODUCTION AND SECTION SUMMARY\nIn everyday life, we consider issues and make judgments and decisions for our benefit and that \nof our communities. Logical reasoning in this session examines the process of using logic to \nevaluate information, make decisions, and reach conclusions. This section will also explore the \nrelationship between two or more characteristics of any subject of interest and how they change \nor remain stable relative to one another. We refer to this as variation. The concepts of logical \nreasoning and variation are essential for developing critical thinking, problem-solving, and \nanalytical skills, which will improve learners’ academic performance and prepare them for the \nworld of work. Logical reasoning allows learners to assess information, identify patterns, and \nmake informed choices. Both logical reasoning and variation are key components of Home \nEconomics, Science, Engineering, and Economics. The SHS 3 learner is equipped to integrate \ninto society through the concepts learnt in logical reasoning and variation. To maximise these \nconcepts, we will engage learners through experiential learning strategies and regular individual \npractice.\nThe weeks, the indicators with their respective focal areas covered by the section are:','e127bdc297f3ee80ac3f16a5d0c3114095bdf79f61119034468307c7d674a68d','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(15,1,1,14,26,26,'2\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & VariationsSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nWeek 1\nInvestigate to establish differences between variables in situational growth (symbolic logic) and \napply it to real-world situations.\n• Focal Area 1: Concept of Logical Reasoning\n• Introduction to logical reasoning\n• Making declarative sentences\n• Identify true or false statements from declarative statements.\n• Focal Area 2: Real-World Applications of logical reasoning\n• Situational Growth and Symbolic Logic: Robotics; Game Design; Artificial \nIntelligence; Computer Science; Philosophy; Law:\n• Model and solve real-world issues involving logical reasoning\nWeek 2\nMake intelligent guesses to establish valid arguments and draw logical conclusions\n• Focal Area 1: Intelligent guessing\n• Establish valid arguments and draw logical conclusions\n• Decision-making in solving problems\n• Focal Area 2: Using tables and diagrams in decision-making\n• Use of Venn diagrams\n• Use of Truth tables\n• Model and solve real-life issues involving logical reasoning\nWeek 3\nCarry out mini real-life investigations to solve problems involving logical reasoning, within \ntheir local community.\n• Focal Area: Real-life applications of logical reasoning\n• Generate and test the validity of logical arguments from peers.\n• Investigations to solve problems involving logical reasoning, within their local \ncommunity\nWeek 4\nUse proportional reasoning to investigate the various types of variation (direct and inverse) and \nextend this to make generalisations.\n• Focal Area: Variation 1\n• Concept, examples and visualisation of proportional reasoning\n• Direct proportions\n• Inverse proportions','2659f7cc31522f9ec6ecaf284952e235f398283e626a6b80937b5c9814579927','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(16,1,1,15,27,27,'3\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\n• Joint variations\nWeek 5\nUse proportional reasoning to investigate partial variations, make generalisations and solve \nreal-life issues involving partial variations.\n• Focal Area: Variation 2\n• Partial variations\n• Model and solve real-life situations involving proportional reasoning\nSUMMARY OF PEDAGOGICAL EXEMPLARS\nThis session aims to nurture Senior High School learners who value their own perspectives and \nthose of others, while developing critical thinking skills to address personal and national issues. \nThe teaching of logical reasoning and variations will be guided by strategies that promote \nindependent thought, teamwork, and problem-solving. Learners will engage in activities such \nas think-pair-share, problem-based learning, peer review, and debates to analyse arguments, \ndraw logical conclusions, and communicate effectively. Group work will support collaborative \nlearning, encouraging learners to explore types of variation—direct, inverse, joint, and partial—\nand provide solutions to real-life problems. Independent tasks will build skills in evaluating \nstatements, defending positions with logical arguments, and constructing summaries of real-\nlife cases. Class discussions will reinforce foundational knowledge, while presentations will \nfoster communication skills. Throughout, a gender-responsive, learner-centred approach will \nhelp learners apply concepts of logic and variation to everyday situations.\nASSESSMENT SUMMARY\nThis provides a summary of the assessment used against the indicator. It makes provision for \ninformation on the assessment strategies and their highest DOK levels, to improve learning \noutputs.\nTable 1: Indicators, DOK levels, assessment modes and strategies\nIndicators DOK LevelAssessment Strategy and/or Mode\nInvestigate to establish differences \nbetween variables in situational \ngrowth (symbolic logic) and apply it \nto real world situations.\nLevel 2 Assessment Mode: Home work.\nAssessment Strategy: Standard questions \nfor individuals.\nMake intelligent guessing to establish \nvalid arguments and draw logical \nconclusions\nLevel 3 Assessment Mode: Debate\nAssessment Strategy: Whole class debate \nbuilt in groups and built through jigsaw \ndiscussion.\nCarry out mini real-life investigations \nto solve problems involving logical \nreasoning, within their local \ncommunity.\nLevel 4 Assessment Mode: Group project\nAssessment Strategy: Research on real-\nlife application in learner’s environment.','8d8544b52203830eb8dd75e092b8f7790b8b81cbd5802545e562581f6543feea','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(17,1,1,16,28,28,'4\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & VariationsSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nUse proportional reasoning to \ninvestigate the various types of \nvariation (direct and inverse) and \nextend this to make generalisations.\nLevel 2 Assessment Mode: Assignment.\nAssessment Strategy: Individual \nassignment on essay questions\nUse proportional reasoning to \ninvestigate partial variations, make \ngeneralisations and solve real-life \nissues involving partial variations.\nLevel 3 Assessment Mode: Problem posing & peer \nreview. \nAssessment Strategy: Generating \nquestions on real-life issues for peers to \nanswer and give feedback','691f765dd1289c04bf39d36c8ac4c7e6f98d87b607d28f9fedabbaee76b3eae4','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(18,1,1,17,29,29,'5\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nWEEK 1\nLearning Indicator: Investigate to establish differences between variables in situational \ngrowth (symbolic logic) and apply it to real world situations\nFOCAL AREA 1: CONCEPT OF LOGICAL REASONING\nUnderstanding Logical Reasoning\nDiscuss with learners in which ways these statements can be useful in mathematical thinking:\na. The use of rules\nb. The use of evidence to reach conclusions\nc. Analysis of information\nd. Identification of patterns\ne. Making sound judgments\nChallenge learners to give their own definition of logical reasoning.\nWhat is Logical Reasoning?\nLogical reasoning can be said to be the systematic process of forming conclusions, making \ndecisions, or solving problems based on available facts and evidence using structured rules of \ninference upon which mathematical thinking is built.\nInterrogative and Declarative Statements\nMathematical thinking involves asking questions about the environment and phenomena. We \nwill then be interrogating issues of concern.\nFor example, “Do Ghanaians use questions in response to questions?” is an interrogative \nstatement, as it asks a question.\nHowever, the statement “Ghanaians use questions in response to questions” is a declarative \none, as it is not a question and ends in a full stop.\nExample 1\n1. Convert these interrogative statements into declarative statements.\na. “Do you think mathematics cannot be fun?”\nb. “Is Accra the capital of Ghana?”\nc. “Will Ghana win the next world cup?”\nd. “Does success not depend on corruption?”','0595054fa4bd75d08a9171ab28b1b73d81b115cb4358aade1b64b8dd125eccea','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(19,1,1,18,30,30,'6\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & VariationsSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nSolution\nTable 1.1: Interrogative and declarative statements\nItemInterrogative statement Declarative statement\nA “Do you think mathematics cannot be \nfun?”\n“Mathematics cannot be fun.”\nB “Is Accra the capital of Ghana?” “Accra is the capital of Ghana.”\nC “Will Ghana win the next world cup?”“Ghana will win the next world cup”\nD “Does success not depend on \ncorruption?”\n“Success does not depend on corruption”\nPositive and Negative Statements\nThe statement, “Mathematics cannot be fun” is a negative statement because of the use of \n“cannot”. However, “Mathematics can be fun” is a positive statement .\nFor Example:\n1. Positive statement: Asamoah played for the Ghana Black Stars.\nNegative statement: Asamoah did not play for the Ghana Black Stars.\n2. Positive statement: {1,2,3,4,6} are proper factors of 12.\nNegative statement: {1,2,3,4,6} are not proper factors of 12.\n3. Positive statement: Respect for environmental cleanliness is a Ghanaian value.\nNegative statement: Respect for environmental cleanliness is not a Ghanaian value\nNOTE\nMostly, we represent statement with capital letters of the English alphabet and we \nattach “~” to mean negation.\nExample, B: Serious students use their time well ~B: Serious students do not use their \ntime well\nD: Galamsey destroys cocoa farms ~D: Galamsey does not destroy cocoa farms\nA: x ≤ 10 ~ A: x > 10\nAre declarative statements always true?\nDeclarative statements can either be negative or positive. A negative declarative statement can \nbe true or false or debatable.','2766790f5b4fdf8eeefff0088c67aa2f9203504ef8dd5b9857d197913f74908c','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(20,1,1,19,31,31,'7\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nExample 2\n1. A: 2 is an even-prime number.\nThis is a true statement because “2” is an even number and a prime number.\n2. P: Ghana was the highest producer of gold in the world in 2024.\nThe statement is false because China was the leading producer of gold with 380 metric \ntons\n3. T: Students score high marks if what teachers teach come in the examination.\nThis is quite debatable . If students do not learn, the efforts of teachers will not produce \nthe desired result.\nConjunctions and Disjunctions\nStatements which carry one simple idea is termed a simple statement .\nFor example:\nA: Fati is a farmer, Q: Her first born is a boy and R: Her first born is a girl, are simple statements.\nThese simple statements can be connected with words (connectives) such as like, and, or, not, \nand if ... then to form compound statements. We can join two statements to get compound \nstatements which can be a conjunction or a disjunction.\nExamples of Conjunctions\n1. “Fati is a farmer” and “her first born is a boy” Making the statement a conjunction.\n2. “The sky is dark and it will rain today.” Making the statement a conjunction\n3. “I am a student and I own a poultry farm.” Making the statement a conjunction\nA conjunction is true if both statements are true, otherwise, it is a false conjunction.\nBoth parts need to be true for the conjunction to be true\nExamples of Disjunctions\n1. “Her first born is a boy” or “her first born is a girl” is a disjunction\n2. “I will go to school or I will go to the stadium.” is a disjunction\n3. “The traffic light is on red or the traffic light is on green” is a disjunction\nNote\nA disjunction is true if at least one of the parts is true at a time. Therefore, a disjunction \nis still true as long as at least one part or simple statement is true.\nUsing the symbols “⋏” and “⋎”','2bd19108b3e985eeadb2add8f1ebdc0661c8826e1cb596df99ba70ab5dc01691','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(21,1,1,20,32,32,'8\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & VariationsSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nConsider the use of “and” and “or” in forming compound statements. We denote \n“and” with “⋏” and “or” with “⋎”\nExample 3\nForm a compound statement from the simple statements using symbols and state whether it is \nTrue or False\n1. A: 2 is an even number\nB: 2 is an odd number\nSolution\nA ⋏ B: 2 is an even number and 2 is an odd number (False conjunction)\nA ⋎ B: 2 is an even number or 2 is an odd number (True disjunction)\n2. P: Plane shapes do not have volume\nQ: Solid shapes have volumes\nP ⋏ Q: Plane shapes do not have volume and solid shapes have volume (True conjunction)\nP ⋎ Q: Plane shapes do not have volume or solid shapes have volume (True conjunction)\nExample 4\n1. In a whole class debate, share views on the usefulness of logical reasoning in mathematics \nand in everyday life activities.\nSolution\nLogical reasoning helps us to make informed decisions, predict events based on identified \npatterns and solve problems in an acceptable way.\n2. In pairs discuss the statement “All equivalent sets are equal” as true or false.\n3. Provide evidence to support your answer.\nSolution\nCorrect identification of statement as false\nClear and logical explanation of why the statement is false\nRelevant example\nClear demonstration of understanding of equivalent sets and equal sets.','5611e35413118ae11d48b59f03b7de58cf00c9b0a3e56bbfb92bdccf973424b2','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(22,1,1,21,33,33,'9\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nExample 5\nIndividually ask learners to think about the interrogative statements, then pair with a classmate \nand deduce a corresponding declarative statement.\na. “Do students wear uniforms to school?”\nb. “Is my view about other tribes really good?”\nc. “Can geometric construction be done without compass?”\nd. “Will cleaning of the home by a boy change his gender?”\ne. “Does studying of Ghanaian languages improve fluency in English?”\nSolution\na. Interrogative: “Do students wear uniforms to school?”\nb. Declarative: “Students wear uniforms to school.”\nc. Interrogative: “Is my view about other tribes really good?”\nd. Declarative: “My view about other tribes is really good.”\ne. Interrogative: “Can geometric construction be done without compass?”\nf. Declarative: “Geometric construction can be done without compass.”\ng. Interrogative: “Will cleaning of the home by a boy change his gender?”\nh. Declarative: “Cleaning of the home by a boy changes his gender.”\ni. Interrogative: “Does studying of Ghanaian languages improve fluency in English?”\nj. Declarative: “Studying of Ghanaian languages improve fluency in English”.\nIndividual Task:\nIndependently, complete the table with the correct interrogative statements or declarative \nstatements.\nTable 1.2: Individual task on interrogative and declarative statements\nItemInterrogative statements Declarative statements\na. “Must mathematics teachers smile \nduring lessons?”\nb. “I think Ghana will develop if we collaborate.”\nc. “Whole numbers are subsets of natural \nnumbers”\nd. “Farmers will go to farm if it rains today”\ne. “The heart is the biggest organ in humans”','b1297e2f297ec036c32029fde4ec84cc56d1f582b2a3267527736e4e17d7c6e6','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(23,1,1,22,34,34,'10\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & VariationsSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nSolution\na. Interrogative statement: “Must mathematics teachers smile during lessons?”\nDeclarative statement: “Mathematics teachers must smile during lessons”\nb. Interrogative statement: “Do I think Ghana will develop if we collaborate?”\nDeclarative statement: “I think Ghana will develop if we collaborate.”\nc. Interrogative statement: “Are whole numbers subsets of natural numbers?”\nDeclarative statement: “Whole numbers are subsets of natural numbers”\nd. Interrogative statement: “Will farmers go to farm if it rains today?”\nDeclarative statement: “Farmers will go to farm if it rains today”\ne. Interrogative statement: “Is the heart the biggest organ in humans?”\nDeclarative statement: “The heart is the biggest organ in humans”\nFOCAL AREA 2: REAL-WORLD APPLICATIONS OF LOGICAL \nREASONING\nWe will now consider whether we can prove a statement to be true or false by making \nimplications of our statements.\nExample 1\nConsider the following statements:\nF: A person is bitten by a mosquito\nG: Mosquitoes cause malaria\nRepresent the statements on a Venn diagram and write an implication for the 2 statements \nindicating whether the implication is true or false. Ntim is bitten by a mosquito but has not got \nmalaria.\nSolution\nLet U = persons\nF = persons bitten by mosquitoes\nG = persons who have malaria\nn = Ntim','b12ec9d0cb0c834f9d736a630d0921c7bf700d3d1bfa41ae90b042a9527d1b14','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(24,1,1,23,35,35,'11\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nExample 2\nConsider the following statements:\nC: Student are critical thinkers.\nD: Students study mathematics.\na. Draw a Venn diagram to illustrate the above statements.\nb. Deduce which of the following statements are valid conclusions based on C and D, stating \nwhich statement are true and which are false.\ni. Apah is a student ⇒ Apah studies mathematics.\nii. Afoa is a critical thinker ⇒ Afoa is a student\niii. Quaye studies mathematics ⇒ Quaye is a critical thinker\nSolution\nLet U = persons, P = critical thinkers, Q = students and R = people who study mathematics\nFigure 1.2: Venn diagram for example 4\nExample 3\nConsider the following statements:\n• M: Ghana made cars are attractive.\n• N: Many Ghana made cars do not last long.\na. Draw a Venn diagram to illustrate the above statements.\nb. Identify any 3 regions on your Venn diagram and deduce what they mean.\nc. Mr. Boateng’s car has lasted a long time and is attractive. Is it Ghana made?','f776126e230ddf4c8265b476dc7110f3bda104c551f1602a5e808459ec76bafd','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(25,1,1,24,36,36,'12\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & VariationsSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nGive logical reasons for your answer in (c).\nSolution\na. Denote G = Ghana made cars\nA = Attractive cars\nL = Cars that last longer\nFigure 1.3: Venn diagram for example 5\nb. Region p = Ghana made cars which are attractive but do not last long.\nRegion s = Ghana made cars which are attractive and last long.\nRegion f = Foreign cars which are neither attractive nor last long.\nc. There are some cars which are attractive but not made in Ghana (Regions “t” and “n”) and \nothers which last long but not made in Ghana (i.e. Regions “y” and “n”).\nIt is not conclusive therefore that all attractive and lasting cars are made in Ghana.\nTherefore, Mr. Boateng’s car may or may not be made in Ghana.\nLearning Tasks\nIn a debate, the boys on one side said that “If we build more railway systems, traffic \ncongestion in Accra will decrease.” However, the girls on the other side argued that \nincreased railway system will rather increase traffic in Accra. Individually, evaluate \nthe logical implications of the boy’s statement and that of the girls.\nLearners to demonstrate\n1. Clear understanding of the boy’s statements: “If we build more railway systems, \ntraffic congestion in Accra will decrease” OR\nThe girls’ statement: “Increased railway systems will rather increase traffic in Accra.”\n(That is explaining what is meant by traffic congestion, giving the associated challenges \nand stating what railway transport is).\n(2 marks for 2 points raised; 1 mark for 1 point; 0 mark for no point raised.)\n2. Provision of 2 appropriate reasons in support of boy’s statement.','ecb796643d48f470da079c25ca1a1cfdbbee60dba69db893643563938e60d3fa','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(26,1,1,25,37,37,'13\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\n(2 marks for 2 points; 1 additional mark for any additional point(s) raised)\n3. Logical explanation of 2 likely reasons in support of girls’ statement.\n(2 marks for 2 points; 1 additional mark for any additional point(s) raised\n4. Analysing the statements Learners should compare both arguments, identify \nstrengths/weaknesses, and relate to Accra’s transport realities\n5. Drawing appropriate conclusion or position. Learners must state their position \nand support it with logical reasoning.\nPEDAGOGICAL EXEMPLARS\nCollaborative Learning\n1. Through think-pair-share, engage learners to establish the concept of logical reasoning, \nand use it to compare statements and quantifiers.\n2. In small groups engage learners to make intelligent guessing and judge the validity of \nlogical arguments, (including positive and negative statements) ensure participation by \nall learners.\n3. Engage learners through think-pair-share, to apply logical reasoning in making intelligent \nguessing and judge the validity of logical arguments, (including positive and negative \nstatements) ensure participation by all learners.\n4. In collaborative and gender responsive groups, investigate to establish Situational Growth \nand Symbolic Logic as well as Model and solve real-world issues involving logical \nreasoning\nIndependent Learning\n1. Individual students to convert interrogative sentences into declarative statements.\n2. Engage individuals to analyse statements and label them as True, False or Debatable\n3. Engage individual students to apply logical reasoning in making intelligent guessing and \njudge the validity of logical arguments\n4. Engage individuals to investigate to establish Situational Growth and Symbolic Logic as \nwell as Model and solve real-world issues involving logical reasoning\nKEY ASSESSMENT LEVEL 2: HOMEWORK\n1. Analyse these statements and label each as True, False, or Debatable.\na. Ghana is a country located in West Africa. …………………….\nb. If n is a number, then 5n is greater than n. …………………….\nc. Oti Region in Ghana shares border with the Bono East Region to the South. ……………\nd. Ghana gained independence from British colonial rule in 1967. …………………….\ne. Mathematics is more important than Robotics. …………………….','bc2e0917161209930f4dfcab84734103c199b6216f5a82400d44694aad548417','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(27,1,1,26,38,38,'14\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & VariationsSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nf. A square is a rectangle. …………………….\ng. All prime numbers are odd …………………….\nh. If x > 5 then x > 3 …………………….\ni. The sum of the interior angles of a triangle is 360o …………………….\n9 marks (DOK Level 1)\n1. Write a conjunction and a disjunction for each pair of statements and state whether it is \n“True” or “False”.\na. M: Factors divide a given number.\nb. N: All factors are composite numbers. 4 marks\nc. S: For x ≥ 9, x is a negative integer.\nd. T: For x ≥ 9, x is a factor of 3. 4 marks\ne. G: Mappings have rules.\nf. H: Mappings have ordered pairs. 4 marks\n12 marks (DOK Level 2)\n2. A farmer in the Volta Region of Ghana wants to plant maize and cassava on his 10-acre \nfarm.\n3. He wants to plant at least 3 acres of maize and at least 2 acres of cassava.\n4. If maize yields 2 tons per acre and cassava yields 3 tons per acre, how many acres of each \ncrop should the farmer plant to maximise his total yield and why?\n5 marks (DOK Level 2)\n5. Consider the following statements:\nP: Teaching staff go on holidays.\nQ: No kitchen staff go on holidays.\na. Draw a Venn diagram to illustrate the above statement 3 marks\nb. Deduce which of the following statements are valid conclusions based on P  and Q \nstating which statement are true and which are false.\ni. Mr. Asiedu is a kitchen staff ⇒ Mr. Asiedu does not go on holidays. 2 marks\nii. Ms. Freda goes on holidays ⇒ Ms. Freda is a teaching staff. 2 marks\niii. Anowa does not go on holidays ⇒ is Anowa is a kitchen staff. 2 marks\nTotal 9 marks (DOK Level 2)\n6. Consider the following two statements:\n• Statement K: Being sincere is a good virtue.\n• Statement L: Good virtues are Ghanaian values.','484d451f23aa6d7674fd19a5f75dec037b08585e68ce9ae73d73cac4b44aa2d1','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(28,1,1,27,39,39,'15\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nExamine the following three statements. For each one, determine whether the conclusion is \ndefinitely true, possibly true, or false based on Statements K and L. Provide a reason for your \nanswer.\na. Auntie Rukia is a Ghanaian.\nb. ⇒ Can we conclude that she has good virtues? 3 marks\nc. Uncle Mensah is very sincere.\nd. ⇒ Can we conclude that he is a Ghanaian? 3 marks\ne. Kojo Baadu is sincere.\nf. ⇒ Can we conclude that he has a Ghanaian value? 3 marks\nTotal 9 marks (DOK Level 2)\nPLC Session 1\n45 minutes Planning & Reviewing the Learning Plan\n1. Begin the session by recapping lessons from the previous year/session/week, focusing on \nhow learning was applied.\n2. Use your subject specific app to explore the content before planning. Ask the app to\na. explain the week’s concepts in a practical way that will make it easier for learners to \nunderstand\nb. identify two or three common misconceptions learners may have on the week’s content \nand how to address them using contexts familiar to Ghanaian learners. (NTS 3a, 3g \nand 3m).\n3. Use your subject specific app to plan your lesson. Ask the app to\na. suggest the most effective method for teaching the week’s concepts to learners with \ndiverse backgrounds.\nb. suggest learning activities, differentiation and essential questions based on the method \nof teaching (NTS 3a-3q)\nc. review your prepared learning plan and address any challenging issues you may have \nidentified in your subject area (NTS 3a-3c).\nNote\n• Take note of the recap topics linked to Years 1 and 2 while preparing for your \nlesson.\n• Refer to the Values Learning Community Handbook and link the values for the \nweek in your lesson to ensure that learners are applying what they learn.\n4. Plan the week’s key assessment with your app','3bf02481a5ae21b444b08d97805144620618aade5d5d378c5f624d7faf8229cd','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(29,1,1,28,40,40,'16\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & VariationsSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\na. The key assessment for the week is homework . Chat with your app to plan this \nassessment, indicating what you and your learners will do before, during and after the \nhomework (NTS 3k-3q).\n10 minutes Content Exploration\n5. As a subject group, select one problem or idea/term in the week’s content that is challenging \n(not a routine procedural question, but one that requires conceptual understanding) and \nthen\na. use the app, ask it to solve the problem step by step and explain the reasoning behind \neach step (you may be assisted by a teacher who is strong in the content area)\nb. all teachers in the group work through a similar problem individually for 3–4 minutes\nc. the group then works through the problem together step by step\nd. highlight the most common errors made at each step and discuss (NTS 1c, 1e, 2c, 2e, \n3e-3j).\nNote\n• This part is not just about ‘How do I teach this?’ but how do I teach it for learners \nto understand?\n• If the group still shares the same content gap, use the app: ask it to solve the \nproblem step by step and explain the reasoning behind each step.\n• Problems/ideas discussed should be sourced from the Teacher Manual content, not \ninvented separately. This keeps the content exploration directly connected to what \nteachers will deliver.\n30 minutes Enactment\n6. Enact a teaching activity based on your learning plan (NTS la-1b, 1e-lg, 2c, 2e, 2f, 3c-3j \nand 3o).\na. Colleagues observe with specific attention to clarity of concept explanation, use of \nfamiliar Ghanaian examples and how misconceptions are handled if they arise.\nb. After the enactment, ask for structured feedback from the group\ni. was the concept explained in a way that a learner with no prior knowledge could \nfollow?\nii. were the examples grounded in contexts familiar to Ghanaian learners?\niii. did any misconceptions surface and if so, how were they addressed?','283c68a2ef04a4ce3d6a505b897c95877a3aa012e55b79becf39f7f9b7571610','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(30,1,1,29,41,41,'17\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\n5 minutes Reflection\n7. Reflect and share your views on the session. One thing that worked well, what did not work \nwell and what you will do differently. (NTS 1a, 1b).\n8. Identify a critical friend to observe and provide feedback on your lesson in relation to the \nPLC Session conducted (NTS 1f, 1g).\n9. Remember to\na. transfer your chats into your learning planner template.\nb. read your Teacher Manual and related Learner Material in preparation for the next \nsession. Take note of the recap weeks, which are linked to Years 1 and 2 (NTS 3a).\nc. integrate National Values, GESI, SEL, and 21st century skills into your learning \nactivities, assessment tasks, and rubrics/mark schemes.','ce1b9593858d78a95a85419b6fe9863959b264bd08ae41a385a9e509bd6a93ae','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(31,1,1,30,42,42,'18\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & VariationsSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nWEEK 2\nLearning Indicator: Make intelligent guessing to establish valid arguments and draw \nlogical conclusions\nFOCAL AREA 1: INTELLIGENT GUESSING\nObserve the pattern of Fibonacci numbers: 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, …\nTo make an intelligent guess of the next number, a careful study of the trend must be made. \nAsking useful questions will help. For example: How are successive terms behaving? The next \nquestion to ask may be: What is the nature of the change that is taking place?\nIn this case, the value at a particular term is added to the previous one to obtain the next term.\nAn intelligent guess can now be made.\nExample 1\nObserve the pattern and make a guess of the next 3 terms: 1, 3, 6, 10, 15, . . .\nJustify why you think your guess is correct and provide a generalisation to support similar \nworks.\nSolution\nThe next 3 numbers will be 21, 28 and 36.\nThe guess is correct because the successive difference between the terms increases by 1\nThat is: 1 + 2=3,\n3 + 3=6,\n6 + 4=10,\n10 + 5 = 15\nSo, the next number will be\n15 + (5 + 1) = 21,\n21 + (6 + 1) = 28 and so\n28 + (7 + 1) = 36\nThe rule for the pattern = \n()nn\n2\n1+\n\nTo verify, the 8\nth\nterm = \n()\n2\n881+\n= \n2\n72\n= 36','bada9462656e7086b8e3cbd9caa1a6cda2875db0e3ef88693da110084baf5be6','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(32,1,1,31,43,43,'19\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nExample 2\nA worker is tasked to fill a 6400 cubic litre tank with water using a twenty-litre bucket. The \nworker can fill the tank with 5 buckets a day. However, the tank leaks out 2 litres of water each \nday. How many days will it take for the labourer to fill the tank to the brim?\nSolution\nThere is a pattern by which the tank is being filled\n5 times 20 litres a day = 100 litres of water a day being put in\nReduction of 2 litres a day means there is 98 litres of water remaining in the tank a day.\nThe number of days to fill the tank = 6400 ÷ 98\n= 65 days with 30 litres more to be filled.\nTherefore, on the 66\nth\nday, the worker would have completed the work since the tank will be \nfull.\nExample 3\nMary Akugiri is making a decision on which course to offer at the tertiary level and has three \ncondition, A, B and C to consider. The conditions have been put in a Venn diagram as below.Figure 2.1: Venn diagram on conditions for choice of a course\na. Guess any three of such potential conditions you will consider and describe what region \n‘t’ means to students who find themselves in that region.\nb. How will you describe students who finds themselves in C ∩ (A ∪ B)\nI\nSolution\na. Three likely conditions will be the cost involved in offering the course, the prospects of \nthe course and the duration of the course.\nIf A = cost of the course, B = prospect of the course and C = duration of the course \nthen, region “t”: That is the intersection of all three regions, suggests that all three \nconditions will have to be considered to make a decision on the choice of course to offer. \nCourse cost, duration and prospects are all concern','139d16a28e9766a17cdc8591e6170d5cfaab8d6bc08d1ef3331f96446c7b1dfe','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(33,1,1,32,44,44,'20\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & VariationsSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nb. C ∩ (A ∪ B)\nI\ncan be read as C only. This means that students in that region consider only \nduration of the course in making decision of a particular course to offer.\nDecision Making In Solving Problems\nLogical reasoning provides the opportunity to use established patterns to predict the likely \noccurrence of situations and so be able to decide on how to solve problems. To make a decision, \none must consider certain factors. The process of considering such factors implies you are \nreasoning logically.\nExample 4: Deciding on which mode of transportation\nIn Ghana, some of the transportation modes are tro-tro, STC, airplane, private car hiring and \nokada.\nFor each of the following scenarios, explain why you will decide on a particular mode of \ntransportation.\na. Travelling from Goaso to Accra, partly along a dusty road, for an interview after school\nb. Travelling from Kaneshi with a family of 7 to Madina, both within Accra\nc. Travelling from home to buy wakye at a Wakye joint through a traffic prone area\nSolution\na. Interviews are time bound and one must reach there neat, punctually and less stressed.\nSTS is more accessible than airplane, less expensive than hiring a private car, more \ncomfortable and can be faster since they don’t make multiple stops like tro-tros.\nI will make a logical decision to go for STC.\nb. From Kaneshi to Madina, both being within Accra.\nTro-tro is less expensive and more intra-city friendly than STC and hiring a private car, \nand can hold the family size.\nBased on these logical reasons, I will decision to go for tro-tro.\nc. Traffic congestion requires a swifter transportation mode. It is not cost effective to spend \nmore money on the means of transportation than the cost of the wakye.\nOkada is swift within the traffic prone areas and less expensive.\nI decide to go for the okada based on these reasons.\nExample 5: Deciding where to sell farm produce\nIn a farming community in Ahafo Region, a farmer has the opportunity to either sell their crops \nto market-women who come to the village to buy foodstuffs or to send their produce to the \nmarket on a market day to sell. Suggest the logical reasons on which the farmer will decide to','7b627b58bf22d589da02dc745a56e05bbc31e50910a8bd9f73cc3bc4cc7ad8e0','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(34,1,1,33,45,45,'21\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nsell the foodstuff to market-women who come to the village or to send them to the market to \nsell.\nSolution\nSelling to the market-women who come to the village:\nWhen the foodstuffs are sold to market-women who come to the village, the farmer will avoid \ntransportation costs.\nThere is the guarantee of a ready market once the buyers are at the village rather than sending \nthe items to the market.\nStress is reduced as there is no conveying the goods to the market.\nThe price of the foodstuffs might be higher since the number of farmers selling their goods is \nfewer than the main market on a market day.\nBased on these reasons, the farmer should sell the foodstuffs to the market women who come \nto the village.\nOn the other hand, selling their produce to the market:\nWhen the foodstuffs are sent to the market, more buyers will be available than selling to the \nfew women who come to the village. Demand will increase thereby maybe getting a better \nprice.\nGoing to the market gives the farmer the opportunity to access other items they might need \nwhich will not be found in the village.\nThe farmer will have the opportunity to compare prices from other farmers of the same produce \nand so may not be cheated by the women who go to the village.\nBy weighing these reasons logically, the farmer should send the crops to the market on the \nmarket day.\nExample 6\nDuring a game of Ludo, Odeibea realised that:\nX: If she plays a 6, she can capture Yaw’s marble.\nY: If she captures Yaw’s marble, she will have her final marble in the play zone of Yaw and \ncould be captured by the opponent.\nZ: Yaw will take his turn throwing the dice, and Odeibea notices that Yaw’s marble is in a \nvulnerable position.\nUsing intelligent guessing and logical reasoning, should Odeibea aim at playing a 6 and capture \nYaw’s marble or not? Support your conclusion with valid arguments.','2e9d4eb02050d95a1bb7dcff7b1c0b1764e6d63c480b11f1a621ee620f619eb7','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(35,1,1,34,46,46,'22\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & VariationsSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nSolution\nCapturing Yaw’s marble could prevent Yaw’s progress which will be to the advantage of \nOdeibea.\nOdeibea’s final marble has the risk of being captured by Yaw or not.\nPlaying a 6 gives Odeibea another chance to play again ahead of Yaw.\nHence capturing Yaw’s marble will delay Yaw’s chances of taking a turn and also from \ncompleting the game first.\nThere are 6 numbers on the ludo die and it is not a definite event that Yaw will play a number \nthat will help him to capture Odeibea’s marble. Yaw has a slim chance of capturing Odeibea’s \nmarble and so Odeibea should target playing a 6 and go ahead to capture Yaw’s marble.\nLearning Tasks\nYour school has received funding to build four new facilities (a science laboratory, \na library for e-learning, an athletics field and a 300-capacity dormitory) at four \ndifferent zones of the campus.\nIn your group, discuss the logical constraints, and develop and explain your decision-\nmaking strategy that satisfy all requirements about the placement of the new facilities \nat your school.\nFOCAL AREA 2: USING TABLES AND DIAGRAMS IN \nDECISIONS MAKING\nIn the previous focal area, we considered making decisions in solving problems based on \ncertain factors that are logically reasonable. Here we will represent statements on Venn diagrams \nand make decisions from existing Venn diagrams having considered these logical factors.\nExample 7\nA student is planning whether to attend an optional extra class and whether to study at home. \nThe decision is based on the following conditions:\nP: The student attends the extra class.\nQ: The student studies at home.\nThe student believes: “If I attend the extra class and I also study at home, then I will pass the \ntest.” This can be expressed as: (P ∧ Q) → R, where R means “I will pass the test”.\nUse a truth table to:\na. Complete the truth values for the compound statement (P ∧ Q) → R.\nb. Based on your truth table, explain under which combinations of actions the student is \nguaranteed not to fail the test.','3027c22aa5c6822f2039b38937e0070d29b9a3b721257a9c664845aa935adccb','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(36,1,1,35,47,47,'23\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nc. Justify which action(s) the student should take to maximise the chance of passing , using \nthe truth table and logical reasoning.\nSolution\na. Given that\nP: Student attends the extra class\nQ: Student studies at home\nR: Student passes the test\nFor: (P ∧ Q) → R\nTable 2.1: Truth Table\nPQR P ∧ Q (P ∧ Q) → R\nTTT T T\nTTF T F\nTFT F T\nTFF F T\nFTT F T\nFTF F T\nFFT F T\nFFF F T\na. The student does not fail when R is true (i.e., passes the test).\nFrom the table, R is true in Rows 1, 3, 5 and 7. So, the student is not guaranteed to pass \njust by acting in a certain way. The outcome depends on R, which is a result and not a \ndirect action.\nBut if we look at when (P ∧ Q) → R is false, that only happens in Row 2.\nThus, if the student attends the extra class and studies at home, but still fails, then the \nlogical statement fails.\nb. To maximise the chance of passing, the student should attend the extra class (P) and study \nat home (Q). This makes P ∧ Q = T, and to keep the implication true, R must be T. So, if \nthe student believes the implication is valid, this is the safest strategy to ensure passing.\nExample 8\nConsider the following statements:\n• x: There is no short man who is cruel.\n• y: Some cruel men are intelligent.','d2ff106f949b6ff846ab5703e3b820d6cad8dd118fc9dc14c30c51206c50eaa6','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(37,1,1,36,48,48,'24\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & VariationsSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nDraw a Venn diagram to illustrate the above and deduce which of the following statements are \nvalid conclusions from the statements x and y, and which are false.\ni. Yaw is a short man ⇒ Yaw is not intelligent.\nii. Kwame is a short and intelligent man ⇒ Kwame is not cruel.\niii. Kofi is tall ⇒ Kofi is cruel but not intelligent.\niv. Which of these will you choose as a friend and why?\nSolution\nLet U = {Man}\nC = {Cruel men}\nS = {Short men}\nI = {Intelligent men}\nFrom S ∩ C = Ф\nFigure 2.2\ni. Yaw is a short man ⇒ Yaw is not intelligent.\nThis is not valid the statement x and y do not connect. There is therefore no justification \nfor the statement.\nii. Kwame is a short and intelligent man ⇒ Kwame is not cruel.\nThis is a valid statement since Kwame is short then he is not cruel.\nThat is, C ∩ S = Ф\niii. Kofi is tall ⇒ Kofi is cruel but not intelligent.\nThere is no justification since there is no direct implication between tallness and cruelty \nor intelligence.\niv. Which of these will you choose as a friend and why?\nThe decision to choose any of the persons should be out of the good values of the \nindividual, not based on height or intelligence. An individual’s decision can be subject \nto personal preference.','a5de0ed459a63de321f995d3f3af1b07da5710bcc3267be5cd0d8ff8abe9151e','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(38,1,1,37,49,49,'25\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nExample 9\nConsider these statements\nThe new curriculum offers students opportunity to undertake these combinations.\nS: Students who do Elective Science do Additional Math\nR: Students who do Additional Math do not do Core Math\na. Represent the implications of the information using symbols\nb. Illustrate R and S on a Venn diagram and justify whether the implications are valid or not\ni. Esi offers Additional Mathematics ⇒ Esi is a science student\nii. Mintah does not do core mathematics ⇒ Minta is not a science student\niii. Fio is a science student ⇒ Fio does not study core Mathematics\nc. Explain to your Junior sibling who will be coming to SHTS 1, which of these subjects he/\nshe will be doing if they want to offer Robotics.\nSolution\nNote: Accept appropriate alternative notations and explanations\na. Represent the implications of the information using symbols\nS: Students who do Elective Science do Additional Math\nR: Students who do Additional Math do not do Core Math\nTherefore, by implication, S⇒R\nb. Illustrate R and S on a Venn diagram and justify whether the implications are valid or not\nFigure 2.3\nLet E be students who do elective science\nM be students who do Additional mathematics\nC be students who do core mathematics\ni. Given that E ⇒ M does not imply M ⇒ E\nTherefore, Esi offers Additional Mathematics ⇒ Esi is a science student is not valid.\nii. Mintah does not do core mathematics ⇒ Minta is not a science student','94d0711cf25d647f3aff1700c73a56ebd44aa40e8a7cde0503cffd9057a0d92a','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(39,1,1,38,50,50,'26\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & VariationsSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nC\n1\n⇒ R or C\n1\n⇒ E\nSince there are two likely options, Mintah’s condition satifies only “C\n1\n⇒E”.\nThe statement is invalid.\niii. Fio is a science student ⇒ Fio does not study core Mathematics\nE ⇒ M and M ⇒ C\n1\n, therefore, E ⇒ C.\nHence the statement is valid.\nc. Explain to your Junior sibling who will be coming to SHS 1 next year, which of these \nsubjects he/she will be doing if they want to offer Robotics.\nRobotics is a science related subject which applies elective mathematics concepts.\nThey will therefore offer Elective science and Additional mathematics.\nLearning Tasks\nThe class believes that the development of Ghana’s natural resources should be \nguided by mathematical modelling rather than meeting immediate economic needs.\nIn your group, select a motion to speak for or against. Prepare to participate in the \ndebate discussing your points through pyramid discussion – here, learners jot down \ninitial thoughts, examples, and evidence. share their ideas with a partner, building on \neach other’s thoughts. Two pairs combine to form groups of four, and the combining \nof small groups continue until a single group is formed with a single set of thoughts, \nexample and evidence is gathered.\nPEDAGOGICAL EXEMPLARS\nCollaborative Learning\n1. Engage learners in groups, learners discuss to establish and analyse arguments to carry \nout investigation on logical reasoning including conjunctions.\n2. Engage learners in mixed ability groups through problem-based learning approaches to \ninvestigate the situation where they logically establish valid arguments and draw logical \nconclusions in solving problems (in compound statements), ensuring equity at all stages.\n3. Engage learners in groups, to discuss, establish and analyse arguments using diagrams \n(truth tables) to carry out investigations on logical reasoning including conjunctions\nIndependent Learning\n1. Engage individual learners to debate and defend their position on a decision using logical \nand valid statements.\n2. Engage individual learners to write a summary of a real-life case they know and suggest \nsolutions by giving logical reasoning and valid arguments.','356ac43bb847c3efcb1e5c00a63e447af05aa63322bb658a69d465922f40f045','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(40,1,1,39,51,51,'27\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nKEY ASSESSMENT LEVEL 3: DEBATE\n1. Consider the following statements:\n• p: All farmers are hardworking.\n• q: No idle person is hardworking.\nUsing intelligent guessing and logical reasoning state whether or not each of the following is a \nvalid conclusion from p and q:\ni. All farmers are not idle people. 2 marks\nii. Farmers who are idle are not hardworking. 2 marks\niii. Kudjo is hardworking, therefore he is a farmer. 2 marks\nTotal 6 marks (DOK Level 2)\n2. Consider the following statements:\n• p: Madam Kukua has leadership skills.\n• q: Madam Kukua heads an SHS.\nIf p⇒q, which of the following statements are valid?\ni. If Madam Kukua does not head an SHS, then she does not have leadership skills.\n2 marks\nii. If Madam Kukua heads an SHS, then she has leadership skills. 2 marks\niii. If Madam Kukua has leadership skills, then she heads an SHS. 2 marks\nTotal 6 marks (DOK Level 2)\n3. Generate a motion to be debated on with your classmates. As a panel member of the debate \nexplain at least 4 tangible points for and 4 tangible points against, that you would expect \nfrom the debaters.\nTotal 12 marks (DOK Level 3)\n4. Adwoa gave these points during a presentation on the growing some flowers.\nThe flowers grown in the store room had leaves which were very deep green and very \nhealthy.\nWhen the flowers were brought into the open Adwoa noticed that, after one week, that \nthe leaves had started turning yellow and were withering.\nOn reflection, Danso was of the view that since sunshine supports plant growth, Adwoa’s \npresentation was false.\nAnalyse the validity of Danso’s view.\nTotal 10 marks (DOK Level 3)','f1ba633d2f80dc59384ea48b5703a2a60e5d7da8374d56c72b70304142b931c9','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(41,1,1,40,52,52,'28\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & VariationsSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nPLC Session 2\n45 minutes Planning & Reviewing the Learning Plan\n1. Begin the session by recapping lessons from the previous year/session/week, focusing on \nhow learning was applied.\n2. Use your subject specific app to explore the content before planning. Ask the app to\na. explain the week’s concepts in a practical way that will make it easier for learners to \nunderstand\nb. identify two or three common misconceptions learners may have on the week’s content \nand how to address them using contexts familiar to Ghanaian learners. (NTS 3a, 3g \nand 3m).\n3. Use your subject specific app to plan your lesson. Ask the app to\na. suggest the most effective method for teaching the week’s concepts to learners with \ndiverse backgrounds.\nb. suggest learning activities, differentiation and essential questions based on the method \nof teaching (NTS 3a-3q)\nc. review your prepared learning plan and address any challenging issues you may have \nidentified in your subject area (NTS 3a-3c).\nNote\n• Take note of the recap topics linked to Years 1 and 2 while preparing for your \nlesson.\n• Refer to the Values Learning Community Handbook and link the values for the \nweek in your lesson to ensure that learners are applying what they learn.\n4. Plan the week’s key assessment with your app\na. The key assessment for the week is debate . Chat with your app to plan this assessment, \nindicating what you and your learners will do before, during and after the debate (NTS \n3k-3q).\n10 minutes Content Exploration\n5. As a subject group, select one problem or idea/term in the week’s content that is challenging \n(not a routine procedural question, but one that requires conceptual understanding) and \nthen\na. use the app, ask it to solve the problem step by step and explain the reasoning behind \neach step (you may be assisted by a teacher who is strong in the content area)\nb. all teachers in the group work through a similar problem individually for 3–4 minutes\nc. the group then works through the problem together step by step','3481b66abeb0bdd6273b9455cebdf2db976aabb3fe4b439004e43e470a84c1af','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(42,1,1,41,53,53,'29\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nd. highlight the most common errors made at each step and discuss (NTS 1c, 1e, 2c, 2e, \n3e-3j).\nNote\n• This part is not just about ‘How do I teach this?’ but how do I teach it for learners \nto understand?\n• If the group still shares the same content gap, use the app: ask it to solve the \nproblem step by step and explain the reasoning behind each step.\n• Problems/ideas discussed should be sourced from the Teacher Manual content, not \ninvented separately. This keeps the content exploration directly connected to what \nteachers will deliver.\n30 minutes Enactment\n6. Enact a teaching activity based on your learning plan (NTS la-1b, 1e-lg, 2c, 2e, 2f, 3c-3j \nand 3o).\na. Colleagues observe with specific attention to clarity of concept explanation, use of \nfamiliar Ghanaian examples and how misc`onceptions are handled if they arise.\nb. After the enactment, ask for structured feedback from the group\ni. was the concept explained in a way that a learner with no prior knowledge could \nfollow?\nii. were the examples grounded in contexts familiar to Ghanaian learners?\niii. did any misconceptions surface and if so, how were they addressed?\n5 minutes Reflection\n7. Reflect and share your views on the session. One thing that worked well, what did not work \nwell and what you will do differently. (NTS 1a, 1b).\n8. Identify a critical friend to observe and provide feedback on your lesson in relation to the \nPLC Session conducted (NTS 1f, 1g).\n9. Remember to\na. transfer your chats into your learning planner template.\nb. read your Teacher Manual and related Learner Material in preparation for the next \nsession. Take note of the recap weeks, which are linked to Years 1 and 2 (NTS 3a).\nc. integrate National Values, GESI, SEL, and 21st century skills into your learning \nactivities, assessment tasks, and rubrics/mark schemes.','b9a7ab8b42d625c9bd6b3c286aa2a104901e9aeaba45248e638d7a5755c3c686','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(43,1,1,42,54,54,'30\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & VariationsSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nWEEK 3\nLearning Indicator: Carry out mini real-life investigations to solve problems involving \nlogical reasoning, within their local community\nFOCAL AREA: REAL-LIFE APPLICATIONS OF LOGICAL \nREASONING\nStudents make arguments among peers and, occasionally, those who can shout louder, win the \nargument (debate) of the day. This might not be a fair situation since some winners might not \nnecessarily have raised the most valid points.\nHere we will look at how we can support learners to generate and test the validity of logical \narguments from peers and investigate to solve problems involving logical reasoning, within \ntheir local community.\nExamples of situations learners argue on include:\n1. Can Ghana ever win a world cup?\n2. Which school will win the next National Science and Mathematics Quiz?\n3. Who will become the next school prefect?\n4. Will the mathematics teacher come to class today?\nSome of the responses may seem obvious but that is as a result of a set of logical occurrences \nover a long period of time. Other issues are not easy to make judgement and conclusions on \nthem. It is easy to make claims on some statements as valid or not.\nExample 1\n1. The class prefect is the first to come to class ⇒ The class prefect was the first to come to \nclass today. Verify the statement as valid or not.\nSolution\nDoes the class prefect really come to class first?\nDid the class prefect come to class before the teacher?\nIf one statement is false then we do not have a valid statement.\n2. A student’s subjects that require improvement are Mathematics and Science ⇒ A student \nfailed in mathematics in the previous examination.\nSolution\nIs the student’s subjects that require improvement Mathematics and Science?\nAre my weakest subjects that require improvement Mathematics and Science?','d46e3c39002c2b4cb41e4bed51974ddd5615245465b84929875a2615bf8b0e67','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(44,1,1,43,55,55,'31\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nIf Mathematics and Science are my weakest subjects, did I fail the previous examination?\nWhat logical conclusions do we make on this statement – valid or not?\nInvestigating Arguments\nTo solve a problem, we may consciously ,take some steps or perform certain activities. These \nsteps may not be answers in themselves, but are leading us towards answers. In that case, we \nsay we are investigating . These steps to approaching a problem can be:\n1. Identify the objective for the investigation\n2. Gather data on the subject using interview, survey, etc.\n3. Analyse data to identify patterns and findings\n4. Conclude on the argument\nSimilarly, Jerome Brunner is of the view that one needs to understand a problem, design a plan, \nimplement the plan and finally look back (Okafor, 2019).\nSome challenging situations that might require a solution\n1. Your food-box is empty.\n2. The end of semester exams is coming up but the expected scope of work has not been \ncovered.\n3. The landlord is ejecting you from a room for no fault of yours.\nNote: Guide learners to pose a problem for their peers to generate their own steps in solving \na problem.\nExample 2\nSHS 3 students are expected to be 17 years. We are in SHS 3 so every member is 17 years.\nSolution\nWe wish to verify the argument of the age of SHS 3 students.\nTo do that each student should state their age (E.g. 17, 20, 18, 16, 16, 17, 17, 18, 16, 16, 17, 18).\nIs everybody 17 years of age?\nThe expected age is the mean age. Let’s find the mean age of the class?\nx206=/\n= 17.17\nThe mean age = \n12\n206\n= 17 years.\nWhat conclusion can we draw?','acdf2de682076f5bbb3f680cb12cfbf5d816e4014c2288d38b98fe49589d0134','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(45,1,1,44,56,56,'32\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & VariationsSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nExample 3\nThe school has received a technology grant of GH¢ 20,000.00 that must be spent on either \nupgrading the computer lab or providing tablets for the mathematics department.\nIn your groups, suggest logical reasons for which of the options will be most appropriate for \nthe school.\nSolution\nUpgrading the computer lab and providing tablets for the Mathematics Department are both \ngood. Upgrading the computer lab will secure the existing lab from deterioration and have a \nfacelift.\nHowever, the number of computers in the school are few and have a series of challenges so the \ntablets will rather increase access to ICT tools.\nUpgrading the computer lab is capital intensive and the amount may not cater for the lab as well \nas bringing the outmoded computers to current standard and capacity.\nThe tablets are handy and can be taken along for field trips and where research will be carried \non. The desktops at the computer lab are too bulky to be carried about.\nThe challenge with power outages is makes it unreliable when using the laptop. In case of \npower outage, the tablets can be used for some time without going off.\nThese factors among others are cogent enough to consider providing tablets and upgrading the \ncomputer lab for the Mathematics Department.\nNote\nEngage learners in groups to gather information on the most logical way to optimise \nthe use of water and electricity in your school. Let them carry out their plan of gathering \ninformation and present their findings appreciating the findings of other groups.\nYou may look out for:\nLearning Tasks\nLearners to undertake research on real-life application of logical reasoning and \npresent their findings to the class:\nLearners plan in groups, how they can gather information on the most logical way to \noptimise the use of water and electricity in their school. They carry out their plans in \ngathering information and present their findings while appreciating the findings of \nother groups.\nLearners to demonstrate knowledge and skill in:\n1. Well-developed appropriate tool for gathering information\n2. Collaborative implementation of data collection process','27923fbac21388cae6488f4edc166ddd94f79b329bf8ba27d4ccd7942285125c','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(46,1,1,45,57,57,'33\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\n3. Well-organised data to be presented on paper or orally or power point\n4. Valid conclusion drawn on the means to optimise the use of water and electricity \nin your school\nPEDAGOGICAL EXEMPLARS\n1. In collaborative and gender responsive groups, learners generate and test the validity of \nlogical arguments from peers, then investigations to solve problems involving logical \nreasoning, within their local community\n2. Using Problem-Based Learning approaches learners, in mixed ability groups, carry out \nmini projects by visiting interesting areas in the community to generate and analyse \nsituational growth and intelligent guessing and present their report to the whole class for \nintelligent discourse.\n3. Individuals to share their appreciation of their involvement in the projects.\nKEY ASSESSMENT LEVEL 4: GROUP PROJECT\n1. If the computer lab is upgraded, all students will have access, but each student will have \nlimited time. Explain 3 reasons why you think the motion is or is not valid.\nTotal 8 marks (DOK Level 4)\n2. In a school, the head of the Mathematics Department is requesting tablets. The head of \nthe Agricultural Science department thinks that if tablets are purchased for the \nmathematics department, only mathematics students will benefit and they will have \ncontinuous access during mathematics lessons at the expense of Agricultural science \ndepartment.\nGive 3 reason why you think the Agricultural Science Department head’s view is or is \nnot valid?\nTotal 8 marks (DOK Level 4)\n3. There is erosion in every part of your home or school community. In convenient groups \nof where members stay (boarding or day), verify from the stakeholders of your community \nwhich of these two: growing of grass and use of stones, to solve the erosion problem will \nbe reasonable.\nPresent your findings for discourse in class.\nTotal 15 marks (DOK Level 4)\nHint\nAssign the group project to learners and give them a reasonable time to complete it. Use \nthe SS App to generate the project and the rubric. Learners’ projects should be marked \nimmediately, and scores entered into the SAP.','b8689e8c463ab988aaffebe1d645e53ec411ed83179fdce5cef7c55f9b66e4b7','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(47,1,1,46,58,58,'34\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & VariationsSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nPLC Session 3\n45 minutes Planning & Reviewing the Learning Plan\n1. Begin the session by recapping lessons from the previous session, focusing on how learning \nwas applied.\n2. Use your subject specific app to explore the content before planning. Ask the app to\na. explain the week’s concepts in a practical way that will make it easier for learners to \nunderstand.\nb. identify three common misconceptions learners may have on the week’s content and \nhow to address them using contexts familiar to Ghanaian learners (NTS 3a, 3g and \n3m).\n3. Use your subject specific app to plan your lesson. Ask the app to\na. suggest the most effective method for teaching the week’s concepts to learners with \ndiverse backgrounds.\nb. suggest learning activities, differentiation and essential questions based on the method \nof teaching (NTS 3a-3q).\nc. review your prepared learning plan and address any challenging issues you may have \nidentified in your subject area (NTS 3a-3c).\nNote\n• Take note of the recap topics linked to Years 1 and 2 while preparing for your \nlesson.\n• Refer to the Values Learning Community Handbook and link the values for the \nweek in your lesson to ensure that learners are applying what they learn.\n4. Use your app to plan and administer the mode of assessment for the Student Assessment \nPortal (SAP).\na. The key assessment for the week is group project . Chat with your app to plan this \nassessment by reviewing or developing the assessment items/tasks and marking \nschemes/rubrics for the project (NTS 3k-3q).\n10 minutes Content Exploration\n5. As a subject group, select one problem or topic/idea/term in the week’s content that is \nchallenging (not a routine procedural question, but one that requires conceptual \nunderstanding) and then\na. use the app, ask it to solve the problem step by step and explain the reasoning behind \neach step (you may be assisted by a teacher who is strong in the content area)\nb. all teachers in the group work through the problem individually for 3–4 minutes','8eae37bbff5422b54501b59f03659fc8f2cca41aa03ed68c32664ee6da3074f0','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(48,1,1,47,59,59,'35\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nc. the group then works through it together the problem, step by step\nd. highlight the most common errors made at each step (NTS 1c, 1e, 2c, 2e, 3e-3j).\nNote\n• This part is not ‘How do I teach this?’ but how do I teach it for learners to understand?\n• If the group still shares the same content gap, use the app: ask it to solve the \nproblem step by step and explain the reasoning behind each step.\n• Problems/ideas discussed should be sourced from the Teacher Manual content, not \ninvented separately. This keeps the content exploration directly connected to what \nteachers will deliver.\n30 minutes Enactment\n6. Enact a teaching activity based on your learning plan (NTS la-1b, 1e-lg, 2c, 2e, 2f, 3c-3j \nand 3o).\na. Colleagues observe with specific attention to clarity of concept explanation, use of \nfamiliar Ghanaian examples and how misconceptions are handled if they arise.\nb. After the enactment, ask for structured feedback from the group\ni. was the concept explained in a way that a learner with no prior knowledge could \nfollow?\nii. were the examples grounded in contexts familiar to Ghanaian learners?\niii. did any misconceptions surface and if so, how were they addressed?\n5 minutes Reflection\n7. Reflect and share your views on the session. One thing that worked well, what did not work \nwell and what you will do differently. (NTS 1a, 1b).\n8. Identify a critical friend to observe and provide feedback on your lesson in relation to the \nPLC Session conducted (NTS 1f, 1g).\n9. Remember to\na. transfer your chats into your learning planner template.\nb. read your Teacher Manual and related Learner Material in preparation for the next \nsession. Take note of the recap weeks, which are linked to Years 1 and 2 (NTS 3a).\nc. integrate National Values, GESI, SEL, and 21st century skills into your learning \nactivities, assessment tasks, and rubrics/mark schemes.','fca5215e6b7e9f047a452e0a36e045fce64e6850fcfc563529aface4196fdf6c','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(49,1,1,48,60,60,'36\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & VariationsSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nWEEK 4\nLearning Indicator: Use proportional reasoning to investigate the various types of \nvariation (direct and inverse) and extend this to make generalisations\nFOCAL AREA: VARIATION 1: INTRODUCTION TO VARIATION\nIn many real-life situations, we observe that changes in one quantity leads to predictable \nchange(s) in another. For example, the more time you spend studying, the better your \nunderstanding tends to become. In mathematics, such relationships between variables are \nstudied under the concept of variation .\nIn this section, we are going to look at four types of variation:\n1. Direct Variation\n2. Inverse Variation\n3. Joint Variation\n4. Partial Variation\n1. Direct Variation\nA quantity is said to vary directly as another when an increase (or decrease) in one \ncauses a proportional increase (or decrease) in the other. This is also known as direct \nproportionality.\nMathematical Representation:\nIf y varies directly as x , then:\nyxα or y = kx\nwhere k is the constant of proportionality.\nExample 1\na. The cost of bananas is directly proportional to the weight purchased.\ni. If 1 kg costs GH¢5.00, then 2 kg costs GH¢ 10.00, 3 kg costs GH¢ 15.00, and so on.\nii. This forms the relationship: C = 5 w, where C is the cost and w is the weight in kg.\nb. The distance travelled by a car moving at constant speed is directly proportional to the \ntime taken:\nd = st\nwhere s is the speed and t is time.','860c4027a8386cbe71879685fa230a3dcabf6348ee6dc56a544c2d39574b1ce9','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(50,1,1,49,61,61,'37\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\n2. Inverse Variation\nA quantity is said to vary inversely as another when an increase in one causes a \nproportional decrease in the other.\nMathematical Representation:\nIf y varies inversely as x , then:\n1\ny\nx\nα\nor \nk\ny\nx\n=\nwhere k is the constant of proportionality.\nExample 2\na. The time taken to complete a job is inversely proportional to the number of people working \non it.\ni. More people = less time;\nii. Fewer people = more time.\nb. The speed of a vehicle is inversely proportional to the time taken for a fixed distance:\nd\ns\nt\n=. Rearranged as \nd\nt\ns\n=, which shows inverse variation between t and s .\nUsing Proportional Reasoning\nProportional reasoning involves comparing ratios and using them to solve problems involving \nvariation.\na. In direct variation, the ratio \ny\nx\nis constant.\nb. In inverse variation, the product xy is constant.\nTable 4.1: Comparison:\nType of Variation Relationship General FormConstant Quantity\nDirect Variation y ∝ x y = kx\nInverse Variation\ny\nx\n1\na y\nx\nk\n=\nxy = k\nGeneralisations from Variation\nBy investigating different scenarios, students can generalise:\na. In direct variation, doubling one variable doubles the other.\nb. In inverse variation, if one variable is doubled, the other is halved (assuming a constant \nproduct).\nc. The graphs of direct variation are straight lines through the origin.\nx\ny\nk=','f49ba10a4d47f5075be35894f11eaed95d822fea95028447f639b770be88e437','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(51,1,1,50,62,62,'38\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & VariationsSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nd. The graphs of inverse variation are rectangular hyperbolas, or a ‘reciprocal graph’.\nFigure 4.1: Graph of linear variation\nFigure 4.2: Graph of inverse variation\nExample 3\n1. A bag of rice costs GH¢ 120.00. The cost (C) varies directly with the number of bags (n).\na. Express the cost (C) in terms of n .\nb. If a customer pays GH¢ 600.00, how many bags did they buy?\nSolution\na. C ∝ n. this implies C = kn\n120 = k (1)\nk = 120\nC = 120n\nb. C = 120n\nGiven, C = 600\n600 = 120n\n\n600\n120\nn=\nn = 5\nThe customer paid 600 for 5 bags.','0c512f4f6fe9e9274e7ec27e3d076a99340276ac0427db4d7895c32db328fbfd','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(52,1,1,51,63,63,'39\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\n2. The time (T) taken to travel from Accra to Kumasi is inversely proportional to the speed \n(S) of a vehicle: At 60 km/h, the trip takes 4 hours.\na. Derive the equation and find k (the constant of proportionality).\nb. Calculate the time if speed increases to 80 km/h.\nc. Debate: “Why might this inverse model fail during the festive seasons?”\nSolution\na. Deriving the equation\n1\nT\nS\nα\nk\nT\nS\n⇒=\nGiven, T = 4 hours and S = 60km/h\n4\n60\nk\n⇒=\n240k=\n240\nT\nS\n=\nb. Calculate time when speed is 80 km/h\n240\nT\nS\n=\n240\n80\nT=\nT = 3 hours\nThe time when speed is 80 km/h is 3 hours\nc. Debate: Why might this inverse model fail during the festive seasons?\nDuring the festive season, factors such as increased traffic congestion, delays, roadblocks, \nor accidents may occur. These factors break the assumption of such a high constant \nspeed and free flow because traffic will cause stationary positions and therefore there \nwill be no constant speed.\nIt can be concluded that the model is limited during unforeseen circumstances.\n3. A farming research team is developing a mathematical model to understand how \nenvironmental factors affect crop yield. They discover that crop yield (Y) varies directly \nwith rainfall (R) and inversely with temperature (T). In a specific trial: When R = 100 mm \nand T = 30°C, the yield Y = 50 kg.\na. Using the relationship described, derive the model that expresses Y in terms of R and \nT, and explain what the constant of proportionality represents in real-life context.\nb. Use the model to predict the yield when R = 150 mm and T = 25°C, and justify \nwhether this prediction is reasonable for a real-world setting.','d5be941c7d02e0c9de6225fbd489bfad8325d1de3ad436f69345f22c4f072780','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(53,1,1,52,64,64,'40\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & VariationsSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nc. Explain how the model might change if fertiliser use is introduced as an additional \nfactor that enhances yield regardless of rainfall and temperature. Support your model \nwith reasoning or hypothetical data.\nSolution\na. Derive the model and interpret constant\nR\nY\nT\nα\nkR\nY\nT\n⇒=\n100\n50\n30\nk\n=\n50 30 100k×=\n15k=\nThe model that expresses Y in terms of R and T is given by \n15R\nY\nT\n= .\nInterpretation k = 15: Each mm of rain per °C contributes 15 kg of yield\nb. Predict and justify reasonableness\n15R\nY\nT\n=\n15(150)\n25\nY=\n90Y=\nWith other factors that influence crop yield being constant, as rainfall increased from \n100mm (with temperature 30°C) to 150mm (with a lower temperature of 25°C), crop \nyield increased from 50kg to 90kg. This means that, more rain and lower temp should \nlead to higher yield of 90 kg.\nc. Predict and justify reasonableness\nkR\nYf\nT\n= + , where f is the fertiliser impact\nPlants will grow with or without fertiliser. However, fertiliser acts independently to \nincrease yield, especially in poor conditions.\nSo where R = 100 mm and T = 30°C with K = 15, Y = 50 kg\nIf f = 20kg/ha then\nY = 70kg\nIt is obvious that fertiliser has impact and as such the model might change.\n3. Joint variation\nJoint variation is a variation where two or more quantities relate to each other. For \nexample:\nThe volume V of a cylinder varies jointly with the square of the radius r and the height \nh, i.e.,\nV = kr2h','7a7148f2484884c0eddeb16dd1d1fe1017ac30566f353a0dc0c9a3aeb4aad123','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(54,1,1,53,65,65,'41\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nExample 4\n1. If y varies jointly as x and z , and y = 24 when x = 2 and z = 3, find the equation connecting \ny, x and z .\nSolution\ny αxz\ny = kxz\n24 = k × 2 × 3\n24 = 6k\nk = 4\nTherefore, the equation connecting the y, x and z.\ny = 4xz\n2. The volume of a cone, V, varies jointly as its height, h , and the square of its radius, r.\nA cone with a radius measuring 6 feet and a height measuring 10 feet has a volume of \n120 cubic feet. Find the volume of a cone having a radius of 12 feet and a height of 2 \nfeet.\nSolution\nV αhr\n2\nV = khr\n2\n120π = k(10 × 6\n2\n)\n120π = 360k\nk = \n360\n120r\nk = \n3\nr\nV = \n3\nr\n× 2 × 12\n2\nV = \n3\nr\n× 2 × 144\nV = 96π cubic feet\nLearning Tasks\nLearners to use direct or inverse variation in modelling a real-world situation and \nmake predictions. They pose problems (questions) on real-life application of direct or \ninverse variation issues and swap them with their peers to answer and give feedback.\nPEDAGOGICAL EXEMPLARS\n1. Using small group, engage learners to discuss inverse and joint variations, model and solve \nreal-life situations involving proportional reasoning.','d645c22bf4556d5f672f1b8de34a9718fad3ac73d6830d38831a4f0aed65b74b','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(55,1,1,54,66,66,'42\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & VariationsSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\n2. Relate the variation to scientific concepts and use them to solve real life problems.\nKEY ASSESSMENT LEVEL 2: ASSIGNMENT\nMCQs (DOK Level 1)\n1. Which equation best represents a situation where y varies directly with the square of x?\na. y kx=\nb. \n2\nk\ny\nx\n=\nc. \n2\ny kx=\nd. y kx=\n2. If y varies directly as x , and y = 12 when x = 4, what is the value of y when x = 10?\na. 30\nb. 48\nc. 33\nd. 22\n3. A mechanic observes that the time, t, taken to complete a job varies inversely with the \nnumber of workers w assigned. If 4 workers take 6 hours to finish the task, how many \nworkers would be required to complete the same job in 2 hours?\na. 8\nb. 10\nc. 12\nd. 14\n4. The area A of a triangle varies jointly as the base b and height h . Given that A = 30 cm² \nwhen b = 5 cm and h = 12 cm,\na. Find the constant of variation k . 3 marks\nb. Determine the area when b =10 cm and h = 15 cm. 2 marks\nc. Discuss how doubling both the base and height affects the area. 3 marks\nTotal 8 marks (DOK Level 2)\n5. The number of minutes needed to solve an exercise set of variation problems varies directly \nas the number of problems and inversely as the number of people working to solve the \nproblems. It takes 4 people 36 minutes to solve 18 problems.\nHow many minutes will it take 8 people to solve 24 problems?\nTotal 5 marks (DOK Level 2)\n6. The pressure P of a gas varies jointly as the temperature T and the number of moles n and \ninversely as the volume V . If P = 100 when n =2, T = 300 and V =10,','4f0b1cde3409b855f6b662d9e80c557518ce1d0dd93081cb9bad31eeb52e5822','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(56,1,1,55,67,67,'43\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\na. Find k . 3 marks\nb. Find P when n = 3. T = 250 and V = 12. 2 marks\nTotal 5 marks (DOK Level 2)\nPLC Session 4\n45 minutes Planning & Reviewing the Learning Plan\n1. Begin the session by recapping lessons from the previous year/session/week, focusing on \nhow learning was applied.\n2. Use your subject specific app to explore the content before planning. Ask the app to\na. explain the week’s concepts in a practical way that will make it easier for learners to \nunderstand\nb. identify two or three common misconceptions learners may have on the week’s content \nand how to address them using contexts familiar to Ghanaian learners. (NTS 3a, 3g \nand 3m).\n3. Use your subject specific app to plan your lesson. Ask the app to\na. suggest the most effective method for teaching the week’s concepts to learners with \ndiverse backgrounds.\nb. suggest learning activities, differentiation and essential questions based on the method \nof teaching (NTS 3a-3q)\nc. review your prepared learning plan and address any challenging issues you may have \nidentified in your subject area (NTS 3a-3c).\nNote\n• Take note of the recap topics linked to Years 1 and 2 while preparing for your \nlesson.\n• Refer to the Values Learning Community Handbook and link the values for the \nweek in your lesson to ensure that learners are applying what they learn.\n4. Plan the week’s key assessment with your app\na. The key assessment for the week is assignment . Chat with your app to plan this \nassessment, indicating what you and your learners will do before, during and after the \nassignment (NTS 3k-3q).\n10 minutes Content Exploration\n5. As a subject group, select one problem or idea/term in the week’s content that is challenging \n(not a routine procedural question, but one that requires conceptual understanding) and \nthen','74642eba9cf4c3dd4139a15a1d28e9f5ab42d4f94d1ad571d837b226d9e17e70','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(57,1,1,56,68,68,'44\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & VariationsSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\na. use the app, ask it to solve the problem step by step and explain the reasoning behind \neach step (you may be assisted by a teacher who is strong in the content area)\nb. all teachers in the group work through a similar problem individually for 3–4 minutes\nc. the group then works through the problem together step by step\nd. highlight the most common errors made at each step and discuss (NTS 1c, 1e, 2c, 2e, \n3e-3j).\nNote\n• This part is not just about ‘How do I teach this?’ but how do I teach it for learners \nto understand?\n• If the group still shares the same content gap, use the app: ask it to solve the \nproblem step by step and explain the reasoning behind each step.\n• Problems/ideas discussed should be sourced from the Teacher Manual content, not \ninvented separately. This keeps the content exploration directly connected to what \nteachers will deliver.\n30 minutes Enactment\n6. Enact a teaching activity based on your learning plan (NTS la-1b, 1e-lg, 2c, 2e, 2f, 3c-3j \nand 3o).\na. Colleagues observe with specific attention to clarity of concept explanation, use of \nfamiliar Ghanaian examples and how misconceptions are handled if they arise.\nb. After the enactment, ask for structured feedback from the group\ni. was the concept explained in a way that a learner with no prior knowledge could \nfollow?\nii. were the examples grounded in contexts familiar to Ghanaian learners?\niii. did any misconceptions surface and if so, how were they addressed?\n5 minutes Reflection\n7. Reflect and share your views on the session. One thing that worked well, what did not work \nwell and what you will do differently. (NTS 1a, 1b).\n8. Identify a critical friend to observe and provide feedback on your lesson in relation to the \nPLC Session conducted (NTS 1f, 1g).\n9. Remember to\na. transfer your chats into your learning planner template.\nb. read your Teacher Manual and related Learner Material in preparation for the next \nsession. Take note of the recap weeks, which are linked to Years 1 and 2 (NTS 3a).','ac705ffd5d8377aef4e41a7d299c0c65f2c1eb6c5a86f857926305a6950192af','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(58,1,1,57,69,69,'45\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nc. integrate National Values, GESI, SEL, and 21st century skills into your learning \nactivities, assessment tasks, and rubrics/mark schemes.','5512881d13bb7f3e61af63d20bbfaa57456c532055d79f2811078bba469bf4ce','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(59,1,1,58,70,70,'46\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & VariationsSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nWEEK 5\nLearning Indicator: Use proportional reasoning to investigate partial variations, make \ngeneralisations and solve real-life issues involving partial variations\nFOCAL AREA: VARIATION 2\nIntroduction\nIn everyday life, we often encounter situations where a cost or value does not start from zero \nbut includes a fixed component and a variable component. This type of relationship is known \nas partial variation. Understanding partial variation is essential in budgeting, planning \nservices, and interpreting data. It builds on direct variation but adds a constant starting value.\nUnderstanding Partial Variation\nPartial variation occurs when one quantity is made up of:\n• A fixed value (also called the constant or starting value), and\n• A variable part that changes in direct proportion to another quantity.\nThis can be modelled mathematically as:\ny = kx + c\nWhere:\n• y is the dependent variable,\n• x is the independent variable,\n• k is the rate of change (constant of proportionality),\n• c is the fixed component (non-zero starting value).\nThis equation represents a linear relationship where the graph is a straight line that does not \npass through the origin.\nExample 1\nA taxi charges a flat rate of GH¢ 8.00, plus GH¢ 2.00 per kilometre travelled.\nTotal fare (F) = 2x + 8\nWhere x is the number of kilometres travelled. The graph of this relationship is a straight line \nwith slope 2 and a y intercept of 8.','cb4c3d6f2ff0ba60eba570633c8b49a1c245a30e1adb0d3b7c8a13dbfd482e79','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(60,1,1,59,71,71,'47\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nFigure 5.1: Graph of y = 2x + 8\nExample 2\nA mobile phone company charges a fixed monthly fee of GH¢ 30.00 and an additional GH¢ \n0.10 per minute of call time. Ama’s monthly phone bill was GH¢ 65.00.\na. Form an equation that models this situation.\nb. How many minutes did Ama use?\nc. If her call time increases by 50 minutes next month, estimate her next bill and explain \nyour reasoning.\nSolution\na. T = 30 + 0.10x, where x represents the number of minutes Ama was making calls and T \nrepresents the total bill charge.\nb. 65 = 30 + 0.10x\nx = 350 minutes\nc. For her next bill: 350 + 50 = 400\nNew bill: 30 + 0.10 × 400 = 70\nAma has a fixed cost of GH¢ 30.00 to pay, plus her calls. The increase in her calls will increase \nher bill from GH¢ 65.00 to GH¢ 70.00.\nExample 3\nA student investigated the cost of printing at a shop. The cost to print 10 pages was GH¢ 14.00, \nand to print 30 pages was GH¢ 26.00.\na. Use proportional reasoning to determine the fixed charge and the cost per page.','4316201da96077182e67ce06b6cbb546eb0cd69b9069a8519f03e1245e900b18','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(61,1,1,60,72,72,'48\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & VariationsSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nb. Write the partial variation equation for the cost C and number of pages p.\nc. Plot the graph and describe its slope and intercept in context.\nd. Predict the cost for 50 pages and justify your approach.\nSolution\na. Let C be the cost, k the cost per page and b the fixed charge.\nC = kp + b\nWe have two data points:\n(10, 14) and (30, 26)\n14 = 10k + b …............1\n26 = 30k + b …...........2\nSolve equations 1 and 2 simultaneously:\nk = 0.6 and b = 8\nTherefore, the fixed charge is GH¢ 8\nCost per page = GH¢ 0.60\nb. Write the partial variation equation for the cost C and number of pages p:\nC = 0.6p + 8\nc. Plot the graph and describe its slope and intercept in context.\n• Plot points: (10, 14), (30, 26)\nFigure 5.2: Graph of the line passing through (10, 14) and (30, 26)\n• The slope = 0.6 means, for every extra page, cost increases by GH¢ 0.60\n• The intercept on the y-axis = 8 means, even if no page is printed, a setup or service fee \nof GH¢ 8 is charged\nd. Predict the cost for 50 pages and justify your approach.\nUsing the equation\nC = 0.6p + 8\nC = 0.6(50) + 8','955f13e9d2835e6d203b5173fb672a75568aa901557dfbef272f9570110690da','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(62,1,1,61,73,73,'49\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nC = 38\nTherefore, the cost is GH¢ 38, assuming the linear relationship holds.\nJustification\nWe used the linear model based on fixed and variable costs, which fits the trend \nobserved from earlier data.\nLearning Task\nLearners to use partial variations, make generalisations and solve real-life issues \ninvolving partial variations. They pose problems (questions) on real-life application \nof partial variation issues and swap them with their peers to answer and give feedback.\nPEDAGOGICAL EXEMPLARS\n1. Using group/collaborative strategies, engage learners to revise the concepts of variation \n(direct, inverse, and joint) and establish the concept of partial.\n2. Relate variation to scientific concepts and use them to solve real life problems\n3. Individuals to solve problems and model problem involving variation.\nKEY ASSESSMENT LEVEL 3: PROBLEM POSING & PEER REVIEW\n1. A plumber charges GH¢ 40.00 for a service call and GH¢ 25.00 for each hour of work.\na. Write an equation for the total charge T for h hours of work. 1 mark\nb. Determine how much a customer would pay for 6.5 hours of work. 2 marks\nc. Explain how the relationship differs from direct variation. 2 marks\nd. Modify the equation if the hourly rate increases by GH¢ 5.00 and explain how this \naffects the graph and total cost. 3 marks\nTotal 8 marks (DOK Level 3)\n2. A car rental agency charges GH¢ 120.00 per day plus GH¢ 1.50 for each kilometer driven.\na. Write a formula for the total cost C in terms of the number of days d and kilometers \nk. 1 mark\nb. A customer rents a car for 3 days and drives 200 km. What is the total cost?\n2 marks\nc. If a customer has a budget of GH¢ 500.00, what is the maximum number of kilometers \nthey can drive in 2 days? 3 marks\nTotal 6 marks (DOK Level 3)','678d8f85cab5c8891dc5141e4e7b59bd07887cba8db9f7e2db73f9babd73c5ed','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(63,1,1,62,74,74,'50\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & VariationsSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nPLC Session 5\n45 minutes Planning & Reviewing the Learning Plan\n1. Begin the session by recapping lessons from the previous year/session/week, focusing on \nhow learning was applied.\n2. Use your subject specific app to explore the content before planning. Ask the app to\na. explain the week’s concepts in a practical way that will make it easier for learners to \nunderstand\nb. identify two or three common misconceptions learners may have on the week’s content \nand how to address them using contexts familiar to Ghanaian learners. (NTS 3a, 3g \nand 3m).\n3. Use your subject specific app to plan your lesson. Ask the app to\na. suggest the most effective method for teaching the week’s concepts to learners with \ndiverse backgrounds.\nb. suggest learning activities, differentiation and essential questions based on the method \nof teaching (NTS 3a-3q)\nc. review your prepared learning plan and address any challenging issues you may have \nidentified in your subject area (NTS 3a-3c).\nNote\n• Take note of the recap topics linked to Years 1 and 2 while preparing for your \nlesson.\n• Refer to the Values Learning Community Handbook and link the values for the \nweek in your lesson to ensure that learners are applying what they learn.\n4. Plan the week’s key assessment with your app\na. The key assessment for the week is problem posing & peer review . Chat with your \napp to plan this assessment, indicating what you and your learners will do before, \nduring and after the peer review (NTS 3k-3q).\n10 minutes Content Exploration\n5. As a subject group, select one problem or idea/term in the week’s content that is challenging \n(not a routine procedural question, but one that requires conceptual understanding) and \nthen\na. use the app, ask it to solve the problem step by step and explain the reasoning behind \neach step (you may be assisted by a teacher who is strong in the content area)\nb. all teachers in the group work through a similar problem individually for 3–4 minutes\nc. the group then works through the problem together step by step','c5f238675882e5b2202610941339cc867abddaa991d5e02f7336f199d5de7401','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(64,1,1,63,75,75,'51\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nd. highlight the most common errors made at each step and discuss (NTS 1c, 1e, 2c, 2e, \n3e-3j).\nNote\n• This part is not just about ‘How do I teach this?’ but how do I teach it for learners \nto understand?\n• If the group still shares the same content gap, use the app: ask it to solve the \nproblem step by step and explain the reasoning behind each step.\n• Problems/ideas discussed should be sourced from the Teacher Manual content, not \ninvented separately. This keeps the content exploration directly connected to what \nteachers will deliver.\n30 minutes Enactment\n6. Enact a teaching activity based on your learning plan (NTS la-1b, 1e-lg, 2c, 2e, 2f, 3c-3j \nand 3o).\na. Colleagues observe with specific attention to clarity of concept explanation, use of \nfamiliar Ghanaian examples and how misconceptions are handled if they arise.\nb. After the enactment, ask for structured feedback from the group\ni. was the concept explained in a way that a learner with no prior knowledge could \nfollow?\nii. were the examples grounded in contexts familiar to Ghanaian learners?\niii. did any misconceptions surface and if so, how were they addressed?\n5 minutes Reflection\n7. Reflect and share your views on the session. One thing that worked well, what did not work \nwell and what you will do differently. (NTS 1a, 1b).\n8. Identify a critical friend to observe and provide feedback on your lesson in relation to the \nPLC Session conducted (NTS 1f, 1g).\n9. Remember to\na. transfer your chats into your learning planner template.\nb. read your Teacher Manual and related Learner Material in preparation for the next \nsession. Take note of the recap weeks, which are linked to Years 1 and 2 (NTS 3a).\nc. integrate National Values, GESI, SEL, and 21st century skills into your learning \nactivities, assessment tasks, and rubrics/mark schemes.','0cee90839bfbb365e09747c6fba2b01441cdf1416f8b9a78921cd1ca8dd4f504','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(65,1,1,64,76,76,'Section 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations \n52\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & VariationsSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nSection Review\nThe essential learning outcomes during weeks 1 to 5 has been for learners to establish \nthe validity of logical arguments and use it to make relevant decisions in solving \nproblems and analyse the impact of variations and conduct simple investigations in \nsolving date-to-day problems.\nThe weeks’ activities have been used to establish the concept of logical reasoning, \nreal-world applications of logical reasoning, intelligent guessing, using tables and \ndiagrams in decisions making, real-life applications of logical reasoning and solve \nreal-life situations involving proportional reasoning. There is the opportunity for \nlearners to practice in groups as well as independently.\nEssentially, the application of 21st century skills have been integrated in the delivery \nprocedures during this period. It is intended that learners will develop:\n• Communication and collaborative skills\n• Critical thinking\n• Analytical skills among others.','e3a0f398c180826b115677a0bd0052c31de5dbc5131447d3ac3dd3e379152671','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(66,1,1,65,77,77,'53\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations Section 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nMARKING SCHEMES FOR THE KEY ASSESSMENTS\nWEEK 1\nMarking Scheme\n1. 1 mark for each correct answer\ni. True\nii. Debatable\niii. False\niv. False\nv. Debatable\nvi. True\nvii. False\nviii. True\nix. False\nTotal = 9 marks\n2. \na. M ⋏ N: Factors divide a given number and all factors are composite numbers. (1 \nmark) False (1 mark)\ni. M ⋎ N: Factors divide a given number or all factors are composite numbers. ( 1 \nmark)\nii. True (1 mark)\nb. S ⋏ T: For x ≥ 9, x is a negative integer and x is a factor of 3. (1 mark) \nFalse (1 mark)\ni. S ⋎ T: For x ≥ 9, x could be a multiple of 3 and never a factor of 3. (1 mark)\nii. False (1 mark)\nc. G ⋏ H: Mappings have rules and mappings have ordered pairs. (1 mark)\ni. True (1 mark)\nii. G ⋎ T: Mappings have rules or mappings have ordered pairs. ( 1mark)\niii. True (1 mark)\n3. 3 acres of maize will yield 3 × 2 tons = 6 tons M 1\n7 tons of cassava will yield 7 × 3 tons = 21 tons M 1 A 1 (all correct answers)\nTotal = 27 tons A 1','f9358b3871e301a3945de5cb9b7643577222e7d21669a75b9df867a2a4f2c3df','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(67,1,1,66,78,78,'54\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & VariationsSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nSince cassava yields greater tons per acre, the higher concentration should be on more \nacres for cassava and lesser for maize. B 1\nTotal = 5 marks\n4. \na. Let T = teaching staff\nK = kitchen staff\nH = people who go on holidays\nU = Workforce\nFigure 1.4: Venn diagram for key assessment 4\nb. Valid or invalid conclusions\ni. Valid statement – because all kitchen staff do not go for holidays.\nK ∩ H\n1\n= K B1\nTrue Statement B1\nii. Invalid statement – because Freda belongs to H and T is a subset of H. Freda can \nequally belong to A\n1\n.\nFalse statement B1, B1\niii. Invalid statement – because kitchen staff is a subset of H1 which includes other \nworkforce.\nFalse statement B1, B1\nTotal = 9 marks\n5. \na. Auntie Rukia is a Ghanaian ⇒ Auntie Rukia has good virtues.\n• This is reversing the original logic.\n• We know that good virtues are Ghanaian values, but we don’t know if all Ghanaians \npossess good virtues.\n• So being Ghanaian does not guarantee good virtues. (Explanation. 2 marks)\nConclusion: False – We cannot conclude that all Ghanaians have good virtues.\n(Correct conclusion 1 mark)\nb. Uncle Mensah is very sincere ⇒ Uncle Mensah is a Ghanaian.\n• Sincerity is a good virtue, and good virtues are Ghanaian values.\n• But having a Ghanaian value does not necessarily mean the person is Ghanaian.\n• A non-Ghanaian can also show Ghanaian values. (Explanation. 2 marks)\nConclusion: False – Sincerity alone does not prove Uncle Mensah is Ghanaian.\n(Correct conclusion 1 mark)','7402686e3c69502a192d74bfe523961c6ceb01b414688830b869c44b87ba63d4','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(68,1,1,67,79,79,'55\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nc. Kojo Baadu is sincere ⇒ Kojo Baadu has a Ghanaian value.\n• From Statement K: Sincerity is a good virtue.\n• From Statement L: Good virtues are Ghanaian values.\nTherefore, sincerity is a Ghanaian value . (Explanation. 2 marks)\nConclusion: True – If Kojo Baadu is sincere, he possesses a Ghanaian value.\n(Correct conclusion 1 mark)\nWEEK 2\nMarking Scheme\n1.\ni. All farmers are not idle people.\nAll hardworking people are not idle. Since farmers are hardworking, then all farmers \nare not idle. B1 - logical reason\nThe statement is valid. B1 - statement as valid\nii. Farmers who are idle are not hardworking.\nSince all farmers are hardworking and no idle person is hard working, then no farmer \nis idle in the first place. B1 - logical reason\nThe statement is therefore invalid as no farmers are idle. B1 - statement as \ninvalid\niii. Kudjo is hardworking, therefore he is a farmer.\nFarmers form part of hardworking people but there are other non-farmers who are \nhard working. B1 - logical reason\nThis means that Kudjo can be hardworking but may not necessarily be a farmer. \nTherefore, the statement is invalid. B1 - statement as \ninvalid\n2. \ni. If Madam Kukua does not head an SHS, then she does not have leadership skills.\nMadam Kukua does not head an SHS because she has leadership skills.\nB1 - logical reason\nThe statement is invalid. B1 - statement as invalid\nii. If Madam Kukua heads an SHS, then she has leadership skills.\nSince p⇒q is not equal to q⇒p B1 - logical reason\nthen the statement is invalid. B1 - statement as invalid\niii) If Madam Kukua has leadership skills, then she heads an SHS.','4cf501a6d940d01bda2da2dd2e340164febf6b0d1b95b29a524255bdf91f3f4f','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(69,1,1,68,80,80,'56\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & VariationsSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nGiven the condition p⇒q, the statement is valid. B1 - logical reason,\nB1 - statement as valid\n3.\nTable 2.2: Rubrics for key assessment 3\nIndicators (Areas to consider) Score\nMotion\nA relatable motion statement, without ambiguity, clearly identifies two \nopposing viewpoints as “for” or “against”,\n4\nA relatable motion statement, without ambiguity, opposing viewpoints as \n“for” or “against” cannot be clearly identified.\n3\nA relatable motion statement, is ambiguous, opposing viewpoints, ie. “for” or \n“against” cannot be clearly identified.\n2\nMotion statement is not relatable, ambiguous, opposing viewpoints, ie. “for” \nor “against” cannot be clearly identified.\n1\nExpected points for the motion\n4 or more points that support the motion 4\n3 appropriate points that support the motion 3\n2 appropriate points that support the motion 2\n1 appropriate point that supports the motion 1\nExpected points against the motion\n4 or more points that is against the motion 4\n3 appropriate points that is against the motion 3\n2 appropriate points that is against the motion 2\n1 appropriate point that is against the motion 1\nTotal score 12\n4. \nTable 2.3: Rubrics for key assessment 4\nIdentifying and justifying Danso’s accession. 2 marks\nIdentifying at 3 likely justifications in points raised in \nAdwoa’s presentation as opposing Danso’s accession (e.g., \nThere could be light in Adwoa’s room)\n2 for each point = 6 \nmarks\nDrawing conclusion on the validity of Danso’s accession2 marks\nTotal 10 marks','a38fad7e5a3e7059270e4a136bc93297978e030df35b1ab620101ce5c63e3454','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(70,1,1,69,81,81,'57\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nWEEK 3\nMark Scheme\n1. \nTable 3.1: Sample rubric for key assessment 1\nDescription of response Score\nStating of position as whether the motion is or is not valid1 mark\nReasons for their position as whether the motion is or is not \nvalid. Stating the importance of choosing a computer lab \nover time of access by learners or vice versa\n1 mark for each point stated \n(1 × 3 = 3 marks)\nExplaining the point raised on the importance of choosing a \ncomputer lab over time of access by learners or vice versa\n1 mark for each point \nexplained (1 × 3 = 3 marks)\nDrawing conclusion on position taken based on the reasons \nraised\n1 mark\nTotal 8 marks\n2. \nTable 3.2: Sample rubric for key assessment 2\nDescription of response Score\nStating of position as whether the motion is or is not valid 1 mark\nReasons for their position as whether the motion is or is not valid. \nStatement in support of or against the head of the Agricultural \nScience Department’s view.\n1 mark for each point \nstated (1 x 3 = 3 marks)\nExplaining the statement made in support of or against the head of \nthe Agricultural Science Department’s view.\n1mark for each point \nexplained (1 x 3 = 3 \nmarks)\nDrawing conclusion on position taken based on the reasons raised1 mark\nTotal 8 marks\n3. \nTable 3.3: Sample rubric for key assessment 3\nAreas to consider Details of expected response Marks\nStructure of \npresentation document\nInformation includes headings on \nintroduction, process of gathering \ninformation, findings from \nstakeholders, plan to integrate \nfindings in solving erosion problem and \nconclusion\n1 mark for each of any 2 \nheadings clearly stated (1× \n2 = 2marks)','ff3cdf11903660934044f18e6d3f2523a2bdf473886d806ba580f398409015a0','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(71,1,1,70,82,82,'58\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & VariationsSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nContent of presentation \ndocument\nStatement of introduction 1mark for clearly stating \nwhat the report is about \npoint explained\nContent of presentation \ndocument\nExplanation of process of gathering \ninformation should include mentioning \nof a tool for collecting data, the process \nused and the individuals contacted\n1 mark for each point \nexplained (1× 3=3marks)\nContent of presentation \ndocument\nAny 3 findings from stakeholders 1mark for each point \nexplained (1× 3=3marks)\nContent of presentation \ndocument\nAny 2 statements on plan to integrate \nfindings in solving erosion problem\n1mark for each point \nexplained (1× 2=2marks)\nContent of presentation \ndocument\nStatement of conclusion 1mark for a statement of \nsummary\nPortfolio Include availability of tool used, pictures, \nrelated research reference or documents \non erosion, write-up, audio, slides, \npictures, concept maps, etc.\n1mark for each neat and \nappropriate material. \nsubmitted (1× 3=3marks)\nTotal score 15 marks\nWEEK 4\nMarking Scheme\n1. C\n2. A\n3. C\nTable 4.2: Mark scheme for key assessment 4\nSection Task Marks\n(a) Correct formula: A = kbh 1 mark\nSubstitution and solving for k correctly\n30 = k×5×12\n30 = 60k\nk = 0.5\n1 mark\nCorrect final answer: k = 0.5 1 mark\n(b) Correct substitution using k = 0.5, b = 10, h = 15\nA = 0.5 × 10 × 15\n1 mark\nCorrect multiplication and result:\nA = 75 cm\n2\n1 mark','80cdaa16891edaa7ccd857e7bd3c80d75038a784d8a99198ca3cdf2c46316ed9','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(72,1,1,71,83,83,'59\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\n(c) Correct explanation using algebra or logical \nreasoning\nLet the original base be b and height be h.\nOriginal area:\nA = 0.5 × b × h\nNew base = 2b, new height = 2h\nNew area: A′ = 0.5 × 2b × 2h\n= 0.5 ×4bh\n= 4(0.5bh\n= 4A\n2 marks\nConclusion:\nDoubling both the base and height causes the area \nto become four times larger.\n1 mark\nTotal marks\n5. \nTable 4.3: Marking scheme for key assessment 5\nTask Marks\nCorrectly identifies the variation:\nT\nN\nkP\n= , where P = the number of problems\nN = number of people solving\n1 mark\nSubstitutes given values\nk\n36\n4\n18#\n=\n1 mark\nSolving for k correctly:\n36 × 4 = k × 18\nk\n18\n364#\n=\nk8=\n1 mark\nSubstitutes k = 8, P = 24, N = 8 into the equation\nT\n8\n824#\n=\n1 mark\nCorrect computation and final answer:\nT = 24 minutes\n1 mark\nTotal marks','01e30777927387a9b20b6febcaa31653af45397285d4a62f142bcfe8669ff5bc','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(73,1,1,72,84,84,'60\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & VariationsSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\n6. \nTable 4.4: Marking scheme for key assessment 6\nTask Marks\nWrites correct formula:\nP\nv\nknT\n=\n1 mark\nCorrect substitution with given values\n100 = \nk\n10\n2300##\n100 = \nk\n100\n10\n600\n=\n100 = 60k\n1 mark\nSolving for k = \n3\n5 1 mark\nSubstituting the k = \n3\n5\n, n = 3, T = 250, V \n= 12 into P = \n12\n3\n5\n3250##\n1 mark\nSolving for P\nP = \n12\n5250#\nP = \n12\n1250\nP = 104.17\n1 mark\nTotal 5 marks\nWEEK 5\nMarking Scheme\n1. Table 5.1: Marking scheme for Key assessment 1\nPart Criteria Marks\na. Correctly writing the equation in terms of T and h\nT = 40 + 25h\n1 mark\nb. Correct substitution of h = 6.5 and solving for T.\nT = 40 + 25×6.5\nT = 202.50\n2 marks\nc. In a direct variation, the relationship is of the form T = kh, where \nthe graph passes through the origin (0,0).\nHere, the equation is T = 40 + 25h, which includes a fixed starting \namount (GH¢40), so the graph does not pass through the origin.\n2 marks','164d7b019ccb1fa7658b5ca949bfc72a1881c88553d6f1d6282393cc9a2928f4','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(74,1,1,73,85,85,'61\nSection 1: Logical Reasoning & Variations\nd. T = 40 + 30h\nExplanation:\nThe slope of the line increases, meaning the graph becomes \nsteeper.\nFor every hour worked, the total cost increases more rapidly than \nbefore.\n3 marks\nTotal 8 marks\n2. Table 5.2: Marking scheme for Key Assessment 2\nPart Criteria Marks\na. C = 120d + 1.50k 1 mark\nb. Cost = 120 × 3 + 1.50 × 200\n=360 + 300\n=GH¢660\n2 marks\nc. 500 = 120 × 2 + 1.50k\n⇒240 + 1.50k = 500\n⇒1.50k = 260\n.\nk\n150\n260\n=\nk = 173.33\n2 marks\nMaximum whole number of kilometres: 173 km 1 mark\nTotal 6 marks','f3c6ea28aad1fef35f4ef259d1c3c1380e7be9a5f29b7df4f4b476ecf2a3303c','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(75,1,1,74,86,86,'62\nSection 2: Algebraic Reasoning\nSECTION 2: ALGEBRAIC REASONING\nSTRAND: ALGEBRAIC REASONING\nSub-Strand: Patterns and Relationships\nWeeks 6,7,8, and 9\nLearning Outcome: Solve problems on quadratic functions and equations, including real-\nlife problems.\nContent Standard: Demonstrate understanding of the concept of quadratic functions and \nequations, and solve real-life problems with them.\nINTRODUCTION AND SECTION SUMMARY\nImagine you are trying to launch a rocket to get the maximum possible height, or perhaps you \nare an architect designing a parabolic arch for a bridge. Maybe you’re even an economist trying \nto model the profit of a business based on price. Surprisingly, all these scenarios can be \ndescribed and analysed using quadratic functions and equations. The quadratic function \ndescribes a particular type of relationship between variables that results in a parabolic curve, \nwhile quadratic equations help us find specific values of the input variable that make the \nfunction equal to zero. They are fundamental tools for modelling and solving problems involving \ncurved paths, optimisation, and various other real-world scenarios.\nIn this section, we will learn how to solve quadratic equations using the method of grouping, \nthe quadratic formula and graphs. We will also learn how to graph the quadratic function, find \nits line of symmetry, maximum and minimum values, and their domains for specific descriptions \nof the function. We will also apply these concepts to answer real-life problems.\nAs we delve deeper into this topic, you will see just how versatile and essential quadratic \nfunctions and equations are for understanding and solving problems in the world around us.\nThe weeks, the indicators with their respective focal areas covered by the section are:\nWeek 6\nIdentify and solve quadratic equations\nFocal Area: Identifying and solving quadratic equations\n• Factorisation of quadratic equations\n• Model and solve quadratic functions and equations involving real-life applications','3de39298e3be571afa890c76a590976846cdca6e279c1b85749ca0dbe257ee6c','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(76,1,1,75,87,87,'63\nSection 2: Algebraic Reasoning\nWeek 7\nSolve quadratic equations graphically and find the maximum and minimum points of quadratic \ngraphs\nFocal Area: Solving quadratic equations using graphs\n• Graphing and solving quadratic equations\n• Maximum and minimum\n• Model and solve real-life situations involving quadratic functions\nWeek 8\nIdentify and explain the axis of symmetry, write its equation and solve linear and quadratic \nequations simultaneously using graphs.\nFocal Area: Finding equations of axes of symmetry and solving linear and quadratic \nequations simultaneously using graphs\n• Finding axis of symmetry from quadratic graphs\n• Finding axis of symmetry from quadratic equations\n• Solve linear and quadratic equations simultaneously using graphs.\n• Solve related real-life problems involving quadratic equations\nWeek 9\nUse quadratic graphs to solve related equations and solve real-life problems to find the range \nof values of (x) for which the other value (y) is increasing or decreasing and the range of values \n(x) for which the other value (y) is positive or negative.\nFocal Area: Real-life problems involving linear and quadratic equations\n• Find the range of values of (x) for which the other value (y) is increasing or decreasing.\n• Find the range of values (x) for which the other value (y) is positive or negative.\n• Model and solve real-life problems involving linear and quadratic equations.\nSUMMARY OF PEDAGOGICAL EXEMPLARS\nThis section requires hands-on activities where learners engage in practical activities with \nquadratic equations and functions. Learners should be given the platform to work in groups to \ndevelop their real-life questions and find answers. Therefore, experiential learning activities \nand mixed-ability groupings should dominate the lessons on these concepts. All learners, \nirrespective of their learning abilities, should be assisted to take part fully in investigations and \npresentation of findings. However, make considerations and accommodations for the different \ngroups. That is, offer approaching proficiency learners the opportunity to make oral presentations. \nThen, extend activities for the above-average/highly proficient learners to use formulae and \ncomputer applications to solve problems.','846a7108907d29b0bc87b2e0caf3604699eedcd5fe1532a81d17a01a2a05197e','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(77,1,1,76,88,88,'64\nSection 2: Algebraic ReasoningSection 2: Algebraic Reasoning\nASSESSMENT SUMMARY\nTable 1 contains the key assessment for each indicator in the section. The highest level of DOK \nfor the indicator is denoted. The mode of assessment to be carried out and strategies that will \naid in achieving the indicators are documented.\nTable 2: Indicators, DOK levels, assessment modes and strategies\nIndicators DOK \nLevel\nAssessment Strategy and/or \nMode\nIdentify and solve quadratic equations. 3 Strategy: closed and open-ended \nstandard questions\nMode: Classwork\nSolve quadratic equations graphically and \nfind the maximum and minimum points of \nquadratic graphs.\n3 Strategy: Reflective exercise\nMode: Classwork\nIdentify and explain the axes of symmetry, \nwrite its equation and solve linear and \nquadratic equations simultaneously using \ngraphs.\n3 Strategy: Groupwork\nMode: Presentation\nUse quadratic graphs to solve related \nequations and solve real-life problems to \nfind the range of values of (x) for which the \nother value (y) is increasing or decreasing \nand the range of values (x) for which the \nother value (y) is positive or negative.\n3 Strategy: Independent assessment\nMode: Classwork','3eb449ee86f14a92c01e7cd8f73d568c43ba336d961190a5b761182cd697f6dd','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(78,1,1,77,89,89,'65\nSection 2: Algebraic Reasoning\nWEEK 6\nLearning Indicator: Identify and solve quadratic equations\nFOCAL AREA 1: FACTORISATION OF QUADRATIC \nEQUATIONS\nWhat is a Quadratic Equation?\nIt is a polynomial equation of degree 2. This means the highest exponent, or power, of the \nvariable is 2.\nA quadratic equation in a variable, x , is of the form\naxbxc\n2\n++=0, where a, b and c are \nconstants and a0!\nSimilarly, a quadratic equation in a variable, , is of the form , where and are constants and\nExample 1\nWhich of the following is a quadratic equation?\nSolution\na. IS a quadratic equation with a = 1, b = 3, and c = 0\nb. IS a quadratic equation with a = 3, b = −5, and c = 7\nc. NOT a quadratic equation.\nd. Remove the denominator by multiplying both sides of the equation by x ,\ne. xx x\nx\n12\n##+ xx40#-=\nxx14\n3\n+- = 0. This is a cubic equation because the highest power of x is 3.\nf. NOT a quadratic equation. This is a linear equation because the highest power of x is 1','5d800506b96b43e8d14265dba59fe5ea33938302f8a0661f091a820d1ddff167','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(79,1,1,78,90,90,'66\nSection 2: Algebraic ReasoningSection 2: Algebraic Reasoning\ng. IS a quadratic equation with a = 1, b = 0, and c = −1\nh. IS a quadratic equation\nMultiply out the bracket and bring everything to the same side:\nxx12 9\n2\n-+ =\nxx28\n2\n-- = 0\nTherefore, a = 1, b = −2, and c = −8\ni. IS a quadratic equation with a = 8, b = 0, and c = 0\nj. IS a quadratic equation.\nk. Remove the denominator by multiplying both sides of the equation by ,\nl. xx x23#+ x\nx\n1\n#=\nxx23\n2\n+ = 1\nxx23 1\n2\n+- = 0\nTherefore, a = 2, b = 3, and c = −1\nFactorising And Finding The Roots Of A Quadratic Equation Using The Method \nOf Grouping\nThe roots of the quadratic equation, \naxbxc\n2\n++ = 0, are the values of x that satisfy\naxbxc\n2\n++= 0\nSteps for Solving Quadratic Equations ( axbxc\n2\n++= 0) by Grouping:\n1. Identify a, b, and c\n2. Calculate the product of a and c, i.e., ac ac#=\n3. Find two numbers whose product is equal to ac and whose sum is equal to b .\n4. Rewrite the middle term by replacing the term bx with the two numbers you found in step \n3.\n5. Group the first two terms and the last two terms, then factor out common factors from each \ngroup.\n6. Factor out the common binomial factor to obtain the factored form of the quadratic \nequation.\n7. Set each factor equal to zero and solve for x to find the solutions of the quadratic equation.\nLet us use the steps to answer a few questions:\nExample 1\nSolve \nxx23 5\n2\n+- = 0','686e6a5d767b78bc13e02bfe184759f055f5a1b8e63ff0c138a201ce3c36a419','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(80,1,1,79,91,91,'67\nSection 2: Algebraic Reasoning\nSolution\nStep 1\nComparing xx23 5\n2\n+- = 0 to axbxc\n2\n++ = 0, a = 2, b = 5, and c = −5\nStep 2\na × c = 2 × − 5= −10\nStep 3\nThis means we need two numbers whose product is −10. The negative value tells us that one \nof the numbers must be negative and the other must be positive. We can consider the factors of \n−10 to help with this:\n• The positive factors of are 1, 2, 5, 10\n• The negative factors of are −1, −2, −5, −10\n• Therefore, the possible combinations are (1, −10), (2, −5), (5, −2) and (10, −1)\n• We also need our combination to have a sum equal to b , in this case b = 3.\n• So, which of these combinations has a sum of 3?\n(1, −10) → 1 + (−10) = −9 ≠ 3\n(2, −5) → 2 + (−5) = −3 ≠ 3\n(5, −2) → 5 + (−2) = 3 = 3\nTherefore, the desired values are and since their product will give us and their sum is \n3.\nStep 4\nxx x35 2=- or xx x32 5=-+\nRewrite xx23 5\n2\n+- = 0 as either xx x25 2\n2\n+-−5 = 0 or xx x22 5\n2\n-+−5 = 0\nStep 5\nxx x25 2\n2\n+- −5 = 0 or xx x22 5\n2\n-+ 50-=\n(xx25\n2\n+)−(xx25+) = 0 or (xx22\n2\n-) + (x55-) = 0\nStep 6\nor \nor \nor \nStep 7','f2fe1ab3f3e368a73557f5cbbcb00d82df5615b448f8d651d2af98f2f43c24a0','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
