(122,1,1,121,128,128,'104\nSection 2: Algebraic ReasoningSection 2: Algebraic Reasoning\nExample 2\nThe diagram below shows the graph of . Use the graph to solve\na. \nb. \nSolution\na. .\nThe drawn graph is . So, we have to modify the equation, \n, to have a component of the graph, \nSince the coefficients of the x and the x\n2\nterms are the same for both functions, we will \njust subtract 3 to both sides of the equation\nx632-+ x03\n2\n-= -\nThis gives xx32\n2\n+- = −3\nWe know that , therefore, our equation becomes y = −3\nAn alternative method is to find the difference between the graph and the equation.\nThis means that drawing or following the line y = −3 on the graph will lead us to the \nsolution.','0fe9508eeef53c1fc65ebcc1b15bf15b018a8565550acd1663d684a3d0d053f1','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(123,1,1,122,129,129,'105\nSection 2: Algebraic Reasoning\nFrom the graph, the solution of is either .x165=- or .x365=\nb. xx20\n2\n+- =\nAdd x to both sides of the equation\nxx2++ xx0\n2\n-= +\nxx x22\n2\n+- =\nAdd 1 to both sides of the equation\nx21 2++ xx 1\n2\n-= +\nxx32\n2\n+- = x1+\nyx 1=+\nDraw the line yx 1=+ on the graph and write the values of x at which it intersects the curve \nas the solution.\nFrom the graph, the curve and line intersect at the solution, so the solution is x1=- and x2=\nLearning Tasks\nGuide learners to solve tasks related to understanding quadratic equations, quadratic \ngraphs and functions. Provide support systems for learners who may encounter \ndifficulties.\nLearners are to:\n1. Identify and write the equation of line of symmetry for quadratic graphs.\n2. solve linear and quadratic simultaneous equations.','0c0add49c0779f234e24109232fd7ebd1aeab8e55a58987a2b5d77f5b822e1f6','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(124,1,1,123,130,130,'106\nSection 2: Algebraic ReasoningSection 2: Algebraic Reasoning\nPEDAGOGICAL EXEMPLARS\nThe aim of the lessons for the week is for all learners to be able solve problems involving \nquadratic graphs with specific emphasis on using the graph to answer linear and quadratic \nfunctions simultaneously. The following pedagogical approach is suggested for facilitators to \ntake learners through.\n1. Collaborative and gender responsive groups. Use an integrated learning approach to \nestablish, analyse and interpret quadratic graphs using diagrams to carry out investigations \non quadratic equations concurrently through problem solving approaches.\n2. Problem-based learning. Task learners to determine the solution set of equations in a \nwhole class discussion and identify and brainstorm the meaning of axes of symmetry.\n3. Small gender responsive grouping. Identify and brainstorm the connection between \nstraight line and quadratic equations and find their points of intersections.\nKEY ASSESSMENT LEVEL 3: GROUP PRESENTATION\n1. \na. Copy and complete the table of values for the relation \nyx x35\n2\n=- 4+ for x24##- \nx -2 -1 0 1 2 3 4\ny 26 2 32\n2 marks\nb. Using a scale of 2 cm to 1 unit on the x-axis and 2 cm to 5 units on the y-axis, draw \non the graph of = \nxx35 4\n2\n-+.\n3 marks\nc. Use your graph to solve xx35 4\n2\n-+ x103=+\n4 marks\nTotal 10 marks\nPLC Session 8\n45 minutes Planning & Reviewing the Learning Plan\n1. Begin the session by recapping lessons from the previous year/session/week, focusing on \nhow learning was applied.\n2. Use your subject specific app to explore the content before planning. Ask the app to\na. explain the week’s concepts in a practical way that will make it easier for learners to \nunderstand','8d6f56a0ada3ebbea92e900c8920d67274eb0d3fb3f7096ba5f8cae941b68179','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(125,1,1,124,131,131,'107\nSection 2: Algebraic Reasoning\nb. identify two or three common misconceptions learners may have on the week’s content \nand how to address them using contexts familiar to Ghanaian learners. (NTS 3a, 3g \nand 3m).\n3. Use your subject specific app to plan your lesson. Ask the app to\na. suggest the most effective method for teaching the week’s concepts to learners with \ndiverse backgrounds.\nb. suggest learning activities, differentiation and essential questions based on the method \nof teaching (NTS 3a-3q)\nc. review your prepared learning plan and address any challenging issues you may have \nidentified in your subject area (NTS 3a-3c).\nNote\n• Take note of the recap topics linked to Years 1 and 2 while preparing for your \nlesson.\n• Refer to the Values Learning Community Handbook and link the values for the \nweek in your lesson to ensure that learners are applying what they learn.\n4. Plan the week’s key assessment with your app\na. The key assessment for the week is group presentation . Chat with your app to plan \nthis assessment, indicating what you and your learners will do before, during and after \nthe presentations (NTS 3k-3q).\n10 minutes Content Exploration\n5. As a subject group, select one problem or idea/term in the week’s content that is challenging \n(not a routine procedural question, but one that requires conceptual understanding) and \nthen\na. use the app, ask it to solve the problem step by step and explain the reasoning behind \neach step (you may be assisted by a teacher who is strong in the content area)\nb. all teachers in the group work through a similar problem individually for 3–4 minutes\nc. the group then works through the problem together step by step\nd. highlight the most common errors made at each step and discuss (NTS 1c, 1e, 2c, 2e, \n3e-3j).\nNote\n• This part is not just about ‘How do I teach this?’ but how do I teach it for learners \nto understand?\n• If the group still shares the same content gap, use the app: ask it to solve the \nproblem step by step and explain the reasoning behind each step.','9f9cc1c622a838b89a696d8721b38d09397b68c1bc6a4355722c57d491e5360d','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(126,1,1,125,132,132,'108\nSection 2: Algebraic ReasoningSection 2: Algebraic Reasoning\n• Problems/ideas discussed should be sourced from the Teacher Manual content, not \ninvented separately. This keeps the content exploration directly connected to what \nteachers will deliver.\n30 minutes Enactment\n6. Enact a teaching activity based on your learning plan (NTS la-1b, 1e-lg, 2c, 2e, 2f, 3c-3j \nand 3o).\na. Colleagues observe with specific attention to clarity of concept explanation, use of \nfamiliar Ghanaian examples and how misconceptions are handled if they arise.\nb. After the enactment, ask for structured feedback from the group\ni. was the concept explained in a way that a learner with no prior knowledge could \nfollow?\nii. were the examples grounded in contexts familiar to Ghanaian learners?\niii. did any misconceptions surface and if so, how were they addressed?\n5 minutes Reflection\n7. Reflect and share your views on the session. One thing that worked well, what did not work \nwell and what you will do differently. (NTS 1a, 1b).\n8. Identify a critical friend to observe and provide feedback on your lesson in relation to the \nPLC Session conducted (NTS 1f, 1g).\n9. Remember to\na. transfer your chats into your learning planner template.\nb. read your Teacher Manual and related Learner Material in preparation for the next \nsession. Take note of the recap weeks, which are linked to Years 1 and 2 (NTS 3a).\nc. integrate National Values, GESI, SEL, and 21st century skills into your learning \nactivities, assessment tasks, and rubrics/mark schemes.','59fdcff1047efdb9f96bfb1b88ac8ac52c312b29cd4392fdc921d68e40310ca4','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(127,1,1,126,133,133,'109\nSection 2: Algebraic Reasoning\nWEEK 9\nLearning Indicator: Use quadratic graphs to solve related equations and solve real-life \nproblems to find the range of values of (x) for which the other value (y) \nis increasing or decreasing and the range of values (x) for which the \nother value (y) is positive or negative\nFOCAL AREA 1: REAL-LIFE PROBLEMS INVOLVING LINEAR \nAND QUADRATIC EQUATIONS\n1. Find the range of values of (x) for which the other value (y) is increasing or decreasing.\n2. Find the range of values (x) for which the other value (y) is positive or negative.\n3. Model and solve real-life problems involving linear and quadratic equations.\nFinding the Range of Values of for which is Increasing or \nDecreasing\n1. A maximum graph\nReading the curve from left to right, we can see that it increases steadily (i.e., the gradient is \npositive) from A and peaks at B. The range of values of x for which the y value increases is \ngiven by xB1\nThe quadratic curve also decreases (i.e., the gradient is negative) from the peak, B, towards C. \nThe range of values of x for which the y value decreases is given by xB2.\nExample 1\nUse the graph below to find the range of values when the graph is:\na. increasing\nb. decreasing','b1d584161966cb6bf9d6a2097f14b7c09c92561fc8f20cb0c225f152b4b3688c','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(128,1,1,127,134,134,'110\nSection 2: Algebraic ReasoningSection 2: Algebraic Reasoning\nSolution\na. The graph is increasing when x21.\nb. The graph is decreasing when x22.\n2. Minimum graph\nExample 2\nUse the graph below to find the range of values of x for which;\na. y increases as x increases\nb. y decreases as x increases.\na. The range of values of x for which y increases as x increases is given by x 52-.\nb. The range of values of x for which y decreases as x increases is given by x 51-.\nFinding the Range of Values of x for which is y Positive or Negative\nThe value of y is negative (i.e., y < 0) if the curve is below the x–axis and positive (i.e. y > 0) \nif the curve is above the x–axis.','6aa0ef3ad40de057d34c8837dd5e36f4690b7920cf25869bdf8a8bc4d511dbaf','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(129,1,1,128,135,135,'111\nSection 2: Algebraic Reasoning\n1. Minimum graph\nFrom the above graph, the range of values of x for which y0# is given by Ax B## and the \nvalues of x for which y02 is x < A or x > B.\nExample 1\nUse the curve below to answer the following questions.\nFind:\na. the range of values of x for which y0#\nb. the range of values of x for which y02.\nSolution\na. From the graph the range of values for which y0# is x20 10##- .\nb. From the graph the range of values of x for which y > 0 is x < −20 and x > 10\n2. The maximum graph','94ea5b4708e85755c3d25d6a8d53ced4ce947ec0314ead4b556ee3d436819d63','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(130,1,1,129,136,136,'112\nSection 2: Algebraic ReasoningSection 2: Algebraic Reasoning\nThe range of values of x for which y0$ is Ax B## and the range of values of x for which\ny < 0 is x <A or x > B.\nExample 2\nUse the quadratic curve to answer the following questions.\nFind:\na. the range of values of x for which y is negative.\nb. the range of values of x for which y is positive.\nSolution\na. From the graph, the range of values of x for which y is negative is x < −2 and x > 6.\nb. From the graph, the range of values of x for which y is positive is −2 ≤ x ≤ 6\nExample 3\na. Copy and complete the following table for the relation y = \nxx43\n2\n+- for the interval \nx25##-\nx -2-101234 5\ny 0 4 40\nb. Using a scale of 2 cm to 1 unit on the x–axis and 2 cm to 2 units on the y–axis, draw the \nrelation.\nc. From the graph, find\ni. The range of values of x for which y decreases as x increases.\nii. The range of values of x for which y increases as x increases.\niii. The range of values of x for which y is negative.','4a49de8743fdcde47360a5a887556a7daf24e3847327af3a9c20155f481f80a3','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
