ot physically possible. Therefore, this \nsolution is not valid. \nTherefore, we conclude that the value of ???? is 5.\n\nTotal = 10 marks\n\n<H3>Question 2 \nPerimeter of the shape =8???? \n⟹ 5????+????+4????+3????+????=8 \n12????+2????=8 \n6????+????=4 ………………. (P)\n\nArea of the shape =4????\n2\n\n⟹5????????+\n1\n2\n×4????×3????=4 \n5????????+6????\n2\n=4 …………….(A)','329e192de31d70cf691e245783d137fe4d8acd9b291670faa2fbec5f3ee7855b','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(141,1,1,140,146,146,'From (1), ????=4−6???? \nsubstitute ????=4−6???? into equation (2) \n5????(4−6????)+6????\n2\n=4 \n20????−30????\n2\n+6????\n2\n=4 \nA correct method for solving the quadratic, \nfor example: \n−24????\n2\n+20????−4=0 \n6????\n2\n−5????+1=0 \n6????\n2\n−3????−2????+1=0 \n3????(2????−1)−1(2????−1)=0 \n(3????−1)(2????−1)=0 \n????=\n1\n3\nor ????=\n1\n2\n\nIf ????=\n1\n3\n, ????=4−6×\n1\n3\n=4−2=2 \nIf ????=\n1\n2\n, ????=4−6×\n1\n2\n=4−3=1 \nIn conclusion, either ????=\n1\n3\nand ????=2 \nOr ????=\n1\n2\nand ????=1 \nM1 for correctly attempting to form equation \n(P) \nA1 for the correct equation or its equivalence\n\nM1 for forming equation (A) \nA1 for the correct equation or its equivalence\n\nM1 for forming quadratic equation by \nsubstitution \nA1 for correction equation \n(6????\n2\n−5????+1=0 or its equivalence)\n\nM1 correct method attempted for solving for \nx\n\nA1 for ????=\n1\n2\nand ????=\n1\n3\n(-0.5 each error)\n\nM1 for attempting to input their x to solve for \ny\n\nA1 for y=1 and y=2 (-0.5 each error) \nTotal =10 marks\n\n<H1>WEEK 7 \n<H3>Question One \nM1 for correctly attempting to form equation (P)\nA1 for the correct equation or its equivalence\nM1 for forming equation (A)\nA1 for the correct equation or its equivalence\nM1 for forming quadratic equation by substitution\nA1 for correction equation\n(6x\n2\n− 5x + 1 = 0 or its equivalence)\nM1 correct method attempted for solving for x\nA1 for \nx\n2\n1\n= and x\n3\n1\n= (−0.5 each error)\nM1 for attempting to input their x to solve for y\nA1 for y = 1 and y = 2 (−0.5 each error)\nTotal =10 marks\nWEEK 7\nQuestion One\n1.\nx -2 -1 0 1 2 3 4\ny 11 1 -5 -7-5 1 11 B2 (0.5 for each correct entry)','4f54694285ef35bdc9c34606cdd45204745930b587db7652a847ba47826b938c','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(142,1,1,141,147,147,'123\nSection 2: Algebraic Reasoning\nb.\nB3 for the graph\nFor example:\nB1 for a correct shape.\nB1 for going through the correct \npoints\nB1 for a smooth curve drawn\nc. From the graph\ni. the minimum value is −7\nii. minimum point is (1, −7)\niii. the roots of xx24\n2\n-- 50-= are −0.85 and 2.85\nA1 for the minimum value\nA1 for the minimum point\nM1 for reading roots\nA1 for the roots (0.5 for each)\nAllow follow through if their \ngraph is incorrectly drawn but their \nanswers to part c are correct for their \ngraph.\nTotal 9 marks\nQuestion 2\na.\nt0 1 2 3 4 5 6 6.5\nH123244484432 12 -1 B3 for table (0.5 for each correct \nentry)\nb.\nB3 for graph\nFor example:\nB1 for a correct shape\nB1 for going through the correct \npoints\nB1 for a smooth curve drawn)','6a0d57dab1a3146694eb89f184b79a804eb00db7d0fe5bd53023c1bf0a118280','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(143,1,1,142,148,148,'124\nSection 2: Algebraic ReasoningSection 2: Algebraic Reasoning\nc.\ni. The rockets maximum height is 48 metres\nii. In 5.5 seconds, the rocket was 23 metres above the ground.\niii. It took the rocket about 6.45 seconds to hit the ground.\nA1 for maximum height (48 metres)\nM1 for reading from graph, or \nsubstituting t = 5.5 into the relation\nA1 for 23 metres, or correct reading \nfrom their graph.\nA1 for time the rocket hit the \nground\nAllow follow through if their \ngraph is incorrectly drawn but their \nanswers to part c are correct for \ntheir graph.\nTotal 10 marks\nWEEK 8\na.\nx -2 -10 1 2 3 4\ny 26 124 2 6 1632 B2 for table (0.5 for each correct entry)\nb. B3 for graph\nFor example\nB1 for a correct shape\nB1 for going through the correct points\nB1 for a smooth curve drawn\n127\n\nB3 for graph \n(For example: \nB1 for a correct shape \nB1 for going through \nthe correct points \nB1 for a smooth \ncurve drawn)\n\nFrom the graph the solution for \n3????\n2\n−5????+4=10+3???? is \n????=−0.6 and ????=3.3 \nB2 for linear graph correctly drawn on the \ngraph in part b \nM1 for reading their intersection ???? values \nfrom their graph, even if graphs incorrect \nA1 for -0.6 and 3.3 (0.5 for each)\n\n<H1>WEEK 9 \n<H3>Question 1\n\n???? 0 1 2 3 4 5 \n????=60????−12????\n2\n0 48 72 72 48 0\n\nB3 (0.5 for each correct entry)\n\nB3 for the graph \n(For example: \nB2 for linear graph correctly drawn on the \ngraph in part b\nM1 for reading their intersection values from \ntheir graph, even if graphs incorrect\nA1 for -0.6 and 3.3 (0.5 for each)','dbc44ea1d66d642a43f1a82a76e2faa4f10d10d4e47f8f84a2061c14e9cd0739','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(144,1,1,143,149,149,'125\nSection 2: Algebraic Reasoning\nWEEK 9\nQuestion 1\na.\nt\n0 1 2 3 4 5\nHt t6012\n2\n=-\n0 487272480\nB3 (0.5 for each correct entry)\nB3 for the graph\nFor example:\nB1 for a correct shape\nB1 for going through the correct points\nB1 for a smooth curve drawn)\nb.\ni. when H is less than 63m, t < 1.5 or t > 3.5\nii. 1 < t < 4\nii. t < 2.5\niii. t > 2.5\nc. The maximum height is 75m. It took 2.5 seconds to reach \nthe maximum height\nd. It took 5 seconds for the object to hit the ground.\nM1 for reading correctly from their graph\nA1 for t < 1.5 and t > 3.5 (0.5 for each), \nallow follow through from their graph\nM1 for reading correctly from their graph, \nor the table of values\nA1 for 1 < t < 4, allow follow through\nA1 for t < 2.5\nA1 for t > 2.5\nM1 for reading their maximum height from \nthe graph\nA1 for 75m\nA1 for 2.5seconds\nA1 for the time it took to hit the ground\nTotal 16 marks','e09fddef14394b99fba42ef7442592709d8376c462fc58497aea1f89d845acc4','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(145,1,1,144,150,150,'126\nSection 2: Algebraic Reasoning\nQuestion 2\na.\nx012345678910\nH011202732353635322720\nB3 (0.5 for each correct entry)\nb.\nB3 for the graph\n(For example:\nB1 for a correct shape\nB1 for going through the correct \npoints\nB1 for a smooth curve drawn)\nc.\ni. from the graph x = 3.55 and x = 8.45\nii.\n2.7 < x < 9.3\niii. maximum height feet = 36 feet\nM1 for reading from their graph\nA1 for x = 3.55 and x = 8.45 (0.5 for each), allow \nfollow through from their graph\nM1 for reading from their graph\nA1 for 2.7 < x < 9.3, allow follow through from their \ngraph\nA1 for max height = 36 feet\nTotal 11 marks','0480f98b393fc89f7ef7b481105999f05f252fc8b96d17b664652afa6a53bc3f','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(146,1,1,145,151,151,'127\nSECTION 3: CIRCLE THEOREMS & GEOMETRIC \nCONSTRUCTION\nSTRAND: GEOMETRY AROUND US\nSub-Strand: Spatial Sense\nWeeks 10 & 11\nLearning Outcome: Draw circles for given radii and use the circle theorems; identify the \ntangent as perpendicular to the radius at the point of contact and \nverify that tangents drawn from an external point to the same circle \nare equal when measured from their point of contact.\nContent Standard: Demonstrate a conceptual understanding of spatial sense with respect to \ncircles and their theorems, and apply their properties to solve everyday \nlife problems.\nWeeks 12 & 13\nLearning Outcome: Perform geometric construction of quadrilaterals and given loci.\nContent Standard: Demonstrate knowledge and understanding of geometrical construction, \nuse the knowledge to construct plane shapes, and apply these in the \nworld around them.\nINTRODUCTION AND SECTION SUMMARY\nCircle theorems and geometric constructions are fundamental pillars of Euclidean geometry, \noffering powerful tools for understanding the properties of circles and for creating precise \ngeometric figures using only a compass and straightedge. These topics have been central to \nmathematical inquiry for centuries, laying the groundwork for more advanced concepts in \ngeometry, trigonometry, and even architecture and engineering.\nUnder the circle theorem, we will delve into a collection of proven statements that describe the \nrelationships between various parts of a circle, such as its chords, tangents, angles, and arcs. \nUnderstanding these theorems allows for the deduction of unknown angles and lengths within \ncircular figures, providing a logical framework for solving a wide range of geometric problems.\nUnder geometric constructions, we will focus on the art and science of accurately drawing \ngeometric shapes and figures using only two primary tools: a straightedge (for drawing straight \nlines) and a compass (for drawing circles and arcs). While seemingly simple, these tools enable','4e3187bae9769a9ee71873a8da2415a03f195afedf5b68a7f2bf495bd19d925f','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(147,1,1,146,152,152,'128\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nthe construction of remarkably complex figures and are crucial for visualising and proving \ngeometric principles. Geometric constructions emphasise precision, logical thinking, and the \nfoundational axioms of geometry. They provide a hands-on approach to understanding \ngeometric relationships and are an essential skill for anyone delving deeper into mathematical \nconcepts or practical design.\nThe weeks, the indicators with their respective focal areas covered by the section are:\nWeek 10\na. Identify parts of a circle and draw circles for given radii and through points.\nb. Discuss the circle theorems by identifying the statements, proofs, examples, and \napplications.\nFocal Area 1: Circle and Circle Theorem\n• Identify the parts of a circle\n• Draw circles for given radii and through points.\nFocal Area 2: Applications of Circle Theorems\n• Identify the circle theorem statements.\n• Model and prove with examples the circle theorems.\nWeek 11\na. Identify the tangent as perpendicular to the radius at the point of contact and verify the \nAlternate Segment Theorem.\nb. Verify that tangents drawn from an external point to the same circle are equal when \nmeasured from their point of contact.\nFocal Area 1: The tangent of a circle 1\n• Identify and prove that the tangent as perpendicular to the radius at the point of contact.\n• Verify the Alternate Segment Theorem.\nFocal Area 2: The tangent of a circle 2\n• Prove with examples that tangents drawn from an external point to the same circle are \nequal when measured from their point of touch.\nWeek 12\nRecall the construction of various angles such as 750, 1050, 1350, and 1500.\nFocal Area: Understanding Geometric Constructions\n• Review of angles from Junior High School\n• Constructing angles of 75˚,105˚,135˚ and 150˚','4d66ba17d429a749f45d2f0c113bba2a0ac6fd9993faa9ae3b83ca72624adc24','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(148,1,1,147,153,153,'129\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nWeek 13\na. Construct a triangle or a quadrilateral under given conditions.\nb. Construct a particular locus for a given condition.\nFocal Area 1: Construction of quadrilaterals Concept, examples and visualisation of surds\n• Construct a triangle.\n• Construct a quadrilateral.\nFocal Area 2: Construction of locus\n• Theorems and conditions of locus\n• Construction of locus\nSUMMARY OF PEDAGOGICAL EXEMPLARS\nThis section requires hands-on activities where learners engage with the construction of plane \nshapes. Learners should be given the opportunity to work in groups to develop their real-life \nquestions and find answers. Therefore, experiential learning activities and mixed-ability \ngroupings should dominate the lessons on these concepts. All learners, regardless of their \nlearning abilities, should be supported to participate fully in investigations and presentations \nof findings. However, considerations and accommodations must be made for the different \ngroups. Specifically, offer approaching-proficiency learners the chance to make oral \npresentations. Then, extend activities for above-average or highly proficient learners to use \ncomputer applications to solve problems.\nASSESSMENT SUMMARY\nTable 1 contains the key assessment for each indicator in the section. The highest of DOK for \nthe indicator is denoted. The mode of assessment to be carried out and strategies that will aid \nin achieving the indicators are documented.\nTable 1: Indicators, DOK levels, assessment modes and strategies\nIndicators DOK LevelAssessment Strategy and/or Mode\n1. Identify parts of a circle and draw \ncircles for given radii and through \npoints.\n2. Discuss the circle theorems by \nidentifying the statements, proofs, \nexamples, and applications.\n3 Strategy: The questions will contain ten (10) \ncompulsory questions (Q1 to Q10) from all \nsub-strands of the curriculum.\nThere will be eight (8) questions (Q11 to Q18) \nwith two (2) questions from each strand. \nCandidates must select six (6) questions to \nanswer, with at least one question from each \nstrand.\nMode: Mock Exams','6205ac3ccb89058b336d5d5e4b2387a16a067aafeae1e17b321e14f973dbf737','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(149,1,1,148,154,154,'130\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\n1. Identify the tangent as \nperpendicular to the radius at the \npoint of contact and verify the \nAlternate Segment Theorem.\n2. Verify that tangents drawn from \nan external point to the same circle \nare equal when measured from \ntheir point of contact.\n3 Strategy: Learners through reflective \nquestions (or KWL) and justify their solutions \nto questions to the mock exams.\nMode: Class Exercises\nRecall the construction of various \nangles such as 75\n0\n, 105\n0\n, 135\n0,\nand \n150\n0\n.\n2 Strategy: Standard level 2 essay questions\nMode: Home Work\nConstruct a triangle or a quadrilateral \nunder given conditions.\nConstruct a particular locus for a given \ncondition.\n2 Strategy: Standard level 2 essay questions\nMode: Group work and presentation','746d1daa21353faf8716b7377dbc93b3cc2dad7eb91e3b1bab16d340ff7d4214','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(150,1,1,149,155,155,'131\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nWEEK 10\nLearning Indicators\n1. Identify parts of a circle and draw circles for given radii and through points\n2. Discuss the circle theorems by identifying the statements, proofs, examples and \napplications\nFOCAL AREA 1: CIRCLE AND CIRCLE THEOREMS\nA Circle\nA circle is a two-dimensional figure formed by a set of points that are at a constant or at a fixed \ndistance (radius) from a fixed point (centre) on the plane.\nParts of a Circle\nFigure 10.1 is an example of a circle. A, B, C, D and E are points on the circumference of the \ncircle. Point O is the centre of the circle.\nFigure 10.1: A diagram illustrating parts of a circle.\n1. Centre\nThe centre of the circle is the fixed point from which all points on the boundary of the \ncircle are equidistant. In Figure 10.1 , the centre is point O.\n2. Circumference\nThe perimeter of a circle is called the circumference . It is the distance around the circle \nas shown in Figure 10.2 .\nFigure 10.2: Circumference of a circle','835d616468937101e390591e9f8223a31bf1243c28d5fe2448d6fdf0e3396bd8','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(151,1,1,150,156,156,'132\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\n3. Arc\nA part of the circumference is called an arc , as shown in figures 10.3 and 10.4.\nMajor arc: A major arc is greater than half the circumference.\nMinor arc: A minor arc is less than half the circumference.\nFigure 10.3: Illustration of an arc of a circle Figure 10.4: Arc of a circle\n4. Radius\nThe shortest distance between the centre and any point on the circumference of the \ncircle is called the radius. In Figure 10.1 , \n135\n\nFigure 10.2: Circumference of a circle\n\n3. Arc \nA part of the circumference is called an arc, as shown in figures 10.3 and 10.4. \nMajor arc – A major arc is greater than half the circumference. \nMinor arc – A minor arc is less than half the circumference.\n\nFigure 10.3: Illustration of an arc of a circle Figure 10.4: Arc of a circle\n\n4. Radius \nThe shortest distance between the centre and any point on the circumference of the circle is \ncalled the radius. In Figure 10.1, |????????|=|????????|=|????????|=|????????|=|????????|= Radius(????). \nRadius(r).\n5. Diameter\nThe diameter of a circle is any straight-line segment that passes through the centre of the \ncircle and whose endpoints lie on the circumference of the circle.\nThe diameter is the longest chord of the circle.\nIn Figure 10.1, |BE| = diameter = 2 × radius = 2r .\n6. Sector\nFigure 10.5 is an example of a sector . The region is bounded by two radii and an \narc. Point O is the centre of the circle from which the sector was obtained, and lines OA \nand OB are radii.\nFigure 10.5: Sector of a circle\nMajor sector: A major sector has a central angle which is more than 180˚.','997076c6515e0de5587e8865b85d0766515b4fa8c5a425965d01d006d9b17837','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(152,1,1,151,157,157,'133\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nMinor sector: A minor sector has a central angle which is less than 180˚.\n7. Semicircle: Half of a circle. The central angle of a semicircle is 180˚.\n8. Quadrant: A quarter of a circle created by two radii perpendicular to each other.\n9. Chord: Line CD is called a chord . A straight line connecting two points on the \ncircumference of a circle.\nFigure 10.7: Line CD\nNote that the diameter is also a chord. It is the longest chord.\n10. Segment: The space shaded pink is called a segment. This is the region bounded by a \nchord and an arc\nFigure 10.6: A segment of a circle\nMajor segment: a segment where the arc is greater than half the circumference.\nMinor segment: a segment where the arc is less than half the circumference.\n11. Geometric Secant: A secant to a circle is a line that crosses the circle at exactly two \ndistinct points.\n12. Tangent: A tangent to a circle is a straight line that intersects the circle at exactly one \npoint, i.e., a tangent just touches the circumference of the circle.\nHow To Construct A Circle Of A Given Radius\nWe need a ruler and a pair of compasses to construct a circle with a given radius.\nExample 1\nConstruct a circle with radius 5cm\nSolution\nThe steps below will help you with the construction\nStep 1: With the aid of a ruler, open your compass to a distance of 5cm.\nStep 2: Mark a point O on a piece of paper. This point will be the centre of the circle you are \nabout to construct.\nStep 3: Place the pointer of the compass at point O.\nStep 4: Turn the compass slowly through 360˚ to draw a circle. Figure 10.8 shows an example \nof the expected result.','4cfae4f14e2b1212af654fa1af84cb7708706ab2ced7de607b3842eb5371745f','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(153,1,1,152,158,158,'134\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nFigure 10.8: Construction of a circle with radius 5cm.\nNote that an endless number of circles can have the same centre\nFigure 10.9: Many circles with the same centre, O.\nConstruct a circle through three points\nAn endless number of circles can travel through a single point, as illustrated in Figure 10.10.\nFigure 10.10: Some circles passing through point O\nLikewise, there are infinitely many circles that can pass through two points. Figure 10.11 \nshows some circles passing through two points, X and Y.','6e2c78d17e68427d9c4118163089b128a6bb01add924f4e1e2a4c6e472dd12ab','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(154,1,1,153,159,159,'135\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nFigure 10.11: Some circles passing through points X and Y\nHowever, only ONE circle can go through three points which are NOT on a straight line (non-\ncollinear)\nWhen such a circle is created through the vertices of a triangle, it is called a circumscribed \ncircle. The centre of the circle is called a circumcentre . The circumcentre is equidistant from \nall three vertices.\nHow do we create such a special circle?\nLet us use the example to learn how to do this.\nExample 2\nFigure 10.12 shows the position of three points X, Y and Z.\nUsing a ruler and a pair of compasses only, construct a circle to intersect all three points.\nLabel the centre of the circle, O.\nFigure 10.12: Three points, X , Y and Z.\nSolution\nAll three points X, Y and Z must be equidistant from the circle’s centre, O.\nThis means that we seek to find a point, O, such that |OX|=|OY|=|OZ|\nThe steps below will help you with the construction\n1. Step 1: Join points X and Y with a straight line and draw a perpendicular bisector,l1 , of \n|XY|. This is the same as constructing the locus of all points equidistant from points X and \nY. Any point on l1 will be equidistant from X and Y.','a68d04df556aa43711b7b03135cfe83fc95afc4caf4b87b17a837103d4f0d8b7','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(155,1,1,154,160,160,'136\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nFigure 10.13: Three points X, Y and Z and a perpendicular bisector of line XY\n2. Step 2: Join points X and Z with a straight line and draw a perpendicular bisector,l2 , of \n|XZ|. This is the same as constructing the locus of all points equidistant from points X and \nZ. Any point on l2 will be equidistant from X and Z.\nFigure 10.14: Points X, Y and Z, perpendicular bisectors of XY and XZ\n3. Step 3: Indicate the point of intersection of l1and l2 as O. Point O is equidistant from X, \nY, and Z. This means that |OX|=|OY|=|OZ|\nFigure 10.16: Locating a point equidistant from X, Y and Z.\n4. With the aid of a pair of compasses, draw a circle with centre O and radius |OX| or |OY| or \n|OZ|.\nFigure 10.17: A circle intersecting three points','dd70363c07bc107907ced6b538668d3a95f117735453dd56cab82123efd2243a','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(156,1,1,155,161,161,'137\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nExample 3\nMake a copy of the triangle below and construct a circumscribed circle (i.e., a circle where the \ncircumference touches the points of the triangle).\nFigure 10.18: Triangle PQR\nSolution\nThe steps below will help you with the construction.\nStep 1: Construct a copy of the triangle. (the orientation of your copy of PQR is not important)\nFigure 10.19: Triangle PQR\nStep 2: Construct a perpendicular bisector of any two sides of the triangle. Label the point of \nintersection as O\nFigure 10.20: Perpendicular bisectors of sides of a triangle.\nStep 3: With the pin of the compass at O and using |OA| or |OB| or |OC| as the radius, construct \na circle.\nFigure 10.21: A cyclic triangle PQR with centre O.','41daa880205456c2a1fa7d1bfd8747b7506ae0667e129e3db20a45bdc5df1d58','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(157,1,1,156,162,162,'138\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nExample 4\na. Using a ruler and pair of compasses only, construct a right-angled isosceles triangle DEF \nwith its hypotenuse = 113mm\nb. Construct a circle to intersect all three vertices of the triangle.\nc. Measure the radius of the circle\nd. Calculate the perimeter of triangle DEF\nSolution\na. \nFigure 10.22: Right-angle isosceles triangle DEF with hypotenuse 113mm (not 113mm here, but yours \nshould be!)\nb. To find the centre of the circle, we will construct a perpendicular bisector of lines DF and \nEF.\nUsing |OD| as the radius and O as the centre, construct a circle.\nFigure 10.23: Cyclic triangle DEF\nc. Use your ruler to measure the radius of the circle.\nRadius of the circle = 56.5 mm','2c988845acccafd1d9b94ae21630a9a95f567f92f3e352195d49cbb473cbe367','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(158,1,1,157,163,163,'139\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nNote, that in this case, the hypotenuse of the right-angled triangle is the diameter of \nthe circle. \nHence, the radius is half of the hypotenuse = \n2\n113\n= 56.5mm . We will go on to learn \nthat any right-angled cyclic triangle will always have its hypotenuse as the diameter of \nits circumcircle.\nd. Perimeter of triangle DEF = 80mm + 80mm + 113mm = 273mm\nCircle Theorems\nCircle theorems are a set of rules or properties related to angles, lines, and points on or in a \ncircle. These theorems are used in geometry to answer questions relating to circles.\nCircle Theorem Statements\nStatement One\nThe angle subtended by a chord at the centre is twice the angle subtended by it at the \ncircumference.\nIn Figure 10.24, A, B and C are points on the circle. O is the centre of the circle. The angle \nsubtended by the chord, |AB|, at the centre is and at the circumference is .\nThe circle theorem statement one means that:\n144\n\nd. Perimeter of triangle DEF=80????????+80????????+113????????=273????????\n\n<H2>Circle Theorems \nCircle theorems are a set of rules or properties related to angles, lines, and points on or in a \ncircle. These theorems are used in geometry to answer questions relating to circles. \n<H3>Circle Theorem Statements \n<BodySL>Statement One \nThe angle subtended by a chord at the centre is twice the angle subtended by it at the \ncircumference. \nIn Figure 10.24, A, B and C are points on the circle. O is the centre of the circle. The angle \nsubtended by the chord, |AB|, at the centre is ???? and at the circumference is ????. \nThe circle theorem statement one means that: \n????=2???? or ????=\n1\n2\n????\n\nFigure 10.24: Cyclic triangle ABC with centre O\n\n<BodySL>Statement Two \nThe angle subtended by the diameter at the circumference is a right angle. \nIn Figure 10.25, O, A, and B are points on the circumference of the circle with centre V. Line \nAVB represents the diameter of the circle. \nFigure 10.24: Cyclic triangle ABC with centre O\nStatement Two\nThe angle subtended by the diameter at the circumference is a right angle.\nIn Figure 10.25, O, A, and B are points on the circumference of the circle with centre V. Line \nAVB represents the diameter of the circle.','eb402f8e772e41467ee8bc01a8db4eb16aea55b785504935e21a0a7856f3a2cf','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(159,1,1,158,164,164,'140\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nFigure 10.25: Cyclic triangle OAB with centre V\nAngle AOB = 90˚ \nStatement Three\nThe angles subtended at the circumference by the same chord or arc in the same segment are \nequal.\nIn Figure 10.26, O, A, B and C are points on the circumference of the circle.\nAngle OCA = angle OBA since they are subtended at the circumference by the same chord \n|OA|\nFigure 10.26: A circle with points O, A, B and C on its circumference\nStatement Four\nTwo equal chords subtend equal angles at the centre of the circle.\nStatement Five\nIf the angles subtended by two chords at the centre are equal, then the two chords are equal. \nStatement Five is similar to Statement Four. One implies the other.\nIn Figure 10.27, point O is the centre of the circle and points X, Y, A and B are points on the \ncircumference of the circle. Chord |XY|=|AB|.\nFrom Statement Four, if |XY| is equal to |AB|, then angle XOY = angle AOB.\nFrom Statement Five, if angle XOY=angle AOB, then |XY|=|AB|','c92553d90aed291caa81c18e6bf95c655f145d1204ccbbfd3a9d50ea396a8b0a','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(160,1,1,159,165,165,'141\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nFigure 10.27: Circle with points Y, X, A and B on its circumference. It has centre O.\nStatement Six\nThe opposite angles in a cyclic quadrilateral are supplementary (add up to 180˚)\nFigure 10.28 shows a cyclic quadrilateral with internal angles a, b. c and d . Angles and are \nopposite to each other. Likewise, b and d .\nThis means that: a + c = 180 ˚ and b + d = 180˚\nFigure 10.28: A cyclic quadrilateral with internal angles a, b, c and d.\nStatement Seven\nThe angle between a tangent and a chord at a point of contact is equal to the angle in the \nalternate segment.\nIn Figure 10.29, ABC is a circumscribed triangle. The circle is tangent to Line DAE at point \nA. This means that angle BAE is equal to angle ACB. This is the same as saying x = y .\nFigure 10.29: Line DAE tangent to a circle at point A','40a6ea716da4256d51d7870db301036fb671252444c551231e61bedf544e160a','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(161,1,1,160,166,166,'142\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nProof of Circle Theorem Statements\nLet us prove some of these statements.\nPoof of Statement One\nStatement: The angle subtended by a chord at the centre is twice the angle subtended by it at \nthe circumference.\nFigure 10.30: Cyclic triangle ABC with centre O.\nProof\nIn Figure 10.30, we seek to prove that x = 2y\nDraw a straight line from point C, through point O, to intersect the circle at point D.\nFigure 10.31: Cyclic triangle ABC with centre O and CD as a diameter.\nThis will generate two triangles. They are triangle AOC and triangle BOC.\nBoth triangles are isosceles since two of their sides are radii. For triangle AOC, angle OAC = \nangle OCA = P.\nFor triangle BOC, angle OBC = angle OCB = s\nRemember that the exterior angle of a triangle theorem states that, for any triangle, the measure \nof the exterior angle of a triangle is equal to the sum of the measures of the two non-adjacent \ninterior angles. Therefore, from this, we have AOD = 2p and BOD = 2s','f2fa8fdf69408d82a313091b6c2af95f109d7f87801096152d07bfda6cd9ade5','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(162,1,1,161,167,167,'143\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nFigure 10.32: Cyclic triangle ABC with centre O and CD as a diameter.\nThis means that the angle at the centre\n149\n\n????=2????+2???? \n????=2(????+????) …………………….(1) \nbut ????=????+???? \nsubstitute ????=????+???? into equation (1) \nThis gives ????=2???? which proves the theorem.\n\n<H3>Proof of Statement Two \nStatement: The angle subtended by the diameter at the circumference is a right angle.\n\nFigure 10.33: Cyclic triangle OAB with centre V. \n<BodySL>Proof: \nWe need to prove that ∠AOB = 90° \nFrom Statement 1, \'The angle subtended by a chord at the centre is twice the angle subtended by \nit at the circumference.’ \nSo, we have ∠????????????= 2× ∠????????????. \nWe know that the angle at a point on a straight line is equal to 180°. \nThis implies that ∠AVB = 180° as AB is a straight line (diameter). \nSo, we have 2 × ∠???????????? = 180° which implies that ∠AOB = 90°.\n\n<H3>Proof of Statement Three \nStatement: The angles subtended at the circumference by the same arc are equal. \nProof of Statement Two\nStatement: The angle subtended by the diameter at the circumference is a right angle.\nFigure 10.33: Cyclic triangle OAB with centre V.\nProof\nWe need to prove that ∠AOB = 90°\nFrom Statement 1, ‘The angle subtended by a chord at the centre is twice the angle subtended \nby it at the circumference.’\nSo, we have \n149\n\n????=2????+2???? \n????=2(????+????) …………………….(1) \nbut ????=????+???? \nsubstitute ????=????+???? into equation (1) \nThis gives ????=2???? which proves the theorem.\n\n<H3>Proof of Statement Two \nStatement: The angle subtended by the diameter at the circumference is a right angle.\n\nFigure 10.33: Cyclic triangle OAB with centre V. \n<BodySL>Proof: \nWe need to prove that ∠AOB = 90° \nFrom Statement 1, \'The angle subtended by a chord at the centre is twice the angle subtended by \nit at the circumference.’ \nSo, we have ∠????????????= 2× ∠????????????. \nWe know that the angle at a point on a straight line is equal to 180°. \nThis implies that ∠AVB = 180° as AB is a straight line (diameter). \nSo, we have 2 × ∠???????????? = 180° which implies that ∠AOB = 90°.\n\n<H3>Proof of Statement Three \nStatement: The angles subtended at the circumference by the same arc are equal. \n.\nWe know that the angle at a point on a straight line is equal to 180˚.','710218ab3eb3d7ccf27246ce27b4b08e11135f4aa16f171ee1f3aa5674520e95','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(163,1,1,162,168,168,'144\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nThis implies that ∠AVB = 180° as AB is a straight line (diameter).\nSo, we have which implies that 2 × ∠AOB = 90°.\nProof of Statement Three\nStatement: The angles subtended at the circumference by the same arc are equal.\nProof: Consider the following figure, which shows an arc AB subtending angles ACB and \nADB at two arbitrary points, C and D, on the circumference. O is the centre of the circle.\n\nFigure 10.34: Circle ABCD with centre O.\nWe need to prove that ∠ACB = ∠ADB.\nUsing the circle theorem, ‘The angle subtended by a chord at the centre is twice the angle \nsubtended by it at the circumference,’\n150\n\nProof \nConsider the following figure, which shows an arc AB subtending angles ACB and ADB at two \narbitrary points, C and D, on the circumference. O is the centre of the circle.\n\nFigure 10.34: Circle ABCD with centre O. \nWe need to prove that ∠ACB = ∠ADB. \nUsing the circle theorem, \'The angle subtended by a chord at the centre is twice the angle \nsubtended by it at the circumference,’ \nWe know that ∠????????????= \n1\n2\n× ∠???????????? ……..…………(1) \nLikewise, ∠????????????= \n1\n2\n× ∠???????????? …………………(2) \nFrom equations (1) and (2), we get ∠ACB = ∠ADB, which proves the theorem.\n\nProof of Statement four: Two equal chords subtend equal angles at the centre of the circle. \n<BodySL>Proof \nConsider the circle given below with centre O and two chords |AB| and |XY|, such that |AB| = \n|XY|. \nWe seek to prove ∠???????????? = ∠????????????\n\nFrom equations (1) and (2), we get ∠ACB = ∠ADB, which proves the theorem.\nProof of Statement Four\nTwo equal chords subtend equal angles at the centre of the circle.\nProof: Consider the circle given below with centre O and two chords |AB| and |XY|, such \nthat |AB| = |XY|.\nWe seek to prove ∠AOB = ∠ XOY\nFigure 10.35: Circle with centre O with points Y, X, A and B on its circumference.','29b5c8f4a89402202774187e26b603c01ffe493aa63f493283fb6eb8ca705e34','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(164,1,1,163,169,169,'145\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nIn triangles XOY and AOB, we have OA = OB (Radii), OX = OY (Radii) and AB = CD \n(Given)\nSo, triangles XOY and AOB are congruent by the Side-Side-Side (SSS) congruence rule.\nSo, we have ∠XOY = ∠AOB since corresponding sides and angles are the same.\nProof of Statement Five\nStatement: If the angles subtended by two chords at the centre are equal, then the two chords \nare equal.\nProof: Consider Figure 10.36. It is a circle with centre O and two chords, |AB| and |CD|, \nsuch that ∠AOB = ∠COD.\nFigure 10.36: Circle ABCD with centre O\nWe need to prove that |AB| = |CD|.\nIn triangles AOB and COD, we have |OA| = |OC|=|OB| = |OD|= (Radii) and ∠AOB = ∠COD \n(Given)\nSo, triangles AOB and COD are congruent by the Side-Angle-Side congruence rule.\nSo, we have |AB| = |CD| (Corresponding parts of congruent triangles).\nThe proof of the remaining two statements will be dealt with in our next lesson.\nExample 1\nIn Figure 10.37, A and C are points on the circumference of the circle with centre O. Line AT \nis tangent to the circle at point A, angle OAC = and angle ATO = 28˚.\nCalculate the measure of angle x\nFigure 10.37: A tangent to a circle at point T.','5caaf59d49c07104c62229f192628cb1c858670b7200a3802ce96167fb73e5d5','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(165,1,1,164,170,170,'146\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nSolution\nFrom the theorem: “The angle between the radius and the tangent at the point of contact is 90” \nWe have ∠OAT = 90°.\nThis implies that\n152\n\nIn triangles AOB and COD, we have |OA| = |OC|=|OB| = |OD|= (Radii) and ∠AOB = ∠COD \n(Given) \nSo, triangles AOB and COD are congruent by the Side-Angle-Side congruence rule. \nSo, we have |AB| = |CD| (Corresponding parts of congruent triangles). \nThe proof of the remaining two statements will be dealt with in our next lesson. \n<H4>Example 1 \nIn Figure 10.37, A and C are points on the circumference of the circle with centre O. Line AT is \ntangent to the circle at point A, angle OAC = ???? and angle ATO=28°. \nCalculate the measure of angle ????\n\nFigure 10.37: A tangent to a circle at point T. \n<BodySL>Solution \nFrom the theorem: “The angle between the radius and the tangent at the point of contact is 90°” \nWe have ∠OAT = 90°. \nThis implies that \n90° + 28° + ∠????????????= 180° \n⇒ ∠???????????? = 180° − (90° + 28°) \nangle ????????????= 62° \nSince ????????= ???????? (???????????????????? ???????? ????ℎ???? ????????????????????????)⇒ ∠???????????? = ∠???????????? = ???? \nUsing the exterior angle theorem, we have ∠???????????? + ∠????????????= 62° \n⇒ ???? + ???? = 62° \n⇒ 2???? = 62° \n⇒ ???? = 31°\n\n152\n\nIn triangles AOB and COD, we have |OA| = |OC|=|OB| = |OD|= (Radii) and ∠AOB = ∠COD \n(Given) \nSo, triangles AOB and COD are congruent by the Side-Angle-Side congruence rule. \nSo, we have |AB| = |CD| (Corresponding parts of congruent triangles). \nThe proof of the remaining two statements will be dealt with in our next lesson. \n<H4>Example 1 \nIn Figure 10.37, A and C are points on the circumference of the circle with centre O. Line AT is \ntangent to the circle at point A, angle OAC = ???? and angle ATO=28°. \nCalculate the measure of angle ????\n\nFigure 10.37: A tangent to a circle at point T. \n<BodySL>Solution \nFrom the theorem: “The angle between the radius and the tangent at the point of contact is 90°” \nWe have ∠OAT = 90°. \nThis implies that \n90° + 28° + ∠????????????= 180° \n⇒ ∠???????????? = 180° − (90° + 28°) \nangle ????????????= 62° \nSince ????????= ???????? (???????????????????? ???????? ????ℎ???? ????????????????????????)⇒ ∠???????????? = ∠???????????? = ???? \nUsing the exterior angle theorem, we have ∠???????????? + ∠????????????= 62° \n⇒ ???? + ???? = 62° \n⇒ 2???? = 62° \n⇒ ???? = 31°\n\nExample 2\nFigure 10.38 shows a cyclic quadrilateral ABCD with centre O, angle BAD = 63˚, angle BOD \n= y and angle BCD = x .\nFigure 10.38: Cyclic quadrilateral ABCD with centre O.\nCalculate the measure of angle:\ni. x\nii. y\nSolution\ni. Since the angle subtended at the centre is twice the angle subtended at the circumference, \nwe have y = 2 × 63 = 126˚.\nii. Since opposite angles in a cyclic quadrilateral add up to 180˚, we have x + 63 = 180˚\nx = 180 −63 = 117˚','696446452a25777d50a505d3aecf46ea6a33c9ab3d5c5ad55c1327d4109673df','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(166,1,1,165,171,171,'147\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nLearning Tasks\nThis learning module aims to equip learners with an understanding of circle theorems \nand their applications. Implement differentiated learning support mechanisms to \naddress individual learning challenges.\nUpon completion, learners will be able to:\n1. Identify and state relevant circle theorems for given geometric situations.\n2. Apply circle theorems to calculate unknown angles in circles.\n3. Utilise circle theorems to solve geometric problems involving circles.\nPEDAGOGICAL EXEMPLARS\nWhen teaching circle theorems, a variety of pedagogical approaches can be employed to make \nthe concepts more accessible, engaging, and memorable for learners.\nTwo examples of such are:\n1. Inquiry-Based Learning/Discovery Approach\nWith this strategy, instead of directly stating the theorems, guide learners to discover the \nrelationships themselves through hands-on activities. To do this, first provide pre-drawn \ncircles with various points and lines (chords, tangents, angles at the centre). Then, ask \nlearners to measure angles using protractors and observe patterns. For example, have \nthem draw several angles subtended by the same arc at the circumference and compare \ntheir measures. Finally, pose guiding questions that lead to the formulation of the \ntheorem. Encourage group discussions and conjectures.\n2. Group work/collaborative learning\nUsing this strategy:\na. Review learners’ knowledge of parts of a circle. Address their misconceptions about \nsuch concepts, and in pairs, draw circles with various radii.\nb. In groups task learners to engage in discussions to recall the definition of circles and \ntheir properties.\nc. Review learners’ knowledge on the various circle theorems and their proofs.\nd. Engage learners to apply the circle theorem statements to answer questions.\nKEY ASSESSMENT LEVEL 3: RESEARCH\n1. Provide a comprehensive note with supported diagrams and examples on any five (5) of \nthe circle theorems you have learnt.\n2. Research and write a page about the usefulness of these circle theorems in real life.','1784058c02fb88e027719d0a1aabfbf34a916d1bec5826a86e4f34b80b3ca605','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(167,1,1,166,172,172,'148\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nPLC Session 10\n45 minutes Planning & Reviewing the Learning Plan\n1. Begin the session by recapping lessons from the previous year/session/week, focusing on \nhow learning was applied.\n2. Use your subject specific app to explore the content before planning. Ask the app to\na. explain the week’s concepts in a practical way that will make it easier for learners to \nunderstand\nb. identify two or three common misconceptions learners may have on the week’s content \nand how to address them using contexts familiar to Ghanaian learners. (NTS 3a, 3g \nand 3m).\n3. Use your subject specific app to plan your lesson. Ask the app to\na. suggest the most effective method for teaching the week’s concepts to learners with \ndiverse backgrounds.\nb. suggest learning activities, differentiation and essential questions based on the method \nof teaching (NTS 3a-3q)\nc. review your prepared learning plan and address any challenging issues you may have \nidentified in your subject area (NTS 3a-3c).\nNote\n• Take note of the recap topics linked to Years 1 and 2 while preparing for your \nlesson.\n• Refer to the Values Learning Community Handbook and link the values for the \nweek in your lesson to ensure that learners are applying what they learn.\n4. Plan the week’s key assessment with your app\na. The key assessment for the week is research . Chat with your app to plan this assessment, \nindicating what you and your learners will do before, during and after the research \n(NTS 3k-3q).\n10 minutes Content Exploration\n5. As a subject group, select one problem or idea/term in the week’s content that is challenging \n(not a routine procedural question, but one that requires conceptual understanding) and \nthen\na. use the app, ask it to solve the problem step by step and explain the reasoning behind \neach step (you may be assisted by a teacher who is strong in the content area)\nb. all teachers in the group work through a similar problem individually for 3–4 minutes\nc. the group then works through the problem together step by step','34064b36cf92db708a1f619f8e18e3cb8c8169ee9618e7f54f0c7c18c742a9e8','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(168,1,1,167,173,173,'149\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nd. highlight the most common errors made at each step and discuss (NTS 1c, 1e, 2c, 2e, \n3e-3j).\nNote\n1. This part is not just about ‘How do I teach this?’ but how do I teach it for learners \nto understand?\n2. If the group still shares the same content gap, use the app: ask it to solve the \nproblem step by step and explain the reasoning behind each step.\n3. Problems/ideas discussed should be sourced from the Teacher Manual content, not \ninvented separately. This keeps the content exploration directly connected to what \nteachers will deliver.\n30 minutes Enactment\n6. Enact a teaching activity based on your learning plan (NTS la-1b, 1e-lg, 2c, 2e, 2f, 3c-3j \nand 3o).\na. Colleagues observe with specific attention to clarity of concept explanation, use of \nfamiliar Ghanaian examples and how misconceptions are handled if they arise.\nb. After the enactment, ask for structured feedback from the group\ni. was the concept explained in a way that a learner with no prior knowledge could \nfollow?\nii. were the examples grounded in contexts familiar to Ghanaian learners?\niii. did any misconceptions surface and if so, how were they addressed?\n5 minutes Reflection\n7. Reflect and share your views on the session. One thing that worked well, what did not work \nwell and what you will do differently. (NTS 1a, 1b).\n8. Identify a critical friend to observe and provide feedback on your lesson in relation to the \nPLC Session conducted (NTS 1f, 1g).\n9. Remember to\na. transfer your chats into your learning planner template.\nb. read your Teacher Manual and related Learner Material in preparation for the next \nsession. Take note of the recap weeks, which are linked to Years 1 and 2 (NTS 3a).\nc. integrate National Values, GESI, SEL, and 21st century skills into your learning \nactivities, assessment tasks, and rubrics/mark schemes.','503de9862e10d4b938ed58d1ee20fff00e1e599cbb1663c5c523182748c4d5d0','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(169,1,1,168,174,174,'150\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nWEEK 11\nLearning Indicators\n1. Identify the tangent as perpendicular to the radius at the point of contact and verify the \nAlternate Segment Theorem\n2. Verify that tangents drawn from an external point to the same circle are equal when \nmeasured from their point of contact\nFOCAL AREA 1: THE TANGENT OF A CIRCLE 1\nA tangent is a straight line drawn from an external point that touches a circle at exactly one \npoint on the circumference of the circle. There can be an infinite number of tangents to a circle. \nThese tangents follow certain properties that can be used as identities to perform mathematical \ncomputations on circles.\nWe will learn how to prove two of those properties in detail.\nOur focus is to prove that the tangent at any point of a circle is perpendicular to the radius \nthrough the point of contact.\nIn Figure 11.1, O is the Centre of the circle and line L is tangent to the circle at point A.\nFigure 11.1: A tangent to a circle at point A\nFrom prior knowledge, we know that for all line segments drawn from a point (say O) to a line \n(say L), the shortest line is perpendicular to L.\nIn Figure 11.1, |OA|=|OC| as they are radii of the circle.\nAlso, |OB| = |OC| + |CB|. So clearly, |OC| < |OB| and |OA| < |OB|\nThis logic holds for every point B on the tangent, L, except point A.\nSo |OA| is shorter than any other line segment joining O to any point on L. Hence, OA is \nperpendicular to line L\nTherefore, the tangent at any point of a circle is perpendicular to the radius through the point \nof contact.\nLet us use this understanding to answer a question.','3360059c9fd1d51dd19fc53f77d1086f4f85bb6f724b8db6648c30cec1c80c6c','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(170,1,1,169,175,175,'151\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nExample 1\nIn the diagram, O is the centre of the circle.\nLine AC is tangent to the circle at B and BOC = 120˚ . Calculate the value of ABD .\nFigure 11.2: A tangent to a circle at point B\nSolution\nThe triangle BOD is isosceles, since |OB|=|OD|=radius. This implies angle OBD = angle ODB\n158\n\nLet us use this understanding to answer a question. \n<H4>Example 1 \nIn the diagram, O is the centre of the circle. \nLine AC is tangent to the circle at B and ????????????=120°. Calculate the value of ????????????.\n\nFigure 11.2: A tangent to a circle at point B \nSolution \nThe triangle ???????????? is isosceles, since |OB|=|OD|=radius. This implies angle OBD = angle ODB \nLet OBD = ODB = ???? \n????+????+120°=180° \n2????=180°−120° \n2????=60° \n????=30° \nBut ????????????=90° \n????????????=90°−????=90−30=60° \n∴ ???????????? =60°\n\nFigure 11.3: Triangle BOD\n\n<H3>Theorem 2 \n<H4>Alternate Segment Theorem \nThe Alternate Segment Theorem states that “For any circle, the angle formed between the \ntangent and the chord through the point of contact of the tangent is equal to the angle formed by \nthe chord in the alternate segment”. \nFigure 11.3: Triangle BOD\nTheorem 2\nAlternate Segment Theorem\nThe Alternate Segment Theorem states that “For any circle, the angle formed between the \ntangent and the chord through the point of contact of the tangent is equal to the angle formed \nby the chord in the alternate segment”.\nThe alternate segment theorem is also known as the tangent-chord theorem.\nLet us use Figure 11.4 to break down the theorem.\nIn the Figure, ABC is a cyclic triangle with centre \nO. The circle is tangent to Line DAE at point A. \nThe shaded part is a minor segment. Its alternate \nsegment is the unshaded part of the circle. \nAccording to the alternative segment theorem, \nangle BAE is equal to ACB. i.e. x = y\nFigure 11.4: Tangent to a circle at point A','51ceebb57b8933d479b7d3dec7f364570ab55da3f5e7461250d6207b7d84ddab','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(171,1,1,170,176,176,'152\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nProof \nFrom our previous proof, we established that \nthe angle the radius or the diameter makes with \nthe tangent at the point of tangency is 90˚. \nTherefore, OAE = 90\nThis implies that OAB + x = 90\nsolving for OAB, we have OAB = 90 − x\nSince triangle OAB is isosceles,\nOAB = OBA = 90 − x \nFigure 11.5: Tangent to a circle at point A\n159\n\nThe alternate segment theorem is also known as the tangent-chord theorem. \nLet us use Figure 11.4 to break down the \ntheorem. \nIn the Figure, ABC is a cyclic triangle with \ncentre O. The circle is tangent to Line DAE at \npoint A. The shaded part is a minor segment. \nIts alternate segment is the unshaded part of \nthe circle. According to the alternative \nsegment theorem, angle BAE is equal to \nACB. i.e. ????=????\n\nFigure 11.4: Tangent to a circle at point A\n\n<H4>Proof \nFrom our previous proof, we established that \nthe angle the radius or the diameter makes \nwith the tangent at the point of tangency is \n90°. Therefore, OAE=90 \nThis implies that OAB+????=90 \nsolving for OAB, we have OAB=90−???? \nSince triangle OAB is isosceles, \n????????????=????????????=90−????\n\nFigure 11.5: Tangent to a circle at point A \nAlso, ????????????+(90−????)+(90−????)=180° \nsolving for AOB, we have ????????????=180−90−90+2???? \nThis implies ????????????=2????………….(1) \nRecall that the angle at the centre of the circle is twice the angle at the circumference of the \ncircle. This means that, ????????????=2????…………..(2) \nComparing (1) to (2), we have 2????=2???? \nThis means ????=????, proving the theorem.\n\n<H4>Example 2 \nFind the unknown angles (angle ACP and angle BAC) in the figure, given that the chord BC \nmakes the angle 65° with the tangent line PQ. \nExample 2\nFind the unknown angles (angle ACP and angle BAC) in the figure, given that the chord BC \nmakes the angle 65° with the tangent line PQ.\nFigure 11.6: A tangent to a circle at point C\nSolution\nGiven that, ∠QCB = 65°. By using the alternate segment theorem, we can say that ∠CAB = \n65°. Similarly, by using the angles in the alternate segment, ∠PCA = 65° Therefore, ∠CAB = \n65° and ∠PCA = 65°.','6e73372e5a456522f9f1d0b54745a59be3566539b82a7e01e7eb6973f2782a2c','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(172,1,1,171,177,177,'153\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nExample 3\nFind the angle ∠QPS in the given figure.\nFigure 11.7: A tangent to a circle at point P\nSolution\nGiven that, ∠PRQ = 70°.\nBy using the alternate segment theorem, ∠QPS = ∠PRQ. Hence, ∠QPS = 70°.\nAlternate Segment Theorem for Quadrilaterals\nIn Figure 11.8, line DAE is a tangent to the circle at point A. BCD are points on the circumference \nof the circle and ABCD is a cyclic quadrilateral.\nFigure 11.8: Line DAE tangent to cyclic quadrilateral ABCD at point A\nFrom the alternate segment theorem, ∠w = ∠u ……..... (1)\nWe need to prove that ∠ = ∠\nWe know from our previous theorems that opposite angles in a cyclic quadrilateral is equal to \n180. This means ∠y + ∠u = 180° …………..................…(2)\nSince ∠w and ∠x are adjacent at point A on a straight line, we can conclude that\n∠w+ ∠x = 180° …………................................................….(3)\nFrom (3) and (4), we have ∠y + ∠u = ∠w+ ∠x\nfrom (1), we have ∠w = ∠u\nTherefore, ∠y + ∠w = ∠w+ ∠x\nEliminating ∠w, we have ∠y = ∠x','a79c5481cdfcd407bc00a6d9a02de464c679deeddd346f67c3ec9aaca96f7469','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(173,1,1,172,178,178,'154\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nTheorem 3\nTwo tangents drawn from an external point to the same circle are equal when measured from \ntheir point of contact.\nFigure 11.9: Lines PA and PB tangent to a circle with centre O at points A and B.\nIn Figure 11.9, lines AP and BP are tangents to circle, S, at points A and B. point O is the centre \nof the circle. According to the equal tangent theorem,\n|PA| = |PB|. Also, ∠POA = ∠POB and ∠APO = ∠BPO\nProof: All three parts will be proved if we show that ΔPAO is congruent to ΔPBO.\nComparing the two triangles, we see that:\n1. OA = OB (radii of the same circle)\n2. OP = hypotenuse of both triangles.\n3. From our first theorem, ∠OAP = ∠OBP = 90. So, by the Side-Angle-Side property of \ntriangles, triangle OAP is congruent or the same as triangle OBP. This follows that the \nlengths of the tangents BP and AP are equal. Also, line OP bisects BPA and AOB.\nHence, angle BOP = angle AOP and angle BPO = angle APO.\nExample 4\nConsider a chord AB of length 9 cm in a circle of radius 5 cm. Tangents at A and B intersect \nat C, as shown below:\n\nFigure 11.10: Lines AC and BC are tangent to a circle at points A and B, with the centre at O.','8dbecf511c3abaea840a93d61ec4a303cacceb75f807d92d7355b604c31743d5','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(174,1,1,173,179,179,'155\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nWhat are the lengths of the tangents CA and CB?\nSolution\nConnect point O to C with a straight line to intersect AB at D\nFigure 11.11: Lines AC and BC tangent to a circle with centre O at points A and B\n163\n\nFigure 11.11: Lines AC and BC tangent to a circle with centre O at points A and B \nSince AB = 9 units, AD = \n1\n2\n×9=4.5 units. \nSin(AOC)=\n4.5\n5\n\nAOC=Sin\n−1\n(0.9)=64.16 \nTan 64.16=\n|????????|\n5\n\n|????????|=5????????????64.16=10.32 units. \nTherefore, the lengths of the tangents AC and BC is 10.32 units\n\n<TH>Learning Tasks \nThis learning module aims to equip learners with an understanding of circle theorems and their \napplications. Implement differentiated learning support mechanisms to address individual \nlearning challenges. \nUpon completion, learners will be able to: \n1. Identify and state relevant circle theorems for given geometric situations. \n2. Apply circle theorems to calculate unknown angles in circles. \n3. Utilise circle theorems to solve geometric problems involving circles.\n\n<H1>PEDAGOGICAL EXEMPLARS \nWhen teaching circle theorems, a variety of pedagogical approaches can be employed to make \nthe concepts more accessible, engaging, and memorable for learners. \nTwo of such are: \n1. Inquiry-Based Learning/Discovery Approach \nLearning Tasks\nThis learning module aims to equip learners with an understanding of circle theorems \nand their applications. Implement differentiated learning support mechanisms to \naddress individual learning challenges.\nUpon completion, learners will be able to:\n1. Identify and state relevant circle theorems for given geometric situations.\n2. Apply circle theorems to calculate unknown angles in circles.\n3. Utilise circle theorems to solve geometric problems involving circles.\nPEDAGOGICAL EXEMPLARS\nWhen teaching circle theorems, a variety of pedagogical approaches can be employed to make \nthe concepts more accessible, engaging, and memorable for learners.','c5e720ab654c94e14b5f1d8750a798c200bf629c9d2a57bd348cbf1aa966a90e','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(175,1,1,174,180,180,'156\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nTwo of such are:\n1. Inquiry-Based Learning/Discovery Approach\nWith this strategy, instead of directly stating the theorems, guide learners to discover the \nrelationships themselves through hands-on activities. To do this, first provide pre-drawn \ncircles with various points and lines (chords, tangents, angles at the centre). Then, ask \nlearners to measure angles using protractors and observe patterns. For example, have \nthem draw several angles subtended by the same arc at the circumference and compare \ntheir measures. Finally, pose guiding questions that lead to the formulation of the \ntheorem. Encourage group discussions and conjectures.\n2. Group work/collaborative learning.\nUsing this strategy:\na. Review learners’ knowledge of parts of a circle. Address their misconceptions about \nsuch concepts, and in pairs, draw circles with various radii.\nb. In groups task learners to engage in discussions to recall the definition of circles and \ntheir properties.\nc. Review learners’ knowledge on the various circle theorems and their proofs.\nd. Engage learners to apply the circle theorem statements to answer questions.\nKEY ASSESSMENT LEVEL 3: CLASS EXERCISE\n1. In Figure 11.12, X, Y and Z are points on the circumference of the circle. V is the centre \nof the circle. OZ and OX are tangents to the circle, OX = 6 cm and XYZ = 113˚.\nFind\na. XOZ 5 marks\nb. Perimeter of quadrilateral OXVZ 4 marks\nTotal 9 marks\nFigure 11.12: Lines OX and OZ tangent to a circle at points X and Z\n2. In Figure 11.13, OABC is a cyclic quadrilateral. Line DE is a tangent to the circle at point \nO. |OB| is the diameter of the circle, BOC = 57˚ and ACB = 50˚','dad3536ff880822e5da019838c80d245fcfeccdf997c10f9ebbdfa18a3787445','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(176,1,1,175,181,181,'157\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\na. Calculate\ni. ABC 3 marks\nii. AOE 2 marks\nb. If the diameter of the circle is 10cm, find the perimeter of the quadrilateral OABC \n6 marks\nTotal 11 marks\nFigure 11.13: Line DOE tangent to cyclic quadrilateral OABC at point O\nPLC Session 11\n45 minutes Planning & Reviewing the Learning Plan\n1. Begin the session by recapping lessons from the previous year/session/week, focusing on \nhow learning was applied.\n2. Use your subject specific app to explore the content before planning. Ask the app to\na. explain the week’s concepts in a practical way that will make it easier for learners to \nunderstand\nb. identify two or three common misconceptions learners may have on the week’s content \nand how to address them using contexts familiar to Ghanaian learners. (NTS 3a, 3g \nand 3m).\n3. Use your subject specific app to plan your lesson. Ask the app to\na. suggest the most effective method for teaching the week’s concepts to learners with \ndiverse backgrounds.\nb. suggest learning activities, differentiation and essential questions based on the method \nof teaching (NTS 3a-3q)\nc. review your prepared learning plan and address any challenging issues you may have \nidentified in your subject area (NTS 3a-3c).','ddceb89d228e47b88b4a6f46dcebc9a151bc40675e44f2a227c3dc0574403b06','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(177,1,1,176,182,182,'158\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nNote\n• Take note of the recap topics linked to Years 1 and 2 while preparing for your \nlesson.\n• Refer to the Values Learning Community Handbook and link the values for the \nweek in your lesson to ensure that learners are applying what they learn.\n4. Plan the week’s key assessment with your app\na. The key assessment for the week is class exercise . Chat with your app to plan this \nassessment, indicating what you and your learners will do before, during and after the \nexercise (NTS 3k-3q).\n10 minutes Content Exploration\n5. As a subject group, select one problem or idea/term in the week’s content that is challenging \n(not a routine procedural question, but one that requires conceptual understanding) and \nthen\na. use the app, ask it to solve the problem step by step and explain the reasoning behind \neach step (you may be assisted by a teacher who is strong in the content area)\nb. all teachers in the group work through a similar problem individually for 3–4 minutes\nc. the group then works through the problem together step by step\nd. highlight the most common errors made at each step and discuss (NTS 1c, 1e, 2c, 2e, \n3e-3j).\nNote\n• This part is not just about ‘How do I teach this?’ but how do I teach it for learners \nto understand?\n• If the group still shares the same content gap, use the app: ask it to solve the \nproblem step by step and explain the reasoning behind each step.\n• Problems/ideas discussed should be sourced from the Teacher Manual content, not \ninvented separately. This keeps the content exploration directly connected to what \nteachers will deliver.\n30 minutes Enactment\n6. Enact a teaching activity based on your learning plan (NTS la-1b, 1e-lg, 2c, 2e, 2f, 3c-3j \nand 3o).\na. Colleagues observe with specific attention to clarity of concept explanation, use of \nfamiliar Ghanaian examples and how misconceptions are handled if they arise.\nb. After the enactment, ask for structured feedback from the group','0368fe35e5069fb3e01e3a1e88decb347ab0523a9a70069e908bb392b0c00721','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(178,1,1,177,183,183,'159\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\ni. was the concept explained in a way that a learner with no prior knowledge could \nfollow?\nii. were the examples grounded in contexts familiar to Ghanaian learners?\niii. did any misconceptions surface and if so, how were they addressed?\n5 minutes Reflection\n7. Reflect and share your views on the session. One thing that worked well, what did not work \nwell and what you will do differently. (NTS 1a, 1b).\n8. Identify a critical friend to observe and provide feedback on your lesson in relation to the \nPLC Session conducted (NTS 1f, 1g).\n9. Remember to\na. transfer your chats into your learning planner template.\nb. read your Teacher Manual and related Learner Material in preparation for the next \nsession. Take note of the recap weeks, which are linked to Years 1 and 2 (NTS 3a).\nc. integrate National Values, GESI, SEL, and 21st century skills into your learning \nactivities, assessment tasks, and rubrics/mark schemes.','ced37c4ebbb7fc53df403dc9d738d5b1f493f6d9b47a81a63cb91e766a28f46a','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(179,1,1,178,184,184,'160\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nWEEK 12\nLearning Indicator: Recall the construction of various angles, such as 75°, 105°, 135° and \n150°\nFOCAL AREA: UNDERSTANDING GEOMETRIC \nCONSTRUCTIONS\nConstruction of 150° angle\nMethod 1\nStep 1: Construct 120°\nFigure 12.1: Construction of angle 120 °\nStep 2: Bisect angle AOB\nFigure 12.2: Construction of 150 °\nMethod 2\nStep 1: Construct 90°\nFigure 12.3: Construction of 90 °\nStep 2: With the pin of the compass at S and using radius |PS|, construct an arc to intersect arc \nTS at O.','8d9a9e06a1ed2de3514afdc772bfc9b5f1bd7e2448dee3af27aebc7da74b11b4','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(180,1,1,179,185,185,'161\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nFigure 12.4: Construction of 150 °\nUse your protractor to verify your answer.\nConstruction of 75°\nMethod 1\nConstruct a 60° angle adjacent to a 90° angle and bisect the angle between them.\nThis gives 60° + \n2\n1\n× 30° = 60° + 15° = 75°\nStep 1: Construct 60° adjacent to 90°\nFigure 12.5: Construction of 90 °\nStep 2: Bisect acute angle TPR.\nFigure 12.6: Construction of 75 °\nFigure 12.7: Construction of 75 °','df221eb5b5b2becd50d9f4ef03ace04a241a7dfc50d344c0195eb811e4d9748e','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(181,1,1,180,186,186,'162\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nMethod 2\nFirst construct 150°, as above, and then bisect it.\nStep 1: Construct 150°\nFigure 12.8: Construction of 150 °\nStep 2: Bisect 150°\nFigure 12.9: Bisection of 150 °\nConstruction of 105°\nConstruct 120° adjacent to 90° and bisect the angle between them.\nStep 1: Construct 120° adjacent to 90°.\nFigure 12.10: Construction of 90 ° adjacent to 120°','342848b5dd4d7de586fb66d34d0cb94bc29793fc2127dd1b7d0412c8c771d934','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(182,1,1,181,187,187,'163\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nStep 2: Bisect AOB\nFigure 12.11: Construction of 105 °\nConstruction of 135°\nStep 1: Construct 90°\nFigure 12.12: Construction of 90 °\nStep 2: Bisect BOA\nFigure 12.13: Construction of 135°\nLearning Tasks\nGuide learners to solve tasks related to the construction of acute and obtuse angles \nusing appropriate instruments. Provide support systems for learners who may \nencounter difficulties.\nLearners are to use a straight edge (for example, a ruler), a pencil, and a pair of \ncompasses only to construct','1a5dbaba7228321fd805b07c7294087bafdad184e9751953289809f6a317ee73','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(183,1,1,182,188,188,'164\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\n1. 75°\n2. 105°\n3. 135°\n4. 150°\nPEDAGOGICAL EXEMPLARS\nThe aim of the lessons for the week is for all learners to be able to use appropriate instruments \nto construct standard angles. The following pedagogical approach is suggested for teachers to \ntake learners through.\n1. Think-pair-share activities:\n2. In pairs, engage learners to review on the construction of angles from the Junior High \nSchool.\n3. Group discussions:\n• In convenient groups, learners discuss and draw various angles using the appropriate \nmathematical tools.\nKEY ASSESSMENT: PRACTICE PAPER 1\nRecommended mode of assessment for week 12 is homework on standard level 2 essay questions.\nHome Work\nConstruction of Angles\nName: ……………………………………………………………………………………………\nInstructions and information\n• Use a pencil, ruler, and pair of compasses only to answer the questions.\n• Answer all questions.\n• Answer the questions in the spaces provided– there may be more space than you need.\n• You must show all your working, including any construction marks.\n• The marks for each question are shown in brackets.\nAdvice\n• Please read each question carefully before you start to answer it.\n• Use your protractor to validate your answers.','7c6e52ebc32c1ef32c8cdeab20f1010d4611bf5a36597afd8c33dda80ced01db','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(184,1,1,183,189,189,'165\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\n1. Construct the bisector of AOB (2 marks)\nFigure 12.14: Lines OA and OB intersecting at point O\n2. Construct a line from point O to intersect line L at right angles. (2 marks)\n\nFigure 12.15: Line L and point O\n3. \na. Construct AOB = 30° (2 marks)\nb. Construct OAB = 105° (3 marks)\nFigure 12.16: Vertical line OA','85c938a6aacaf1e940a1566d678ae8c3047fafe239d0b0c3e9bab44f710ba7f9','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(185,1,1,184,190,190,'Section 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction \n166\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\n4. \na. Construct a line from point A to intersect arc L at D such that BAD = 135° (2 marks)\nb. Construct a line from point B to intersect arc M at C such that ABC = 150° \n( 2 marks)\nc. Join points C and D with a straight line and measure the perimeter of the quadrilateral \nABCD. ( 1 mark)\nFigure 12.17: Line AB and arcs L and M\n5. \na. Construct a line from point O to intersect line L at B such that AOB = 75° (2 marks)\nb. Measure ABO ( 1 mark)\nFigure 12.18: lines m and L intersecting at point A\nHint\nConduct Practice Paper 1. It should cover contents taught from Year 1 to 3. Refer to the \nTable of Specification and Structure of the Paper at the end of this section. Learners’ \npapers should be marked immediately and scores entered into the SAP.','a6ca5c1826d7cd875b11d86c9913911f4169500d61a616b51eb20710c94325d5','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(186,1,1,185,191,191,'167\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction Section 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nPRACTICE MOCK 1\nTables of Specification\nTest Specification (MCQ)\nSTRAND SUB-STRAND\nDOK LEVELS\nTOTAL\n1 2 3\nNumbers for everyday \nlife\nReal Number and Numeration System 1 2 3 5\nProportional Reasoning 2 0 2\nAlgebraic Thinking Applications of expressions, \nequations, and inequalities\n5 2 7 13\nPatterns and relationships 1 3 2 6\nGeometry Around Us Spatial sense 2 5 2 9 14\nMeasurement 2 3 5\nMaking sense of and \nusing data.\nStatistical reasoning and its \napplication in real life\n3 3 6 8\nChance 2 2\nTOTAL 16 \n(40%)\n20 \n(50%)\n4 \n(10%)\n40 \n(100%)\n40 \n(100%)\nPAPER II\nSection A\nSection A Test Specification\nSTRAND SUB-STRAND DOK LEVELS TOTAL\n1 2 3\nNumbers for everyday lifeReal Number and Numeration \nSystem\n1 1 2 3\nProportional Reasoning 1 1\nAlgebraic Thinking Applications of expressions, \nequations and inequalities\n1 2 3 3\nPatterns and relationships 0\nGeometry Around Us Spatial sense 1 1 2\nMeasurement 1 1\nMaking sense of and using \ndata.\nStatistical reasoning and its \napplication in real life\n2 2 2\nChance 0\nTOTAL 1 \n(10%)\n7 \n(70%)\n2 \n(20%)\n10 \n(100%)\n10 \n(100%)','da79ab153692a3fcf2bcda044149b8af9b526c32a50b76c7dce248c1b16a2923','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(187,1,1,186,192,192,'168\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nPart II-Section B\nTest Specification\nSTRAND SUB-STRAND DOK LEVELS TOTAL\n1 2 3\nNumbers for everyday \nlife\nReal Number and Numeration \nSystem\n1 1 3\nProportional Reasoning 1 1 2\nAlgebraic Thinking Applications of expressions, \nequations, and inequalities\n1 1 2\nPatterns and relationships 1 1\nGeometry Around Us Spatial sense 1 1 2\nMeasurement 1 1\nMaking sense of and \nusing data.\nStatistical reasoning and its \napplication in real life\n1 1 2 3\nChance 1 1\nTOTAL 2040 \n(80%)\n20 \n(20%)\n10 \n(100%)\n10 \n(100%)\nTest Specification (with Indicators)\nMultiple Choice Questions\nSTRAND SUB-STRAND Learning Indicators DOK LEVELS TOTAL\n1 2 3\nNumbers for \neveryday life\nReal \nNumber and \nNumeration \nsystem\n1.1.1.LI.1 Determine the \nproperties of subsets (for \ntwo-set problems), their \nvocabulary and operations, \nand use them to solve real-\nlife problems. \n1 1 \n1.1.2.LI.1 Establish the \nconcept of fractions and \ninvestigate the connections \nbetween fractions and \ndecimal numbers. \n1 1 5\n2.1.1.LI.2 Explain the \nconcepts of indices and \nlogarithms with examples. \n1 1','fce76f331560cb6f2add59894b2410a7a910ce130dad5e0dcc0655edc33e528f','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(188,1,1,187,193,193,'169\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nProportional \nReasoning\n3.1.2.LI.1 Use proportional \nreasoning to investigate the \nvarious types of variation \n(direct and inverse) and \nextend this to make \ngeneralisations. \n1 1 2 \nAlgebraic \nThinking\nApplications \nof expressions, \nequations and \ninequalities\n1.2.1.LI.1 Use numbers, \npatterns, and variables to \nformulate mathematical \nexpressions and apply \nthe algebraic order of the \nfour operations to solve \nproblems. \n13\n1.2.1.LI.2 Factorise algebraic \nexpressions involving \nquadratic trinomials. \n1 1\n1.2.1.LI.4 Analyse and \napply operations on \nsimple algebraic fractions \ninvolving monomial and \nbinomial denominators \nand determine the \nconditions under which an \nalgebraic fraction is zero or \nundefined. \n1 1 2\n1.2.1.LI.1 Construct and \ninterpret formulae for a \ngiven task and apply them \nto problems involving a \nchange of subjects. \n1 1\n1.2.1.LI.2 Solve linear \nequations and inequalities \nin one variable for a given \nproblem and relate it to \nreal-life situations. \n2 1 3\n2.2.1.LI.1 Solve \nsimultaneous linear \nequations involving \ntwo variables using the \ngraphical method and \ninterpret them\n\n2.2.1.LI.2 Analyse two \nlinear equations in two \nvariables and solve them \nusing the elimination and \nsubstitution methods.','b84b0cae263db4cea1a763e7403b1baf3bbdbba94bf0b777221d7b51e318333d','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(189,1,1,188,194,194,'170\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\n2.2.1.LI.3 Analyse, model, \nand solve word problems \nof simultaneous linear \nequations involving \nnumbers, age, etc.\n\nPatterns and \nrelationships \n1.2.2.AS.1 Extend \nthe knowledge of the \ncoordinates of two points \nto find the gradient and \nequation of a straight line. \n1 1 2\n2.2.2.LI.2 Recognise and \nfind the nth term and the \nsum of the nth term of an \narithmetic progression (AP) \nor linear sequence. \n1 1\n3.2.2.L1.1 Identify and solve \nquadratic equations. \n1 1\n3.2.2.L1.2 Solve quadratic \nequations graphically \nand find the maximum \nand minimum points of \nquadratic graphs. \n1 1\n3.2.2.L1.3 Identify \nand explain the axis \nof symmetry, write its \nequation and solve linear \nand quadratic equations \nsimultaneously using \ngraphs. \n1 1\nGeometry \nAround Us\nSpatial sense 1.3.1.LI.1 Draw and describe \nangles with various \nmeasures, including acute, \nright, straight, obtuse, and \nreflex angles, and solve \nproblems on them. \n1 1 14\n1.3.1.LI.2 Solve problems \nthat involve parallel \nlines, perpendicular lines \ntransversal, and pairs of \nangles formed between \nthem. \n1 1\n1.3.1.LI.3 State and apply \nthe exterior angle theorem \nof a triangle to solve \nproblems and identify \nvarious properties of special \ntriangles. \n1 1','3b187afb7d106faa434aaba227e0cf0be1a44185102bd826218b4f574f1f4b9e','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(190,1,1,189,195,195,'171\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\n1.3.1.LI.5 State and use the \nproperties of quadrilaterals \nand calculate the sums of \ninterior angles and exterior \nangles of a polygon. \n1 1 2\n3.3.1.LI.1 Identify parts of \na circle and draw circles for \ngiven radii and through \npoints. \n1 1\n3.3.1.LI.3 Identify the \ntangent as perpendicular \nto the radius at the point \nof contact and verify \nthe Alternate Segment \nTheorem. \n1 1 1 3\n3.3.1.LI.2 Discuss the circle \ntheorems by identifying \nthe statements, proofs, \nexamples and applications.\n\nMeasurement 1.3.2.LI.3 Solve problems \nusing the three primary \ntrigonometric ratios for \nangles from 0° to 360° in \nstandard position. \n1 1\n1.3.2.LI.3 Solve a contextual \nproblem that involves the \nperimeter and area of a \nregular, a composite or an \nirregular 2-D shape [kites, \nparallelogram, rhombus and \ntrapezoids].\n\n1.3.2.LI.1 Investigate the \nthree basic trigonometric \nratios (tangent, sine and \ncosine) of an acute angle in \ndegrees.\n\n1.3.2.LI.3 Solve problems \nusing the three primary \ntrigonometric ratios for \nangles from 0° to 360° in \nstandard position. \n1 1\n1.3.2.LI.4 Determine the \nvolume of prisms and solve \neveryday life problems on \nthem. \n1 1','a50274842efab78043173d6b24fde17302289498668ae6f7fe4a4b841186f6e2','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(191,1,1,190,196,196,'172\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\n1.3.2.LI.2 Represent a vector \nin two-space geometrically \nas a directed line segment, \nwith directions expressed in \ndifferent ways (e.g., 320°, \nN40°W) and algebraically; \nthen recognise vectors \nwith the same magnitude \nand direction but different \npositions as equal vectors. \n1 1\n1.3.2.LI.2 Estimate the \nperimeter and area of a \ngiven regular, composite or \nirregular 2D shape [kites, \nparallelogram, rhombus and \ntrapezoids]. \n1 1\nMaking \nsense of and \nusing data.\nStatistical \nreasoning and \nits application \nin real life\n1.4.3.LI.2 Analyse (including \nusing appropriate computer \napplications) and interpret \ndata using descriptive \nstatistics (i.e., measures \nof central tendency/\nlocation and minimum \nand maximum values) and \njustify which of the averages \nbest represents the data. \n1 1 \n1.4.1.LI.2 Organise and \npresent data (grouped/\nungrouped) using frequency \ntables, line graphs, pie \ncharts, multiple bar graphs, \ninfographics, etc., including \ngenerating 3D graphs/\ncharts with appropriate \ndigital technology (where \navailable) and solving \nproblems on them. \n1 1 \n2.4.1.LI.2 Analyse and \ninterpret data using \nmeasures of dispersion \nand justify which of these \nmeasures best suits the \ndata. \n1 1 2','5164c4dcce7f85c1c2bacf84afd314571fcad79ab928b7ea852ec2db7461fa71','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(192,1,1,191,197,197,'173\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\n2.4.1.LI.2 Evaluate a given \nset of data and/or its \ninstrument by identifying \npotential problems related \nto bias, use of language, \ngender, ethics, cost, time, \nprivacy, cultural sensitivity, \netc. \n1 1 \n1.4.1.LI.3 Identify and \nvalidate qualitative \ndata collection methods \n(interviews, observations, \nfocus groups, oral histories, \nonline tracking, social \nmedia monitoring, etc.) \nand use them to collect \neveryday-life data. \n1 1 \nChance 1.4.2.LI.1 List the elements \nof the sample space from \na simple or compound \nexperiment involving two \nindependent events. \n1 1 \n1.4.2.LI.2 Determine \nthe probabilities of \nindependent events \nand express the results \nas fractions, decimals, \npercentages, and/or ratios. \n1 1 8\nTOTAL 16 20 4 40 40\n-40%-50%-10%-100% -100%\nPAPER II\nSection A (with indicators)\nStrand Sub-Strand Learning Indicators DOK LEVELS TOTAL\n1 2 3\nNumbers \nfor \neveryday \nlife\nReal \nNumber and \nNumeration \nsystem\n1.1.1.LI.3 Establish the \nrelationship between \nand among three sets, \nincluding set equations \nand De Morgan’s law\n\n2.1.1.LI.2 Explain the \nconcepts of indices \nand logarithms with \nexamples. \n3','b37fe3de248090e8de8b39bbdaebb92dbc2af3fdeba227d56bf20065eebbc8ce','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(193,1,1,192,198,198,'174\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\n2.1.1.LI.3 Compose and \ndecompose logarithm \nlaws and properties with \nexponents and apply the \nconcepts to solve real-\nlife problems. \n1 1 \n2.2.1.LI.2 Analyse two \nlinear equations in two \nvariables and solve them \nusing the elimination \nand substitution \nmethods.\n\n2.2.1.LI.3 Analyse, \nmodel, and solve \nword problems of \nsimultaneous linear \nequations involving \nnumbers, age, etc.\n\n3.1.2.LI.1 Investigate to \nestablish differences \nbetween variables in \nsituational growth \n(symbolic logic) and \napply it to real world \nsituations.\n\n3.1.2.LI.2 Make \nintelligent guessing \nto establish valid \narguments and draw \nlogical conclusions. \n1 1 \nProportional \nReasoning\n1.1.2.LI.2 Analyse \ndaily activities/issues/\nbusinesses involving \npercentage change, \nincluding simple \nproblems involving \npersonal or household \nfinance (such as utility \nbills, exchange rates, \nproject budgeting, \nschool fees, shopping, \netc.)\n\n2.1.2.LI.1 Explain the \nconcepts ratios and \nrate as a comparison of \nquantities. \n1 1','1342b59e36455106e60db3a623f6a6c6affa6e86268d1157e3a25477e782c96a','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(194,1,1,193,199,199,'175\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\n2.2.2.LI.5 Analyse, \nmodel, and solve real-\nlife problems involving \nexponential growth.\n\nAlgebraic \nThinking\nApplications \nof \nexpressions, \nequations and \ninequalities\n1.2.2.LI.2 Draw graphs \nof linear functions and \ninterpret them. \n1 1 3\n2.2.1.LI.1 Solve \nsimultaneous linear \nequations involving \ntwo variables using the \ngraphical method and \ninterpret them. \n1 1 \n3.2.2.LI.1 Identify \nand solve quadratic \nequations.\n\n3.2.2.LI.2 Solve \nquadratic equations \ngraphically and find the \nmaximum and minimum \npoints of quadratic \ngraphs.\n\n3.2.2.LI.3 Identify \nand explain the axis \nof symmetry, write its \nequation and solve linear \nand quadratic equations \nsimultaneously using \ngraphs.\n\n3.2.2.LI.4 Use quadratic \ngraphs to solve related \nequations and solve \nreal-life problems to \nfind the range of values \nof (x) for which the other \nvalue (y) is increasing \nor decreasing and the \nrange of values (x) for \nwhich the other value (y) \nis positive or negative.','a7b016260534cb5e9b90e4cc5f92ff31499de86aa8adc180868369ef23ec6f83','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(195,1,1,194,200,200,'176\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\n1.2.1.LI.2 Solve \nlinear equations and \ninequalities in one \nvariable for a given \nproblem and relate it to \nreal-life situations. \n1 1 \nPatterns and \nrelationships \n0\nGeometry \nAround Us\nSpatial sense 1.3.1.LI.2 Solve problems \nthat involve parallel \nlines, perpendicular \nlines, transversal, and \npairs of angles formed \nbetween them. \n1 1 2\n1.3.1.LI.4 Solve problems \non the Pythagorean \ntheorem by identifying \nsituations that involve \nright triangles, verify the \nformula and apply it.\n\n3.3.1.LI.1 Identify parts \nof a circle and draw \ncircles for given radii and \nthrough points.\n\nMeasurement 1.3.2.LI.2 Estimate the \nperimeter and area of a \ngiven regular, composite \nor irregular 2D shape \n[kites, parallelogram, \nrhombus, and \ntrapezoids]. \n1 1\n1.3.2.LI.3 Solve a \ncontextual problem that \ninvolves the perimeter \nand area of a regular, a \ncomposite or an irregular \n2-D shape [kites, \nparallelogram, rhombus \nand trapezoids].\n\n2.3.2.LI.1 Determine the \ninverse of trigonometric \nratios (graphs excluded) \nand talk about their \napplications in the \nfields of astronomy, \nengineering, physics, \ngeometry and \nnavigation.','e56f761ed50ef49c3c516a7d09ef1902cfe121e3803c5c64182a759b5789d0ac','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(196,1,1,195,201,201,'177\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nMaking \nsense of \nand using \ndata.\nStatistical \nreasoning and \nits application \nin real life\n1.4.1.LI.2 Organise and \npresent data (grouped/\nungrouped) using \nfrequency tables, line \ngraphs, pie charts, \nmultiple bar graphs, \ninfographics, etc., \nincluding generating \n3D graphs/charts with \nappropriate digital \ntechnology (where \navailable) and solving \nproblems on them. \n1 1 2\n1.4.3.LI.2 Analyse \n(including using \nappropriate computer \napplications) and \ninterpret data using \ndescriptive statistics \n(i.e., measures of central \ntendency/location and \nminimum and maximum \nvalues) and justify which \nof the averages best \nrepresents the data.\n\n2.1.2.LI.1 Apply the \nconcept of ratios, rates, \nand proportions to solve \nproblems in financial \nmathematics, health, \nsports, etc.\n\n3.4.1.LI.1 Establish \nsimple mathematical \nrelationships between \ntwo variables in a \ngiven observational or \nexperimental context; \nillustrate using scatter \ngraphs and use them \nto solve and/or pose \nproblems.','da95e7224fddef020301a162a0cc17884b479f6ce250cb150f4f5409769686cb','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(197,1,1,196,202,202,'178\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\n3.4.1.LI.2 Collect data \nfrom an experimental \nstudy in which the \ninterest is based on \ntreatment and non-\ntreatment (control) \ngroups. Illustrate the \ndata using scatter \ngraphs and find the \nrelationship between \nthe variables, if any. \n1 1\nChance 3.4.2.LI.2 Solve \neveryday life problems \ninvolving the probability \nof two dependent and \nindependent events, \nincluding addition and \nmultiplication laws.\n\nTOTAL\n\n1 7 2 10\n10\n(10%)-70%-20%-100%\n-100%\nPAPER II\nSection B (with indicators)\nSTRAND SUB-STRAND LEARNING \nINDICATORS\nDOK LEVELS TOTAL\n1 2 3\nNumbers for \neveryday life\nReal Number \nand Numeration \nSystem\n2.1.1.LI.1 Carry \nout operations \non surds and \nrationalize monomial \ndenominators.\n\n2.1.1.LI.3 Compose and \ndecompose logarithm \nlaws and properties \nwith exponents and \napply the concepts \nto solve real-life \nproblems.\n\n1.1.1.LI.2 Organise \ninformation visually \nto establish the \nrelationship between \nand among sets of \nitems (three sets) and \napply these to conduct \nmini-surveys in the \nschool community and \nbeyond. \n1 1 3','f6a5d6efaa938cc765292d730530482c1bc9ee80d4f83d2199a716525d6ec4fd','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(198,1,1,197,203,203,'179\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\n2.1.1.LI.1 Investigate \nreal-life problems \nusing laws and \nproperties of indices \nand logarithms.\n\n2.1.2.LI.2 Establish \nthe relationships \namong ratio, rates and \nproportions.\n\nProportional \nReasoning\n1.1.2.LI.1 Establish the \nconcept of fractions \nand investigate the \nconnections between \nfractions and decimal \nnumbers.\n\n1.1.2.LI.2 Analyse \ndaily activities/issues/\nbusinesses involving \npercentage change, \nincluding simple \nproblems involving \npersonal or household \nfinance (such as utility \nbills, exchange rates, \nproject budgeting, \nschool fees, shopping, \netc.)\n\n2.1.2.LI.1 Explain the \nconcepts of ratios and \nrates as a comparison \nof quantities.\n\n2.1.2.LI.2 Establish \nthe relationships \namong ratio, rates and \nproportions. \n1 1 \n2.1.2.LI.1 Apply the \nconcept of ratios, \nrates, and proportions \nto solve problems in \nfinancial mathematics, \nhealth, sports, etc. \n1 1','d97b8092d4dd51f286de84a32c08d5a2b7ddf7ed19286a5b61d4c9834e1f96d3','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(199,1,1,198,204,204,'180\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\n3.1.2.LI.1 Use \nproportional reasoning \nto investigate the \nvarious types of \nvariation (direct \nand inverse) and \nextend this to make \ngeneralisations.\n\nAlgebraic \nThinking\nApplications \nof expressions, \nequations, and \ninequalities\n1 1 2\n2.2.2.LI.5 Analyse, \nmodel, and solve real-\nlife problems involving \nexponential growth. \nPatterns and \nrelationships \n3.2.2.LI.2 Solve \nquadratic equations \ngraphically and find \nthe maximum and \nminimum points of \nquadratic graphs. \n1 1\nGeometry \nAround Us\nSpatial sense \nMeasurement 1.3.2.LI.1 Recognise \na vector as a quantity \nwith both magnitude \nand direction, and \nidentify, gather, and \ninterpret information \nabout real-world \napplications of vectors.\n\n1.3.2.LI.2 Represent \na vector in two-\nspace geometrically \nas a directed line \nsegment, with \ndirections expressed \nin different ways (e.g., \n320°, N40°W) and \nalgebraically; then \nrecognise vectors with \nthe same magnitude \nand direction but \ndifferent positions as \nequal vectors.','82884216725406253d2dba6361c89a311a2a6484fe18621353d64174fd5be423','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(200,1,1,199,205,205,'181\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\n1.3.2.LI.2 Estimate \nthe perimeter and \narea of a given \nregular, composite \nor irregular 2D shape \n[kites, parallelogram, \nrhombus and \ntrapezoids]. \n1 1 \n1.3.2.LI.3 Solve a \ncontextual problem \nthat involves the \nperimeter and area of a \nregular, a composite or \nan irregular 2-D shape \n[kites, parallelogram, \nrhombus and \ntrapezoids].\n1 1 \n2.3.2.LI.1 Perform \naddition, subtraction, \nand scalar \nmultiplication on \nvectors represented as \ndirected line segments \nin two-space and in \nCartesian form in two \nand three-space. \n2\n2.3.2.LI.3 Solve \nproblems involving \nthe addition, \nsubtraction, and scalar \nmultiplication of \nvectors,\n\nincluding problems \narising from real-\nworld applications. \n2.3.2.LI.1 Determine \nthe inverse of \ntrigonometric ratios \n(graphs excluded) \nand talk about their \napplications in the \nfields of astronomy, \nengineering, physics, \ngeometry and \nnavigation.','84c1c0b7b6e6cec0e795805fa9251cd55abbeb914b975177a3d6182e68fa3f05','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(201,1,1,200,206,206,'182\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nMaking \nsense of and \nusing data.\nStatistical \nreasoning and \nits application \nin real life\n1.4.1.LI.2 Organise and \npresent data (grouped/\nungrouped) using \nfrequency tables, line \ngraphs, pie charts, \nmultiple bar graphs, \ninfographics, etc., \nincluding generating \n3D graphs/charts with \nappropriate digital \ntechnology (where \navailable) and solving \nproblems on them. \n3\n1.4.3.LI.2 Analyse \n(including using \nappropriate computer \napplications) and \ninterpret data using \ndescriptive statistics \n(i.e., measures of \ncentral tendency/\nlocation and minimum \nand maximum values) \nand justify which of \nthe averages best \nrepresents the data.\n\n2.4.1.LI.1 Organise \nand present data \n(grouped/ungrouped) \nby means of the ogive, \nwaffle diagrams, box \nand whisker plots, etc., \nincluding generating \n3D graphs and \nsolving and/or posing \nproblems. \n1 1\n2.4.1.LI.2 Analyse and \ninterpret data using \nmeasures of dispersion \nand justify which of \nthese measures best \nsuits the data. \n1 1\nChance 2.4.2.LI.1 List the \nelements of the \nsample space from a \nsimple or compound \nexperiment involving \ntwo dependent events.','6396d626fba696e510b3986a5554b67326057209bdfd5bdfcbf51bb39badbf15','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(202,1,1,201,207,207,'183\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\n2.4.2.LI.2 Solve \neveryday life \nproblems involving \nthe probability of two \nindependent events.\n\n2.4.2.LI.2 Solve \neveryday life \nproblems involving \nthe probability of two \nindependent events. \n1 1\nTOTAL 2 6 2 10 10','5e20f812ddd4854dd528827fc5dd47fcd8504eba21f55eb091f1c0117ce20052','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(203,1,1,202,208,208,'184\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nPLC Session 12\n45 minutes Planning & Reviewing the Learning Plan\n1. Begin the session by recapping lessons from the previous session, focusing on how learning \nwas applied.\n2. Use your subject specific app to explore the content before planning. Ask the app to\na. explain the week’s concepts in a practical way that will make it easier for learners to \nunderstand.\nb. identify three common misconceptions learners may have on the week’s content and \nhow to address them using contexts familiar to Ghanaian learners (NTS 3a, 3g and \n3m).\n3. Use your subject specific app to plan your lesson. Ask the app to\na. suggest the most effective method for teaching the week’s concepts to learners with \ndiverse backgrounds.\nb. suggest learning activities, differentiation and essential questions based on the method \nof teaching (NTS 3a-3q).\nc. review your prepared learning plan and address any challenging issues you may have \nidentified in your subject area (NTS 3a-3c).\nNote\n• Take note of the recap topics linked to Years 1 and 2 while preparing for your \nlesson.\n• Refer to the Values Learning Community Handbook and link the values for the \nweek in your lesson to ensure that learners are applying what they learn.\n4. Use your app to plan and administer the mode of assessment for the Student Assessment \nPortal (SAP).\na. The key assessment for the week is practice paper 2 . Chat with your app to plan this \nassessment by reviewing or developing the assessment items/tasks and marking \nschemes/rubrics for the practice paper (NTS 3k-3q).\n10 minutes Content Exploration\n5. As a subject group, select one problem or topic/idea/term in the week’s content that is \nchallenging (not a routine procedural question, but one that requires conceptual \nunderstanding) and then\na. use the app, ask it to solve the problem step by step and explain the reasoning behind \neach step (you may be assisted by a teacher who is strong in the content area)\nb. all teachers in the group work through the problem individually for 3–4 minutes','163a9cb0db2b2e6891bdb3f225f34bdeaffa2b7117dc699abb48669e995dd44b','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(204,1,1,203,209,209,'185\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nc. the group then works through it together the problem, step by step\nd. highlight the most common errors made at each step (NTS 1c, 1e, 2c, 2e, 3e-3j).\nNote\n• This part is not ‘How do I teach this?’ but how do I teach it for learners to understand?\n• If the group still shares the same content gap, use the app: ask it to solve the \nproblem step by step and explain the reasoning behind each step.\n• Problems/ideas discussed should be sourced from the Teacher Manual content, not \ninvented separately. This keeps the content exploration directly connected to what \nteachers will deliver.\n30 minutes Enactment\n6. Enact a teaching activity based on your learning plan (NTS la-1b, 1e-lg, 2c, 2e, 2f, 3c-3j \nand 3o).\na. Colleagues observe with specific attention to clarity of concept explanation, use of \nfamiliar Ghanaian examples and how misconceptions are handled if they arise.\nb. After the enactment, ask for structured feedback from the group\ni. was the concept explained in a way that a learner with no prior knowledge could \nfollow?\nii. were the examples grounded in contexts familiar to Ghanaian learners?\niii. did any misconceptions surface and if so, how were they addressed?\n5 minutes Reflection\n7. Reflect and share your views on the session. One thing that worked well, what did not work \nwell and what you will do differently. (NTS 1a, 1b).\n8. Identify a critical friend to observe and provide feedback on your lesson in relation to the \nPLC Session conducted (NTS 1f, 1g).\n9. Remember to\na. transfer your chats into your learning planner template.\nb. read your Teacher Manual and related Learner Material in preparation for the next \nsession. Take note of the recap weeks, which are linked to Years 1 and 2 (NTS 3a).\nc. integrate National Values, GESI, SEL, and 21st century skills into your learning \nactivities, assessment tasks, and rubrics/mark schemes.','76a2c706b807ccf8f83a83e5f371e4bbaed28e20eb84ef65b66499575d71dcea','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(205,1,1,204,210,210,'186\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nWEEK 13\nLearning Indicators\n1. Construct a triangle or a quadrilateral under given conditions\n2. Construct a particular locus for a given condition\nFOCAL AREA 1: CONSTRUCTION OF QUADRILATERALS \nCONCEPT, EXAMPLES, AND VISUALISATION OF SURDS\nConstructing Triangles\nA triangle can be constructed using a ruler, pencil, and compass only if the following information \nis provided\n1. All three sides of the triangle are provided\n2. Two sides and the included angle are given\n3. One side and two angles are given.\nExample 1\nUsing a ruler and a pair of compasses only, construct triangle ABC such that |AB| = 8cm, |AC| \n= 10cm, and |BC| = 9cm\nSolution\nWe can choose to construct any of the lines first.\nLet us construct line |AC| first.\nFigure 13.1: Line AC = 10 cm\nTo construct |AB| open your compass to a radius of 8cm.\nWith the pin of the compass at point A , draw an arc\nFigure 13.2: Line AC and an 8cm arc','00d4be04d6f877d623efd6f354fd6f4c49f546ad433bdd36cea08d97f675f3a0','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(206,1,1,205,211,211,'187\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nNext, open the compass to a length of 9cm. With the pin of the compass at C , draw an arc to \nintersect the arc in the previous step. Indicate the point of intersection of the two arcs as B .\nFigure 13.3: Construction of triangle ABC\nFinally, join the points AC and BC with straight lines\nFigure 13.4: Triangle ABC\nExample 2\nUsing a ruler and a pair of compasses only, construct triangle XYZ such that |XY| = 12cm, \n|XZ| = 10cm, and < YXZ = 75°. Measure |YZ|\nSolution\nConstruct line |XY|, indicating the points X and Y , which are 12cm apart.\nFigure 13.5: Line XY=12cm\nUsing point X as the vertex and |XY| as the baseline, construct angle 75° (as taught in Week \n12) at X .\nFigure 13.6: Construction of 75°','6afc962d9da210f0efe4b545559009b9a7a796985d215769328e183df9987ab1','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(207,1,1,206,212,212,'188\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nLocate the position of point Z on the arm of the angle such that it is 10cm from X .\nComplete the triangle by joining the points Z and Y with a straight line.\nFigure 13.7: Triangle XYZ\nWith a ruler, measure line | YZ | = 13.5 cm\nExample 3\nUsing a ruler and a pair of compasses only, construct triangle PQR such that |QR | 8cm,\n< PQR105, < PRQ 45°. Measure:\na. |PQ| \nb. |PR|\nSolution\ni. First construct line QR indicating clearly the points Q and R which are 8cm apart.\nFigure 13.8: Line QR =8cm\nii. Construct angle 105° at Q\nFigure 13.9: Construction of 105°','a281b694614e108a860afa6ec4e70462fb9f4bf6ba7dc584eeb8072d21096c82','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(208,1,1,207,213,213,'189\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\niii. Construct angle 45° at R.\niv. Name the point of intersection as P to complete the construction.\nFigure 13.10: Triangle PQR\nConstruction of Quadrilaterals\nExample 4\nUsing a ruler and a pair of compasses only construct a quadrilateral ABCD , such that\n|AB| = |BC| = 11cm, |AD| = 7.5cm, angle ABC = 105° and AD//BC.\nSolution\na. First construct line |AB|\nFigure 13.11: Line AB = 11 cm\nb. Next, construct angle 105° at B,\nFigure 13.12: Construction of 105° at point B','cb1432c8acf130e58bf00a9e65ad73fdae2146d35afa420f455c490eb287cc1e','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(209,1,1,208,214,214,'190\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nc. Now, since BC//AD (line BC is parallel to line AD ), we construct angle 75° at A (from \nproperties of parallel lines, co-interior angles add up to 180°)\nFigure 13.13: Construction of 75° at point A and 105° at point B on line AB = 11 cm\nd. Locate D , which is 7.5cm from A .\nFigure 13.14: Construction of 75° at point A and 105° at point B on line AB = 11 cm\ne. Likewise, locate C , which is 11cm from B , and join the points D and C with a straight \nline.\nFigure 13.15: Quadrilateral ABCD','63ff55053d4c884b466dbf531520525ff22cea0c0ae19c321700c6e337e0ce72','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(210,1,1,209,215,215,'191\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nFOCAL AREA 2: CONSTRUCTION OF LOCUS\n• Theorems and conditions of locus\n• Construction of locus\nLocus\nA locus is a set of all points that satisfy a given condition or set of conditions. Think of it as a \npath traced by a point moving according to specific rules.\nLocus theorem 1\nLocus of points equidistant from a single point\nMethod: Using the point as the centre and the given distance as the radius, construct a circle. \nThe locus is the circle.\nExample 5\nFigure 13.16 shows triangle ABC .\nFigure 13.16: Triangle ABC\na. With the aid of a ruler and a pair of compasses only, make a copy of triangle ABC .\nb. Construct the locus p, of points 5cm from A .\nSolution\nOpen your compass to a radius of 5cm. With the pin of the compass at A , construct a complete \ncircle.\nFigure 13.17: Triangle ABC and circle P with centre A','af41010559cc46b3d55d6a17aa0a99d44afced65d48c86b252185419a6b44b90','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(211,1,1,210,216,216,'192\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nLocus theorem 2\nLocus of points equidistant from two points\nMethod: Construct a perpendicular bisector of the line segment determined by the two points.\nThe locus is the perpendicular bisector.\nExample 6\nFig 13.18 shows triangle ABC .\nFigure 13.18: Triangle ABC\na. Using a ruler and a pair of compasses only, make a copy of triangle ABC.\nb. Construct the locus, m, of points equidistant from A and B .\nSolution\nFigure 13.19: Triangle ABC and line m bisecting line AB\nLine m is the locus','57c5eb25c2a760998bdf0c58d58ae07c5082901e900160f48a4f59e12ea5fab7','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(212,1,1,211,217,217,'193\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nLocus theorem 3\nLocus of points equidistant from two intersecting lines.\nMethod: Bisect the angle of intersection of the two lines. The locus is the line bisecting the \nangle.\nExample 7\nFigure 13.20a shows the intersection of line AB and CD .\nFigure 13.20a: lines AB and CD\na. Using a ruler and a pair of compasses only, make a copy of the figure\nb. Construct the locus P, of points equidistant from AB and CD.\nSolution\nFigure 13.20b: Construction of loci equidistant from AB and CD\nLocus theorem 4\nLocus of points equidistant from two parallel lines\nThe locus equidistant from two parallel lines, |AB| and |CD| is a line parallel to both AB and \nCD and halfway between them.\nFigure 13.21: line m equidistant from AB and CD\nIn the diagram above, the locus is line m. Line m is parallel to and equidistant from lines AB \nand CD . Line m is also parallel to AB and CD .','edbd5f02489569eacd1248ccaf69afca920d566bd8051877b6191c81ed733abd','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(213,1,1,212,218,218,'194\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nExample 8\nThe diagram below represents a construction of two parallel lines m\n1\nand m\n2\n.\nFigure 13.22: Parallel lines m\n1\nand m\n2\nConstruct the locus d , equidistant m\n1 \nfrom and m\n2\n.\nSolution\n1. Step 1: Construct a perpendicular at any point O on line m\n1\nto intersect line m\n2\nat point A .\nFigure 13.23: A perpendicular transversal intersecting parallel lines m\n1\nand m\n2\nat point O and A\n2. Step 2: Construct a perpendicular bisector of line |OA|\nFigure 13.24: Line d equidistant from parallel lines m\n1\nand m\n2\nLine d is the locus of points parallel to lines m\n1\nand m\n2\n.\nLocus theorem 5\n1. Locus of points equidistant from a line.\n2. This is a pair of parallel lines a distance away from the given line.\n3. This theorem is illustrated in Figure 13.25\n4. The figure illustrates the locus of points, p , of points m units from line AB .\n5. The locus, p , represents the dotted lines on opposite sides of line AB .','55e6c48fd62d62cbfdc5f6e6eca00eb051554b6adfe1cc2d32b06752e54c26e8','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(214,1,1,213,219,219,'195\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nFigure 13.25: Loci p equidistant from line AB\nLocus theorem 6\nConstructing a perpendicular from a point to a line.\nMethod: Open the compass to a distance more than the perpendicular distance from the point \nO, to the line.\n1. Step 1: Draw an arc to intersect the line at the two points X and Y.\nFigure 13.26: Construction of an arc from point O to line XY\n2. Step 2: With the compass point at X and using the radius |OX|, draw an arc on the other \nside of the line.\nFigure 13.27: Construction of a perpendicular from O to line XY','64554f9d2a1c04510511b8611d65c4f2dfff5a4b93d3bdbb403f52b5abe1daab','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(215,1,1,214,220,220,'196\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\n3. Step 3: With the compass point at Y and using the same radius as used in 1, draw an arc \nto intersect the one drawn in 1 at Z.\nFigure 13.28: Construction of a perpendicular from point O to line XY\n1. Finally, join the points O and Z with a straight line.\nFigure 13.29: Construction of a perpendicular from point O to line XY\nLocus theorem 7\nLocus of points equidistant from three fixed points.\nMethod\n1. First, join the three points to make a triangle.','7fdda63d76feeae1a4b1100ec65348c4a56548ea709855ee62fde548ad7ad40a','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(216,1,1,215,221,221,'197\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\n2. Construct the perpendicular bisectors of any two sides of the triangle.\n3. The intersection of the two perpendicular bisectors is the locus. This locus is a single \npoint.\nNOTE\nThis locus is a single point called the circumcentre. This point can be used to construct \na circumscribed circle.\nExample 9\n1. Consider the triangle below.\nFigure 13.30: Triangle ABC\n2. Find the point, O , equidistant from A , B, and C .\nSolution\nWe first construct the perpendicular bisector of any of the two sides. Let’s choose sides AB and \nAC.\nFigure 13.31: Perpendicular bisectors of sides AB and AC of triangle ABC\nThe intersection of the two perpendicular bisectors is the locus.\nThe locus is just a single point which is equidistant from all three points, A , B and C.','933491ebacaaef39459825c3976262b296096463677cf4eee53b989beef33948','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(217,1,1,216,222,222,'198\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nExample 10\na. Using a ruler and a pair of compasses only, construct a parallelogram PQRS such that \n|PQ| = 8cm, |QR| = 6cm and = 11.5cm.\nb. Construct the locus u, 5cm from Q\nc. Construct the locus of points t , equidistant from |PS| and |SR|\nd. Indicate the point of intersection of u and t as A and B\nSolution\nFirst construct triangle PQR. This is a triangle with all sides given.\nFigure 13.32: Triangle PQR\nTo locate the last vertex of the parallelogram, open your compass to a distance of 8cm. with \nthe pin of the compass at R , scribe an arc [arc should be in line with PQ ]\nFigure 13.33: Triangle PQR\nLikewise, open your compass to a distance of 6cm. with the pin of your compass at P scribe an \narc to intersect the previous one. Indicate the point of intersection of the two arcs as S.','9c37e32836bb66744c61cb32f36b4643b1b99a3cac7b09595766da2b8d7b02c2','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(218,1,1,217,223,223,'199\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nFigure 13.34: Construction of parallelogram PQRS\nFinally, join P and S , R and S with straight lines to complete the (a) part of the question.\nFigure 13.35: Parallelogram PQRS\nWe then construct the locus, 5cm from Q . That means using Q as our centre, construct a circle \nwith a radius of 5cm.\nFigure 13.36: Parallelogram PQRS and circle u with centre Q\nConstruct the locus of points equidistant from |PS| and |SR|. This is a bisector of angle PSR.','72a0f4595c94af0f0ef12c72f8df0cfe40176a1390bd72cfc50642754ea67a89','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(219,1,1,218,224,224,'200\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nFigure 13.37: Parallelogram PQRS and circle u with centre Q\nWe then indicate the points of intersection of loci u and t as A and B to complete the construction.\nFigure 13.38: Parallelogram PQRS and circle u with centre Q\nLearning Tasks\nGuide learners to apply their knowledge of the construction of angles to construct \ntriangles and quadrilaterals using appropriate instruments. Provide support systems \nfor learners who may encounter difficulties.\nPEDAGOGICAL EXEMPLARS\nThe aim of the lessons for the week is for all learners to be able to use the appropriate instrument \nto construct triangles, quadrilaterals, and loci. The following pedagogical approach is suggested \nfor facilitators to take learners through.','14133cb3c2d4730f341c9c856c0815f582ce35bdf8dce3945ffc6d7a7d9c3b18','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(220,1,1,219,225,225,'201\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\n1. Group discussions\nIn small groups, learners draw various triangles and quadrilaterals using the appropriate \ntools. Learners discuss the concept of loci and draw various loci for triangles and \nquadrilaterals using the appropriate mathematical tools.\n2. Using experiential learning, engage learners to construct and verify a triangle or a \nquadrilateral under given conditions\n3. Using talk for learning, guide learners to discuss and work out examples for the various \nlocus theorems.\nKEY ASSESSMENT LEVEL 2: GROUP WORK & PRESENTATION\n1. The diagram shows the scale plan of a restaurant. |AB| = 6cm, |AD| = 5cm, DAB = 105 \nand\n2. ABC = 90°, with 1cm = 1m in real life\nFigure 13.39: Quadrilateral ABCD\nScale 1cm:1m\na. Using a ruler and a pair of compasses only, make a scaled construction of ABCD\n[ 5 marks]\nb. A washing basin in the restaurant is equidistant from BC and CD and exactly 5.5m \nfrom A. On the diagram, mark the position of the basin with a dot and label it as O\n[ 4 marks]\nc. Find the perimeter of the floor space of the restaurant. [2 marks]\n[Total 11 marks ]\n3. Town M is 20km from town N and 22km from P while N is 18km from P. A market is to \nbe built to serve the three towns. It is to be located such that traders from N and M will \nalways travel equal distance to access the market while traders from P will travel exactly \n10km to reach the market.','0b40c3450790b02b2b91c69f9e6ff8e621c37f7adc24b54061502ea5b0a1b0e2','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(221,1,1,220,226,226,'202\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\na. Using a ruler and a pair of compasses only, find by construction, the possible locations, \nA and B, for the market. Use a scale of 1cm to 2km. [9 marks]\nb. Find the distance between the two locations? [1 mark]\nc. Measure and record the distances of the locations from town N. [2 marks]\nd. Which of the all the locations will be convenient for the three towns. Justify your \nanswer. [ 1 mark]\n[Total 13 marks]\nPLC Session 13\n45 minutes Planning & Reviewing the Learning Plan\n1. Begin the session by recapping lessons from the previous year/session/week, focusing on \nhow learning was applied.\n2. Use your subject-specific app to explore the content before planning. Ask the app to\na. explain the week’s concepts in a practical way that will make it easier for learners to \nunderstand\nb. identify two or three common misconceptions learners may have on the week’s content \nand how to address them using contexts familiar to Ghanaian learners. (NTS 3a, 3g \nand 3m).\n3. Use your subject-specific app to plan your lesson. Ask the app to\na. suggest the most effective method for teaching the week’s concepts to learners with \ndiverse backgrounds.\nb. suggest learning activities, differentiation and essential questions based on the method \nof teaching (NTS 3a-3q)\nc. review your prepared learning plan and address any challenging issues you may have \nidentified in your subject area (NTS 3a-3c).\nNote\n• Take note of the recap topics linked to Years 1 and 2 while preparing for your \nlesson.\n• Refer to the Values Learning Community Handbook and link the values for the \nweek in your lesson to ensure that learners are applying what they learn.\n4. Plan the week’s key assessment with your app\na. The key assessment for the week is group work & presentation . Chat with your app \nto plan this assessment, indicating what you and your learners will do before, during \nand after the presentations (NTS 3k-3q).','bea421d5c74b7fee496b459b59acdef8fe63d660128bca723f053369b32680bd','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(222,1,1,221,227,227,'203\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\n10 minutes Content Exploration\n5. As a subject group, select one problem or idea/term in the week’s content that is challenging \n(not a routine procedural question, but one that requires conceptual understanding) and \nthen\na. use the app, ask it to solve the problem step by step and explain the reasoning behind \neach step (you may be assisted by a teacher who is strong in the content area)\nb. all teachers in the group work through a similar problem individually for 3–4 minutes\nc. the group then works through the problem together step by step\nd. highlight the most common errors made at each step and discuss (NTS 1c, 1e, 2c, 2e, \n3e-3j).\nNote\n• This part is not just about ‘How do I teach this?’ but how do I teach it for learners \nto understand?\n• If the group still shares the same content gap, use the app: ask it to solve the \nproblem step by step and explain the reasoning behind each step.\n• Problems/ideas discussed should be sourced from the Teacher Manual content, not \ninvented separately. This keeps the content exploration directly connected to what \nteachers will deliver.\n30 minutes Enactment\n6. Enact a teaching activity based on your learning plan (NTS la-1b, 1e-lg, 2c, 2e, 2f, 3c-3j \nand 3o).\na. Colleagues observe with specific attention to clarity of concept explanation, use of \nfamiliar Ghanaian examples and how misconceptions are handled if they arise.\nb. After the enactment, ask for structured feedback from the group\ni. was the concept explained in a way that a learner with no prior knowledge could \nfollow?\nii. were the examples grounded in contexts familiar to Ghanaian learners?\niii. did any misconceptions surface and if so, how were they addressed?\n5 minutes Reflection\n7. Reflect and share your views on the session. One thing that worked well, what did not work \nwell and what you will do differently. (NTS 1a, 1b).\n8. Identify a critical friend to observe and provide feedback on your lesson in relation to the \nPLC Session conducted (NTS 1f, 1g).','48ca7d2f734b0291c3a3c6c5011b5d8b1b0bfec6a6d2645cd7f82b21451ef651','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(223,1,1,222,228,228,'Section 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction \n204\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\n9. Remember to\na. transfer your chats into your learning planner template.\nb. read your Teacher Manual and related Learner Material in preparation for the next \nsession. Take note of the recap weeks, which are linked to Years 1 and 2 (NTS 3a).\nc. integrate National Values, GESI, SEL, and 21st century skills into your learning \nactivities, assessment tasks, and rubrics/mark schemes.\nSection 3 Review\nThis section provided a recap of the key concepts covered during weeks 10 to 13. It \nserved as an overview of what learners should have understood during this period. \nThese weeks laid a solid groundwork for further exploration into geometry. We \nreviewed the various parts of a circle and learned how to construct common acute \nand obtuse angles. Using our understanding of circle components, we delved into \ncircle theorems, including their statements and proofs.\nAdditionally, we explored the construction of triangles and quadrilaterals. To \nconclude, we examined the concept of loci and applied it to solve practical, real-\nworld problems.\nTo enhance teaching and learning, the following resources are recommended:\nA complete set of mathematical instruments, a whiteboard, educational videos, \nmini whiteboards, dry erase markers and erasers, calculators, graph/grid paper, and \ndigital tools such as computers, mobile phones, and YouTube videos.','5122a99e85de9d7a69d05023c650df968256811572046f52d38ba41385a8f70e','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(224,1,1,223,229,229,'205\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction Section 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nMARKING SCHEMES FOR THE KEY ASSESSMENTS\nWEEK 11\nSolution Marks\nQuestion One\nSince the angle at the centre of a circle is twice the angle at the \ncircumference, we have reflex angle XVZ=2 × 113 = 226°.\nSince the sum of angles at a point is 360, the obtuse angle XVZ \n= 360- 226 = 134\nSince the sum of angles in a quadrilateral (OXVZ) =360,\nWe have, XOZ + 90° + 134° + 90° = 360°,\nOr VOX = 180° – 90°- 67° = 23°, so XOZ = 2 x 23\nXOZ = 46°\nConnect points V and O with a straight line. From the equal triangle \ntheorem, triangle OXV is congruent (equal) to triangle OZV. So, angle \nXOV = \n2\n1\n4623# c=\nFigure 11.14: Lines OZ and OX tangent to a circle with centre V\nFrom triangle OXV,\nFigure 11.15: Triangle OXV\nTan23 = \nxv\n6\n, |XV| = tan623 = 2.568cm\nPerimeter of OXVZ = 6 + 6 + 2.568 + 2.568 = 17.09cm\nM1 for finding XVZ\nA1 for XVZ = 134\nM1 for 360 – their 226\nM1 for correct method to \nfind XOZ\nA1 for XOZ=46\nM1 for correct method to \nfind the radius of the circle\nA1 for 6tan 23 or 6/\ntan67, or 2.5468 seen or \nequivalent.\nM1 for correct method for \nfinding the perimeter\nA1 for answers which round \nto 17cm','2dbc764ec0248b5fc2a16ddcd248e0a63ab4569c6bb48c6a20fd154a41169c4e','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(225,1,1,224,230,230,'206\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nQuestion Two\na. \ni. \nSince |OB| is the diameter, it implies that\nBCO = BAO = 90°\nThis means ACO + 50°, So ACO = 40°\nSince ABO and ACO are subtended at the circumference on chord |AO|, \ntheir measures are equal. This implies ABO = 40\nFrom triangle OBC, we have OBC + 90° + 57° = 180°\nOBC = 33°\nTherefore, ABC = 40° + 33° = 73°\nii.\nFrom the alternative segment theorem, AOE = ABO = 90°\nAlternative solution\ni. Since ACB and AOB are subtended by chord |AB| at the \ncircumference,\nACB = AOB = 50°\nThis implies AOC = 57° + 50° = 107°\nAlso, since opposite angles in a cyclic quadrilateral sum up to 180°,\nwe have ABC + AOC = 180°\nABC = 180° −107° = 43°\nii.\nSince the radius or the diameter is perpendicular to the tangent,\nAOB + AOE = 90°\nAOE = 90° − 50° = 40°\nb.\nFrom OBC,\n\nFigure 11.16: Triangle OBC\nWe have Sin 57°= \n||BC\n10\n, |BC| = Sin1057c\nAlso, Sin 33° = \n||OC\n10\n, |OC| = Sin1033c\ni. \nB1 for finding AOB,\nM1 for applying the \nopposite angle theorem A1 \nfor ABC = 73\nOR\nB1 for ABO,\nM1 for finding OBC\nA1 for ABC=73\nii.\n\nM1 for applying the \nalternate angle theorem or \nthe tangent theorem.\nA1 for AOE=40\nb.\n\nM1 for correct method to \nfind side |OC| or |BC|\nA0.5 for |OC|\nA0.5 for |BC|\nM1 for solving for either \n|OA| or |AB|\nA0.5 for |OA|\nA0.5 for |AB|','fac0af1164b4a653d2967454b52e754482774950e5fa234d0137ea6a3d0606bb','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(226,1,1,225,231,231,'207\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\n\nFigure 11.17: Triangle OAB\nFrom OAB, we have sin50° = \n||AB\n10\n, |AB| = sin1050\nAlso, sin40° = \n||OA\n10\n, |OA| = sin1040\nPerimeter of OABC = 10sin57 + 10sin33 + 10sin50 + 10sin40\n= 27.92cm\nM1 for correct method to \nfind perimeter\nA1 for answers which round \nto 28\nWEEK 12\nQuestion One\nFigure 12.19: Angle bisector of AOB\nM1 A1 for angle bisector.\nNote, for method marks, \nconstruction markings on \nthe diagram need to be seen \nthroughout the answers.\nFor accuracy marks allow a \ntolerance of +/- 3 degrees.\nQuestion Two\nFigure 12.20: A perpendicular from point O to line L\nM1A1 for correct construction','e3483e005a8b987ff3bec3005f17fac7aa0ddd0f8353d7fc5b4ebc4acaf1ba42','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(227,1,1,226,232,232,'208\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric ConstructionSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nQuestion Three\nFigure 12.21: Triangle AOB\na. M1/A1 for \nconstruction AOB 30°\nb. M1 for construction of \n90-degree angle\nM1 for bisecting 30° \nA1 for final angle of \n105° degrees\nQuestion Four\nFigure 12.22: Quadrilateral ABCD\na. M1/A1 for locating point D\nb. M1/A1 for locating point C\nc. A1 for correctly adding the lengths of their perimeter together.\nQuestion Five\nFigure 12.23: triangle AOB\na. M1/A1 for angle AOB = 75°\nb. A1 for correctly measuring their ABO','2cb3f981dfeff0971ccb2e5b690f4c282a6fe893ed8a87692334aacbe8d15c48','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(228,1,1,227,233,233,'209\nSection 3: Circle Theorems & Geometric Construction\nWEEK 13\nQuestion One\nFigure 13.40: Quadrilateral ABCD\na. A1 line AB\nM1/A1 for angle BAD=105\nA1 for line BC\nA1 for line CD\nb. M1/A1 for the angle \nbisector of BCD\nM1/A1 for locating O\nc. A1 for CD = 7.8m +/- 0.5m\nA1 for their perimeter.\n5+6+7.5+7.8 = 26.3m\nQuestion Two\nFigure 13.41: Triangle MNP and a circle with centre P\nb. The distance between A and B = 9.2cm.\nThis is the same as 18.4km in reality.\nc. On paper, |AN|=6.2cm. In reality, AN = 12.4km\nOn paper, |BN|=13.9cm. In reality, |BN|=27.8km\nd. Location A, which is 12.4km from N, rather than 27.8km.\na. A1 for line MP\nM1/A1 for MN\nM1/A1 for NP\nM1/A1 for perpendicular \nbisector of line MN\nM1 for circle radius 5cm \nfrom P\nA1 for locating points A and \nB\nb. A1 for either 9.2cm or \n18.4km\nc. A1 for AN\nA1 for BN\nd. B1 for choosing location A \nand justifying it because it \nis the closer location','de92c069cd242515b01d385d39308065dbb21156b44f077811714e228226d282','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(229,1,1,228,234,234,'210\nSection 4: Trigonometry GraphsSection 4: Trigonometry Graphs\nSECTION 4: TRIGONOMETRY GRAPHS\nSTRAND: GEOMETRY AROUND US\nSub-Strand: Measurement\nWeeks 14 & 15\nLearning Outcome: Draw graphs of given trigonometric functions and use them to \ndetermine equations and solve related problems.\nContent Standard: Demonstrate conceptual understanding of trigonometric graphs and use \nthem to solve trigonometric equations.\nINTRODUCTION AND SESSION SUMMARY\nThe basic trigonometric functions are sine (sin), Cosine (cos), and Tangent (tan). The graphs of \nthese functions give a visual representation of their behaviour over a range of angles. These \nfunctions extend into waveforms when graphed on the Cartesian plane.\nEach trigonometric function exhibits a periodic pattern , meaning the graph repeats itself at \nregular intervals. These patterns are especially useful for modelling real-world phenomena \nsuch as sound waves, light waves, tides, and circular motion.\nHere are the key characteristics of the three primary trigonometric graphs:\n1. Sine Graph (y = sin x)\n• Domain: All real numbers\n• Range: [−1,1]\n2. Cosine Graph (y = cos x)\n• Domain: All real numbers\n• Range: [-1, 1]\n3. Tangent Graph (y = tan x)\n• Domain: All real numbers except 90°, 270°, 450°, 630° …..\n• Range: All real numbers\nUnderstanding these graphs involves recognising how changes to the equation affect their \nappearance. Mastery of trigonometry graphs is foundational for more advanced topics in maths, \nphysics, and engineering.','28c75d29b056c0c914f0a5996b5b480e136b21d153650c21af9943af596f3ec4','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(230,1,1,229,235,235,'211\nSection 4: Trigonometry Graphs\nIn this section, we will learn how to graph these functions over a given range of angles. This \nwill help us make inferences and use them to solve equations\nThe weeks, the indicators with their respective focal areas covered by the section are:\nWeek 14\n• Draw graphs of given trigonometric functions and use them to solve related problems\nFocal Area: Trigonometric graphs\n• Draw trigonometric graphs and use them to solve related problems.\nWeek 15\n• Use trigonometric graphs to determine equations.\nFocal Area: Determining equations using trigonometric graphs\n• Use trigonometric graphs to determine equations and solve related problems.\nSUMMARY OF PEDAGOGICAL EXEMPLARS\nThis section is designed to be highly interactive, focusing on hands-on activities where learners \nactively engage with graphing trigonometric functions. To foster collaborative learning, students \nwill work in groups to complete tables of values for provided trigonometric functions and then \ngraph them.\nTherefore, the lessons on these concepts should primarily feature experiential learning \nactivities and mixed-ability groupings. All learners, irrespective of their current proficiency \nlevels, will be fully supported to participate in investigations and present their findings.\nTo ensure effective differentiation and accommodation for diverse learning needs:\n• Approaching-proficiency learners will be given dedicated opportunities to make oral \npresentations of their work, building confidence and communication skills.\n• Above-average or highly proficient learners will have extended activities that involve \nusing computer applications (e.g., graphing software, online calculators) to solve more \ncomplex problems related to trigonometric functions and their graphs.\nASSESSMENT SUMMARY\nTable 1 contains the key assessment for each indicator in the section. The highest DOK for the \nindicator is denoted. The mode of assessment to be carried out and strategies that will aid in \nachieving the indicators are documented.','153478253c64ec837bae8c69e0e8db85ebaaf2d97e065e219d07500279778c5f','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(231,1,1,230,236,236,'212\nSection 4: Trigonometry GraphsSection 4: Trigonometry Graphs\nTable 1: Indicators, DOK levels, assessment modes, and strategies\nIndicators DOK Level Assessment Strategy and/or Mode\nDraw graphs of given trigonometric \nfunctions and use them to solve \nrelated problems\n2 The assessment mode for Week 14 is \nGroup work and presentation\nStrategy: Provide essay questions on \ntrigonometric graphs for learners to \nanswer in groups and present their \nanswers to the class\nUse trigonometric graphs to \ndetermine equations.\n3 The assessment mode for week 15 is \nmultiple representations.\nStrategy: Provide learners with real-\nlife situations for learners to move \nbetween multiple representations (verbal \ndescription → graph → writing equation)','7ee80031249a487ad97c1e2ee8e465bcc84ab96c9eb19a9dd8413ba4d715d61c','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(232,1,1,231,237,237,'213\nSection 4: Trigonometry Graphs\nWEEK 14\nLearning Indicator: Draw graphs of given trigonometric functions and use them to solve \nrelated problems\nFOCAL AREA: TRIGONOMETRIC GRAPHS\nDrawing trigonometric graphs and using them to solve related problems.\nTrigonometry Graphs\nThe graph of a trigonometric function is a visual representation of the function for a given range \nof angles. The horizontal axis of a trigonometric graph represents the angle, usually written \nas θ, and the y-axis represents the function of that angle.\nThroughout this lesson, we will examine the graphs of trigonometric functions for angles \nexpressed in both degrees and radians. Before we consider the graphs of trigonometric functions, \nlet us look at the relationship between degrees and radian measurement.\nRelationship Between Degree and Radian Measurement\nIn mathematics, an angle can be measured in degrees (°) or in radians (rad).\nA degree is a traditional unit of measurement for angles. One full circle is 360° .\nA radian is another way to measure angles. It is based on the length of the arc of a circle.\nOne radian is the angle subtended at the centre of a circle by an arc whose length is equal to \nthe radius of the circle.\nThe Relationship\nThe circumference of a circle = 2πr\nOne full circle = 360°.\nIn radians, one full circle = \nradius\narclength\n= \nr\nr2r\n= 2r\nSo, 360° = radians2r\nDividing both sides by 360, we have:\n\n224\n\n<WH>WEEK 14 \n<LI>Learning Indicator: Draw graphs of given trigonometric functions and use them to solve \nrelated problems\n\n<FA>FOCAL AREA: TRIGONOMETRIC GRAPHS \nDrawing trigonometric graphs and using them to solve related problems.\n\n<H1>Trigonometry Graphs \nThe graph of a trigonometric function is a visual representation of the function for a given range \nof angles. The horizontal axis of a trigonometric graph represents the angle, usually written as θ, \nand the y-axis represents the function of that angle. \nThroughout this lesson, we will examine the graphs of trigonometric functions for angles \nexpressed in both degrees and radians. Before we consider the graphs of trigonometric functions, \nlet us look at the relationship between degrees and radian measurement. \n<H2>Relationship Between Degree and Radian Measurement \nIn mathematics, an angle can be measured in degrees (°) or in radians (rad). \nA degree is a traditional unit of measurement for angles. One full circle is 360°. \nA radian is another way to measure angles. It is based on the length of the arc of a circle. \nOne radian is the angle subtended at the centre of a circle by an arc whose length is equal to the \nradius of the circle. \n<H3>The Relationship \nThe circumference of a circle = 2πr \nOne full circle = 360°. \nIn radians, one full circle =\n???????????? ????????????????????ℎ\n????????????????????????\n=\n2????????\n????\n=2???? \nSo, 360°=2???? ???????????????????????????? \nDividing both sides by 360, we have: \n360°\n360\n=\n2????\n360\n????????????????????????????\n\n360×1°\n360\n=\n2????\n360\n????????????????????????????\n\n1°=\n????\n180\n????????????????????????????\n\n224\n\n<WH>WEEK 14 \n<LI>Learning Indicator: Draw graphs of given trigonometric functions and use them to solve \nrelated problems\n\n<FA>FOCAL AREA: TRIGONOMETRIC GRAPHS \nDrawing trigonometric graphs and using them to solve related problems.','34e77d57272796695e66ca2a681e054daa6204764f0f415c41aec0de145933d2','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(233,1,1,232,237,237,'<H1>Trigonometry Graphs \nThe graph of a trigonometric function is a visual representation of the function for a given range \nof angles. The horizontal axis of a trigonometric graph represents the angle, usually written as θ, \nand the y-axis represents the function of that angle. \nThroughout this lesson, we will examine the graphs of trigonometric functions for angles \nexpressed in both degrees and radians. Before we consider the graphs of trigonometric functions, \nlet us look at the relationship between degrees and radian measurement. \n<H2>Relationship Between Degree and Radian Measurement \nIn mathematics, an angle can be measured in degrees (°) or in radians (rad). \nA degree is a traditional unit of measurement for angles. One full circle is 360°. \nA radian is another way to measure angles. It is based on the length of the arc of a circle. \nOne radian is the angle subtended at the centre of a circle by an arc whose length is equal to the \nradius of the circle. \n<H3>The Relationship \nThe circumference of a circle = 2πr \nOne full circle = 360°. \nIn radians, one full circle =\n???????????? ????????????????????ℎ\n????????????????????????\n=\n2????????\n????\n=2???? \nSo, 360°=2???? ???????????????????????????? \nDividing both sides by 360, we have: \n360°\n360\n=\n2????\n360\n????????????????????????????\n\n360×1°\n360\n=\n2????\n360\n????????????????????????????\n\n1°=\n????\n180\n????????????????????????????\n\n224\n\n<WH>WEEK 14 \n<LI>Learning Indicator: Draw graphs of given trigonometric functions and use them to solve \nrelated problems\n\n<FA>FOCAL AREA: TRIGONOMETRIC GRAPHS \nDrawing trigonometric graphs and using them to solve related problems.\n\n<H1>Trigonometry Graphs \nThe graph of a trigonometric function is a visual representation of the function for a given range \nof angles. The horizontal axis of a trigonometric graph represents the angle, usually written as θ, \nand the y-axis represents the function of that angle. \nThroughout this lesson, we will examine the graphs of trigonometric functions for angles \nexpressed in both degrees and radians. Before we consider the graphs of trigonometric functions, \nlet us look at the relationship between degrees and radian measurement. \n<H2>Relationship Between Degree and Radian Measurement \nIn mathematics, an angle can be measured in degrees (°) or in radians (rad). \nA degree is a traditional unit of measurement for angles. One full circle is 360°. \nA radian is another way to measure angles. It is based on the length of the arc of a circle. \nOne radian is the angle subtended at the centre of a circle by an arc whose length is equal to the \nradius of the circle. \n<H3>The Relationship \nThe circumference of a circle = 2πr \nOne full circle = 360°. \nIn radians, one full circle =\n???????????? ????????????????????ℎ\n????????????????????????\n=\n2????????\n????\n=2???? \nSo, 360°=2???? ???????????????????????????? \nDividing both sides by 360, we have: \n360°\n360\n=\n2????\n360\n????????????????????????????\n\n360×1°\n360\n=\n2????\n360\n????????????????????????????\n\n1°=\n????\n180\n???????????????????????????? \nAnd dividing both sides by 2π:','f3589fa306632699c6cdcd4e716af88fa752f8fc78fc8b1d11a3c99cfa8e87da','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
