raph for −360°≤????≤360 \nTo convert degrees to radians : multiply the degree by = \n180\nr\nTo convert radians to degrees : multiply the radian by = \n180\nr\n\n227\n\nAnd dividing both sides by 2π: \n360°\n2π\n= \n2π×1radians\n2π\n\n180°\nπ\n=1 radian\n\nTo convert degrees to radians: multiply the degree by \nπ\n180\n\nTo convert radians to degrees: multiply the radian by \n180\nπ\n\nAngle in degrees Angle in radians Angle in degrees Angle in radians \n????????° \nπ\n12\n\n195° 13π\n12\n\n????????° \nπ\n6\n\n210° 7π\n6\n\n????????° \nπ\n4\n\n225° 5π\n4\n\n????????° \nπ\n3\n\n240° 4π\n3\n\n????????° 5π\n12\n\n255° 17π\n12\n\n????????° \nπ\n2\n\n270° 3π\n2\n\n????????????° 7π\n12\n\n285° 19π\n12\n\n????????????° 2π\n3\n\n300° 5π\n3\n\n????????????° 3π\n4\n\n315° 7π\n4\n\n????????????° 5π\n6\n\n330° 11π\n6\n\n????????????° 11π\n12\n\n345° 23π\n12\n\n????????????° π 360° 2π\n\n<BodySL>The graph of ????=???????????????? \nFigure 14.1 shows a diagram of the sine graph for −360°≤θ≤360 \nThe graph of y = sini\nFigure 14.1 shows a diagram of the sine graph for 360c#- 360c#i\nFigure 14.1: Graph of y = sini for 360c#- 360c#i','65f53a29da1420c227d41d8ae8c9cdd03637649c0e29e4910f41e7d4c835edc5','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(235,1,1,234,239,239,'215\nSection 4: Trigonometry Graphs\nProperties of the graph of siny i=\n1. The graph does not start at the origin, but it does pass through it.\n2. The graph is continuous and repeats every 360 °\n3. The maximum value is +1\n4. The minimum value is\n5. The zeros are n180i= where is an integer. The zeros = {…, -360°, −180°, 0°, 180°, 360°, \n…..}\n6. The domain is all real numbers. Domain = {:R!ii }\n7. The range is 1#i- 1#\nThe graph of cosy i=\nThe diagram shows a graph of for \nFigure 14.2: Graph of y = Cosi for 360c#- 360c#i\nProperties of the graph of cosy i=\n1. The graph does NOT pass through the origin, unlike the Sin and the Tan graphs\n2. The graph is continuous and repeats every 360\n3. The maximum value is +1\n4. The minimum value is − 1\n5. The zeros are 90ci= n180+ where is an integer. Zeros = {…,−270°, −90°, 90°, 270°, …..}\n6. The domain is all real numbers. Domain = {:R!ii }\n7. The range is 11##i-\nThe graphs of siny i= and cosy i-\nFigure 14.3: Graph of y = sini and y = cosi for 360c#i- 360c#','e1bd3418454aa46a3083757240e0ed7f787d7b40ff3b7d94da03c50d71847981','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(236,1,1,235,240,240,'216\nSection 4: Trigonometry GraphsSection 4: Trigonometry Graphs\nRelationship between the sine and the cosine graph\n1. The graphs have the same shape\n2. If you shift any of the graphs horizontally, either to the left or right by 90c, you will get \nthe other graph. This means ()cos90i- sini= . Likewise, ()sin90i- cosi= .\nSo, we also conclude that if coss inxy= , it means xy 90c+=\nThe graph of tany i=\nThis graph has a unique shape compared with the sine and cosine graphs\nFigure 14.4 shows a diagram of the tan graph for 360c#- 360c#i\nFigure 14.4: Graph of y = tani for 360c#i- 360c#\nProperties of the graph of tany i=\n1. The graph passes through the origin.\n2. The graph is NOT continuous and repeats every 180 °.\n3. It has no maximum value (maximum value approaches ∞)\n4. It has no minimum value (minimum value approaches − ∞)\n5. The zeros are n180i= where is an integer. Zeros = {…,-360°, −180°, 0°, 180°, 360°,..}\n6. Domain = {:R!ii except i=90° + 180°n where n is an integer}.\n7. The range is 3#i- 3# or yR!\n8. Vertical asymptotes at  i=90° + 180°n where n is an integer.','9fab254a396f839919ec2c42b8ca1a3d225f50cc3748a64af2ec6eec91d6e5c4','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(237,1,1,236,241,241,'217\nSection 4: Trigonometry Graphs\nDrawing Trigonometric Graphs\nDrawing a trigonometric graph follows the same procedure as drawing a quadratic graph.\nExample 1\na. Copy and complete the Table 14.1\nTable 14.1: Partially completed table for the relation \nx 0°30° 60° 90° 120° 150° 180° 210° 240° 270° 300° 330° 360°\ny = 2sinx0 1.73 1.73 -1.73\nb. Using a scale of 2 cm to 60° on the x-axis and 2cm to 1 unit on the y – axis, draw the \ngraph of y = 2sinx , for 0° ≤ x ≤ 360°.\nc. Use your graph to find the (i) maximum point (ii) minimum point.\nSolution\nTable 14.2: Completed table for the relation y = 2sinx\nx 0°30°60° 90° 120° 150° 180° 210° 240° 270° 300° 330° 360°\ny = 2sinx011.732 1.731 0 −1 −1.73−2 -1.73−1 0\nFor example\nWhen x = 180 °\ny = 2sin180°\ny = 0\nWhen x = 240 °\ny = 2sin240°\ny = −1.73\nNow we plot the above table values\nFigure 14.5: Graph of y = 2Sinx for 0° ≤ x ≤ 360 °','db8acdcae00120f42c6426df03e933fb1dd1fb3e246b5bffe60e0e288c80f8bd','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(238,1,1,237,242,242,'218\nSection 4: Trigonometry GraphsSection 4: Trigonometry Graphs\nc. From the graph, the\ni. maximum point is (90°, 2)\nii. minimum point is (270°, −2)\nExample 2\nCopy and complete the table of the function \nsinyx2= cosx2- for the interval 0° ≤ x ≤ 180°.\nTable 14.3: Partially completed table for the relation y = 2sinx − cos2x\nx 0° 30° 60° 90° 120° 150° 180°\nsinyx2= cosx2- 0.5 -1.0\nUsing a scale of 2 cm to 30° on the x-axis and 2cm to 1 unit on the y–axis draw the graph of \ny = 2sinx − cos2x , for 0° ≤ x ≤ 180°\nSolution\nTable 14.4: Completed table for the relation y = 2sinx − cos2x\nx 0° 30° 60° 90° 120° 150° 180°\nsinyx2= cosx2- −1 0.52.233 2.230.5-1.0\n\n231\n\nWhen ????=0° \n????=2sin(0)−cos2(0) \n????=−1 \nWhen ????=60° \n????=2sin(60)−cos2(60) \n????=2.23 \nWhen ????=90° \n????=2sin(90)−cos2(90) \n????=3 \nWhen ????=120° \n????=2sin(120)−cos2(120) \n????=2.23 \nWhen ????=150° \n????=2sin(150)−cos2(150) \n????=0.5 \nPlotting the points on the graph\n\nFigure 14.5: Graph of y=2sin????−cos2???? for 0°≤????≤180°\n\n<H1>Transformation of Trigonometric Graphs','d11cd1f6c9375363ddd819919fbe6ef98c5d41cc6d2b4410ce418c189667e961','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(239,1,1,238,243,243,'219\nSection 4: Trigonometry Graphs\nPlotting the points on the graph\nFigure 14.5: Graph of y = 2sinx − cos2x , for 0° ≤ x ≤ 180°\nTransformation of Trigonometric Graphs\nAmplitude, Period, and Phase Shift\nThe Amplitude is the vertical height from the centre line to the peak (trough). We can also \nmeasure the height from the highest to the lowest point and divide the result by 2. That is the \nmaximum value minus the minimum value, and divide the result by 2\nExamples are illustrated below\nFigure 14.6: Trigonometric transformation\nThe centre line is the line y = 0 . The amplitude is the vertical distance from the centre line to \neither the maximum or the minimum value. With the graph illustrated above, the amplitude is \n1 or Amplitude = \n()\n2\n11\n1\n--\n=\nThe Period is the distance required for the graph to complete a full cycle. The period goes from \none peak to the next (or from one point to the next matching point)','5c23b9c5a37e13d6e305b4af43b427ee953f49d30a1293b284837ac127d6c3a5','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(240,1,1,239,244,244,'220\nSection 4: Trigonometry GraphsSection 4: Trigonometry Graphs\nFigure 14.7: Transformation of Trigonometric graph (Period)\nThe period in the trigonometric graph above = 450° + 90° = 360°\nA phase shift is how far the function has shifted horizontally from the usual position to the \nright.\nFigure 14.8: Transformation of Trigonometric graph (phase shift)\n• The dotted curve is a phase shift of the green curve by +45° (to the right).\n• The green curve is a phase shift of the dotted curve by −45° (to the left).\n• A shift to the right is positive, and a shift to the left is negative.\n• A vertical shift is how far a graph is vertically up from the usual position.\nFigure 14.9: Transformation of Trigonometric graph (Vertical shift)','8c22872a62a2adc94ca5f02450e28a2e7ede8bc9fc67c613616a47c758f65ee9','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(241,1,1,240,245,245,'221\nSection 4: Trigonometry Graphs\nThe dotted graph is the same as the green graph but has been shifted upwards(vertical) by 0.5\nA shift up is positive and a shift down is negative.\nIn General,\n\n233\n\nThe period in the trigonometric graph above =450°−90°=360° \nA phase shift is how far the function has shifted horizontally from the usual position to the right.\n\nFigure 14.8: Transformation of Trigonometric graph (phase shift) \n• The dotted curve is a phase shift of the green curve by +45\n∘\n(to the right). \n• The green curve is a phase shift of the dotted curve by −45\n∘\n(to the left). \n• A shift to the right is positive, and a shift to the left is negative. \n• A vertical shift is how far a graph is vertically up from the usual position.\n\nFigure 14.9: Transformation of Trigonometric graph (Vertical shift) \nThe dotted graph is the same as the green graph but has been shifted upwards(vertical) by 0.5 \nA shift up is positive and a shift down is negative. \nIn General, ???????? ????=????????????????(????????+????)+???? or ????=????????????????(????????+????)+???? \n|????| is amplitude |A| is amplitude\nB\n360\nis the period\nB\nc\n- is the phase shift\nD is the vertical shift\nFor example, if 2sin(3x − 90) + 1\nThis implies, Amplitude = 2, meaning the graph will be twice as tall as y = sinx\nPeriod = \n3\n360\n= 120°, it means the graph will repeat itself every 120°\nPhase shift = \n3\n90\n-\n-\n= 30° , it implies the graph will be shifted to the left by 30° (negative \ngoes to the right)\nVertical shift = 1, this tells us the centre line will be instead of so it will be shifted upwards \nby 1 unit.\nNote that the Tangent graph has no Amplitude and its range = {−∞ ≤ x ≤ +∞}. It has no \nmaximum or minimum values.\nThe range of the cosine and sine graphs = {−|A| ≤ x ≤ |A| }\nThe graph of sinyx= (blue curve) and siny2= ()x3901-+ (red curve)\nare illustrated below.\nTable 14.5: Table for the relation \nsiny2= ()x3901-+ and y = sinx\n