(246,1,1,245,249,249,'225\nSection 4: Trigonometry Graphs\nTo find the phase shift for a graph of the form y = aSin(bx + c) + d , we identify one basic cycle \nof the graph that begins at y = 0. Find the x -coordinates of the end points of the cycle [x\n0\n, x\n1\n]. \nThe value of the phase shift is the lower end point of the interval of the basic cycle: phase shift \n= x\n0\n.\nFigure 14.16: Making inferences with a trigonometric graph (Sine equation)\nFrom the graph, we have x\n0\n= 165° ⇒ Phase Shift = 165°\nwe then equate the phase shift to \nB\nc\n- and solve for c\n\n239\n\nTo find the phase shift for a graph of the form ????=????????????????(????????+????)+????, we identify one basic \ncycle of the graph that begins at ????=0. Find the ????-coordinates of the end points of the cycle \n[????\n0,????\n1]. The value of the phase shift is the lower end point of the interval of the basic cycle: \nphase shift =????\n0.\n\nFigure 14.16: Making inferences with a trigonometric graph (Sine equation) \nFrom the graph, we have ????\n0=165° ⟹????ℎ???????????? ????ℎ????????????=165° \nwe then equate the phase shift to −\n????\n????\nand solve for ???? \n−\n????\n????\n=165 , note that ????=2 \n−\n????\n2\n=165 \n????=−330° \nHence, the equation of the graph is ????=1.5????????????(2????−330°) or ????=1.5????????????2(????−165°)\n\n<TH>Learning Tasks \nGuide learners to construct tables of values for points satisfying a given trigonometric function. \nSubsequently, guide them to plot these points and draw the resulting graph. Provide \nopportunities for learners to utilise their drawn graphs to answer related questions. \n<H1>PEDAGOGICAL EXEMPLARS \nThe aim of this week\'s lesson is to enable all learners to solve problems involving trigonometric \ngraphs. The following pedagogical approaches are recommended: \nLearning Tasks\nGuide learners to construct tables of values for points satisfying a given trigonometric \nfunction. Subsequently, guide them to plot these points and draw the resulting graph. \nProvide opportunities for learners to utilise their drawn graphs to answer related \nquestions.\nPEDAGOGICAL EXEMPLARS\nThe aim of this week’s lesson is to enable all learners to solve problems involving trigonometric \ngraphs. The following pedagogical approaches are recommended:\n1. Whole-Class Review and Discussion: Initiate a discussion with the entire class to \nreview learners’ existing knowledge of plotting quadratic equations. Address any \nmisconceptions, then extend these concepts to the graphing of trigonometric functions.','c74f752773035cc05212f903136304e1301e9a5cb1c3526624201fecf0956d3a','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(247,1,1,246,250,250,'226\nSection 4: Trigonometry GraphsSection 4: Trigonometry Graphs\n2. Small Group Collaborative Learning: Organise learners into small groups to discuss \nand explore sine, cosine, and tangent graphs, incorporating illustrative examples. Within \nthese groups, learners will also practice drawing sine, cosine, and tangent graphs.\nKEY ASSESSMENT LEVEL 2: GROUP WORK\n1. \na. Copy and complete the table of values for the relation \ncosy12 i=- . 3 marks\nTable 14.6: Partially completed table for the relation cosy12 i=-\ni 0° 45° 90° 135° 180° 225° 270° 315° 360°\ncosy12 i=- -0.41 2.41 -1\nb. Using a scale of 2 cm to 45 on the i-axis and 2cm to 1 unit on the y–axis, draw the \ngraph of cosy12 i=- , for x0 360cc## 4 marks\nc. Use your drawn graph to find the\ni. the maximum value, 1 mark\nii. the coordinate at which the maximum occurs, 1 mark\niii. values of i for y = 0, correct your answer to one significant figure. 2 marks\niv. Line of symmetry 1 mark\nTotal 12 marks\n2. Copy and complete the table of the function sinyx23= for the interval x0 180cc##\n3 marks\nTable 14.7: Partially completed table for the relation sinyx23=\nx 0° 30° 60° 90° 120° 150° 180°\nsinyx23= 2 0\na. Using a scale of 2 cm to on the x -axis and 2cm to 1 unit on the y–axis, draw the \ngraph of sinyx23= , for x0 180cc## 4 marks\nc. Use your drawn graph to find the\ni. the minimum point 1 mark\nii. the maximum point 1 mark\niii. equation of the line of symmetry 1 mark','e45dbfd35bbb32adebc03418bfb73697cbb8192d80ce78d63f6a9c718fa3c875','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(248,1,1,247,251,251,'227\nSection 4: Trigonometry Graphs\nPLC Session 14\n45 minutes Planning & Reviewing the Learning Plan\n1. Begin the session by recapping lessons from the previous year/session/week, focusing on \nhow learning was applied.\n2. Use your subject-specific app to explore the content before planning. Ask the app to\na. explain the week’s concepts in a practical way that will make it easier for learners to \nunderstand\nb. identify two or three common misconceptions learners may have on the week’s content \nand how to address them using contexts familiar to Ghanaian learners. (NTS 3a, 3g \nand 3m).\n3. Use your subject-specific app to plan your lesson. Ask the app to\na. suggest the most effective method for teaching the week’s concepts to learners with \ndiverse backgrounds.\nb. suggest learning activities, differentiation and essential questions based on the method \nof teaching (NTS 3a-3q).\nc. review your prepared learning plan and address any challenging issues you may have \nidentified in your subject area (NTS 3a-3c).\nNote\n• Take note of the recap topics linked to Years 1 and 2 while preparing for your \nlesson.\n• Refer to the Values Learning Community Handbook and link the values for the \nweek in your lesson to ensure that learners are applying what they learn.\n4. Plan the week’s key assessment with your app\na. The key assessment for the week is group work. Chat with your app to plan this \nassessment, indicating what you and your learners will do before, during and after the \ngroup work (NTS 3k-3q).\n10 minutes Content Exploration\n5. As a subject group, select one problem or idea/term in the week’s content that is challenging \n(not a routine procedural question, but one that requires conceptual understanding) and \nthen\na. use the app, ask it to solve the problem step by step and explain the reasoning behind \neach step (you may be assisted by a teacher who is strong in the content area)\nb. all teachers in the group work through a similar problem individually for 3–4 minutes\nc. the group then works through the problem together step by step','57335cfbdc1842c71fc89274587e7878563295ff3c928f27b42445f2830e3e32','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(249,1,1,248,252,252,'228\nSection 4: Trigonometry GraphsSection 4: Trigonometry Graphs\nd. highlight the most common errors made at each step and discuss (NTS 1c, 1e, 2c, 2e, \n3e-3j).\nNote\n• This part is not just about ‘How do I teach this?’ but how do I teach it for learners \nto understand?\n• If the group still shares the same content gap, use the app: ask it to solve the \nproblem step by step and explain the reasoning behind each step.\n• Problems/ideas discussed should be sourced from the Teacher Manual content, not \ninvented separately. This keeps the content exploration directly connected to what \nteachers will deliver.\n30 minutes Enactment\n6. Enact a teaching activity based on your learning plan (NTS la-1b, 1e-lg, 2c, 2e, 2f, 3c-3j \nand 3o).\na. Colleagues observe with specific attention to clarity of concept explanation, use of \nfamiliar Ghanaian examples and how misconceptions are handled if they arise.\nb. After the enactment, ask for structured feedback from the group\ni. was the concept explained in a way that a learner with no prior knowledge could \nfollow?\nii. were the examples grounded in contexts familiar to Ghanaian learners?\niii. did any misconceptions surface and if so, how were they addressed?\n5 minutes Reflection\n7. Reflect and share your views on the session. One thing that worked well, what did not work \nwell and what you will do differently. (NTS 1a, 1b).\n8. Identify a critical friend to observe and provide feedback on your lesson in relation to the \nPLC Session conducted (NTS 1f, 1g).\n9. Remember to\na. transfer your chats into your learning planner template.\nb. read your Teacher Manual and related Learner Material in preparation for the next \nsession. Take note of the recap weeks, which are linked to Years 1 and 2 (NTS 3a).\nc. integrate National Values, GESI, SEL, and 21st century skills into your learning \nactivities, assessment tasks, and rubrics/mark schemes.','b8d888efcf50aa248fa2fd92105ac8a81e91a23e35099f765586dd50787c6dd1','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(250,1,1,249,253,253,'229\nSection 4: Trigonometry Graphs\nWEEK 15\nLearning Indicator: Use a trigonometry graph to determine equations\nFOCAL AREA: DETERMINING EQUATIONS USING \nTRIGONOMETRIC GRAPHS\nUse trigonometric graphs to determine equations and solve related problems.\nFinding the solution set from the trigonometry graph\nWe will use the following examples to learn how to solve trigonometry equations using a graph.\nExample 1\na. Copy and complete the table below for the relation \nsinyx= cosx3- for values x of from \n0° to 180° at intervals of 30°.\nTable 15.1: Partially completed table for the relation \nsinyx= cosx3-\nx 0° 30° 60° 90°120°150°180°\nsinyx= cosx3- −3.00 −2.10−0.63 3.10\nb. Using a scale of 2 cm to 30° on the -axis and 2 cm to 1 unit on the y-axis draw a graph \nof y.\nc. Use your graph to estimate, in the given interval, the truth set of the equations\ni. sinx − 3cosx = 0\nii. sinx − 3cosx = 1\nSolution\na. We complete the above table \nTable 15.2: Completed table for the relation \nsinyx= cosx3-\nx 0° 30° 60° 90°120°150°180°\nsinyx= cosx3- −3.00 −2.10−0.631.002.373.103.00\n\n243\n\n<WH>WEEK 15 \n<LI>Learning Indicator: Use a trigonometry graph to determine equations\n\n<FA>FOCAL AREA: DETERMINING EQUATIONS USING TRIGONOMETRIC \nGRAPHS \nUse trigonometric graphs to determine equations and solve related problems.\n\n<H3>Finding the solution set from the trigonometry graph \nWe will use the following examples to learn how to solve trigonometry equations using a graph.\n\n<H4>Example 1 \n(a) Copy and complete the table below for the relation ????=????????????????−3cos???? for values of \n???? from 0° to 180° at intervals of 30°\n\nTable 15.1: Partially completed table for the relation ????=????????????????−3???????????????? \n???? 0° 30° 60° 90° 120° 150° 180° \n????=????????????????−3 ???????????????? −3.00 −2.10 −0.63 3.10\n\n(b) Using a scale of 2 cm to 30° on the ????-axis and 2 cm to 1 unit on the ????-axis draw a graph \nof y. \n(c) Use your graph to estimate, in the given interval, the truth set of the equations \n(1) ????????????????−3cos????=0 (ii) ????????????????−3cos????=1 \n<BodySL>Solution \n(a) We complete the above table\n\nTable 15.2: Completed table for the relation ????=????????????????−3???????????????? \n???? 0° 30° 60° 90° 120° 150° 180° \n????=????????????????−3 ???????????????? −3.00 −2.10 −0.63 1.00 2.37 3.10 3.00\n\nWhen ????=90° \n????=sin (90)−3 cos (90) \n????=1 \nWhen ????=120° \n????=sin (120)−3 cos (120)','b0294bf882b599a1d5a6212481c7ef729307f7657cbb366655d58308169b30ea','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(251,1,1,250,254,254,'230\nSection 4: Trigonometry GraphsSection 4: Trigonometry Graphs\n\n244\n\n????=2.37 \nWhen ????=180° \n????=sin (180)−3 cos (180) \n????=3 \n(b) Plotting the graph of the function\n\nFigure 15.1: graph of ????=????????????????−3cos???? \n(c) \ni. Comparing the two equations ????????????????−3cos????=0 but \n????=????????????????−3 ????????????????, \n????=0. \nFrom the graph, the curve intersects the line ????=0 at ????≈72° \nAccept any value between 70° ???????? 72° \nb. Plotting the graph of the function\nFigure 15.1: graph of y = sinx − 3cos x\nc. \ni. Comparing the two equations sinyx= cosx3- = 0 but\nsinyx= cosx3-\ny = 0\nFrom the graph, the curve intersects the line y = 0 at x ≈ 72 °\nAccept any value between 70° to 72°.\nFigure 15.2: graphs of y = sinx − 3cos x and y = 1','331407d0ee7d4b312536f8945c1ae7100675623c29f3c46f686b8eb2368df979','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(252,1,1,251,255,255,'231\nSection 4: Trigonometry Graphs\nHence, the solution set of is given by: {x:x ≈ 72°}\nii. Comparing sinyx= cosx3- = 1 and sinyx= cosx3- , y = 1.\nFrom the graph, the curve, and the line y = 1 intersect at x = 90°\nHence, the solution set of sinyx= cosx3- = 1 is given by: {x:x = 90°}\nExample 2\na. Copy and complete the table below for the relation 2cosyx= for values of x from 0° to \n360° at intervals of 45°.\nTable 15.3: Partially completed table for the relation y = 2cos x \nx 0°45°90°135°180° 225°270°315°360°\n2cosyx= 0 −2 0\nb. Using a scale of 2 cm to 45° on the x-axis and 2 cm to 1 unit on the y-axis, draw a \ngraph of y .\nc. Use your graph to estimate, in the given interval, the truth set of the equations\ni. 1 + 2cosx = 0\nii. the minimum point of 2cosyx=\niii. equation of the line of symmetry\nSolution\na. We copy and complete the above table.\nTable 15.4: Completed table of the relation y = 2cos x\nx 0°45°90° 135°180° 225°270°315°360°\n2cosyx= 21.41 0 −1.41 −2 −1.41 0 1.41 2\nb. We plot the above points on the graph and join them with a free-hand curve\nFigure 15.3: Graph of y = 2cos x','74ac27be9fe4ee1b9e593127d931bff07f738b36ef1d21b64e7abb3ac7a4467c','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(253,1,1,252,256,256,'232\nSection 4: Trigonometry GraphsSection 4: Trigonometry Graphs\nc. \ni. Comparing the two equations y = 2cos x and 1 + 2cosx = 0, we obtained y = − 1.\nDraw the line y = −1. on the graph and find its truth set. That is the values of x for \nwhich the line and the curve intersect.\nFigure 15.4: Graph of y = 2cosx and line y = −1\nFrom the graph, the line y = − 1 intersect the curve at x ≈ 121.5° and x ≈ 238.5° as \nshown in the graph above.\nFigure 15.5: A zoom in of a part of the graph of y = 2cosx and y = −1\nZooming in clearly shows that the intersection of the line and the curve falls on the \n7\nth\nsub-line after 90° and also the third sub-line after 225°. Using the given scale, we \nhave\nx ≈ 90 + 7 × 4.5 ≈121.5° and x ≈ 225 + 3 × 4.5 ≈238.5°\nii. The minimum point is the point at which the graph turns concave upwards.','f4245d402bf71235e56485fe12edfd7ea056ecd02cf634a6e137b2db7f37255a','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(254,1,1,253,257,257,'233\nSection 4: Trigonometry Graphs\nFigure 15.6: Graph of y = 2cosx showing the minimum point\nFrom the graph, the minimum point as indicated as (180°, −2). The y-coordinate of this point \nis called the minimum value, which is − 2. The minimum value occurs when x = 180 °.\niii. The line of symmetry divides the curve within the range given into equal halves.\nFigure 15.7: Graph of y = 2cosx showing the equation of the line of symmetry\nFrom the graph, the line of symmetry is indicated by the broken line. It intersects the x-axis at \n180. Hence, the line of symmetry is; x = 180 °\nKEY ASSESSMENT LEVEL 3: COMPUTATIONS & INTERPRETATIONS\n1. \na. Copy and complete the table below for the relation y = 2sinx + 3 for values of x from \n0° to 180° at intervals of 30 °. 2 marks','5e8c2ee570315c669c507d7931d01514ee8710cbd006f7b3f6760d309def189e','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(255,1,1,254,258,258,'234\nSection 4: Trigonometry GraphsSection 4: Trigonometry Graphs\nTable 15.5: Partially completed table of the relation y = 2sin x + 3 \nx 0° 30° 60° 90° 120° 150° 180°\nsinyx23=+ 3.00 4.00 3.00\nb. Using a scale of 2 cm to 30° on the x -axis and 2 cm to 1 unit on the y -axis, draw a \ngraph of sinyx23=+ 4 marks\nc. Use your graph to estimate, in the given interval,\ni. Solve the equation sinyx23=+ − 1 = 0 3 marks\nii. the maximum value of y and the value of x at which it occurs 2 marks\nTotal 11 marks\n2. \na. Copy and complete the table below for the relation cosyx3= sinx2+ for values of \nx from 0° to 180° at intervals of 30°. 2 marks\nTable 15.6: Partially completed table of the relation y = 3cos x + 2sinx\nx 0° 30° 60° 90° 120° 150° 180°\ncosyx3= sinx2+ 3.00 3.23 −3.00\nb. Using a scale of 2 cm to 30° on the x -axis and 2 cm to 1 unit on the y-axis draw a \ngraph of cosyx3= sinx2+ . 4 \nmarks\nc. Use your graph to estimate, in the given interval, the truth set of the equations.\nCorrect your answer to one decimal place.\ni. 6cosx + 4sin x = 1 4 marks\nii. 3cosx + 2sin x = − 1 4 marks\nTotal 14 marks\nPLC Session 15\n45 minutes Planning & Reviewing the Learning Plan\n1. Begin the session by recapping lessons from the previous year/session/week, focusing on \nhow learning was applied.\n2. Use your subject specific app to explore the content before planning. Ask the app to\na. explain the week’s concepts in a practical way that will make it easier for learners to \nunderstand\nb. identify two or three common misconceptions learners may have on the week’s content \nand how to address them using contexts familiar to Ghanaian learners. (NTS 3a, 3g \nand 3m).','35be69d3c1b3e4e522619c3b2732057ba66aeed54f1a2514640ca009a8d31e15','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(256,1,1,255,259,259,'235\nSection 4: Trigonometry Graphs\n3. Use your subject specific app to plan your lesson. Ask the app to\na. suggest the most effective method for teaching the week’s concepts to learners with \ndiverse backgrounds.\nb. suggest learning activities, differentiation and essential questions based on the method \nof teaching (NTS 3a-3q)\nc. review your prepared learning plan and address any challenging issues you may have \nidentified in your subject area (NTS 3a-3c).\nNote\n• Take note of the recap topics linked to Years 1 and 2 while preparing for your \nlesson.\n• Refer to the Values Learning Community Handbook and link the values for the \nweek in your lesson to ensure that learners are applying what they learn.\n4. Plan the week’s key assessment with your app\na. The key assessment for the week is computations . Chat with your app to plan this \nassessment, indicating what you and your learners will do before, during and after the \nassessment (NTS 3k-3q).\n10 minutes Content Exploration\n5. As a subject group, select one problem or idea/term in the week’s content that is challenging \n(not a routine procedural question, but one that requires conceptual understanding) and \nthen\na. use the app, ask it to solve the problem step by step and explain the reasoning behind \neach step (you may be assisted by a teacher who is strong in the content area)\nb. all teachers in the group work through a similar problem individually for 3–4 minutes\nc. the group then works through the problem together step by step\nd. highlight the most common errors made at each step and discuss (NTS 1c, 1e, 2c, 2e, \n3e-3j).\nNote\n• This part is not just about ‘How do I teach this?’ but how do I teach it for learners \nto understand?\n• If the group still shares the same content gap, use the app: ask it to solve the \nproblem step by step and explain the reasoning behind each step.','724bcfc2c59feee6782ebbf5a71e00123f3027f99ac8c5d0a433de3cfa0138bd','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(257,1,1,256,260,260,'236\nSection 4: Trigonometry GraphsSection 4: Trigonometry Graphs\n• Problems/ideas discussed should be sourced from the Teacher Manual content, not \ninvented separately. This keeps the content exploration directly connected to what \nteachers will deliver.\n30 minutes Enactment\n6. Enact a teaching activity based on your learning plan (NTS la-1b, 1e-lg, 2c, 2e, 2f, 3c-3j \nand 3o).\na. Colleagues observe with specific attention to clarity of concept explanation, use of \nfamiliar Ghanaian examples and how misconceptions are handled if they arise.\nb. After the enactment, ask for structured feedback from the group\ni. was the concept explained in a way that a learner with no prior knowledge could \nfollow?\nii. were the examples grounded in contexts familiar to Ghanaian learners?\niii. did any misconceptions surface and if so, how were they addressed?\n5 minutes Reflection\n7. Reflect and share your views on the session. One thing that worked well, what did not work \nwell and what you will do differently. (NTS 1a, 1b).\n8. Identify a critical friend to observe and provide feedback on your lesson in relation to the \nPLC Session conducted (NTS 1f, 1g).\n9. Remember to\na. transfer your chats into your learning planner template.\nb. read your Teacher Manual and related Learner Material in preparation for the next \nsession. Take note of the recap weeks, which are linked to Years 1 and 2 (NTS 3a).\nc. integrate National Values, GESI, SEL, and 21st century skills into your learning \nactivities, assessment tasks, and rubrics/mark schemes.\nSection 4 Review\nThis section summarises the core concepts covered in Weeks 14 and 15, providing \nan essential overview of the learning outcomes during this period. These weeks are \ncrucial for establishing a foundational understanding of trigonometry, paving the \nway for more advanced topics. During this period, learners developed proficiency in:\n1. Understanding Basic Trigonometric Functions\na. Identifying and describing the fundamental characteristics, shapes, and \nproperties of the sine, cosine, and tangent functions.','5282ef29e2ac5ffbfb0ca5a82850121ff50e9c714146a6566882c58a47fd3d74','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(258,1,1,257,261,261,'237\nSection 4: Trigonometry Graphs\n2. Graphing Trigonometric Functions\n• Constructing tables of values for various trigonometric functions.\n• Utilising these tables to accurately plot points and draw the \ncorresponding graphs.\n3. Analysing Trigonometric Graphs\na. Interpreting drawn graphs to answer related questions, including:\n• Determining minimum and maximum values and their \ncorresponding points.\n• Identifying equations of lines of symmetry.\n• Graphically solving trigonometric equations.\nRecommended Resources for Enhanced Teaching and Learning\nTo optimise the teaching and learning experience, the following resources are highly \nrecommended:\n• Complete set of mathematical instruments\n• Whiteboard\n• Educational videos\n• Mini whiteboards\n• Dry erase markers and erasers\n• Calculators\n• Graph/grid paper\n• Digital tools (e.g., computers, mobile phones, YouTube videos)','2ed34606cdb7359f267d2839e22077e8f671e87c0c84ffc42747c0239cec9caa','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(259,1,1,258,262,262,'238\nSection 4: Trigonometry GraphsSection 4: Trigonometry Graphs\nMARKING SCHEMES FOR THE KEY ASSESSMENTS\nWEEK 14\nQuestion One\na.\nTable 14.8: Completed table for the relation y = \ncos12 i-\ni\n0°45°90°135°180°225°270°315° 360°\ny-1-0.411 2.413 2.411 -0.41-1\nb.\nFigure 14.17: Graph of y = cos12 i- for 0c#i360c#\nc. \ni. maximum value = 3\nii. maximum point = (180°, 3)\niii. when y = 0, 60i=, 300°\niv. line of symmetry is 180ci=\nB3 for table\n(−0.5ee)\nB1 for correct axes\nB2 for points plotted \ncorrectly (−0.5 for each \nerror)\nB1 for a smooth curve \njoining the points\nA1 for max value\nA1 for maximum point\nA1 for 60i=\nA1 for 180ci=\nA1 for line of symmetry\nQuestion Two\na.\nTable 14.9: Completed table for the relation y = 2sin3x\nx 0° 30°60°90°120°150°180°\ny = 2sin3x0 2 0 -2 0 2 0\nB3 for table (−0.5 each \nerror)\nB1 for correct axes\nB2 for points plotted \ncorrectly (−0.5 for each \nerror)','a60f504e267d13b561017e1109131287413676c0f83b807cebaac23266a89aee','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(260,1,1,259,263,263,'239\nSection 4: Trigonometry Graphs\nFigure 14.18: Graph of y = 2sin3x for 0° ≤ x ≤ 180 °\nb.\ni. minimum point = (90°, −2)\nii. maximum value = 2\niii. line of symmetry is x = 90°\nB1 for a smooth curve \njoining the points\nA1 for their minimum \npoint\nA1 for their min value\nA1 for line of symmetry\nWEEK 15\nQuestion One\na.\nTable 15.7: Completed table for the relation y = 2sin x + 3\nx 0 30 60 90 120150180\ny3.004.004.735.004.734.003.00\nb. Plotting the above points, we obtain\nFigure 15.8: Graph of y =2sinx + 3 and y = 4\na. B2 for completing table \n(−1 each error)\nb. B1 for correct axes\nB2 for points plotted \ncorrectly (−0.5 for each \nerror)\nB1 for a smooth curve \njoining the points','2c84afcb4818d8e6a38e1d30c6934bf00577c52ef692b9be13314597e6959850','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(261,1,1,260,264,264,'240\nSection 4: Trigonometry Graphs\nc. \ni. Comparing the two equations y = 2sinx + 3\ny = 4\nFrom the graph, when y = 4, x = 30, or x = 150\nii. maximum value = 5\noccurs when x = 90 °\nc. \ni. B1 for y = 4\nM1 for drawing line y \n= 4 on the graph\nA1 for x = 30 and x = \n150 (−0.5 each error)\nii. A1 for max value\nA1 for x = 90°\nQuestion Two\na.\nTable 15.8: Completed table of the relation y = 3cos x + 2sinx\nx 0° 30°60°90°120°150° 180°\ny3.003.603.202.000.23−1.60 −3.00\nb.\nFigure 15.9: Graph of y = 3cosx + 2sinx and y = 4\nc.\ni. y = 0.5\nx = 115.5°\nii. y = −1\nx = 139.5°\na.\nB2 for completing (−0.5 \neach error)\nb.\nB1 for correct axes\nB2 for points plotted \ncorrectly (−0.5 for each \nerror)\nB1 for a smooth curve \njoining the points\nB4 for graph (−0.5 each \nerror)\nc.\ni.\nB1 for y = 0.5\nM1 for drawing line \ny = 0.5\nM1/A1 for reading \nvalue from graph\nx = 115.5° ± 3°\nii.\nB1 for y = − 1\nM1 for drawing line \ny = −1\nM1/A1 for reading \nvalue from graph\nx = 139.5° ± 3°','79141b1df4622e7fe7982f51e8f73a765b10dda06b05dd8106449d664f2f170c','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(262,1,1,261,265,265,'241\nSECTION 5: DATA HANDLING & PROBABILITY\nSTRAND: MAKING SENSE OF AND USING DATA\nSub-Strand 1: Statistical Reasoning And Its Applications In Real Life\nWeeks 16&17\nLearning Outcome: Establish simple mathematical relationships between two variables in \na given observational or experimental context; illustrate using scatter \ngraphs and use them to solve and/or pose problems.\nContent Standards: Demonstrate understanding of data handling involving simple \nmathematical relationships of bivariate data in observational and \nexperimental contexts.\nWeek18\nLearning Outcome: Compare different data sets and use mathematical vocabulary and \nlanguage (concretely, pictorially, symbolically, spoken or written, etc.) \nto contribute to mathematical and everyday discussions using valid \nand reliable data to explain and justify the information produced from \nthe data.\nContent Standard: Demonstrate the ability to compare different data sets and use appropriate \nvocabulary to contribute to mathematical and everyday discussions and \nmake inferences about information gathered.\nSub-Strand 2: Probability/Chance\nWeeks 19&20\nLearning Outcomes: Explain how a given probability from print and electronic media \ninfluences individual decisions; select and analyse real-life data \nto solve problems involving the probability of two dependent and \nindependent events.\nContent Standard: Demonstrate an understanding of the role of probability in society and \napply probability reasoning to analyse and make useful predictions \nabout everyday life events.','61da575c786efb64ce877cd22cb5a4d0679c31fe5d1f43f1684163ea26b995ba','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(263,1,1,262,266,266,'242\nSection 5: Data Handling & ProbabilitySection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nINTRODUCTION AND SECTION SUMMARY\nThis section, spanning weeks 16-20, is divided into two sub-strands: statistical reasoning (weeks \n16-18) and probability (weeks 19-20). The statistical reasoning component will equip learners \nto engage with real-world data and media representations by exploring how to reason with data. \nKey areas of focus include:\n• Bivariate Data Analysis: Establishing relationships between two variables, identifying \nbivariate data sets, differentiating between dependent and independent variables, and \nplotting/interpreting graphs of bivariate data.\n• Experimental Data: Collecting data through simple experiments, constructing scatter \nplots from experimental results, and interpreting the findings.\n• Data Comparison: Comparing different data sets to determine the most representative \none for a given scenario.\n• Media Literacy: Empowering teachers to guide learners in interpreting data presented \nin media, making informed inferences, and drawing logical conclusions.\nThe probability component will examine the application of probability in media and provide \nsupport for learners in solving problems involving both dependent and independent probability \nevents.\nThe weeks and the indicators covered by the section are:\nWeek 16\nEstablish simple mathematical relationships between two variables in a given observational or \nexperimental context; illustrate using scatter graphs and use them to solve and/or pose problems.\nFocal Area: Establishing the relationship between two variables\n• Identify variables in a given data\n• Identify whether or not a given table show a bivariate data.\n• Identify dependent and independent variables.\n• Plot and interpret graphs of bivariate data.\nWeek 17\nCollect data from an experimental study in which the interest is based on a treatment and a \nnon-treatment (control) group. Illustrate the data using scatter graphs and find the relationship \nbetween the variables, if any.\nFocal Area: Data collection on simple experiments\n• Collect data on simple experiments\n• Draw and interpret scatter plots constructed from experimental data.\nWeek 18\n1. Compare the quality of different data sets using knowledge and skills acquired from data \nanalysis techniques learned in earlier statistics work.','0a5daa86cb50fc5fd8e535af121c47b8600a01966b67b2384c734e24ee1bf070','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(264,1,1,263,267,267,'243\nSection 5: Data Handling & Probability\n2. Discuss data information published in local and/or international media platforms (local \nand international TV stations, newspapers, journals, etc.) by making useful inferences \nfrom the data to conclude and/or to explain other phenomena.\nFocal Area 1: Comparing different data sets\n• Justify which of the two given data best represents a given situation.\n• Interpret and make recommendations on a given data.\nFocal Area 2: Interpreting data presented on media platforms\n• Identify and use data from various media platforms\n• Interpret data information presented in the media\n• Make useful inferences and conclude from the data presented in the media\nWeek 19\nIdentify examples from print and electronic media, e.g., newspapers, television, the Internet, \nwhere probability is used, and explain how the given probability influences individual decisions.\nFocal Area: Identification of probability usage in the media\n• Identify examples of the use of probability in real life context including the media\n• Give explanation and justifications on how these probability events influences individual \ndecisions\nWeek 20\nSolve everyday life problems involving the probability of two dependent and independent \nevents, including addition and multiplication laws.\nFocal Area: Real-life problems involving probability\n• Solve problems involving dependent and independent probability events\n• Use addition and multiplication laws to solve probability problems.\nSUMMARY OF PEDAGOGICAL EXEMPLARS\nThis section requires hands-on activities where learners engage in practical activities with \nquadratic equations and functions. Learners should be given the platform to work in groups to \ndevelop their real-life questions and find answers. The following pedagogical approach is \nsuggested for teachers to take learners through.\n1. Reflection: Learners review previous knowledge on types of data and the collection and \norganisation of data.\n2. Think-Pair-Share: Learners pair up to discuss mathematical relationships between two \nvariables and differentiate dependent and independent variables in a bivariate data.\n3. Problem-Based Learning: In convenient groups, learners collect data of interest within \nor outside the school community, present them graphically, and predict future occurrences.','a9acc82882bd804521ccf2c408c48bb35acf0ea12a679cbc5f17ac568c8a7133','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(265,1,1,264,268,268,'244\nSection 5: Data Handling & ProbabilitySection 5: Data Handling & Probability\n4. Collaborative Exploration: Learners, in convenient groups, explore the probabilities \nthat are applied in the media outlets and also identify the likely predictions and decisions.\n5. Project-based Learning: In convenient groups, learners are tasked to collect data of \ninterest within or outside the school community (including using social media platforms \nor any technological means for collecting data). Learners are assigned project work \n(individually or in groups) to obtain current data about contemporary issues of interest, \nthen make conclusions, give criticisms, and make useful recommendations.\n6. Experiential Learning: Learners draw scatter plots for bivariate data. Learners pair to \ncollect data on simple experiments and interpret data presented in graphs (scatter plots), \ndraw conclusions, and give justification for the conclusions. Compare the quality of the \ngiven data sets and conclude on the appropriateness of the data\n7. Using talk for learning: Learners debate where they support their argument with data and \nthe use of appropriate mathematics terminology on probability.\nASSESSMENT SUMMARY\nTable 1 contains the key assessment for each indicator in the section. The highest level of DOK \nfor the indicator is denoted. The mode of assessment to be carried out and strategies that will \naid in achieving the indicators are documented.\nTable 3: Indicators, DOK levels, assessment modes and strategies\nIndicators DOK \nLevel\nAssessment Strategy and/or Mode\nEstablish simple mathematical \nrelationships between two variables in \na given observational or experimental \ncontext; illustrate using scatter graphs and \nuse them to solve and/or pose problems.\nLevel 3 Assessment Mode: Group project work\nAssessment Strategy: Group work and \npresentation to find the relationship \nbetween two variables.\nCollect data from an experimental study in \nwhich the interest is based on a treatment \nand non-treatment (control) groups. \nIllustrate the data using scatter graphs \nand find the relationship between the \nvariables, if any.\nLevel 3 Assessment Mode: Group project\nAssessment Strategy: Learners collect \nbivariate data on their own from the \nschool community, represent their \ndata using scatter plots, and make \nconclusions.\nCompare the quality of different data sets \nusing knowledge and skills acquired data \nanalysis techniques you learned in earlier \nstatistics work.\nDiscuss data information published in local \nand/or international media platforms (local \nand international TV stations, newspapers, \njournals, etc.) by making useful inferences \nfrom the data to conclude and/or to explain \nother phenomena.\nLevel 3 Assessment Mode: Project Work\nAssessment Strategy: Learners collect \nbivariate data from documents on \nelectronic media or print media, or \ntextbooks, and present their data using \nscatter plots and draw conclusions. \nCompare their data with that of other \ngroups and decide which of the data best \nrepresents a given situation.','aab29cd0096a6982662a16709cb5af8804b8a0535a1e9a127d905b6955c66a46','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(266,1,1,265,269,269,'245\nSection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nIdentify examples from print and electronic \nmedia, e.g., newspapers, television, \nthe Internet, where probability is used, \nand explain how the given probability \ninfluences individual decisions.\nLevel 3 Assessment Mode: Individual Research\nAssessment Strategy: Learners to \ninvestigate real-life situations from \nmedia outlets (internet/radio/print/\ninterview/etc) to find out the application \nof probability, and make predictions \nand provide an explanation on how they \ninfluence decisions.\nSolve everyday life problems involving \nthe probability of two dependent and \nindependent events, including addition \nand multiplication laws.\nLevel 3 Assessment Mode: Class Exercise\nAssessment Strategy: Provide standard \nWASSCE-style questions as a class \nexercise.','1d11d5519f7f13b553214f869d01ad3b2549f2b60e7157785ea83f74a262b418','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(267,1,1,266,270,270,'246\nSection 5: Data Handling & ProbabilitySection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nWEEK 16\nLearning Indicator: Establish simple mathematical relationships between two variables in \na given observational or experimental context; illustrate using scatter \ngraphs and use them to solve and/or pose problems\nFOCAL AREA: ESTABLISHING THE RELATIONSHIP \nBETWEEN TWO VARIABLES\nVariable\nIn statistics, a variable is a characteristic or measurement that can be determined for a population. \nVariables may describe values like weight in kg , height in metres, favourite food, satisfaction \nlevel, age, and gender.\nExample 1\nGiven the table below, identify the variables involved.\nTable 16.1: Expenditure versus number of cars sold\nExpenditure on advertisement (GHC) Number of cars sold\n25,000 25\n30,000 40\n35,000 43\n40,000 48\nSolution\nVariable 1: Expenditure on advertisement\nVariable 2: Number of cars sold.\nIndependent Variable\nThe independent variable is the one that is manipulated to see how it affects another variable. \nIt is also referred to as the Predictor or Explanatory variable.\nExample: Hours studied (predicts exam score), Price of an item (determines the quantity sold), \nTemperature (determines the amount of water consumed).\nUsually, the independent variable can be determined when used in a context.\nAsk yourself: “What is being changed, used, or controlled to see its effect?” That is the \nindependent variable.','e4fa7af9ac82c6a9293532a80eea4ed368273f86062d8ab00392d083ef1e6546','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(268,1,1,267,271,271,'247\nSection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nCharacteristics of the Independent Variable\n1. It is also known as an input, predictor, or explanatory variable.\n2. It is the cause or factor being tested.\n3. It does not depend on other variables in the analysis.\n4. In a graph, it is usually displayed on the x-axis.\nDependent Variable\nThe dependent variable is the variable that changes as a result of changes in the independent \nvariable. It is also referred to as the Outcome or Response variable.\nExample: Exam scores (outcome of hours of study), Expenditure (determined by the income), \nAmount of water consumed (determined by the temperature).\nUsually, the dependent variable can be determined when used in a context.\nAsk yourself: “What changes because of what I did or something that was done?” That’s the \ndependent variable.\nCharacteristics Of The Dependent Variable\n1. It is also known as an output, response, or measured variable.\n2. It is what the researcher is trying to explain, predict, or observe.\n3. It is NOT directly controlled. It shows a response to some other change.\n4. In a graph, it is usually displayed on the y-axis.\nExample 2\nState the independent and dependent variables in the following research scenarios:\na. Capacity and sizes of bottles\nb. Speed and Distance Travelled\nc. Effect of study time and test scores\nd. Effect of temperature and sales of ice cream\ne. Area of plane shapes and their dimensions.\nSolution\na. Capacity and sizes of bottles\nIndependent Variable = size of the bottles\nDependent Variable = Capacity of the bottles\nReason: The capacity is dependent on the size of the bottles','514b879f0a87a8e5249606af8b53e587f25fab30a037130f2f10fc92138a592f','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(269,1,1,268,272,272,'248\nSection 5: Data Handling & ProbabilitySection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nb. Speed and Distance Travelled\nIndependent variable = speed\nDependent variable = distance travelled.\nReason: The distance travelled is dependent on the speed of the car.\nc. Effect of study time and test scores\nIndependent variable = Study time\nDependent variable = Test scores\nReason: Your test scores are usually dependent on the amount of time you study.\nd. Effect of temperature and sales of ice cream\nIndependent variable = temperature\nDependent variable = Sales of ice cream\nReason: Sales of ice cream are usually dependent on the temperature of the weather.\ne. Area of plane shapes and their dimensions.\nIndependent variable = dimensions of the plane shape\nDependent variable = Area of the plane shape\nReason: Without the dimensions, you cannot find the area.\nUnivariate Data\nThe word “Uni” means “one,” and “variate” is another word for “variable.” Therefore, \n“univariate data” refers to data with only one variable or a single characteristic. Examples \ninclude the daily temperature in Accra, exam scores for a subject, the weight of babies at birth, \nand the wages of teachers.\nThe table below shows an example of univariate data. It represents the outcome of a survey in \nwhich a sample of learners at Diaso SHS were asked to give their preferred holiday destination.\nTable 16.2: Preferred holiday destination of students\nHoliday Destination Number of students\nFrance 32\nSouth Africa 87\nKenya 43\nEgypt 107\nSaudi Arabia 126\nChina 63\nThe only variable in this data is ‘holiday destination’','d480aacbf58ff3f674b236357027dc11ae907953133e4f947adaa694dc8ba18c','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(270,1,1,269,273,273,'249\nSection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nYou can describe patterns in univariate data using central tendencies (mean, mode, and median) \nand dispersion (range, interquartile range, standard deviation, variance)\nFrequency distribution tables, bar charts, histograms, pie charts, and frequency polygons can \nbe used to present univariate data.\nBivariate Data\n“Bi” means “two”. So, bivariate data” means data that involves exactly two variables. Therefore, \nbivariate data involves two variables that are measured or observed for the same subject or \nevent. The goal is to understand whether and how the two variables are related.\nThe table shows an example of bivariate data. It gives the income and expenditure of eight \ncocoa farmers.\nTable 16.3: Bivariate data of income versus expenditure\nName Naa Nimo Atsu Bushra Yaa Yakoba Yaw Mbo\nIncome 8,00011,2009420 13800 7300 6000 10900 11300\nExpenditure 4700 7500 6500 5200 5100 4800 9200 10100\nThis data contains two variables, income and expenditure.\nExample 3\nAsk learners to give five examples of bivariate data.\nSolution\nExamples could include:\na. hours worked and wages earned,\nb. years of education and annual income,\nc. amount of rainfall and maize yield,\nd. alcohol consumption and cholesterol level\ne. age of cancer patients and length of survival\nDifferences between Univariate and Bivariate data\nTable 16.4: Distinguishing features of univariate and bivariate data\nUnivariate data Bivariate data\nInvolves a single variable Involves two variables\nDoes not deal with cause and relationship Deals with cause and relationship\nThe purpose is to describe The purpose is to explain\nDoes not have any dependent variable Contains only one dependent variable','1b4253201efe05354be052d9e9b8d325631826adf05e30b7426e32f02ac92cff','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(271,1,1,270,274,274,'250\nSection 5: Data Handling & ProbabilitySection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nTo PRESENT bivariate data, we can use a scatter plot (also known as a scatter diagram, or \nscatter graph). It allows us to visualise the relationship between the two variables.\nScatter Plot\nA scatter plot shows how two variables are related. Each point on the graph represents a pair \nof values. Find below an example of a scatter plot. It shows the relationship between daily \ntemperature and the number of people who visit the beach.\nTable 16.5: Table showing average daily temperature and the corresponding number of visitors to the beach\nAverage Daily \nTemp/\n0\nC\n24.525 25.526 26.527 27 27.528 28.529\nNumber of visitors60 100200100150180250350310330400\nFigure 16.1: Scatter plot of beach visitors\nInterpreting Graphs of Bivariate Data\nWhen analysing bivariate data using graphs of scatter plots, the goal is to understand the \nrelationship between the two variables. To do this, we either look at the directions of the \nrelationship, the form of the relationship, or the strength of the relationship. Here, we will \nconcentrate on the direction of the relationship.\nDirection of the Relationship\nThe direction of the relationship could either be\n1. Positive correlation: As one variable increases, the other also increases\n2. (e.g., Hours studied vs. Test score)','fb1def7f0b679c69dc44f2279da61b8c5a81202a084b07208b54f8e607383ccf','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(272,1,1,271,275,275,'251\nSection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nFigure 16.2: A positive correlation Figure 16.3: A positive perfect correlation\n3. Negative correlation: As one variable increases, the other decreases\n4. (e.g., Alcohol consumption vs. Health level)\nFigure 16.4: A negative correlation Figure 16.5: A negative perfect correlation\n5. No correlation: No clear pattern or relationship\n6. (e.g., Shoe size vs. Intelligence)\nFigure 16.6: A no correlation scatter plot','5fdfb707f173549d1b06da2bce1598644a4b52e987fe88f5d76730b20b870d2a','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(273,1,1,272,276,276,'252\nSection 5: Data Handling & ProbabilitySection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nExample 4\nThe tables show the outcomes of the data collected.\n(a) Table 16.6: Number of sides versus size of interior angle\nNumber of sides 5 10 12 13 14 15\nSize of the interior angle108 144 150 152.3 154.3 156\n(b) Table 16.7: Mass of a sample of bags\nMass (kg) 63 64 65 66 67 68\nNumber of bags 5 8 3 20 12 2\n(c) Table 16.8: Number of workers and the time used to complete a task.\nNumber of workers 1 30 45 50 55 60\nCompletion time (days) 160 240 360 400 440 500\n1. Which of the tables shows bivariate data?\n2. State the independent and dependent variables. Justify your response.\nSolution\nTable 16.6 shows bivariate data.\n• Independent variable = Number of sides\n• Dependent variable = Size of interior angles\n• The number of sides of a polygon determines the size of the interior angle.\n• There is a positive correlation between the number of sides and the size of the interior \nangle of a regular polygon. The higher the number of sides, the larger the interior angle.\nTable 16.7 shows univariate data.\n• There is no relationship between the mass of bags and the number of bags.\nTable 16.8 shows bivariate data.\n• Independent variable = number of workers\n• Dependent variable = completion time\n• The completion time is dependent on the number of workers.\n• There is a negative correlation between the number of workers and the completion time. \nThe higher the number of workers, the lower the completion time.','fdc66e08be6d65f709e60b447dddc170e90f97bb2e8c73fb69fc8d49bb00edcf','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(274,1,1,273,277,277,'253\nSection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nHow to Construct A Scatter Plot Manually\nFollow the steps below\nStep 1: Identify the variables. Since we are dealing with bivariate data, it will involve two \nvariables, an independent variable and a dependent variable.\nStep 2: Label the axes and scale them, putting the independent variable on the x axis and the \ndependent variable on the y axis.\nStep 3: Convert each data point into (x, y) coordinates and plot them on the graph. If two or \nmore points fall on the same point, place them side-by-side.\nExample 5\nThe data below shows the temperature of the day and the number of people wearing jackets.\nTable 16.9: Temperature versus number of people wearing jacket.\nTemperature (x) 35 6 17 15 21 20 10 7\nNumber wearing jackets (y)11 4522 30 20 25 32 42\na. Draw a scatter plot for the data.\nb. Interpret your graph\nSolution\n1. Steps involved in drawing a scatter diagram\na. Draw two perpendicular lines to form the x-y plane on a sheet of graph paper.\nb. Choose an appropriate scale for the axes (x-axis representing temperature, the \nindependent variable, and y-axis representing number wearing jackets, the dependent \nvariable)\nc. The ordered pairs (35, 11), (6, 45), (17, 22), (15, 30), (21, 20), (20, 25), (10, 32) and (7, \n42) are plotted in the x-y plane.\nFigure 16.7: Scatter plot of temperature and number of people wearing jackets','b927cb4207737655a8f604af153a4413f254f73c9c807dff2b9826908670561d','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(275,1,1,274,278,278,'254\nSection 5: Data Handling & ProbabilitySection 5: Data Handling & Probability\n2. There is a clear negative correlation shown in the graph, with fewer people wearing jackets \nat higher temperatures and more wearing jackets at lower temperatures.\nHow to Construct A Scatter Diagram Using Excel\nStep 1: Open a new document in the Excel spreadsheet. A section of the interface should look \nlike the picture below\nFigure 16.8: Excel spreadsheet page\nStep 2: Input your data in the first and second columns of the spreadsheet. As an example, let’s \ninput the data in Table 16.9\nTable 16.10: Temperature versus number of people wearing jacket.\nTemperature (x) 35 6 17 15 21 20 10 7\nNumber wearing jackets (y)11 45 22 30 20 25 32 42\nYour spreadsheet should be similar to the one below\nFigure 16.10: Excel spreadsheet page showing data of temperature and number of people wearing jackets.\nStep 3: Select the data, go to Insert , and choose Scatter from the drop-down menu.','9e52c310fdd1e2139fbb9e469acba0776bd3e3307cb45cb0f6318efebc33d880','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(276,1,1,275,279,279,'255\nSection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nFigure 16.10: Excel spreadsheet page showing bivariate data and a scatter plot of temperature and number \nof people wearing jackets.\nStep 4: Insert the title and the description of the x and y axes. To do this, click on the plus sign \nbeside the scatter graph. This will open a dropdown menu titled Chart Elements. Choose “Axis \nTitles” from the dropdown menu.\nFigure 16.11: Excel spreadsheet page showing bivariate data and a scatter plot','71b1803f8e9850be6faa5120abb10d55db7469592064f5ad786ca22395ba53d1','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(277,1,1,276,280,280,'256\nSection 5: Data Handling & ProbabilitySection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nStep 5: Your final plot should be similar to the chart below.\n\n273\n\nFigure 16.11: Excel spreadsheet page showing bivariate data and a scatter plot \n<BodySL>Step 5 \nYour final plot should be similar to the chart below.\n\nFigure 16.12: Scatter plot of temperature and number of people wearing jackets.\n\n<TH>Learning Tasks \nGuide learners in applying their knowledge of plotting points by creating scatter plots. Help them \ndistinguish between univariate and bivariate data, as well as independent and dependent events, \nusing relevant real-life examples. After creating the scatter plots, assist them in identifying the \n0\n5\n10\n15\n20\n25\n30\n35\n40\n45\n50\n0 5 10 15 20 25 30 35 40\nNumber of people wearing Jackets\nTemperature\nScatter plot of Temperature and number of people \nwearing jackets\nFigure 16.12: Scatter plot of temperature and number of people wearing jackets.\nLearning Tasks\nGuide learners in applying their knowledge of plotting points by creating scatter plots. \nHelp them distinguish between univariate and bivariate data, as well as independent \nand dependent events, using relevant real-life examples. After creating the scatter \nplots, assist them in identifying the relationship between the variables. Ensure those \nwho face difficulties receive the necessary support.\nPEDAGOGICAL EXEMPLARS\nThe aim of this week’s lessons is for all learners to establish the relationship between two \nvariables. The following pedagogical approach is suggested for facilitators to guide learners \nthrough.\n1. Reflection: Conduct a review of learners’ prior knowledge concerning data types, as \nwell as methods for data collection and organisation.\n2. Collaborative Learning (Think-Pair-Share): Engage learners in a think-pair-share \nactivity to help them distinguish between dependent and independent variables within a \nbivariate data set.\n3. Project-Based Learning: Assign group tasks where learners collect data relevant to \ntheir interests, either from the school environment or beyond, making use of social media \nor other technological platforms for data gathering.\n4. Experiential Learning: Provide direct guidance to learners as they apply their knowledge \nto draw scatter plots for their bivariate data.','95ecaf8f1e109e97620ac382325576738e735baa6dbcc0a9e193415b4223f1fc','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(278,1,1,277,281,281,'257\nSection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nKEY ASSESSMENT LEVEL 3: GROUP WORK & PRESENTATIONS\nProject: Finding the relationship between two variables using a scatter plot.\nProject Goal: To create and interpret scatter plots and to analyse the relationship between two \nquantitative variables, identify different types of correlations, and draw conclusions from data.\nQuestion\nSelect a real-world topic involving two variables, gather or find data for these related variables, \ncreate a scatter plot, and then analyse the correlation (or absence of one) between them. Present \nyour findings and discuss the implications of your observations.\nThe materials you will need include:\n• Graph paper or graphing software (e.g., GeoGebra, Microsoft Excel, Google Sheets)\n• Rulers\n• Calculators (optional)\n• Access to the internet for data collection (optional)\n• Presentation tools (e.g., poster board, Google Slides, PowerPoint)\nProject Steps\nPhase 1: Brainstorming and Data Selection (Day 1)\na. Introduction to Scatter Plots: In your groups, review what scatter plots are, their \npurpose, and the concept of correlation (positive, negative, no correlation).\nb. Brainstorming Variables: As a small group, brainstorm pairs of quantitative variables \nthat might have a relationship and are easily assessable. For example, you may choose \none of the examples listed below.\n• Hours studied and test scores in a particular subject.\n• Daily temperature and water sales.\n• Height of a student and his/her Shoe size\n• Population of regional capitals in Ghana and the number of JHS.\n• Distance from school and Travel time\n• Mid-term and end-of-term scores.\n• Weight of a student and his/her height.\nc. Topic Selection: Choose ONE pair of variables you are interested in investigating. \nClearly define your independent (x-axis) and dependent (y-axis) variables.\nd. Data Collection Strategy\n• Collect at least 20 data points for your chosen variables.','1bcb8922cd14d567432767d5468b20befb0b49411183c985d6307b8c22127872','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(279,1,1,278,282,282,'258\nSection 5: Data Handling & ProbabilitySection 5: Data Handling & Probability\n• Option A: Self-Collected Data: Collect your own data (e.g., surveying classmates, \nmeasuring physical attributes, etc).\n• Option B: Found Data: You can research data online from reliable sources (e.g., \ngovernment statistics websites, sports statistics, scientific studies, reputable news \norganisations). You must cite your sources.\n• Option C: Teacher-Provided Data: You may ask a teacher or a parent to provide \nyou a data.\nPhase 2: Creating the Scatter Plot (Day 2)\na. Organising Data: Organise your collected data in a table with two columns (one for \neach variable).\nb. Choosing a Method: Decide whether to create the scatter plot by hand on graph paper \nor using technology.\n• By Hand: Provide the scale used for each axis, label the axes, and give the graph a \nclear title.\n• Using Technology: Use an Excel Spreadsheet or any appropriate software.\nPhase 3: Analysis and Interpretation (Day 3-4)\na. Observing the Scatter Plot: Analyse your generated scatter plot.\nb. Addressing Key Questions: Use your scatter plot to answer the following questions\n• What is your independent variable (x-axis)? What are its units?\n• What is your dependent variable (y-axis)? What are its units?\n• Describe the overall pattern you observe in the scatter plot.\n• Does there appear to be a correlation between your two variables? If so, what type \n(positive, negative, or no correlation)?\n• How strong does the correlation appear to be (strong, moderate, weak)? Explain your \nreasoning.\n• What conclusions can you draw about the relationship between your two variables \nbased on your scatter plot?\n• Does correlation imply causation? Explain this concept in the context of your data.\n• What are some limitations of your data collection or analysis?\n• If you were to continue this study, what would you do differently, or what other \nvariables might you consider?\nPhase 4: Presentation (Day 5)\na. Prepare Presentation: Prepare a presentation (e.g., poster, slide show, written report) \nthat includes:','ab44fab2ee91ca6c3312ce76c3a537dbee880e3346171546ba587f2b05b5d1e0','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(280,1,1,279,283,283,'259\nSection 5: Data Handling & Probability\n• Project Title\n• Names of group members/students\n• A brief introduction of the chosen variables and the research question.\n• A displayed data table.\n• Your scatter plot (large and clear).\n• Answers to all analysis questions from Phase 3.\n• A concluding summary of your findings.\n• State the sources you used for the data and sources of your data, research materials \n(if any).\n• Provide a brief statement acknowledging any individual or institution other than your \ngroup members and your teacher who contributed to the successful completion of \nyour project. (Optional)\n• Example: We’d like to express our sincere gratitude to [Name of Individual or \ninstitution], whose insights and support were invaluable to the successful completion \nof this project.\nb. Presentation: You will present your project to your class and answer any questions your \nclassmates ask.\nPLC Session 16\n45 minutes Planning & Reviewing the Learning Plan\n1. Begin the session by recapping lessons from the previous year/session/week, focusing on \nhow learning was applied.\n2. Use your subject-specific app to explore the content before planning. Ask the app to\na. explain the week’s concepts in a practical way that will make it easier for learners to \nunderstand\nb. identify two or three common misconceptions learners may have on the week’s content \nand how to address them using contexts familiar to Ghanaian learners. (NTS 3a, 3g \nand 3m).\n3. Use your subject-specific app to plan your lesson. Ask the app to\na. suggest the most effective method for teaching the week’s concepts to learners with \ndiverse backgrounds.\nb. suggest learning activities, differentiation and essential questions based on the method \nof teaching (NTS 3a-3q)\nc. review your prepared learning plan and address any challenging issues you may have \nidentified in your subject area (NTS 3a-3c).','e40be228522413719cdbc6c4fa2b10af091bb45cedf4268fee180ba2f60efab8','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(281,1,1,280,284,284,'260\nSection 5: Data Handling & ProbabilitySection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nNote\n• Take note of the recap topics linked to Years 1 and 2 while preparing for your \nlesson.\n• Refer to the Values Learning Community Handbook and link the values for the \nweek in your lesson to ensure that learners are applying what they learn.\n4. Plan the week’s key assessment with your app\na. The key assessment for the week is group work & presentation . Chat with your app \nto plan this assessment, indicating what you and your learners will do before, during \nand after the presentation (NTS 3k-3q).\n10 minutes Content Exploration\n5. As a subject group, select one problem or idea/term in the week’s content that is challenging \n(not a routine procedural question, but one that requires conceptual understanding) and \nthen\na. use the app, ask it to solve the problem step by step and explain the reasoning behind \neach step (you may be assisted by a teacher who is strong in the content area)\nb. all teachers in the group work through a similar problem individually for 3–4 minutes\nc. the group then works through the problem together step by step\nd. highlight the most common errors made at each step and discuss (NTS 1c, 1e, 2c, 2e, \n3e-3j).\nNote\n• This part is not just about ‘How do I teach this?’ but how do I teach it for learners \nto understand?\n• If the group still shares the same content gap, use the app: ask it to solve the \nproblem step by step and explain the reasoning behind each step.\n• Problems/ideas discussed should be sourced from the Teacher Manual content, not \ninvented separately. This keeps the content exploration directly connected to what \nteachers will deliver.\n30 minutes Enactment\n6. Enact a teaching activity based on your learning plan (NTS la-1b, 1e-lg, 2c, 2e, 2f, 3c-3j \nand 3o).\na. Colleagues observe with specific attention to clarity of concept explanation, use of \nfamiliar Ghanaian examples and how misconceptions are handled if they arise.','8f3b5d832607f51c934e734326989709ce1ec61bef5c6031456d836d6aa0f6d8','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(282,1,1,281,285,285,'261\nSection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nb. After the enactment, ask for structured feedback from the group\ni. was the concept explained in a way that a learner with no prior knowledge could \nfollow?\nii. were the examples grounded in contexts familiar to Ghanaian learners?\niii. did any misconceptions surface and if so, how were they addressed?\n5 minutes Reflection\n7. Reflect and share your views on the session. One thing that worked well, what did not work \nwell and what you will do differently. (NTS 1a, 1b).\n8. Identify a critical friend to observe and provide feedback on your lesson in relation to the \nPLC Session conducted (NTS 1f, 1g).\n9. Remember to\na. transfer your chats into your learning planner template.\nb. read your Teacher Manual and related Learner Material in preparation for the next \nsession. Take note of the recap weeks, which are linked to Years 1 and 2 (NTS 3a).\nc. integrate National Values, GESI, SEL, and 21st century skills into your learning \nactivities, assessment tasks, and rubrics/mark schemes.','792e42ca16ee78b8af576088e08d7725f2d51646b60c953a75cd491a7e1483af','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(283,1,1,282,286,286,'262\nSection 5: Data Handling & ProbabilitySection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nWEEK 17\nLearning Indicator: Collect data from an experimental study in which the interest is based \non a treatment and a non-treatment (also known as a control) group. \nIllustrate the data using scatter graphs and find the relationship \nbetween the variables, if any\nFOCAL AREA: DATA COLLECTION ON SIMPLE \nEXPERIMENTS\nWhat is an Experimental Study?\nAn experimental study is a method of collecting data by intentionally changing one variable \n(called the independent variable) to observe how it influences another variable (called the \ndependent variable). In an experimental study, the researcher controls or manipulates one \nvariable and measures the outcome of another.\nExamples\nTable 17.1: Examples of experimental study.\nExperiment Topic Independent \nVariable\nDependent \nVariable\nVariable that a \nresearcher may \nchoose to control\nDropping a ball to find out the \nnumber of times it bounces.\nDrop Height Number of \nbounces.\nBall type, surface, \ndrop height.\nFinding out how different \namounts of sunlight affect plant \ngrowth\nHours of \nSunlight\nPlant Height Water, soil, and plant \ntype\nHow study time affects test scoresStudy Time Test Score Test type, room \nconditions\nHow temperature of water affect \ndissolving time?\nWater \nTemperature\nDissolving Time Amount of sugar, \nvolume of water\nHow the mass applied to a rubber \nband affects the stretch.\nMass Applied Length StretchedRubber band type, air \ntemperature\nStructure of an Experimental Study\nThe structure of an experimental study includes.\n• Independent Variable: The variable you change.\n• Dependent Variable: The variable you measure.\n• Controlled Condition(s): Factor(s) kept constant for fairness.\n• Repeated Trials: Performing an activity multiple times for accuracy.','5ac9aa918138d16b980a05ef30980cf97a421b87fc9c9c1c6e898ca494a5eb16','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(284,1,1,283,287,287,'263\nSection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nHow to Conduct an Experimental Study\nStep 1: Identify the Research Question\nAsk a clear question about the relationship between two variables.\nStep 2: Identify the Variables\nStep 3: Plan the Experiment\nDecide on the materials and tools you need, the number of trials you will undertake, \nand the number of times you will repeat each trial for accuracy (repeat each trial at least \n3 times and take the average for accuracy). Also, decide what must be kept constant to \nmake the experiment fair.\nStep 4: Collect the Data\nExperiment as planned and record the data in a table.\nStep 5: Present the data using a scatter plot.\nStep 6: Interpret the pattern you observe.\nLet us use the example below to enhance our understanding of how to conduct a simple \nexperimental study.\nExample 1\nResearch Question\nInvestigating the Relationship Between Drop Height and Bounce Height of a Ball.\nSample Solution\nLet’s first define the variables in the research question.\nIndependent variable = Drop height (Determined by the height from which to drop the ball)\nDependent variable = Bounce height (No control over the highest point the bounce will reach)\nSo, in this research, we will control the height from which we will drop the ball, the type of \nfloor we want to drop the ball on, the type of ball we want to drop, and maybe the air temperature.\nWhen a ball is dropped on a hard surface, all things being equal, it will bounce more than once. \nWe will be only interested in the first bounce, as this will be the highest bounce.\nLet’s use the steps to answer the question\nStep 1: Identify the Research Question\nHow does the height from which a ball is dropped affect how high it bounces?\nStep 2: Identify the Variables\nIndependent Variable = drop height\nDependent Variable = bounce height','1f4c798ce98936c058531518899cb351a2ae8d270b6817efb965ce595ac55ebe','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(285,1,1,284,288,288,'264\nSection 5: Data Handling & ProbabilitySection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nStep 3: Plan the Experiment\nMaterials\nA rubber ball, a metre rule, a flat hard surface, and a notebook for recording data\nTo simplify this example, we will undertake 7 trials. We will drop the ball in intervals \nof 30cm and repeat each trial three times, and take the average bounce height.\nStep 4: Collect the Data\nProcedure\n1. Drop the ball from different heights (30cm, 60cm, 90cm, 120cm, 150cm, 180cm, \n210cm).\n2. Measure the bounce height each time using a ruler.\n3. Repeat each measurement 3 times and record the average.\n4. Record all results in a table.\nTable 17.2: Drop height versus average bounce height data.\nDrop \nHeight(cm)\nBounce Height (cm) Average \nbounce \nheight.\nFirst \ntrial\nSecond \ntrial\nThird \ntrial\n30 19 17 18 18\n60 39 42 39 40\n90 58 59 60 59\n120 72 72 72 72\n150 83 86 83 84\n180 103 103 109 105\n210 146 144 142 144\nStep 5: Present the data using a scatter plot. Don’t forget to:\na. Label the x-axis as Drop Height (independent variable).\nb. Label the y-axis as Bounce Height (dependent variable).\nc. Plot the data points from your table.\nd. Look for a trend or pattern.','6dcb35a6c49090da71eeadfd665046ed2c4e926cf7b369f93686d52b9ad6942f','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(286,1,1,285,289,289,'265\nSection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nFigure 17.1: Scatter plot of drop height and average bounce height\nStep 6: Interpreting the Scatter Plot\nFrom the graph, we can observe:\na. As the drop height increases, the bounce height also increases.\nb. This shows a positive correlation.\nc. A line of best fit can help identify the relationship and predict unknown values. A \nline of best fit should only be drawn if there is a negative or positive correlation. \nOne should never be drawn if there is no correlation. The line should have roughly \nequal numbers of points on either side of it, and does not need to go through any of \nthe points. Readings can then be taken from this line to make estimates for other \nvalues.\nUsing the Graph to Solve Problems\nProblem: If a ball is dropped from 70 cm, what is the expected bounce height?\nSolution\nFigure 17.2: Making inferences based on the scatter plot','54541fc5a889f6529558321e2780eb286ef4bce80eccd1e38f4585e5807492b1','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(287,1,1,286,290,290,'266\nSection 5: Data Handling & ProbabilitySection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nFrom the graph, we can read up from 70cm drop height until we reach the line of best fit and \nthen read across to the bounce height. From this we can see that the expected bounce height is \n42cm\nLet us now turn our attention to an observational study\nObservational study\nAn observational study is a method of collecting data by watching and recording what \nhappens in real-life situations without interfering with or manipulating any variables.\nIn mathematics, particularly in data analysis, we employ observational studies to investigate \nrelationships between two variables in a natural setting. In an observational study, the researcher \ndoes not control or manipulate any of the variables; they only observe and record.\nExamples of Observational Studies\nTable 17.3: Examples of observational study\nObservation Context Variable 1 Variable 2\nObserving people at a bus stop Time of day Number of people waiting\nMonitoring weather and clothing \nchoices\nTemperature (°C) Number of people in \njackets\nWatching students in the library Time spent reading \n(mins)\nNumber of books \nborrowed\nObserving a football field Minutes played Number of goals scored\nHow to Conduct an Observational Study\nSteps\n1. Choose two variables you want to study.\n2. Decide when and where you will observe (e.g., during break time in the school library).\n3. Use a table to collect data at regular intervals.\n4. Draw a scatter plot to visualise the relationship.\n5. Interpret the pattern you observe.\nExample 1\nA Math and a Physics test are administered to 10 learners in SHS 1. Each learner takes  both \ntests during the same week. The ordered pairs below represent each learner’s scores in the \ntwo tests. The data is plotted as a scatter plot to explore the relationship between  Math and \nPhysics test scores.','d3b26bafce9a318a9ffe0117d9d4fc90d73a39d321b70f1b8882b0077cb5f112','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(288,1,1,287,291,291,'267\nSection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nLearner Scores\nLearner Math ScorePhysics \nScore\nA 20 30\nB 30 40\nC 30 35\nD 40 50\nE 46 60\nF 50 60\nG 56 70\nH 60 76\nI 60 70\nJ 70 86\nTask\n• Plot the Math scores on the x-axis and Physics scores on the y-axis .\n• Draw and label the scatter plot.\n• Identify the type of correlation.\n• Interpret the trend.\n• Draw a conclusion.\nSolution\nDistribution Table for the Graph\nMath Scores (x) Physics Scores (y)\n20 30\n30 40\n30 35\n40 50\n46 60\n50 60\n56 70\n60 76\n60 70\n70 86','1e57daa7ca651543dad26b98e3b1a7d2dd39366edf15819421dd53ef09e65ac9','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(289,1,1,288,292,292,'268\nSection 5: Data Handling & ProbabilitySection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nGraph of the relation\nFigure 17.3: Scatter plot\nObservation\n• As Math scores increase, Physics scores also generally increase.\n• The points show a pattern trending upwards , even if not perfectly aligned.\nInterpretation\n• Learners who performed better in Math also tended to perform better in Physics.\n• This suggests a positive correlation between performance in Math and Physics.\nConclusion\nThere is a  moderate to strong positive correlation  between Math and Physics scores. Learners \nwith higher Math scores generally had higher Physics scores. This supports the idea that \nstrengths in one subject may be associated with strengths in another related subject.\nLearning Tasks\nReview learners’ prior knowledge of data collection methods. Support them in \napplying these methods to gather data through simple experimental activities. Guide \nlearners step-by-step through the process of conducting an experimental study. \nAssist them in using scatter plots to represent their collected data and identify simple \nmathematical relationships between the variables involved. Help learners interpret \npatterns and correlations in their scatter plots. Encourage them to use the graphs to \nsolve real-life problems and to pose meaningful questions based on their findings.','9d3cbb1de000b26f62a532ca17eed83a5d60eda2d496bbd7bb3b01230b011ebb','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(290,1,1,289,293,293,'269\nSection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nPEDAGOGICAL EXEMPLARS\nThe aim of this week’s lessons is for all learners to establish the relationship between two \nvariables in an observational or experimental study. The following pedagogical approach is \nsuggested for facilitators to guide learners through.\n1. Reflection: Begin with a review of learners’ prior knowledge on bivariate data and \nvarious data collection methods. This sets the foundation for a deeper understanding.\n2. Collaborative Learning (Think-Pair-Share): Engage learners in a think-pair-share \nactivity to help them differentiate between observational and experimental studies within \nthe context of bivariate data.\n3. Project-Based Learning: Organise learners into groups to carry out a small project. \nEach group should collect either observational or experimental data from their \nsurroundings and present their findings using a scatter plot.\n4. Experiential Learning: Guide learners to apply their understanding of correlation by \ninterpreting their scatter plots and identifying the type of relationship between the two \nvariables in their study.\nKEY ASSESSMENT LEVEL 3: GROUP WORK\n1. The graph below shows bivariate data (Height versus Age) of a sample of SHS students.\n\n287\n\nFigure 17.4: Scatter plot of age and height of SHS students. \na. Propose an appropriate title for the graph (1 mark) \nb. Analyse the trend on the graph (3 marks) \nc. In what ways can this inform the Parent-Teacher Association of your school in \nsupporting teaching and learning? \nGive 3 examples of how the information could be used. (3 marks) \nTotal 7 marks\n\n2. Table 17.6 shows the regional percentage distribution of children, 5-17 years, who worked \nfor 15 hours and above per week and school attendance in Ghana.\n\nTable 17.6: Children who worked for at least 15 hours and school attendance. \nRegion Percentage of children \nwho work for 15 hours \nor more \nPercentage of \nlearners attending \nschool \nWestern 61 13 \nCentral 74 12 \nGreater Accra 35 24 \nVolta 41 20 \nEastern 71 10 \nAshanti 67 10 \nBrong Ahafo 59 18 \nNorthern 33 22 \nUpper East 39 21 \nUpper West 28 25 \nFigure 17.4: Scatter plot of age and height of SHS students.\na. Propose an appropriate title for the graph (1 mark)\nb. Analyse the trend on the graph (3 marks)\nc. In what ways can this inform the Parent-Teacher Association of your school in \nsupporting teaching and learning? Give 3 examples of how the information could \nbe used. ( 3 marks)\nTotal 7 marks','854297d997b1fde1d5a4d6c8d65e9875dd00123b8191e93d57b640d3cea3461a','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(291,1,1,290,294,294,'270\nSection 5: Data Handling & ProbabilitySection 5: Data Handling & Probability\n2. Table 17.6 shows the regional percentage distribution of children, 5-17 years, who worked \nfor 15 hours and above per week and school attendance in Ghana.\nTable 17.6: Children who worked for at least 15 hours and school attendance.\nRegion Percentage of \nchildren who work for \n15 hours or more\nPercentage of \nlearners attending \nschool\nWestern 61 13\nCentral 74 12\nGreater Accra 35 24\nVolta 41 20\nEastern 71 10\nAshanti 67 10\nBrong Ahafo 59 18\nNorthern 33 22\nUpper East 39 21\nUpper West 28 25\na. Show this information on a scatter plot. (4 marks)\nb. Describe the trends in the plot. (2 marks)\nc. Propose any 2 suggestions to stakeholders based on the trends you identified. \n( 2 marks)\nTotal 8 marks\nPLC Session 17\n45 minutes Planning & Reviewing the Learning Plan\n1. Begin the session by recapping lessons from the previous year/session/week, focusing on \nhow learning was applied.\n2. Use your subject specific app to explore the content before planning. Ask the app to\na. explain the week’s concepts in a practical way that will make it easier for learners to \nunderstand\nb. identify two or three common misconceptions learners may have on the week’s content \nand how to address them using contexts familiar to Ghanaian learners. (NTS 3a, 3g \nand 3m).\n3. Use your subject specific app to plan your lesson. Ask the app to\na. suggest the most effective method for teaching the week’s concepts to learners with \ndiverse backgrounds.','92c6fa8b952aa04cb25672e5d69f52f50ca3e7e303b36bb6d2c058eb8c39400e','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(292,1,1,291,295,295,'271\nSection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nb. suggest learning activities, differentiation and essential questions based on the method \nof teaching (NTS 3a-3q)\nc. review your prepared learning plan and address any challenging issues you may have \nidentified in your subject area (NTS 3a-3c).\nNote\n• Take note of the recap topics linked to Years 1 and 2 while preparing for your \nlesson.\n• Refer to the Values Learning Community Handbook and link the values for the \nweek in your lesson to ensure that learners are applying what they learn.\n4. Plan the week’s key assessment with your app\na. The key assessment for the week is group work. Chat with your app to plan this \nassessment, indicating what you and your learners will do before, during and after the \ngroup work (NTS 3k-3q).\n10 minutes Content Exploration\n5. As a subject group, select one problem or idea/term in the week’s content that is challenging \n(not a routine procedural question, but one that requires conceptual understanding) and \nthen\na. use the app, ask it to solve the problem step by step and explain the reasoning behind \neach step (you may be assisted by a teacher who is strong in the content area)\nb. all teachers in the group work through a similar problem individually for 3–4 minutes\nc. the group then works through the problem together step by step\nd. highlight the most common errors made at each step and discuss (NTS 1c, 1e, 2c, 2e, \n3e-3j).\nNote\n• This part is not just about ‘How do I teach this?’ but how do I teach it for learners \nto understand?\n• If the group still shares the same content gap, use the app: ask it to solve the \nproblem step by step and explain the reasoning behind each step.\n• Problems/ideas discussed should be sourced from the Teacher Manual content, not \ninvented separately. This keeps the content exploration directly connected to what \nteachers will deliver.','27ae4c21efb34026bbf42c23a92efa84da93eecbd23d5c8a11f630912ef5e96d','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(293,1,1,292,296,296,'272\nSection 5: Data Handling & ProbabilitySection 5: Data Handling & Probability\n30 minutes Enactment\n6. Enact a teaching activity based on your learning plan (NTS la-1b, 1e-lg, 2c, 2e, 2f, 3c-3j \nand 3o).\na. Colleagues observe with specific attention to clarity of concept explanation, use of \nfamiliar Ghanaian examples and how misconceptions are handled if they arise.\nb. After the enactment, ask for structured feedback from the group\ni. was the concept explained in a way that a learner with no prior knowledge could \nfollow?\nii. were the examples grounded in contexts familiar to Ghanaian learners?\niii. did any misconceptions surface and if so, how were they addressed?\n5 minutes Reflection\n7. Reflect and share your views on the session. One thing that worked well, what did not work \nwell and what you will do differently. (NTS 1a, 1b).\n8. Identify a critical friend to observe and provide feedback on your lesson in relation to the \nPLC Session conducted (NTS 1f, 1g).\n9. Remember to\na. transfer your chats into your learning planner template.\nb. read your Teacher Manual and related Learner Material in preparation for the next \nsession. Take note of the recap weeks, which are linked to Years 1 and 2 (NTS 3a).\nc. integrate National Values, GESI, SEL, and 21st century skills into your learning \nactivities, assessment tasks, and rubrics/mark schemes.','6a1514776e2d60ff8ea953d0ce4c603fd7627e84072281c1364fb70298b092d1','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(294,1,1,293,297,297,'273\nSection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nWEEK 18\nLearning Indicators\n1. Compare the quality of different data sets using knowledge and skills acquired from \ndata analysis techniques you learned in earlier statistics work\n2. Discuss data information published in local and/or international media platforms \n(local and international TV stations, newspapers, journals, etc.) by making useful \ninferences from the data to draw conclusions and/or to explain other phenomena\nFOCAL AREA 1: COMPARING DIFFERENT DATA SETS\nReview of Bivariate Data and Scatter Graphs\nBivariate Data\nBivariate data consists of pairs of values that show how two variables are related. The relationship \nbetween the variables can be positive, negative, or non-existent.\nScatter Graphs\nScatter graphs allow us to visualise the relationship between two variables. Each point represents \na pair of values.\n• If the points show an upward trend → positive correlation\n• If the points show a downward trend → negative correlation\n• If the points are scattered with no pattern → no correlation\nDescribing the relationship between two variables\nThe scatter plot helps us to visualise the relationship between two variables. An alternative way \nto describe the relationship is to use a linear function (a straight line). Note that this is used to \napproximate the relationship between the variables being compared, as the data points do not \nform a straight line in most cases. Hence, the approximated line is called a line of best fit . The \nline of best fit should either pass through most of the points or be closest to most of the points. \nBecause of this, the line of best fit is usually used to predict data values within the given data. \nThis is known as interpolation. When used to predict data values outside a given data, it is \ncalled extrapolation. Pairs of data values (points) relatively far from the line of best fit are \ncalled outliers. In this lesson, we will obtain the line of best fit through observation .\nComparing Two Bivariate Data Sets\nExample 1\nTwo sales representatives, Naa and Yaw, are being considered for a promotion. Their weekly \nperformance over 5 weeks is shown below.','e3d4fba141c48f60e9a206260049a127fc749f20ab495fde5fbf412f78d8684c','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(295,1,1,294,298,298,'274\nSection 5: Data Handling & ProbabilitySection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nTable 18.1: Naa’s sales performance\nHours worked 30 35 40 45 50\nSales made 12 13 16 19 20\nTable 18.2: Yaw’s sales performance\nHours worked 30 35 40 45 50\nSales made 15 16 17 17 18\nAnalysis and Interpretation\n1. Step 1: Plot the data for both Naa and Yaw using scatter graphs.\nFigure 18.1: Scatter plot of Naa’s sales \nperformance\nFigure 18.2: Scatter plot of Yaw’s sales \nperformance\n2. Step 2: Identify the type of relationship\nNaa: Shows a strong positive correlation – as hours increase, so do sales of bicycles.\nYaw: Shows a weak or no correlation – sales do not increase that much despite working \nmore hours.\n3. Step 3: Compare the trends and consistency\nNaa has a steady and consistent increase in sales with more work.\nYaw starts high, but the increase in sales is minimal, showing reduced efficiency with \nmore hours.\n4. Step 4: Conclusion\nNaa’s data better represents a productive relationship between work and results. The \nlinear growth in sales with effort justifies selecting Naa for a promotion.','cc3a283732bb636b1de71a4a8fba1de9c8b233de4dab3c5766a15c7c8fdf72d1','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(296,1,1,295,299,299,'275\nSection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nExample 2\nThe tables below present data on tree growth observed after applying two distinct fertiliser \nbrands: Surge and Boom\nTable 18.3: Tree growth using Surge fertiliser\nWeek 1 2 3 4 5\nHeight (mm) 75 100 100 145 155\nTable 18.4: Tree growth using Boom fertiliser\nWeek 1 2 3 4 5\nHeight (mm) 50 80 120 160 200\nWhich of the fertilisers will you recommend? Justify your choice.\n1. Step 1: Plot the data for both Surge fertiliser and Boom fertiliser using scatter graphs\nFigure 18.3: Scatter plot of tree growth using surge \nfertiliser\nFigure 18.4: Scatter plot of tree growth using boom \nfertiliser\n2. Step 2: Identify the type of relationship\nSurge: Shows a weak positive correlation – the growth of the trees is promising in the \nfirst week, but slows dramatically after that.\nBoom: Shows a strong positive correlation – it shows a consistent growth as the weeks \ngo by.\n3. Step 3: Compare the trends and consistency\n• Boom fertiliser shows steady growth as it is consistent and effective over time.\n• Surge fertiliser gives quick but limited growth since growth stalls early.','ff74d2c85d1182ee67a05de607d97c36115d84c0dbab86dde52c3959d59544a0','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(297,1,1,296,300,300,'276\nSection 5: Data Handling & ProbabilitySection 5: Data Handling & Probability\n4. Step 4: Conclusion\nBoom Fertiliser is more effective for long-term plant development.\nExample 3\nAs a speaker in a school debate on the topic: “The rise of HIV/AIDS cases in Ghana, a cause \nfor worry or a mere public scare,” gather data on HIV/AIDS cases from the Ghana AIDS \nCommission or other reputable sources (radio, TV, newspapers, etc.), analyse the data (including \nusing any suitable technological tools), and use charts or tables to support your arguments for \nor against the motion.\nSolution\nHIV DATA\nTable 18.5: HIV DATA\nMetric Value\nTotal PLHIV (2024 estimate) 334 721 people\nHIV prevalence (ages 15–49) 1.7%\nNew infections (2023) 17 774 (16,076 adults & 1,698 children)\nDaily new infections 50 per day (2025 report)\nAIDS-related deaths (2023) 12 614\nSource: (pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov, facebook.com)\n2019-FACT-SHEET-22-06-2020-revised11.pdf\n[Note: PLHIV = People Living with HIV]\nSupporting Chart\nFigure 18.5: Bar chart showing new HIV infections in Ghana (2023)','010ca1cbfac090fced4ccb6f110db9e3f20fdd1641e28f07aaf2e0192dc57478','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(298,1,1,297,301,301,'277\nSection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nA bar chart showing new HIV infections in Ghana for the year 2023. It shows that the majority \nof new HIV infections in Ghana in 2023 occurred in adults, but with a notable number still \naffecting children, emphasising that the rise in cases is a real cause for concern, not just a scare.\nPoints for Debate: “A Cause for Worry”\n1. Substantial HIV Burden Remains\n• Over 334 000 people live with HIV in Ghana as of 2024 (facebook.com).\n• Though prevalence has stabilized (1.6–1.7%), the number of people living with HIV is \ngrowing.\n2. Disturbingly High Daily New Infections\n• About 50 new cases per day in 2025 (facebook.com).\n• This indicates ongoing transmission and insufficient prevention.\n3. Child Infections: A Critical Gap\n• Nearly 1,700 new child infections in 2023 (3news.com).\n• Mother-to-child transmission remains a public health concern.\n4. Mortality Still High\n• Over 12 000 AIDS-related deaths in 2023 (3news.com, ghanawebbers.com).\n• Despite Antiretroviral Therapy (ART) advances, HIV/AIDS has not declined.\n5. Uneven Regional Burden\n• Urban and southern regions (e.g., Greater Accra, Ashanti) show higher prevalence \n(pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov).\n• Untargeted interventions may widen disparities.\nConclusion & Debate Standpoint\nThe data demonstrate a continued and concerning HIV burden in Ghana:\n• With 50 new cases daily, significant child infections, and thousands of deaths annually, \nHIV remains a serious health issue.\n• Although interventions have improved care and awareness, transmission remains active, \nespecially in key populations and children.\n• Therefore, the rise (or plateau) of HIV/AIDS cases should be considered a valid cause \nfor concern, not just public alarm.','a453d290bd53811ab7db577466528a57e620acf808bdcad58127402a962f5efb','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(299,1,1,298,302,302,'278\nSection 5: Data Handling & ProbabilitySection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nFOCAL AREA 2: INTERPRETING DATA PRESENTED ON \nMEDIA PLATFORMS\nWhat is Media-Reported Data?\nThese are data presented on media platforms. The media platforms include news websites, TV \nstations, Social media platforms (e.g., Facebook, TikTok, WhatsApp, YouTube, etc.) and Online \ndashboards (e.g., COVID-19 trackers)\nBivariate Data in the Media\nBivariate data consists of two related variables. In the media, these usually appear in the form \nof tables, scatter plots, bar or line graphs showing two quantities changing together and \nInfographics.\nHow to analyse bivariate data from a media platform\nAfter validating the data,\n1. Extract the data into a table of ordered pairs (x, y)\n2. Plot the data on a scatter graph\n3. Analyse the trend. Look out for a correlation between the variables. Do you find any \ntrend? Can we estimate or predict values using the trend? Answering these questions will \nhelp you analyse the data.\nExample 1\nScreen Time and Sleep\nAn online health article reported data collected from teens aged 15–18 on average daily screen \ntime and hours of sleep.\nTable 18.6: Screen time and sleep time\nScreen time(hrs) 3 6 8 5 1 2 1 6 4 7\nSleep time (hrs) 7.56.55.26.78.28 8.56.17 5.7\na. Present the data using a scatter plot.\nb. What kind of correlation exists between screen time and sleep duration?\nc. What might be the implication of this trend?\nd. Is this data likely observational or experimental? Explain your answer.','a79417283362a6d676bbf1e8576dbd2cc04432fa124bac09d90a94ae5a4f3b73','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(300,1,1,299,303,303,'279\nSection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nSolution\na. \nFigure 18.6: Scatter plot of screen time versus sleep time.\nb. There is a negative correlation between screen time and sleep duration. As screen time \nincreases, sleep decreases.\nc. Excess screen time may lead to reduced sleep time\nd. The data is observational as no variable was controlled.\nExample 2\nThe number of goals scored versus the number of goals conceded by a sample of seven football \nclubs in the Ghana Premier League for the 2024/2025 season is shown in the bar chart.\nFigure 18.7: Bar chart showing football clubs and number of goals scored in the 2024/2025 season.','5fc54ff23e800bb9d06e2f862c8b253787931baf4c06e429067b76b48dcc2e0f','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(301,1,1,300,304,304,'280\nSection 5: Data Handling & ProbabilitySection 5: Data Handling & Probability\na. Copy and complete the table\nTable 18.7: A partially completed table showing goals scored versus goals conceded\nFootball Club BibianiLionsKotoko NationsDreams Samartex Bechem\nGoals Scored 38 33\nGoals Conceded 25\nb. Present the data using a scatter plot.\nc. What kind of correlation exists between goals scored and goals conceded?\nd. What might be the implication of this trend?\nSolution\na. \nTable 18.8: A completed table showing goals scored versus goals conceded.\nFootball Club BibianiLionsKotoko NationsDreams Samartex Bechem\nGoals Scored 38 38 37 40 31 33 32\nGoals Conceded 21 24 27 21 29 25 28\nb. Present the data using a scatter plot.\nFigure 18.8: Scatter of goals scored versus goals conceded.\nc. What kind of correlation exists between goals scored and goals conceded?\nThere is a negative correlation between goals scored and goals conceded.\nd. Football clubs that score more goals concede fewer goals.','d8e067033dd0925b0e97750cdec848f0501b8e72f5b917cdc03d7de7b4558213','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(302,1,1,301,305,305,'281\nSection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nLearning Tasks\nGuide learners to apply their knowledge of scatter plots and correlation to analyse \nand solve problems using real-life data. This resource will equip learners to critically \nevaluate and determine which of two given data sets most accurately reflects a \ngiven scenario. Also, students will gain proficiency in identifying, analysing, and \ninterpreting bivariate data collected from media platforms.\nPEDAGOGICAL EXEMPLARS\nThis week’s lessons aim to compare the quality of two given data sets and make inferences \nfrom published data. The following pedagogical approach is suggested for facilitators to guide \nlearners through.\n1. Experiential Learning: In convenient groups, task learners to compare the quality of \ngiven data sets and draw conclusions on the appropriateness of the data in relation to the \npurpose of the data and its analysis techniques.\n2. Using the talk for learning strategy, engage learners in a class debate to support their \narguments with data and the use of appropriate mathematical terminology.\n3. Project-based learning: Assign project work to learners (individually or in groups) to \nobtain current data about a contemporary issue of interest, then make conclusions, give \ncriticisms, and make useful recommendations.\n4. Group discussions: In small groups, learners discuss data published on a local media \nplatform and draw conclusions with justifications.\nKEY ASSESSMENT: PRACTICE PAPER 2\nRecommended mode of assessment for week 18 is group Project Work and presentation.\nProject Work (Work in small groups)\nProject Goal: To collect data from a media platform, make inferences about the data, and draw \nconclusions from the data.\nQuestion\nSelect a simple project topic that allows you to gather easily data from media sources such as \nnewspapers, television, radio, or online platforms. Use appropriate charts or graphs to present \nthe data. Analyse the information to identify any patterns or relationships, and then share your \nfindings, highlighting the meaning and implications of your observations.\nProject Steps\nStep 1: Choose a Simple Topic\n• Pick a real-world issue or situation involving two related variables (bivariate data).','f39f20e467622440fc4a4f6337443cea5a573c5a7d8e2334db60b061d618b30f','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(303,1,1,302,306,306,'282\nSection 5: Data Handling & ProbabilitySection 5: Data Handling & Probability\n• Make sure it’s something you can find data on easily from media sources (TV, radio, \nnewspapers, websites, etc.).\nExamples:\n• Fuel prices and transport fares\n• Rainfall and crop harvest\n• Temperature and electricity usage\n• Advertisement spending and product sales\nStep 2: Gather Data\n• Look for reliable sources such as news websites, government reports, social media pages \nof institutions, or TV/radio broadcasts.\n• Record the data clearly in a table or spreadsheet.\n• Make sure both variables are numerical or clearly measurable.\nStep 3: Organise the Data\n• Create a data table showing both variables side by side.\n• Clean up any missing or inconsistent entries.\nStep 4: Represent the Data Visually\n• Use appropriate charts or graphs to show the relationship between the two variables.\nRecommended graphs\n• Scatter plot (best for identifying trends or correlations)\n• Bar chart (if data is categorical)\n• Line graph (if showing changes over time)\nStep 5: Analyse the Data\n• Look for patterns, trends, or correlations:\n• Is the relationship positive, negative, or no clear trend?\n• Are there any outliers (data points that don’t follow the pattern)?\n• Use statistical tools if needed (mean, correlation coefficient, etc.).\nStep 6: Present Your Findings\n• Write a summary of:\n• What the data shows\n• The type of relationship observed','db5e966096e21859bb2f2b2168a2822fe95d0649ebd8fd12e1fe63dc36b1b4d9','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(304,1,1,303,307,307,'283\nSection 5: Data Handling & Probability\n• Key patterns or surprises\nStep 7: Discuss the Implications\n• Explain why the relationship matters.\n• What can people, businesses, or governments learn from this?\n• Suggest possible actions or solutions based on your findings.\nHint\nConduct Practice Paper 2. It should cover contents taught from Year 1 to 3. Refer to the \nTable of Specification and Structure of the Paper at the end of this section. Learners’ \npapers should be marked immediately and scores entered into the SAP.\nPLC Session 18\n45 minutes Planning & Reviewing the Learning Plan\n1. Begin the session by recapping lessons from the previous session, focusing on how learning \nwas applied.\n2. Use your subject-specific app to explore the content before planning. Ask the app to\na. explain the week’s concepts in a practical way that will make it easier for learners to \nunderstand.\nb. identify three common misconceptions learners may have on the week’s content and \nhow to address them using contexts familiar to Ghanaian learners (NTS 3a, 3g and \n3m).\n3. Use your subject-specific app to plan your lesson. Ask the app to\na. suggest the most effective method for teaching the week’s concepts to learners with \ndiverse backgrounds.\nb. suggest learning activities, differentiation and essential questions based on the method \nof teaching (NTS 3a-3q).\nc. review your prepared learning plan and address any challenging issues you may have \nidentified in your subject area (NTS 3a-3c).\nNote\n• Take note of the recap topics linked to Years 1 and 2 while preparing for your \nlesson.\n• Refer to the Values Learning Community Handbook and link the values for the \nweek in your lesson to ensure that learners are applying what they learn.','1bcf99efe2a18cc5b0a926437929be581246bdcc62dff8c08d319aae80c5987c','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(305,1,1,304,308,308,'284\nSection 5: Data Handling & ProbabilitySection 5: Data Handling & Probability\n4. Use your app to plan and administer the mode of assessment for the Student Assessment \nPortal (SAP).\na. The key assessment for the week is practice paper 2 . Chat with your app to plan this \nassessment by reviewing or developing the assessment items/tasks and marking \nschemes/rubrics for the practice paper (NTS 3k-3q).\n10 minutes Content Exploration\n5. As a subject group, select one problem or topic/idea/term in the week’s content that is \nchallenging (not a routine procedural question, but one that requires conceptual \nunderstanding) and then\na. use the app, ask it to solve the problem step by step and explain the reasoning behind \neach step (you may be assisted by a teacher who is strong in the content area)\nb. all teachers in the group work through the problem individually for 3–4 minutes\nc. the group then works through it together the problem, step by step\nd. highlight the most common errors made at each step (NTS 1c, 1e, 2c, 2e, 3e-3j).\nNote\n• This part is not ‘How do I teach this? ’ but how do I teach it for learners to \nunderstand?\n• If the group still shares the same content gap, use the app: ask it to solve the \nproblem step by step and explain the reasoning behind each step.\n• Problems/ideas discussed should be sourced from the Teacher Manual content, not \ninvented separately. This keeps the content exploration directly connected to what \nteachers will deliver.\n30 minutes Enactment\n6. Enact a teaching activity based on your learning plan (NTS la-1b, 1e-lg, 2c, 2e, 2f, 3c-3j \nand 3o).\na. Colleagues observe with specific attention to clarity of concept explanation, use of \nfamiliar Ghanaian examples and how misconceptions are handled if they arise.\nb. After the enactment, ask for structured feedback from the group\ni. was the concept explained in a way that a learner with no prior knowledge could \nfollow?\nii. were the examples grounded in contexts familiar to Ghanaian learners?','3786a88896bf6bbf6071c4cba6f4bc74aa3e54915390e9a19f8552a481bfe3e8','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(306,1,1,305,309,309,'285\nSection 5: Data Handling & Probability\niii. did any misconceptions surface and if so, how were they addressed?\n5 minutes Reflection\n7. Reflect and share your views on the session. One thing that worked well, what did not work \nwell and what you will do differently. (NTS 1a, 1b).\n8. Identify a critical friend to observe and provide feedback on your lesson in relation to the \nPLC Session conducted (NTS 1f, 1g).\n9. Remember to\na. transfer your chats into your learning planner template.\nb. read your Teacher Manual and related Learner Material in preparation for the next \nsession. Take note of the recap weeks, which are linked to Years 1 and 2 (NTS 3a).\nc. integrate National Values, GESI, SEL, and 21st century skills into your learning \nactivities, assessment tasks, and rubrics/mark schemes.','f703cce376980a4c974855dc313b199e1202acf835fe0e216dc5922869d046d3','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(307,1,1,306,310,310,'286\nSection 5: Data Handling & ProbabilitySection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nWEEK 19\nLearning Indicator: Identify examples from print and electronic media, e.g., newspapers, \ntelevision, the Internet, where probability is used, and explain how the \ngiven probability influences individual decisions\nFOCAL AREA: IDENTIFICATION OF PROBABILITY USAGE IN \nTHE MEDIA\nThe Role of Probability in Society\n1. Weather Forecasting in Ghana\nRole: The Ghana Meteorological Agency uses probability to forecast rainfall and other \nweather events.\nExample: During the rainy season, forecasts may say there’s an 80% chance of rain in \nAccra or Kumasi.\nImpact: Farmers rely on these forecasts to decide when to plant or harvest crops.\nThe public and transport services prepare for possible flooding.\n2. Healthcare and Disease Control\nRole: Health authorities use probability to assess disease risks and plan interventions.\nExample: The Ghana Health Service estimates the likelihood of malaria outbreaks \nbased on climate and region. Probabilities are also used to predict HIV transmission \nrisks and track progress.\nImpact: Helps with distributing mosquito nets or ARV drugs to high-risk areas.\n3. Insurance in Ghana (e.g., NHIS and Private Insurance)\nRole: Insurance companies like SIC use probability to assess the likelihood of health \nissues, accidents, or death.\nExample: NHIS may use probability data to estimate future health service costs.\nImpact: Ensures insurance premiums are affordable and sustainable.\n4. Banking and Microfinance\nRole: Banks and fintech companies use probability to assess loan default risks.\nExample: A microfinance institution may estimate that there’s a 30% chance a new \nborrower might not repay a loan.\nImpact: Affects decisions on who qualifies for a loan and at what interest rate.\n5. Elections and Public Opinion','11479472a5f56f755d33377717ca186c857cd07809ea3b73710157218291b73b','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(308,1,1,307,311,311,'287\nSection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nRole: Probability is used in pre-election polls by institutions like CDD-Ghana or \nAfrobarometer.\nExample: A poll might say there’s a 60% chance the incumbent party will win in a \nspecific region.\nImpact: Guides campaign strategies and helps voters and analysts understand political \ntrends.\n6. Sports and Betting\nRole: Betting companies in Ghana (e.g., Betway, Odibets) use probability to set odds.\nExample: The odds for a Hearts of Oak win versus Asante Kotoko reflect the calculated \nprobability of that outcome.\nImpact: Influences public bets and betting strategies.\n7. Education (WASSCE/BECE Analysis)\nRole: Teachers and students use probability to guess which topics are most likely to \nappear in exams.\nExample: If a question on probability appeared three years in a row in BECE, teachers \nmight estimate it has a lower chance this year.\nImpact: Helps students prioritise their studies, although it’s not always accurate and is \na high-risk strategy.\n8. Disaster Preparedness\nRole: Authorities assess the chance of natural disasters like floods or droughts.\nExample: NADMO (National Disaster Management Organisation) uses past data to \nestimate the probability of flood-prone zones in Accra.\nImpact: Helps in allocating emergency resources and planning evacuation drills.\n9. Agriculture (Cocoa Farming, Food Security)\nRole: COCOBOD and other bodies use probability to predict crop yields based on \nrainfall, pests, and market trends.\nExample: A 20% chance of drought in the Ashanti region could mean lower cocoa \nyields.\nImpact: Influences export decisions and farmer support programmes.\n10. Crime Prevention and Policing\nRole: Security services analyse crime data to estimate where crimes are more likely to \noccur.\nExample: Police may deploy more patrols in areas with a high probability of robbery \nbased on past incidents.\nImpact: Improves safety and resource allocation.','0c3b9fefcab3f4ec750b9b6f99a8944897a9c46aa066020fcd7b231156796b35','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(309,1,1,308,312,312,'288\nSection 5: Data Handling & ProbabilitySection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nProbability In The Media\nProbability is a measure of how likely an event is to occur, expressed as a number between 0 \nand 1, or as a percentage. Probability is a powerful tool used in media to influence decisions. \nUnderstanding how it’s calculated and communicated will help you make informed decisions .\nWhy It Matters\nMedia outlets frequently use probability to inform or persuade the public, especially in areas \nsuch as health, weather, elections, and sports.\nFind Below Some Examples Of Media Statements and Their \nImpact\n1. Media Statement: “The vaccine reduces the risk of infection by 90%.”\nMeaning: In a group of people, those vaccinated are 90% less likely to get infected \ncompared with those who are not vaccinated.\nImpact: Influences individual decisions on whether to get vaccinated. It will also affect \npublic policy and funding for healthcare.\n2. Media Statement: “Candidate A has a 65% chance of winning the election.”\nImpact: May affect voter turnout (“my vote won’t matter” effect) or shape campaign \nstrategies and media narratives.\n3. Media Statement: “Team X has a 60% probability of winning tonight’s game.”\nImpact: Affects betting behaviour and fan expectations. Used in fantasy sports decisions.\nExample 1\nThe Ghana Meteorological Service reports a 65% probability of rain in Accra tomorrow.\na. What common misunderstanding might people have about this forecast?\nb. What does this 65% probability actually mean?\nc. Based on this information, what actions might someone choose to take?\nSolution\na. Some people may wrongly think that it will rain over 65% of the area or that it will rain \nfor 65% of the day. Others might believe it means it will definitely rain, just not sure \nwhen.\nb. It means that, based on similar weather conditions in the past, rain occurred 65% of the \ntime. In other words, if these same conditions happened 100 times, it rained on 65 of \nthose cases.','6fb6cdd6d33ab89239eb4f1a449324a4f589b650e178738951153a2406a2efd1','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(310,1,1,309,313,313,'289\nSection 5: Data Handling & Probability\n1. Carry an umbrella or wear a raincoat.\n2. Postpone or adjust outdoor plans or events.\n3. Farmers might prepare their fields or delay irrigation.\n4. Drivers may be more cautious, anticipating wet roads.\nExample 2\nA recent study found that a new flu vaccine is 87% effective.\na. How would you explain this 87% effectiveness to a friend in simple terms?\nb. Why is this information important for individuals and the public?\nc. What is a possible misunderstanding someone might have about this result?\nSolution\na. You can say that if 100 people take the flu vaccine, about 87 of them will be protected \nfrom getting the flu, while about 13 might still get ill despite taking the vaccine, but \ntheir illness may be milder.\nb. It helps people decide whether to get the vaccine. If many people get vaccinated, the \nflu is less likely to spread, which protects the whole community, especially those who \ncan’t get vaccinated.\nc. Some might think the vaccine guarantees they won’t get the flu at all. Others may \nassume the 87% means only 87 out of every 100 doses of the vaccine are effective.\nExample 3\nA sports website predicts that Medeama FC has a 70% chance of winning their next match.”\na. What does this 70% chance mean in context?\nb. How might fans or gamblers respond to this information?\nc. What is the possible misunderstanding someone might have about the prediction?\nSolution\na. It means that, based on previous performance, current form, and other factors, Medeama \nFC is likely to win the match 70 times out of 100 if the same situation happened repeatedly. \nIt shows a high probability, but not a guarantee.\nb. (1) Fans may feel more confident and hopeful about their team’s chances.\nc. (2) Gamblers might be more likely to bet on Medeama FC to win, expecting a strong \nperformance.\nSome might even bet on the opponent, hoping for an upset and a bigger payout.','1ccf69b2376caa694dab0cc1e014c9e78105cf5fb9ca7be0d3d7eaf66a808ebc','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(311,1,1,310,314,314,'290\nSection 5: Data Handling & ProbabilitySection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nd. Some people might wrongly think that Medeama FC is guaranteed to win or that the \nresult is already certain. Others may misinterpret it as meaning the team will win by \n70% of the match duration, which is incorrect.\nExample 4\na. Can the media use probability in misleading ways?\nb. What are some questions you should ask when you see a probability in the media?\nc. Why is it important to understand probability in real life?\nSolution\na. Yes. For example, stating a 90% success rate without explaining the sample size or other \nfactors can mislead people into overestimating effectiveness.\nb. What data is it based on?\nc. Who is providing the information?\nd. How recent and reliable is the source?\ne. It helps you make informed decisions, avoid being misled by the media, and understand \nrisks better.\nLearning Tasks\nGuide learners to examine real-life examples of how probability is used in \nnewspapers, television, and online media. Support them to analyse how these \nprobabilities influence public decisions and opinions. Encourage them to calculate \nsimple probabilities from media data and interpret the results.\nPEDAGOGICAL EXEMPLARS\nThis week’s lessons are designed to support learners to analyse how probability influences \npublic decisions and opinions. The following pedagogical approach is suggested for facilitators \nto guide learners through.\n1. Pair work: Engage learners in pairs to identify examples of the use of probability in \nreal-life contexts, including the media\n2. Group discussions: In small groups, guide and encourage learners to discuss the role of \nprobability in society by applying probability reasoning to investigate and make useful \nguesses about everyday-life events and use them to make judgements and suitable \ndecisions in their everyday activities.','73808bbd0d484a3832efd845599118f7b798dd713c280e01afd4dafac348bc0a','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(312,1,1,311,315,315,'291\nSection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nKEY ASSESSMENT LEVEL 3: INDIVIDUAL RESEARCH WORK\n1. Undertake the following project independently\na. Project Topic\nInvestigating the Impact and Misconceptions of Media Statements that Use Probability.\nb. Project Objective: To investigate how probability is used in media statements (e.g., \nnews, radio, websites) and to assess the public’s understanding, misconceptions, and \nthe real-life effects of such statements.\nc. Instructions\ni. Choose 3 probability media statements from reliable sources (TV, radio, internet, \nor newspapers).\nii. Examples: “There is a 70% chance of rain tomorrow.” “The new malaria vaccine \nis 80% effective.”\niii. For each statement, complete the sections below:\n• Media Statement: (Write the full statement using probability)\n• Example: “There is an 80% chance of rain in Accra tomorrow.”\n• State the source of the statement and the day you assessed the source. (E.g., \nGhana Web, Joy FM, TV3, Daily Graphic, Interview, assessed 23/02/2026)\n• What the statement means: (Give a clear, simple explanation of the probability \nin the context)\n• Provide at least two common misconceptions people may have about the \nstatement. (How might people misunderstand or misinterpret the statement?)\n• Give at least 3 Real-Life Impacts of the statement: (How does this statement \ninfluence decisions, behavior, or planning?)\nd. Reflection Questions: (Answer in full sentences)\ni. What did you learn about how people interpret probability in media?\nii. Why is it important for media outlets to clearly explain probability?\niii. How can schools or public education reduce misunderstandings like these?\nTotal 30 marks\n2. The graph below shows the monthly trends in rainfall and temperature in Ghana, based on \na study by Musah et al. (2018).”','533b39c0ca9fbb0c60baf71a9658974c0263e071c861984efdf1386314bd9634','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(313,1,1,312,316,316,'292\nSection 5: Data Handling & ProbabilitySection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nFigure 19.1: Monthly variations in rainfall and temperature in Ghana\nAssume a temperature (??) is considered high if ?? ≥ 28.80°C, and rainfall (??) is considered heavy \nif ?? ≥ 100 mm.\na. Find the probability of having a low temperature (?? < 28.80°C) within the year. If you are \nto report this probability figure in the media, what might be the impact and possible \nmisconceptions? 5 marks\nb. Determine the probability of experiencing heavy rainfall (?? ≥ 100 mm) within the year. \nIf you are to report this probability figure in the media, state one misconception someone \nmight have. 5 marks\nc. Calculate the probability of experiencing Low temperatures and High rainfall within the \nyear. How will you explain your answer to a layman? 5 marks\nTotal 15 marks\nPLC Session 19\n45 minutes Planning & Reviewing the Learning Plan\n1. Begin the session by recapping lessons from the previous year/session/week, focusing on \nhow learning was applied.\n2. Use your subject-specific app to explore the content before planning. Ask the app to\na. explain the week’s concepts in a practical way that will make it easier for learners to \nunderstand\nb. identify two or three common misconceptions learners may have on the week’s content \nand how to address them using contexts familiar to Ghanaian learners. (NTS 3a, 3g \nand 3m).','3eca61cff5d93a1674fc59337046b8d556dd5356b7fd6b447f7c306311fa7191','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(314,1,1,313,317,317,'293\nSection 5: Data Handling & Probability\n3. Use your subject-specific app to plan your lesson. Ask the app to\na. suggest the most effective method for teaching the week’s concepts to learners with \ndiverse backgrounds.\nb. suggest learning activities, differentiation and essential questions based on the method \nof teaching (NTS 3a-3q)\nc. review your prepared learning plan and address any challenging issues you may have \nidentified in your subject area (NTS 3a-3c).\nNote\n• Take note of the recap topics linked to Years 1 and 2 while preparing for your \nlesson.\n• Refer to the Values Learning Community Handbook and link the values for the \nweek in your lesson to ensure that learners are applying what they learn.\n4. Plan the week’s key assessment with your app\na. The key assessment for the week is individual research . Chat with your app to plan \nthis assessment, indicating what you and your learners will do before, during and after \nthe research (NTS 3k-3q).\n10 minutes Content Exploration\n5. As a subject group, select one problem or idea/term in the week’s content that is challenging \n(not a routine procedural question, but one that requires conceptual understanding) and \nthen\na. use the app, ask it to solve the problem step by step and explain the reasoning behind \neach step (you may be assisted by a teacher who is strong in the content area)\nb. all teachers in the group work through a similar problem individually for 3–4 minutes\nc. the group then works through the problem together step by step\nd. highlight the most common errors made at each step and discuss (NTS 1c, 1e, 2c, 2e, \n3e-3j).\nNote\n• This part is not just about ‘How do I teach this?’ but how do I teach it for learners \nto understand?\n• If the group still shares the same content gap, use the app: ask it to solve the \nproblem step by step and explain the reasoning behind each step.','2564b4cf7ee3ec967353c389518f159d3b3657809f7c4443bfa28c165912c44b','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(315,1,1,314,318,318,'294\nSection 5: Data Handling & ProbabilitySection 5: Data Handling & Probability\n• Problems/ideas discussed should be sourced from the Teacher Manual content, not \ninvented separately. This keeps the content exploration directly connected to what \nteachers will deliver.\n30 minutes Enactment\n6. Enact a teaching activity based on your learning plan (NTS la-1b, 1e-lg, 2c, 2e, 2f, 3c-3j \nand 3o).\na. Colleagues observe with specific attention to clarity of concept explanation, use of \nfamiliar Ghanaian examples and how misconceptions are handled if they arise.\nb. After the enactment, ask for structured feedback from the group\ni. was the concept explained in a way that a learner with no prior knowledge could \nfollow?\nii. were the examples grounded in contexts familiar to Ghanaian learners?\niii. did any misconceptions surface and if so, how were they addressed?\n5 minutes Reflection\n7. Reflect and share your views on the session. One thing that worked well, what did not work \nwell and what you will do differently. (NTS 1a, 1b).\n8. Identify a critical friend to observe and provide feedback on your lesson in relation to the \nPLC Session conducted (NTS 1f, 1g).\n9. Remember to\na. transfer your chats into your learning planner template.\nb. read your Teacher Manual and related Learner Material in preparation for the next \nsession. Take note of the recap weeks, which are linked to Years 1 and 2 (NTS 3a).\nc. integrate National Values, GESI, SEL, and 21st century skills into your learning \nactivities, assessment tasks, and rubrics/mark schemes.','81d6158a95d58d3892ce5ea92cb345f3bab63f64bc599fabaf701896c471ba94','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
(316,1,1,315,319,319,'295\nSection 5: Data Handling & Probability\nWEEK 20\nLearning Indicator: Solve everyday life problems involving the probability of two \ndependent and independent events, including addition and \nmultiplication laws\nFOCAL AREA: REAL-LIFE PROBLEMS INVOLVING \nPROBABILITY\nWhat is Probability?\nProbability is a measure of the likelihood that an event will occur. It ranges from 0 (impossible) \nto 1 (certain). \nTheoretical probability of an event = \nPROBABILITY TERMINOLOGIES\nExperiment\nA planned activity carried out under controlled conditions. This activity can be performed any \nnumber of times and has a well-defined set of possible outcomes.\nExamples of experiments include rolling a dice, spinning a wheel, drawing balls from a bag at \nrandom, and tossing a coin. The result of an experiment is called the outcome. The set of all \npossible outcomes is called the sample space (S).\nEvent (E)\nAn event is a set of outcomes of an experiment. The event is a subset of the sample space.\nP(theoretical probability of an event) =\n\n312\n\n<WH>WEEK 20 \n<LI>Learning Indicator: Solve everyday life problems involving the probability of two \ndependent and independent events, including addition and multiplication laws\n\n<FA>FOCAL AREA: REAL-LIFE PROBLEMS INVOLVING PROBABILITY\n\n<H3>What is Probability? \nProbability is a measure of the likelihood that an event will occur. \nIt ranges from 0 (impossible) to 1 (certain). \nTheoretical probability of an event=\n???????????????????????? ???????? ???????????????????????????????? ???????? ????ℎ???? ????????????????????\n???????????????????? ???????????????????????? ???????? ???????????????????????????????? ????????????????????????????????\n=\n????(????)\n????(????)\n\n<H1>PROBABILITY TERMINOLOGIES \n<H3>Experiment \nA planned activity carried out under controlled conditions. This activity can be performed any \nnumber of times and has a well-defined set of possible outcomes. \nExamples of experiments include rolling a dice, spinning a wheel, drawing balls from a bag at \nrandom, and tossing a coin. The result of an experiment is called the outcome. \nThe set of all possible outcomes is called the sample space (S). \n<H3>Event (E) \nAn event is a set of outcomes of an experiment. The event is a subset of the sample space. \nP(theoretical probability of an event)=\n???????????????????????? ???????? ???????????????????????????????? ???????? ????ℎ???? ????????????????????\n???????????????????? ???????????????????????? ???????? ???????????????????????????????? ????????????????????????????????\n=\n????(????)\n????(????)\n\nAs a number, the probability of an event is between 0 and 1 inclusive. i.e. 0≤????(????)≤1\n\n<H3>The complement of an event \nThe complement of an event A is denoted by ????′ (A prime). From set theory, we know that ????′ \nrepresents all elements that are not in A but are in the universal set. Under probability, the \nuniversal set is equivalent to the Sample Space. \nNote that ????(????)+????(????\n′\n)=1. Applying the basic change of subject, we have \n????(????)=1−????(????\n′\n) and ????(????\n′\n)=1−????(????)\n\n<H3>Probability of the complement of an event. \nLet P(E)=r be the probability that event E will occur. \nAs a number, the probability of an event is between 0 and 1 inclusive. i.e. 0 ≤ P(E) ≤ 1\nThe complement of an event\nThe complement of an event A is denoted by A\' (A prime). From set theory, we know that \nrepresents all elements that are not in A but are in the universal set. Under probability, the \nuniversal set is equivalent to the Sample Space.\n\n312\n\n<WH>WEEK 20 \n<LI>Learning Indicator: Solve everyday life problems involving the probability of two \ndependent and independent events, including addition and multiplication laws\n\n<FA>FOCAL AREA: REAL-LIFE PROBLEMS INVOLVING PROBABILITY\n\n<H3>What is Probability? \nProbability is a measure of the likelihood that an event will occur. \nIt ranges from 0 (impossible) to 1 (certain). \nTheoretical probability of an event=\n???????????????????????? ???????? ???????????????????????????????? ???????? ????ℎ???? ????????????????????\n???????????????????? ???????????????????????? ???????? ???????????????????????????????? ????????????????????????????????\n=\n????(????)\n????(????)','9dd7d93db312111a246c24eb078841b4eaff3c206aa83c2a9d8f19d362fe88da','2026-11-01 01:20:22'),
