(447,1,NULL,'section',92,0,NULL,NULL,'×6????×????=3???????? Therefore, area of BCD =3????(','Area of ABDE =20×????=20???? \nBut ????=\n52−6????\n3\n, area of ABDE =20×(\n52−6????\n3\n)=\n1040−120????\n3\n\nArea of BCD =\n1\n2\n×6????×????=3???????? \nTherefore, area of BCD =3????(\n52−6????\n3\n)=52????−6????\n2\n\nTherefore, area of ABCDE =20????+3????????\n\nArea of ABCDE =\n1040−120????\n3\n+52????−6????\n2\n\nFrom the question, area of ABCDE = 336\n\n1040−120????\n3\n+52????−6????\n2\n=336 \nMultiply through by 3 to remove it from the denominator:\n\n3×\n(1040−120????)\n3\n+3×52????−3×6????\n2\n=3×336',99,'verified',1,'2026-11-01 01:22:41','Indexed directly from the administrator-verified source resource.','2026-11-01 01:22:41'),
(448,1,NULL,'section',94,0,NULL,NULL,'1040−120????+156????−18????','1040−120????+156????−18????\n2\n=1008 \n−18????\n2\n+36????+32=0 \nDivide through by -2 to simplify the equation and then solve: \n9????\n2\n−18????−16=0 \n9????\n2\n+6????−24????−16=0 \n3????(3????+2)−8(3????+2)=0 \n(3????−8)(3+2)=0 \nThis means that 3????−8=0⟹????=\n8\n3\nor 3????+2=0⟹????=−\n2\n3\n\nSince the side of the floor cannot be negative, we ignore ????=−\n2\n3\nand conclude that: \n????=\n8\n3\n=2\n2\n3\n???? \nSubstitute this value of x, into our equation to find y: \n????=\n52−6×\n8\n3\n3\n=\n52−16\n3\n=\n36\n3\n=12 \nTherefore, ????=2\n2\n3\n???? and ????=12????\n\n<TH>Learning Tasks \nGuide learners to solve tasks related to understanding quadratic equations, quadratic graphs and \nfunctions. Provide support systems for learners who may encounter difficulties. \nLearners are to: \n1. Identify and provide reasonable justification to show whether a given equation is quadratic. \n2. Factorise and find the roots of a quadratic equation \n3. Apply the quadratic equation to answer real-life problems. \n<H1>PEDAGOGICAL EXEMPLARS \nThe aim of the lessons for the week is for all learners to be able solve problems involving \nquadratic equations. The following pedagogical approach is suggested for facilitators to take \nlearners through. \n<BodySL>Group work/collaborative learning \nUsing this strategy: \n75\n\n1040−120????+156????−18????\n2\n=1008 \n−18????\n2\n+36????+32=0 \nDivide through by -2 to simplify the equation and then solve: \n9????\n2\n−18????−16=0 \n9????\n2\n+6????−24????−16=0 \n3????(3????+2)−8(3????+2)=0 \n(3????−8)(3+2)=0 \nThis means that 3????−8=0⟹????=\n8\n3\nor 3????+2=0⟹????=−\n2\n3\n\nSince the side of the floor cannot be negative, we ignore ????=−\n2\n3\nand conclude that: \n????=\n8\n3\n=2\n2\n3\n???? \nSubstitute this value of x, into our equation to find y: \n????=\n52−6×\n8\n3\n3\n=\n52−16\n3\n=\n36\n3\n=12 \nTherefore, ????=2\n2\n3\n???? and ????=12????\n\n<TH>Learning Tasks \nGuide learners to solve tasks related to understanding quadratic equations, quadratic graphs and \nfunctions. Provide support systems for learners who may encounter difficulties. \nLearners are to: \n1. Identify and provide reasonable justification to show whether a given equation is quadratic. \n2. Factorise and find the roots of a quadratic equation \n3. Apply the quadratic equation to answer real-life problems. \n<H1>PEDAGOGICAL EXEMPLARS \nThe aim of the lessons for the week is for all learners to be able solve problems involving \nquadratic equations. The following pedagogical approach is suggested for facilitators to take \nlearners through. \n<BodySL>Group work/collaborative learning \nUsing this strategy: \nLearning Tasks\nGuide learners to solve tasks related to understanding quadratic equations, quadratic \ngraphs and functions. Provide support systems for learners who may encounter \ndifficulties.\nLearners are to:\n1. Identify and provide reasonable justification to show whether a given equation is \nquadratic.\n2. Factorise and find the roots of a quadratic equation\n3. Apply the quadratic equation to answer real-life problems.\nPEDAGOGICAL EXEMPLARS\nThe aim of the lessons for the week is for all learners to be able solve problems involving \nquadratic equations. The following pedagogical approach is suggested for facilitators to take \nlearners through.',100,'verified',1,'2026-11-01 01:22:41','Indexed directly from the administrator-verified source resource.','2026-11-01 01:22:41'),
(449,1,NULL,'section',141,0,NULL,NULL,'5????(4−6????)+6????','From (1), ????=4−6???? \nsubstitute ????=4−6???? into equation (2) \n5????(4−6????)+6????\n2\n=4 \n20????−30????\n2\n+6????\n2\n=4 \nA correct method for solving the quadratic, \nfor example: \n−24????\n2\n+20????−4=0 \n6????\n2\n−5????+1=0 \n6????\n2\n−3????−2????+1=0 \n3????(2????−1)−1(2????−1)=0 \n(3????−1)(2????−1)=0 \n????=\n1\n3\nor ????=\n1\n2\n\nIf ????=\n1\n3\n, ????=4−6×\n1\n3\n=4−2=2 \nIf ????=\n1\n2\n, ????=4−6×\n1\n2\n=4−3=1 \nIn conclusion, either ????=\n1\n3\nand ????=2 \nOr ????=\n1\n2\nand ????=1 \nM1 for correctly attempting to form equation \n(P) \nA1 for the correct equation or its equivalence\n\nM1 for forming equation (A) \nA1 for the correct equation or its equivalence\n\nM1 for forming quadratic equation by \nsubstitution \nA1 for correction equation \n(6????\n2\n−5????+1=0 or its equivalence)\n\nM1 correct method attempted for solving for \nx\n\nA1 for ????=\n1\n2\nand ????=\n1\n3\n(-0.5 each error)\n\nM1 for attempting to input their x to solve for \ny\n\nA1 for y=1 and y=2 (-0.5 each error) \nTotal =10 marks\n\n<H1>WEEK 7 \n<H3>Question One \nM1 for correctly attempting to form equation (P)\nA1 for the correct equation or its equivalence\nM1 for forming equation (A)\nA1 for the correct equation or its equivalence\nM1 for forming quadratic equation by substitution\nA1 for correction equation\n(6x\n2\n− 5x + 1 = 0 or its equivalence)\nM1 correct method attempted for solving for x\nA1 for \nx\n2\n1\n= and x\n3\n1\n= (−0.5 each error)\nM1 for attempting to input their x to solve for y\nA1 for y = 1 and y = 2 (−0.5 each error)\nTotal =10 marks\nWEEK 7\nQuestion One\n1.\nx -2 -1 0 1 2 3 4\ny 11 1 -5 -7-5 1 11 B2 (0.5 for each correct entry)',146,'verified',1,'2026-11-01 01:22:41','Indexed directly from the administrator-verified source resource.','2026-11-01 01:22:41'),
(450,1,NULL,'section',233,0,NULL,NULL,'???????????? ????????????????????ℎ','<H1>Trigonometry Graphs \nThe graph of a trigonometric function is a visual representation of the function for a given range \nof angles. The horizontal axis of a trigonometric graph represents the angle, usually written as θ, \nand the y-axis represents the function of that angle. \nThroughout this lesson, we will examine the graphs of trigonometric functions for angles \nexpressed in both degrees and radians. Before we consider the graphs of trigonometric functions, \nlet us look at the relationship between degrees and radian measurement. \n<H2>Relationship Between Degree and Radian Measurement \nIn mathematics, an angle can be measured in degrees (°) or in radians (rad). \nA degree is a traditional unit of measurement for angles. One full circle is 360°. \nA radian is another way to measure angles. It is based on the length of the arc of a circle. \nOne radian is the angle subtended at the centre of a circle by an arc whose length is equal to the \nradius of the circle. \n<H3>The Relationship \nThe circumference of a circle = 2πr \nOne full circle = 360°. \nIn radians, one full circle =\n???????????? ????????????????????ℎ\n????????????????????????\n=\n2????????\n????\n=2???? \nSo, 360°=2???? ???????????????????????????? \nDividing both sides by 360, we have: \n360°\n360\n=\n2????\n360\n????????????????????????????\n\n360×1°\n360\n=\n2????\n360\n????????????????????????????\n\n1°=\n????\n180\n????????????????????????????\n\n224\n\n<WH>WEEK 14 \n<LI>Learning Indicator: Draw graphs of given trigonometric functions and use them to solve \nrelated problems\n\n<FA>FOCAL AREA: TRIGONOMETRIC GRAPHS \nDrawing trigonometric graphs and using them to solve related problems.\n\n<H1>Trigonometry Graphs \nThe graph of a trigonometric function is a visual representation of the function for a given range \nof angles. The horizontal axis of a trigonometric graph represents the angle, usually written as θ, \nand the y-axis represents the function of that angle. \nThroughout this lesson, we will examine the graphs of trigonometric functions for angles \nexpressed in both degrees and radians. Before we consider the graphs of trigonometric functions, \nlet us look at the relationship between degrees and radian measurement. \n<H2>Relationship Between Degree and Radian Measurement \nIn mathematics, an angle can be measured in degrees (°) or in radians (rad). \nA degree is a traditional unit of measurement for angles. One full circle is 360°. \nA radian is another way to measure angles. It is based on the length of the arc of a circle. \nOne radian is the angle subtended at the centre of a circle by an arc whose length is equal to the \nradius of the circle. \n<H3>The Relationship \nThe circumference of a circle = 2πr \nOne full circle = 360°. \nIn radians, one full circle =\n???????????? ????????????????????ℎ\n????????????????????????\n=\n2????????\n????\n=2???? \nSo, 360°=2???? ???????????????????????????? \nDividing both sides by 360, we have: \n360°\n360\n=\n2????\n360\n????????????????????????????\n\n360×1°\n360\n=\n2????\n360\n????????????????????????????\n\n1°=\n????\n180\n???????????????????????????? \nAnd dividing both sides by 2π:',237,'verified',1,'2026-11-01 01:22:41','Indexed directly from the administrator-verified source resource.','2026-11-01 01:22:41'),
(451,1,NULL,'section',243,0,NULL,NULL,'Equate the phase shift to −','4. Equate the phase shift to −\n????\n????\nand solve for ????. \nTo find the phase shift for a graph of the form ????=????????????????(????????+????)+????, we identify one \nbasic cycle of the graph that begins at the maximum value, decreases to the minimum \nvalue and increases to the maximum value. Find the ????−coordinate of the end points of \nthe cycle [????\n0,????\n1], The value of the phase shift is the lower end point of the interval of the \nbasic cycle: phase shift =????\n0 \nTo find the phase shift for a graph of the form ????=????????????????(????????+????)+????, we identify one basic \ncycle of the graph that begins at ????=????,(where ????=???? is the centre line). Find the x-coordinates of \nthe end point of the cycle [????\n0,????\n1]. The value of the phase shift is the lower end point of the \ninterval of the basic cycle: phase shift =????\n0. \nNote that the equation of the sine and cosine differ only by a phase shift as illustrated below \n4. Equate the phase shift to \nB\nc\n- and solve for C\nTo find the phase shift for a graph of the form y = aCos(bx + c) + d , we identify one basic cycle \nof the graph that begins at the maximum value, decreases to the minimum value and increases \nto the maximum value. Find the x- coordinate of the end points of the cycle [x\n0\n, x\n1\n], The value \nof the phase shift is the lower end point of the interval of the basic cycle: phase shift = x\n0\nTo find the phase shift for a graph of the form y = aSin(bx + c) + d , we identify one basic cycle \nof the graph that begins at y = k (where k is the centre line). Find the x -coordinates of the end \npoint of the cycle [x\n0\n, x\n1\n]. The value of the phase shift is the lower end point of the interval of \nthe basic cycle: phase shift = x\n0\n.\nNote that the equation of the sine and cosine differ only by a phase shift as illustrated below\nFigure 14.11: Graphs of y = Sinx and y = Cosx\nSinx is a phase shift of 90° of Cosx\nExample 3\nThe diagram below represents a trigonometric graph.\nFigure 14.12: Graph of a trigonometric function',246,'verified',1,'2026-11-01 01:22:41','Indexed directly from the administrator-verified source resource.','2026-11-01 01:22:41'),
(452,1,NULL,'section',317,0,NULL,NULL,'<H1>PROBABILITY TERMINOLOGIES','<H1>PROBABILITY TERMINOLOGIES \n<H3>Experiment \nA planned activity carried out under controlled conditions. This activity can be performed any \nnumber of times and has a well-defined set of possible outcomes. \nExamples of experiments include rolling a dice, spinning a wheel, drawing balls from a bag at \nrandom, and tossing a coin. The result of an experiment is called the outcome. \nThe set of all possible outcomes is called the sample space (S). \n<H3>Event (E) \nAn event is a set of outcomes of an experiment. The event is a subset of the sample space. \nP(theoretical probability of an event)=\n???????????????????????? ???????? ???????????????????????????????? ???????? ????ℎ???? ????????????????????\n???????????????????? ???????????????????????? ???????? ???????????????????????????????? ????????????????????????????????\n=\n????(????)\n????(????)\n\nAs a number, the probability of an event is between 0 and 1 inclusive. i.e. 0≤????(????)≤1\n\n<H3>The complement of an event \nThe complement of an event A is denoted by ????′ (A prime). From set theory, we know that ????′ \nrepresents all elements that are not in A but are in the universal set. Under probability, the \nuniversal set is equivalent to the Sample Space. \nNote that ????(????)+????(????\n′\n)=1. Applying the basic change of subject, we have \n????(????)=1−????(????\n′\n) and ????(????\n′\n)=1−????(????)\n\n<H3>Probability of the complement of an event. \nLet P(E)=r be the probability that event E will occur.\n\n312\n\n<WH>WEEK 20 \n<LI>Learning Indicator: Solve everyday life problems involving the probability of two \ndependent and independent events, including addition and multiplication laws\n\n<FA>FOCAL AREA: REAL-LIFE PROBLEMS INVOLVING PROBABILITY\n\n<H3>What is Probability? \nProbability is a measure of the likelihood that an event will occur. \nIt ranges from 0 (impossible) to 1 (certain). \nTheoretical probability of an event