"},{\"shape\":\"line\",\"points\":[[0,-5],[0,5]],\"label\":\"Axis of symmetry: x = 0\"}],\"x_label\":\"x\",\"y_label\":\"y\"},\"presenter_notes\":\"Guide learners to calculate the vertex (0,0) and show that the axis passes through it. Highlight that the axis formula gives the same result for this simple case.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"3. Solving linear and quadratic equations together using graphs\",\"key_points\":[\"Both a quadratic and a linear equation can be drawn on the same coordinate system.\",\"Intersection points give the common solutions of the two equations.\",\"The axis of symmetry still helps locate the vertex and anticipate the number of intersections.\"],\"activity\":\"Graph \\\\(y = x^2 - 4\\\\) and \\\\(y = 2x + 1\\\\) on the same axes. Identify the intersection points algebraically and verify them on the graph.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"geometry\",\"x_min\":-5.617999999999999,\"x_max\":5.617999999999999,\"y_min\":-10.854000000000001,\"y_max\":22.854,\"shapes\":[{\"shape\":\"parabola\",\"coefficients\":[1,0,-4],\"x_min\":-5,\"x_max\":5,\"label\":\"y = x^2 - 4\"},{\"shape\":\"line\",\"points\":[[-5,-9],[5,11]],\"label\":\"y = 2x + 1\"},{\"shape\":\"line\",\"points\":[[0,-6],[0,6]],\"label\":\"Axis: x = 0\"}],\"x_label\":\"x\",\"y_label\":\"y\"},\"presenter_notes\":\"Show algebraic solution: set \\\\(x^2 - 4 = 2x + 1\\\\) → \\\\(x^2 - 2x -5 = 0\\\\). Use quadratic formula to find \\\\(x = 1 \\\\pm \\\\sqrt{6}\\\\). Mark these points on the graph to confirm.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"4. Applying the concepts to a real‑life problem\",\"key_points\":[\"Translate a word problem into a quadratic equation.\",\"Use the axis of symmetry to find the maximum or minimum value.\",\"Solve any accompanying linear condition graphically.\"],\"activity\":\"Problem: A farmer designs a parabolic fence to enclose a field. The height of the fence at a distance \\\\(x\\\\) metres from the centre is given by \\\\(h = -0.5x^2 + 6\\\\). The gate is placed 2 m from the centre (linear condition \\\\(x = 2\\\\)). Determine the height of the fence at the gate and the maximum height of the fence.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"table\",\"headers\":[\"Step\",\"Action\",\"Result\"],\"rows\":[[\"1\",\"Write the quadratic function\",\"h = -0.5x^2 + 6\"],[\"2\",\"Find axis of symmetry: x = -b\\/(2a) → x = 0\",\"Axis at x = 0\"],[\"3\",\"Maximum height at vertex: h_max = 6\",\"Maximum height = 6 m\"],[\"4\",\"Evaluate at gate (x = 2)\",\"h = -0.5(2)^2 + 6 = 5 m\"]]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Walk students through each row, emphasizing how the axis tells us the vertex occurs at the centre (x = 0). Highlight that the linear condition simply fixes a point on the graph to evaluate.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Slide 5 – Increasing & Decreasing Intervals on a Quadratic Graph\",\"key_points\":[\"A quadratic function f(x)=ax²+bx+c forms a parabola.\",\"If a<0 the parabola opens downward; the vertex is the maximum point.\",\"The function increases to the right of the vertex and decreases to the left.\",\"Identify the interval of increase (x > h) and decrease (x < h) where h is the x‑coordinate of the vertex.\"],\"activity\":\"In pairs, plot the quadratic f(x) = -x² + 4 on graph paper. Mark the vertex and shade the region where the curve rises (right side) and falls (left side).\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"geometry\",\"x_min\":-5.617999999999999,\"x_max\":5.617999999999999,\"y_min\":-22.668599999999998,\"y_max\":7.6686,\"x_label\":\"x\",\"y_label\":\"y\",\"shapes\":[{\"shape\":\"parabola\",\"coefficients\":[-1,0,4],\"x_min\":-5,\"x_max\":5,\"label\":\"y = -x² + 4\"},{\"shape\":\"line\",\"points\":[[0,4],[2,4]],\"label\":\"Increasing\"},{\"shape\":\"line\",\"points\":[[-2,4],[0,4]],\"label\":\"Decreasing\"}]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Explain the vertex formula h = -b\\/(2a). For f(x) = -x² + 4, h = 0, so the vertex is at (0,4). Emphasise that because a is negative, the parabola opens downward, making the vertex a maximum. Use the plotted graph to illustrate that as x moves right from 0, y decreases, and as x moves left from 0, y also decreases, highlighting the opposite directions of increase\\/decrease relative to the vertex.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Slide 6 – Positive & Negative y‑Values (Range of y)\",\"key_points\":[\"The sign of y depends on whether the graph lies above or below the x‑axis.\",\"For f(x) = -x² + 4, the x‑intercepts occur where y = 0.\",\"Between the intercepts, y is positive; outside them, y is negative.\"],\"activity\":\"Using the same plotted graph, locate the points where the curve meets the x‑axis. Record the x‑values and discuss the intervals where the height (y) is positive versus negative.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"geometry\",\"x_min\":-5.617999999999999,\"x_max\":5.617999999999999,\"y_min\":-22.668599999999998,\"y_max\":7.6686,\"x_label\":\"x\",\"y_label\":\"y\",\"shapes\":[{\"shape\":\"parabola\",\"coefficients\":[-1,0,4],\"x_min\":-5,\"x_max\":5,\"label\":\"y = -x² + 4\"},{\"shape\":\"line\",\"points\":[[-2,0],[2,0]],\"label\":\"x‑axis\"},{\"shape\":\"line\",\"points\":[[-2,0],[-2,0]],\"label\":\"Intercept (‑2,0)\"},{\"shape\":\"line\",\"points\":[[2,0],[2,0]],\"label\":\"Intercept (2,0)\"}]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Derive the intercepts by solving -x² + 4 = 0 → x² = 4 → x = ±2. Highlight that for -2 < x < 2, the parabola lies above the x‑axis (y > 0). Outside this interval, y becomes negative. Relate this to real‑world contexts such as the height of a thrown ball being above ground only between launch and landing points.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Slide 7 – Solving Related Equations Graphically\",\"key_points\":[\"A quadratic equation can be solved by locating its x‑intercepts on the graph.\",\"The quadratic formula gives the same solutions algebraically.\",\"Graphical solutions reinforce understanding of roots and their meaning.\"],\"activity\":\"Students are given the equation -x² + 4 = 0. They sketch the graph, identify the intercepts, and then verify the solutions using the quadratic formula.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"geometry\",\"x_min\":-5.617999999999999,\"x_max\":5.617999999999999,\"y_min\":-22.668599999999998,\"y_max\":7.6686,\"x_label\":\"x\",\"y_label\":\"y\",\"shapes\":[{\"shape\":\"parabola\",\"coefficients\":[-1,0,4],\"x_min\":-5,\"x_max\":5,\"label\":\"y = -x² + 4\"},{\"shape\":\"line\",\"points\":[[-2,0],[2,0]],\"label\":\"x‑axis\"},{\"shape\":\"line\",\"points\":[[-2,0],[-2,0]],\"label\":\"Root x = -2\"},{\"shape\":\"line\",\"points\":[[2,0],[2,0]],\"label\":\"Root x = 2\"}]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Walk through the steps: (1) Set y = 0, (2) Plot the parabola, (3) Read off the x‑coordinates where the curve meets the x‑axis. Show the quadratic formula x = [-b ± √(b²‑4ac)]\\/(2a) with a = -1, b = 0, c = 4, yielding x = ±2. Emphasise that the graphical method provides a visual check on the algebraic result.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Slide 8 – Real‑Life Application: Projectile Motion\",\"key_points\":[\"A projectile’s height h(t) can be modelled by h(t) = -5t² + 20t + 1 (t in seconds).\",\"The range of t for which h(t) > 0 gives the flight time.\",\"The vertex gives the maximum height and the time at which it occurs.\"],\"activity\":\"Groups calculate the flight time by finding where h(t) = 0 using the graph. They also identify the time of maximum height and discuss practical implications (e.g., when a ball lands).\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"geometry\",\"x_min\":-0.309,\"x_max\":5.309,\"y_min\":-26.8428,\"y_max\":24.8428,\"x_label\":\"time (s)\",\"y_label\":\"height (m)\",\"shapes\":[{\"shape\":\"parabola\",\"coefficients\":[-5,20,1],\"x_min\":0,\"x_max\":5,\"label\":\"h(t) = -5t² + 20t + 1\"},{\"shape\":\"line\",\"points\":[[0,0],[5,0]],\"label\":\"ground (h = 0)\"},{\"shape\":\"line\",\"points\":[[2,0],[2,0]],\"label\":\"Landing time ≈ 4.0 s\"},{\"shape\":\"line\",\"points\":[[2,21],[2,21]],\"label\":\"Maximum height ≈ 21 m\"}]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Explain that the coefficients come from physics (gravity ≈ -10 m\\/s², simplified to -5 for the model). Use the quadratic formula to find roots: t = [‑20 ± √(400‑20)]\\/(‑10) → t ≈ 0.05 s and t ≈ 4.0 s (ignore the tiny initial root). The vertex occurs at t = -b\\/(2a) = 2 s, giving h(2) = 21 m. Discuss how the graph instantly shows the time interval (0.05 s < t < 4.0 s) where the ball is above ground.\",\"image\":null}]}',0,'ended','2026-11-01 19:52:53','2026-11-01 07:52:53','2026-11-01 08:02:04','all',0,5,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-11-01 08:02:04','vq4c0aJKyf7aQPSTLb3M9X7pvux4glyb8c2FqBkmLa2VuPxxrf+VJlJeyOil42VdlxkBufkqacQYWU8qeGEgj6M4FprU0/KftSo+ayjdZKoEFyHfMucDLq5fU1Q=',1),
(4,1,'Ε7�i�F�����d2�k�σʕ��!kH<Ӱ','Parabolic and Quadratic Functions – Week 8 presentation A · presentation outline','{\"subject\":\"Mathematics\",\"year\":\"SHS Year 3\",\"template\":\"lesson\",\"visual_theme\":\"modern\",\"slides\":[{\"title\":\"1. Real‑life contexts for quadratic functions\",\"key_points\":[\"Quadratic relationships model many everyday situations (e.g., projectile motion, arch bridges, profit curves).\",\"The general form is \\\\(y = ax^2 + bx + c\\\\) where \\\\(a \\\\neq 0\\\\).\",\"Understanding the shape helps predict maximum\\/minimum values.\"],\"activity\":\"In pairs, list a real‑life scenario that can be described by a quadratic model and write a simple equation (illustrative coefficients). Share with the class.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"table\",\"headers\":[\"Scenario\",\"Illustrative Quadratic Model\"],\"rows\":[[\"Rocket reaching maximum height\",\"h = -5t^2 + 100t\"],[\"Parabolic arch of a bridge\",\"y = -0.02x^2 + 4x\"],[\"Profit vs. price\",\"P = -2p^2 + 30p - 50\"]]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Explain that the coefficients are chosen for illustration only; the focus is on recognising the quadratic form and its curved shape. Emphasise how the sign of \\\\(a\\\\) determines opening upward or downward.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"2. Graphing a quadratic and locating the axis of symmetry\",\"key_points\":[\"The graph of a quadratic is a parabola.\",\"The axis of symmetry is a vertical line that divides the parabola into two mirror images.\",\"For \\\\(y = ax^2 + bx + c\\\\), the axis equation is \\\\(x = -\\\\frac{b}{2a}\\\\).\"],\"activity\":\"Plot the points of \\\\(y = -x^2\\\\) on graph paper, draw the parabola, and sketch the axis of symmetry.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"geometry\",\"x_min\":-5.617999999999999,\"x_max\":5.617999999999999,\"y_min\":-26.854,\"y_max\":6.854,\"shapes\":[{\"shape\":\"parabola\",\"coefficients\":[-1,0,0],\"x_min\":-5,\"x_max\":5,\"label\":\"y = -x^2\"},{\