(5,1,'	z��Z��$�G���=\"����b�w{T�Y�Y�','Parabolic and Quadratic Functions – Week 8 presentation A · presentation outline','{\"subject\":\"Mathematics\",\"year\":\"SHS Year 3\",\"template\":\"lesson\",\"visual_theme\":\"modern\",\"slides\":[{\"title\":\"1. Real‑life contexts for quadratic functions\",\"key_points\":[\"Quadratic relationships model many everyday situations (e.g., projectile motion, arch bridges, profit curves).\",\"The general form is \\\\(y = ax^2 + bx + c\\\\) where \\\\(a \\\\neq 0\\\\).\",\"Understanding the shape helps predict maximum\\/minimum values.\"],\"activity\":\"In pairs, list a real‑life scenario that can be described by a quadratic model and write a simple equation (illustrative coefficients). Share with the class.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"table\",\"headers\":[\"Scenario\",\"Illustrative Quadratic Model\"],\"rows\":[[\"Rocket reaching maximum height\",\"h = -5t^2 + 100t\"],[\"Parabolic arch of a bridge\",\"y = -0.02x^2 + 4x\"],[\"Profit vs. price\",\"P = -2p^2 + 30p - 50\"]]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Explain that the coefficients are chosen for illustration only; the focus is on recognising the quadratic form and its curved shape. Emphasise how the sign of \\\\(a\\\\) determines opening upward or downward.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"2. Graphing a quadratic and locating the axis of symmetry\",\"key_points\":[\"The graph of a quadratic is a parabola.\",\"The axis of symmetry is a vertical line that divides the parabola into two mirror images.\",\"For \\\\(y = ax^2 + bx + c\\\\), the axis equation is \\\\(x = -\\\\frac{b}{2a}\\\\).\"],\"activity\":\"Plot the points of \\\\(y = -x^2\\\\) on graph paper, draw the parabola, and sketch the axis of symmetry.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"geometry\",\"x_min\":-5.617999999999999,\"x_max\":5.617999999999999,\"y_min\":-26.854,\"y_max\":6.854,\"shapes\":[{\"shape\":\"parabola\",\"coefficients\":[-1,0,0],\"x_min\":-5,\"x_max\":5,\"label\":\"y = -x^2\"},{\"shape\":\"line\",\"points\":[[0,-5],[0,5]],\"label\":\"Axis of symmetry: x = 0\"}],\"x_label\":\"x\",\"y_label\":\"y\"},\"presenter_notes\":\"Guide learners to calculate the vertex (0,0) and show that the axis passes through it. Highlight that the axis formula gives the same result for this simple case.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"3. Solving linear and quadratic equations together using graphs\",\"key_points\":[\"Both a quadratic and a linear equation can be drawn on the same coordinate system.\",\"Intersection points give the common solutions of the two equations.\",\"The axis of symmetry still helps locate the vertex and anticipate the number of intersections.\"],\"activity\":\"Graph \\\\(y = x^2 - 4\\\\) and \\\\(y = 2x + 1\\\\) on the same axes. Identify the intersection points algebraically and verify them on the graph.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"geometry\",\"x_min\":-5.617999999999999,\"x_max\":5.617999999999999,\"y_min\":-10.854000000000001,\"y_max\":22.854,\"shapes\":[{\"shape\":\"parabola\",\"coefficients\":[1,0,-4],\"x_min\":-5,\"x_max\":5,\"label\":\"y = x^2 - 4\"},{\"shape\":\"line\",\"points\":[[-5,-9],[5,11]],\"label\":\"y = 2x + 1\"},{\"shape\":\"line\",\"points\":[[0,-6],[0,6]],\"label\":\"Axis: x = 0\"}],\"x_label\":\"x\",\"y_label\":\"y\"},\"presenter_notes\":\"Show algebraic solution: set \\\\(x^2 - 4 = 2x + 1\\\\) → \\\\(x^2 - 2x -5 = 0\\\\). Use quadratic formula to find \\\\(x = 1 \\\\pm \\\\sqrt{6}\\\\). Mark these points on the graph to confirm.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"4. Applying the concepts to a real‑life problem\",\"key_points\":[\"Translate a word problem into a quadratic equation.\",\"Use the axis of symmetry to find the maximum or minimum value.\",\"Solve any accompanying linear condition graphically.\"],\"activity\":\"Problem: A farmer designs a parabolic fence to enclose a field. The height of the fence at a distance \\\\(x\\\\) metres from the centre is given by \\\\(h = -0.5x^2 + 6\\\\). The gate is placed 2 m from the centre (linear condition \\\\(x = 2\\\\)). Determine the height of the fence at the gate and the maximum height of the fence.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"table\",\"headers\":[\"Step\",\"Action\",\"Result\"],\"rows\":[[\"1\",\"Write the quadratic function\",\"h = -0.5x^2 + 6\"],[\"2\",\"Find axis of symmetry: x = -b\\/(2a) → x = 0\",\"Axis at x = 0\"],[\"3\",\"Maximum height at vertex: h_max = 6\",\"Maximum height = 6 m\"],[\"4\",\"Evaluate at gate (x = 2)\",\"h = -0.5(2)^2 + 6 = 5 m\"]]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Walk students through each row, emphasizing how the axis tells us the vertex occurs at the centre (x = 0). Highlight that the linear condition simply fixes a point on the graph to evaluate.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Slide 5 – Increasing & Decreasing Intervals on a Quadratic Graph\",\"key_points\":[\"A quadratic function f(x)=ax²+bx+c forms a parabola.\",\"If a<0 the parabola opens downward; the vertex is the maximum point.\",\"The function increases to the right of the vertex and decreases to the left.\",\"Identify the interval of increase (x > h) and decrease (x < h) where h is the x‑coordinate of the vertex.\"],\"activity\":\"In pairs, plot the quadratic f(x) = -x² + 4 on graph paper. Mark the vertex and shade the region where the curve rises (right side) and falls (left side).\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"geometry\",\"x_min\":-5.617999999999999,\"x_max\":5.617999999999999,\"y_min\":-22.668599999999998,\"y_max\":7.6686,\"x_label\":\"x\",\"y_label\":\"y\",\"shapes\":[{\"shape\":\"parabola\",\"coefficients\":[-1,0,4],\"x_min\":-5,\"x_max\":5,\"label\":\"y = -x² + 4\"},{\"shape\":\"line\",\"points\":[[0,4],[2,4]],\"label\":\"Increasing\"},{\"shape\":\"line\",\"points\":[[-2,4],[0,4]],\"label\":\"Decreasing\"}]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Explain the vertex formula h = -b\\/(2a). For f(x) = -x² + 4, h = 0, so the vertex is at (0,4). Emphasise that because a is negative, the parabola opens downward, making the vertex a maximum. Use the plotted graph to illustrate that as x moves right from 0, y decreases, and as x moves left from 0, y also decreases, highlighting the opposite directions of increase\\/decrease relative to the vertex.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Slide 6 – Positive & Negative y‑Values (Range of y)\",\"key_points\":[\"The sign of y depends on whether the graph lies above or below the x‑axis.\",\"For f(x) = -x² + 4, the x‑intercepts occur where y = 0.\",\"Between the intercepts, y is positive; outside them, y is negative.\"],\"activity\":\"Using the same plotted graph, locate the points where the curve meets the x‑axis. Record the x‑values and discuss the intervals where the height (y) is positive versus negative.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"geometry\",\"x_min\":-5.617999999999999,\"x_max\":5.617999999999999,\"y_min\":-22.668599999999998,\"y_max\":7.6686,\"x_label\":\"x\",\"y_label\":\"y\",\"shapes\":[{\"shape\":\"parabola\",\"coefficients\":[-1,0,4],\"x_min\":-5,\"x_max\":5,\"label\":\"y = -x² + 4\"},{\"shape\":\"line\",\"points\":[[-2,0],[2,0]],\"label\":\"x‑axis\"},{\"shape\":\"line\",\"points\":[[-2,0],[-2,0]],\"label\":\"Intercept (‑2,0)\"},{\"shape\":\"line\",\"points\":[[2,0],[2,0]],\"label\":\"Intercept (2,0)\"}]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Derive the intercepts by solving -x² + 4 = 0 → x² = 4 → x = ±2. Highlight that for -2 < x < 2, the parabola lies above the x‑axis (y > 0). Outside this interval, y becomes negative. Relate this to real‑world contexts such as the height of a thrown ball being above ground only between launch and landing points.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Slide 7 – Solving Related Equations Graphically\",\"key_points\":[\"A quadratic equation can be solved by locating its x‑intercepts on the graph.\",\"The quadratic formula gives the same solutions algebraically.\",\"Graphical solutions reinforce understanding of roots and their meaning.\"],\"activity\":\"Students are given the equation -x² + 4 = 0. They sketch the graph, identify the intercepts, and then verify the solutions using the quadratic formula.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"geometry\",\"x_min\":-5.617999999999999,\"x_max\":5.617999999999999,\"y_min\":-22.668599999999998,\"y_max\":7.6686,\"x_label\":\"x\",\"y_label\":\"y\",\"shapes\":[{\"shape\":\"parabola\",\"coefficients\":[-1,0,4],\"x_min\":-5,\"x_max\":5,\"label\":\"y = -x² + 4\"},{\"shape\":\"line\",\"points\":[[-2,0],[2,0]],\"label\":\"x‑axis\"},{\"shape\":\"line\",\"points\":[[-2,0],[-2,0]],\"label\":\"Root x = -2\"},{\"shape\":\"line\",\"points\":[[2,0],[2,0]],\"label\":\"Root x = 2\"}]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Walk through the steps: (1) Set y = 0, (2) Plot the parabola, (3) Read off the x‑coordinates where the curve meets the x‑axis. Show the quadratic formula x = [-b ± √(b²‑4ac)]\\/(2a) with a = -1, b = 0, c = 4, yielding x = ±2. Emphasise that the graphical method provides a visual check on the algebraic result.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Slide 8 – Real‑Life Application: Projectile Motion\",\"key_points\":[\"A projectile’s height h(t) can be modelled by h(t) = -5t² + 20t + 1 (t in seconds).\",\"The range of t for which h(t) > 0 gives the flight time.\",\"The vertex gives the maximum height and the time at which it occurs.\"],\"activity\":\"Groups calculate the flight time by finding where h(t) = 0 using the graph. They also identify the time of maximum height and discuss practical implications (e.g., when a ball lands).\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"geometry\",\"x_min\":-0.309,\"x_max\":5.309,\"y_min\":-26.8428,\"y_max\":24.8428,\"x_label\":\"time (s)\",\"y_label\":\"height (m)\",\"shapes\":[{\"shape\":\"parabola\",\"coefficients\":[-5,20,1],\"x_min\":0,\"x_max\":5,\"label\":\"h(t) = -5t² + 20t + 1\"},{\"shape\":\"line\",\"points\":[[0,0],[5,0]],\"label\":\"ground (h = 0)\"},{\"shape\":\"line\",\"points\":[[2,0],[2,0]],\"label\":\"Landing time ≈ 4.0 s\"},{\"shape\":\"line\",\"points\":[[2,21],[2,21]],\"label\":\"Maximum height ≈ 21 m\"}]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Explain that the coefficients come from physics (gravity ≈ -10 m\\/s², simplified to -5 for the model). Use the quadratic formula to find roots: t = [‑20 ± √(400‑20)]\\/(‑10) → t ≈ 0.05 s and t ≈ 4.0 s (ignore the tiny initial root). The vertex occurs at t = -b\\/(2a) = 2 s, giving h(2) = 21 m. Discuss how the graph instantly shows the time interval (0.05 s < t < 4.0 s) where the ball is above ground.\",\"image\":null}]}',0,'ended','2026-11-01 20:06:12','2026-11-01 08:06:12','2026-11-01 08:43:01','all',0,5,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-11-01 08:43:01','ewk/hJRqWbQ0fkjnsFQOklvPk9r2hdewd8Mop0yeuT5n6df+0NjlX7p+mx6iYTbNZvnGaxzsX5W/FXGEe+0ThFzWUfcY/S/twEyOAM/2TfTfZ6rGaO6Q/KP8+fY=',1),
(6,1,'��jr�Sby��⦊7Xo\r.N�ϒ)ʃk7','Investigating Partial Variation','{\"subject\":\"Mathematics\",\"year\":\"SHS3\",\"template\":\"inquiry\",\"visual_theme\":\"vibrant\",\"slides\":[{\"title\":\"What is Partial Variation?\",\"key_points\":[\"A relationship with a fixed component (c) and a variable component (k·x)\",\"Graph shifts upward; does not pass through the origin\",\"Common in fees that include a base charge plus usage\"],\"activity\":\"Students list everyday costs that combine a base fee with a usage charge.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Venn‑style diagram showing overlap of fixed and variable parts.\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"geometry\",\"title\":\"Fixed vs Variable Components\",\"x_min\":-0.37079999999999996,\"x_max\":6.3708,\"y_min\":-0.2472,\"y_max\":4.2472,\"shapes\":[{\"shape\":\"circle\",\"center\":[2,2],\"radius\":2,\"label\":\"Fixed component\"},{\"shape\":\"circle\",\"center\":[4,2],\"radius\":2,\"label\":\"Variable component\"}]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Introduce the term \'partial variation\' from the curriculum excerpt. Emphasise the constant term c and relate it to real‑life examples like a monthly subscription fee plus per‑unit usage. Prompt students to think of other scenarios.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Direct vs Partial Variation\",\"key_points\":[\"Direct variation: y = kx, passes through (0,0)\",\"Partial variation: y = kx + c, intercept c ≠ 0\",\"Graph of partial variation is a shifted straight line\"],\"activity\":\"Plot two data sets on graph paper and identify the y‑intercept of each.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Side‑by‑side comparison of the two formulas.\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"comparison\",\"title\":\"Direct vs Partial Variation\",\"items\":[\"Direct Variation\",\"Partial Variation\"],\"descriptions\":[\"y = kx (origin)\",\"y = kx + c (intercept c)\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Use the verified excerpt to explain that partial variation builds on direct variation by adding a constant start value. Highlight how the graph moves up by c units. Ask learners to predict the intercept before plotting.\"},{\"title\":\"Investigating Partial Variation with Data\",\"key_points\":[\"Identify fixed cost (c) from the y‑intercept\",\"Determine variable rate (k) from the slope\",\"Form the equation y = kx + c\"],\"activity\":\"Given sample data, students calculate k and c and write the governing equation.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Table of sample quantities and total costs.\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"table\",\"title\":\"Sample Data\",\"headers\":[\"Units (x)\",\"Total Cost (y)\"],\"rows\":[[\"0\",\"50\"],[\"5\",\"100\"],[\"10\",\"150\"]]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Guide pupils through the table: the cost when 0 units are used reveals the fixed component (c = 50). Use two points to compute the slope k = (Δy)\\/(Δx) = (100‑50)\\/(5‑0) = 10. Write the equation y = 10x + 50 and check with the third row.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Solving Real‑Life Problems\",\"key_points\":[\"Translate the word problem into y = kx + c\",\"Insert known values and solve for the unknown\",\"Verify the solution by substitution\"],\"activity\":\"Solve a budgeting problem: a mobile plan costs GH₵30 base plus GH₵5 per GB. Find the cost for 12 GB.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Process flow of problem‑solving steps.\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"process\",\"title\":\"Problem‑Solving Steps\",\"steps\":[\"Identify fixed cost (c) and variable rate (k)\",\"Write the equation y = kx + c\",\"Substitute the known quantity (x) and solve for y\",\"Check the answer by recalculating\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Link back to the inquiry approach: students first explore the situation, then formulate the equation, compute, and finally reflect on the answer. Emphasise checking work, as required by the curriculum standard on solving real‑life issues.\"},{\"title\":\"Identifying Partial Variation\",\"key_points\":[\"Fixed component added to a variable part\",\"Seen in fees, tariffs, and mixed costs\",\"Differs from direct variation (no fixed start)\"],\"activity\":\"Examine a sample price list and label each charge as fixed or variable.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Cards contrasting fixed vs. variable parts\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"cards\",\"title\":\"Components of Partial Variation\",\"items\":[\"Fixed component (e.g., base fee)\",\"Variable component (e.g., per‑unit charge)\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Introduce the concept with everyday examples such as mobile phone plans or bus fares. Emphasise that the relationship starts from a non‑zero intercept. Prompt students to think of other contexts where a constant charge is added to a usage‑based amount.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Representing Partial Variation Algebraically\",\"key_points\":[\"Equation form: y = k + mx\",\"k = fixed part, m = rate of change\",\"Graph is a straight line with y‑intercept k\"],\"activity\":\"From a given data table, write the equation that models the relationship.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Process diagram of deriving the equation\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"process\",\"title\":\"From Data to Equation\",\"steps\":[\"List pairs (x, y)\",\"Find the constant difference (k)\",\"Calculate the slope (m)\",\"Combine into y = k + mx\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Guide students through each step using a simple example (e.g., cost of printing where there is a setup fee). Highlight how the intercept represents the fixed cost and the slope the variable rate. Encourage questioning: why does the line not pass through the origin?\"},{\"title\":\"Investigating Partial Variation with Data\",\"key_points\":[\"Collect real‑world measurements\",\"Plot points to see linear trend\",\"Determine k and m from the graph\"],\"activity\":\"In groups, plot the supplied data on graph paper and draw the best‑fit line.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Table of sample data for plotting\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"table\",\"title\":\"Sample Data for Partial Variation\",\"headers\":[\"Quantity (x)\",\"Total Cost (y)\"],\"rows\":[[\"1\",\"₵12\"],[\"2\",\"₵22\"],[\"3\",\"₵32\"],[\"4\",\"₵42\"]]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Explain that the data illustrate a fixed charge of ₵2 plus ₵10 per unit. Ask learners to verify the pattern by calculating differences. After plotting, discuss how the line’s intercept and slope correspond to the fixed and variable parts.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Generalising and Solving Real‑Life Problems\",\"key_points\":[\"Use y = k + mx to predict unknown values\",\"Check solutions against context\",\"Reflect on assumptions (linearity, constant rate)\"],\"activity\":\"Solve a budgeting problem: estimate the cost of 7 units given the model.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Comparison of solving with and without proportional reasoning\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"comparison\",\"title\":\"Impact of Using the Model\",\"items\":[\"Guesswork only – may miss fixed cost\",\"Model y = k + mx – accurate prediction\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Present a scenario such as hiring a venue with a booking fee plus hourly charge. Walk through the calculation using the derived equation, then ask students to critique the result (e.g., does it make sense?). Highlight the value of proportional reasoning in planning and budgeting.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Exploring Partial Variation\",\"key_points\":[\"Fixed component added to a variable part\",\"Real‑life examples: service fees, base salaries\",\"Builds on direct variation\"],\"activity\":\"Students list everyday situations that include a base charge plus a usage charge.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Show definition and components on separate cards.\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"cards\",\"title\":\"Partial Variation Basics\",\"items\":[\"Fixed component (c)\",\"Variable part proportional to x (mx)\",\"Total amount = mx + c\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Introduce the concept with a short story (e.g., mobile phone plan). Emphasise that unlike direct variation, the graph does not pass through the origin. Prompt learners to think of other contexts where a constant fee is added. Link to the curriculum indicator on using proportional reasoning for partial variations.\"},{\"title\":\"Formulating the Equation\",\"key_points\":[\"Equation form: y = mx + c\",\"m = rate of change, c = fixed amount\",\"Connects to proportional reasoning\"],\"activity\":\"Given a table of costs, students identify m and c and write the equation.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Display a step‑by‑step process diagram.\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"process\",\"title\":\"Deriving the Equation\",\"steps\":[\"Identify the fixed cost (c) from the intercept\",\"Determine the rate of change (m) from the slope\",\"Combine into y = mx + c\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Guide learners to plot the given points quickly (use graph paper or digital tool). Highlight how the slope represents the variable component per unit change. Ask probing questions: ‘What does the intercept tell us about the situation?’ Reinforce the link between the algebraic form and the real‑world story introduced earlier.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Investigating with Tables\",\"key_points\":[\"Create a value table for x and y\",\"Observe constant increase of y when x increases by 1\",\"Calculate m and c from the table\"],\"activity\":\"In groups, fill in the table and compute the rate and fixed amount.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Show a simple table with illustrative rows.\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"table\",\"title\":\"Sample Values Table\",\"headers\":[\"x\",\"y\"],\"rows\":[[\"1\",\"c + m\"],[\"2\",\"c + 2m\"],[\"3\",\"c + 3m\"]]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Explain that each step up in x adds the same amount m to y, confirming proportionality of the variable part. Have students verify the pattern by calculating differences between successive y‑values. Use this to solidify the concept of constant rate and fixed start value before moving to problem solving.\"},{\"title\":\"Solving a Real‑Life Problem\",\"key_points\":[\"Apply y = mx + c to a budgeting scenario\",\"Check solution against context\",\"Generalise the method for similar problems\"],\"activity\":\"Solve a word problem: a venue charges GHS 150 booking fee plus GHS 25 per hour; find cost for 6 hours.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Compare each problem‑solving step side by side.\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"comparison\",\"title\":\"Problem‑Solving Steps\",\"items\":[\"Set up equation (y = 25x + 150)\",\"Insert known value (x = 6)\",\"Calculate y = 300\",\"Interpret: total cost is GHS 300\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Walk through the problem aloud, encouraging students to identify the fixed and variable parts. After solving, ask them to explain why the answer makes sense (e.g., 6 hours × 25 = 150 plus 150 booking = 300). Prompt them to write a short generalisation: ‘For any partial variation, total = fixed + rate × quantity.’ Connect back to the learning indicator and suggest how this reasoning extends to other contexts.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Slide 13 – Investigating Partial Variation\",\"key_points\":[\"Cost = fixed component + variable component\",\"Relationship is linear with a non‑zero intercept\"],\"activity\":\"Collect taxi‑fare data (base fare + per‑km charge) and plot cost vs distance.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Show a line that starts above the origin to illustrate the fixed component.\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"geometry\",\"title\":\"Partial Variation Line\",\"x_min\":-0.7415999999999999,\"x_max\":12.7416,\"y_min\":-8.652,\"y_max\":148.652,\"shapes\":[{\"shape\":\"line\",\"points\":[[0,20],[10,120]],\"label\":\"Cost = 20 + 10·d\"}]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Explain that many real‑world situations start with a base amount (e.g., a taxi’s flag‑fall). The total cost grows proportionally with distance, giving a straight line that does not pass through the origin. Guide students to identify the fixed (20) and variable (10) components from the plotted points.\"},{\"title\":\"Slide 14 – Generalising and Solving Partial‑Variation Problems\",\"key_points\":[\"Form equation y = mx + c\",\"Solve for the unknown variable\"],\"activity\":\"Solve a budgeting problem: a phone plan charges $15 monthly plus $0.05 per minute; find minutes for a $30 bill.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Display a process flow of the solving steps.\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"process\",\"title\":\"Problem‑Solving Process\",\"items\":[\"Write the equation y = mx + c\",\"Insert known values\",\"Isolate the unknown (x)\",\"Check the solution in the original context\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Model the translation from words to the algebraic form y = 0.05x + 15. Show the algebraic manipulation to find x = (30‑15)\\/0.05 = 300 minutes. Emphasise checking the answer by substituting back into the original situation.\",\"image\":null}]}',0,'ended','2026-11-02 01:18:39','2026-11-01 13:18:39','2026-11-01 13:51:17','all',0,5,'[]',NULL,NULL,NULL,'{\"id\":\"f5e0e7b0-39eb-4f6a-8e5e-ba312e2af145\",\"question\":\"Do you understand the difference between fixed component and variable component\",\"options\":[\"Yes\",\"Partially\",\"No\"],\"open\":false}','2026-11-01 13:51:17','Wi7VE5h9a9vZ6yXWp0Az/AM3mmPZ1ouRdX25Bw7VjDOjemrfdeFQEhEZHKDOhtfL/ybCBBRi+ET3dOLhwXV0eWfdSvWX2sCHZ/keczWiq66QXK/TeSZvqtZxuRo=',1),
