+gjH\\/wB+RR\\/ZPij\\/AKCUf\\/fkUrR\\/mX4\\/5DN7Apc1g\\/2T4n\\/6CMf\\/AH5FH9k+J\\/8AoIx\\/9+RRaP8AMvx\\/yA3utJisH+yfFH\\/QSj\\/78il\\/snxR\\/wBBGP8A78ij3f5l+P8AkBvZoya5\\/wDsjxR\\/0Eo\\/+\\/Io\\/snxR\\/0Eo\\/8AvyKLR\\/mX4\\/5Ab+BS9Olc\\/wD2T4p\\/6CUf\\/fkUv9k+KP8AoIx\\/9+RRaP8AMvx\\/yD5m\\/wBaTArB\\/snxR\\/0Eo\\/8AvyKX+yfE\\/wD0Eo\\/+\\/I\\/xo93+Zfj\\/AJAb1GTWB\\/ZPif8A6CUf\\/fkUf2R4o\\/6CUf8A35FFo\\/zL8f8AIPmb2BS5NYH9k+KP+glH\\/wB+RR\\/ZPij\\/AKCMf\\/fkUWj\\/ADL8f8gN7FLXP\\/2T4p\\/6CUf\\/AH5FH9k+Kf8AoJR\\/9+RRaP8AMvx\\/yA6Ciuf\\/ALJ8U\\/8AQSj\\/AO\\/Io\\/snxT\\/0Eo\\/+\\/IotH+Zfj\\/kBv0YFYH9k+Kf+gjH\\/AN+RR\\/ZHin\\/oJR\\/9+RRaP8y\\/EDoKTrWB\\/ZPin\\/oIx\\/8AfkUf2T4p7ajH\\/wB+RRaP8y\\/EDoOlFYH9k+Kf+gjH\\/wB+RR\\/ZPij\\/AKCMf\\/fkUWj\\/ADL8f8gL2paTp+rwfZtRiEqA7gDkEH1BGCPwqLS9D0rRlZdNgEW\\/7xyWY\\/VmJP61WGk+KO+ox\\/8AfkUf2T4o\\/wCgjF\\/35FPT+b8w0N6isH+yfFH\\/AEEY\\/wDvyKP7J8Uf9BKP\\/vyKXu\\/zIDepa5\\/+yfFH\\/QRj\\/wC\\/Ipf7J8Uf9BKP\\/vyKLR\\/mX9fIDfornzpPintqMf8A35FJ\\/ZPir\\/oJR\\/8AfkUWj\\/Mvx\\/yA6Giuf\\/snxT\\/0Eo\\/+\\/Io\\/snxT\\/wBBKP8A78ii0f5l+P8AkB0FFYH9k+KP+gjH\\/wB+RR\\/ZPij\\/AKCMf\\/fkUWj\\/ADL8f8gN+iuf\\/snxT\\/0Eo\\/8AvyKP7J8U\\/wDQSi\\/78ii0f5l+P+QHQUlc\\/wD2T4q\\/6CUX\\/fkUv9k+Ke+oxf8AfkUWj\\/Mv6+QHQUVz\\/wDZPin\\/AKCUf\\/fkUf2T4q\\/6CMX\\/AH5FFo\\/zIR0FFc\\/\\/AGT4p\\/6CUf8A35FH9k+Kf+glH\\/35FFo\\/zL8f8hnQUVz\\/APZPin\\/oIx\\/9+RS\\/2T4o\\/wCgjH\\/35FFo\\/wAyA36KwP7J8U\\/9BGL\\/AL8ik\\/snxR\\/0Eo\\/+\\/IotH+Zf18gOgorA\\/snxR\\/0Eo\\/8AvyKP7J8U\\/wDQRj\\/78ii0f5l\\/XyAv6rqMOlWMl5KRlRhF7s5+6o+v8uam8O2EmmaLbWcv30TLD0ZiWI\\/AnFU7Lw2I7pdQ1W4e9uIzlC4ConuqDgHjr+PWumqJyVuVEthS0lLWIj\\/\\/1vb6KKKACiiigAooooAKKKKACiiigAooooAKKKKACiiigAoopaAEooooAKKKKACiiigAooooAKKKKACiiloASilpKACiiigAooooAKKKKACiiigAooooAKKKKACiiigAoopaAEooooAKKKKACiiloASiiigAooooAKKKKACilpKACiiigAooooAKKWkoAKKKKACiiigAooooAKKKKACiiigAooooAKKKKACiiigAooooAKKKKACiiigAooooAKKKKACiiigD\\/9k=\",\"width\":756,\"height\":504,\"alt\":\"7D436F05-65A9-4B37-868A-BB9D06CE4276\",\"source_url\":\"\",\"attribution\":\"\",\"license\":\"Teacher-provided image\"}},{\"title\":\"Direct vs Partial Variation\",\"key_points\":[\"Direct variation: y = kx, passes through (0,0)\",\"Partial variation: y = kx + c, intercept c ≠ 0\",\"Graph of partial variation is a shifted straight line\"],\"activity\":\"Plot two data sets on graph paper and identify the y‑intercept of each.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"comparison\",\"title\":\"Direct vs Partial Variation\",\"items\":[\"Direct Variation\",\"Partial Variation\"],\"descriptions\":[\"y = kx (origin)\",\"y = kx + c (intercept c)\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Use the verified excerpt to explain that partial variation builds on direct variation by adding a constant start value. Highlight how the graph moves up by c units. Ask learners to predict the intercept before plotting.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Investigating Partial Variation with Data\",\"key_points\":[\"Identify fixed cost (c) from the y‑intercept\",\"Determine variable rate (k) from the slope\",\"Form the equation y = kx + c\"],\"activity\":\"Given sample data, students calculate k and c and write the governing equation.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"table\",\"title\":\"Sample Data\",\"headers\":[\"Units (x)\",\"Total Cost (y)\"],\"rows\":[[\"0\",\"50\"],[\"5\",\"100\"],[\"10\",\"150\"]]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Guide pupils through the table: the cost when 0 units are used reveals the fixed component (c = 50). Use two points to compute the slope k = (Δy)\\/(Δx) = (100‑50)\\/(5‑0) = 10. Write the equation y = 10x + 50 and check with the third row.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Solving Real‑Life Problems\",\"key_points\":[\"Translate the word problem into y = kx + c\",\"Insert known values and solve for the unknown\",\"Verify the solution by substitution\"],\"activity\":\"Solve a budgeting problem: a mobile plan costs GH₵30 base plus GH₵5 per GB. Find the cost for 12 GB.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"process\",\"title\":\"Problem‑Solving Steps\",\"steps\":[\"Identify fixed cost (c) and variable rate (k)\",\"Write the equation y = kx + c\",\"Substitute the known quantity (x) and solve for y\",\"Check the answer by recalculating\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Link back to the inquiry approach: students first explore the situation, then formulate the equation, compute, and finally reflect on the answer. Emphasise checking work, as required by the curriculum standard on solving real‑life issues.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Identifying Partial Variation\",\"key_points\":[\"Fixed component added to a variable part\",\"Seen in fees, tariffs, and mixed costs\",\"Differs from direct variation (no fixed start)\"],\"activity\":\"Examine a sample price list and label each charge as fixed or variable.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"cards\",\"title\":\"Components of Partial Variation\",\"items\":[\"Fixed component (e.g., base fee)\",\"Variable component (e.g., per‑unit charge)\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Introduce the concept with everyday examples such as mobile phone plans or bus fares. Emphasise that the relationship starts from a non‑zero intercept. Prompt students to think of other contexts where a constant charge is added to a usage‑based amount.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Representing Partial Variation Algebraically\",\"key_points\":[\"Equation form: y = k + mx\",\"k = fixed part, m = rate of change\",\"Graph is a straight line with y‑intercept k\"],\"activity\":\"From a given data table, write the equation that models the relationship.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"process\",\"title\":\"From Data to Equation\",\"steps\":[\"List pairs (x, y)\",\"Find the constant difference (k)\",\"Calculate the slope (m)\",\"Combine into y = k + mx\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Guide students through each step using a simple example (e.g., cost of printing where there is a setup fee). Highlight how the intercept represents the fixed cost and the slope the variable rate. Encourage questioning: why does the line not pass through the origin?\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Investigating Partial Variation with Data\",\"key_points\":[\"Collect real‑world measurements\",\"Plot points to see linear trend\",\"Determine k and m from the graph\"],\"activity\":\"In groups, plot the supplied data on graph paper and draw the best‑fit line.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"table\",\"title\":\"Sample Data for Partial Variation\",\"headers\":[\"Quantity (x)\",\"Total Cost (y)\"],\"rows\":[[\"1\",\"₵12\"],[\"2\",\"₵22\"],[\"3\",\"₵32\"],[\"4\",\"₵42\"]]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Explain that the data illustrate a fixed charge of ₵2 plus ₵10 per unit. Ask learners to verify the pattern by calculating differences. After plotting, discuss how the line’s intercept and slope correspond to the fixed and variable parts.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Generalising and Solving Real‑Life Problems\",\"key_points\":[\"Use y = k + mx to predict unknown values\",\"Check solutions against context\",\"Reflect on assumptions (linearity, constant rate)\"],\"activity\":\"Solve a budgeting problem: estimate the cost of 7 units given the model.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"comparison\",\"title\":\"Impact of Using the Model\",\"items\":[\"Guesswork only – may miss fixed cost\",\"Model y = k + mx – accurate prediction\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Present a scenario such as hiring a venue with a booking fee plus hourly charge. Walk through the calculation using the derived equation, then ask students to critique the result (e.g., does it make sense?). Highlight the value of proportional reasoning in planning and budgeting.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Exploring Partial Variation\",\"key_points\":[\"Fixed component added to a variable part\",\"Real‑life examples: service fees, base salaries\",\"Builds on direct variation\"],\"activity\":\"Students list everyday situations that include a base charge plus a usage charge.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"cards\",\"title\":\"Partial Variation Basics\",\"items\":[\"Fixed component (c)\",\"Variable part proportional to x (mx)\",\"Total amount = mx + c\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Introduce the concept with a short story (e.g., mobile phone plan). Emphasise that unlike direct variation, the graph does not pass through the origin. Prompt learners to think of other contexts where a constant fee is added. Link to the curriculum indicator on using proportional reasoning for partial variations.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Formulating the Equation\",\"key_points\":[\"Equation form: y = mx + c\",\"m = rate of change, c = fixed amount\",\"Connects to proportional reasoning\"],\"activity\":\"Given a table of costs, students identify m and c and write the equation.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"process\",\"title\":\"Deriving the Equation\",\"steps\":[\"Identify the fixed cost (c) from the intercept\",\"Determine the rate of change (m) from the slope\",\"Combine into y = mx + c\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Guide learners to plot the given points quickly (use graph paper or digital tool). Highlight how the slope represents the variable component per unit change. Ask probing questions: ‘What does the intercept tell us about the situation?’ Reinforce the link between the algebraic form and the real‑world story introduced earlier.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Investigating with Tables\",\"key_points\":[\"Create a value table for x and y\",\"Observe constant increase of y when x increases by 1\",\"Calculate m and c from the table\"],\"activity\":\"In groups, fill in the table and compute the rate and fixed amount.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"table\",\"title\":\"Sample Values Table\",\"headers\":[\"x\",\"y\"],\"rows\":[[\"1\",\"c + m\"],[\"2\",\"c + 2m\"],[\"3\",\"c + 3m\"]]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Explain that each step up in x adds the same amount m to y, confirming proportionality of the variable part. Have students verify the pattern by calculating differences between successive y‑values. Use this to solidify the concept of constant rate and fixed start value before moving to problem solving.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Solving a Real‑Life Problem\",\"key_points\":[\"Apply y = mx + c to a budgeting scenario\",\"Check solution against context\",\"Generalise the method for similar problems\"],\"activity\":\"Solve a word problem: a venue charges GHS 150 booking fee plus GHS 25 per hour; find cost for 6 hours.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"comparison\",\"title\":\"Problem‑Solving Steps\",\"items\":[\"Set up equation (y = 25x + 150)\",\"Insert known value (x = 6)\",\"Calculate y = 300\",\"Interpret: total cost is GHS 300\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Walk through the problem aloud, encouraging students to identify the fixed and variable parts. After solving, ask them to explain why the answer makes sense (e.g., 6 hours × 25 = 150 plus 150 booking = 300). Prompt them to write a short generalisation: ‘For any partial variation, total = fixed + rate × quantity.’ Connect back to the learning indicator and suggest how this reasoning extends to other contexts.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Slide 13 – Investigating Partial Variation\",\"key_points\":[\"Cost = fixed component + variable component\",\"Relationship is linear with a non‑zero intercept\"],\"activity\":\"Collect taxi‑fare data (base fare + per‑km charge) and plot cost vs distance.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"geometry\",\"title\":\"Partial Variation Line\",\"x_min\":-0.7415999999999999,\"x_max\":12.7416,\"y_min\":-8.652,\"y_max\":148.652,\"shapes\":[{\"shape\":\"line\",\"points\":[[0,20],[10,120]],\"label\":\"Cost = 20 + 10·d\"}]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Explain that many real‑world situations start with a base amount (e.g., a taxi’s flag‑fall). The total cost grows proportionally with distance, giving a straight line that does not pass through the origin. Guide students to identify the fixed (20) and variable (10) components from the plotted points.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Slide 14 – Generalising and Solving Partial‑Variation Problems\",\"key_points\":[\"Form equation y = mx + c\",\"Solve for the unknown variable\"],\"activity\":\"Solve a budgeting problem: a phone plan charges $15 monthly plus $0.05 per minute; find minutes for a $30 bill.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"process\",\"title\":\"Problem‑Solving Process\",\"items\":[\"Write the equation y = mx + c\",\"Insert known values\",\"Isolate the unknown (x)\",\"Check the solution in the original context\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Model the translation from words to the algebraic form y = 0.05x + 15. Show the algebraic manipulation to find x = (30‑15)\\/0.05 = 300 minutes. Emphasise checking the answer by substituting back into the original situation.\",\"image\":null}]}',13,'ended','2026-11-02 02:34:28','2026-11-01 14:34:28','2026-11-01 14:37:02','item',4,5,NULL,'{\"x\":0.41616091683949114,\"y\":0.05959327293429419,\"color\":\"#e5484d\",\"active\":false,\"updated_at\":1793543820}',NULL,NULL,'{\"id\":\"c4d33db7-6778-4a13-a2e8-361cda9aab85\",\"question\":\"What is partial variation\",\"options\":[\"it has fixed component only\",\"variable component only\",\"has both k and c\"],\"open\":false}','2026-11-01 14:37:02','YwUJIzOQo9QYdOCfGuuOAjrpJe7KKTRao9shHLwOnxGicTyRo8mdZBpk7QljhdCwdpKq3AUrpfPN0vKuJ2DAVsuV+oLI63KVaLS6UZQOuf/4uMU3m6u1Cm1XUxM=',1),
(8,1,'LP���� ����-߸�Q;�_*ʀ_\n�j�','Factorisation of Quadratics and Graphical Solutions (Weeks 6‑7)','{\"subject\":\"Mathematics\",\"year\":\"SHS3\",\"template\":\"flipped\",\"visual_theme\":\"modern\",\"slides\":[{\"title\":\"Week 6 Overview: Factoring Quadratics (Flipped Intro)\",\"key_points\":[\"Quadratic expression: ax² + bx + c\",\"Purpose of factoring: simplify solving equations\",\"Link between factors and roots\"],\"activity\":\"Watch the pre‑class video on factoring methods and complete the online worksheet identifying a, b, c for given quadratics.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Show a step‑by‑step flow diagram of the factoring process.\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"process\",\"steps\":[\"Identify coefficients a, b, c\",\"Find two numbers that multiply to a·c and add to b\",\"Rewrite the middle term using those numbers\",\"Factor by grouping\",\"Write the factored form\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Introduce the relevance of factoring for solving quadratic equations. Emphasise the flipped model: students should have watched the video before class; use the worksheet responses to gauge readiness.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Factoring Method: Grouping\",\"key_points\":[\"Rewrite the middle term to enable grouping\",\"Extract common factors from each group\",\"Combine the common binomial factor\"],\"activity\":\"In pairs, factor the listed quadratics on the board using the grouping method; teacher circulates to correct misconceptions.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Display a table of example quadratics with their factored forms.\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"table\",\"headers\":[\"Quadratic (ax²+bx+c)\",\"Factors (p)(q)\"],\"rows\":[[\"x² + 5x + 6\",\"(x + 2)(x + 3)\"],[\"2x² + 7x + 3\",\"(2x + 1)(x + 3)\"],[\"3x² - 12x + 9\",\"3(x - 1)(x - 3)\"]]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Guide students through each example, highlighting how the two numbers are chosen. Ask them to explain why the final factorisation is correct.\"},{\"title\":\"Applying Factoring to Solve Equations\",\"key_points\":[\"Set the factored expression equal to zero\",\"Use the Zero‑Product Property\",\"Write the solution set\"],\"activity\":\"Students solve three quadratic equations individually, then compare answers in small groups.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Worked‑example table showing equation → factored form → solutions.\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"table\",\"headers\":[\"Equation\",\"Factored Form\",\"Solutions\"],\"rows\":[[\"x² + 5x + 6 = 0\",\"(x + 2)(x + 3) = 0\",\"x = -2, -3\"],[\"2x² + 7x + 3 = 0\",\"(2x + 1)(x + 3) = 0\",\"x = -½, -3\"],[\"3x² - 12x + 9 = 0\",\"3(x - 1)(x - 3) = 0\",\"x = 1, 3\"]]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Stress the logical flow from factoring to solving. Check that students correctly apply the zero‑product rule and write the solution set in proper notation.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Week 7 Overview: Graphical Solution of Quadratics\",\"key_points\":[\"Parabolic shape of y = ax² + bx + c\",\"Vertex indicates maximum or minimum\",\"Axis of symmetry: x = -b\\/(2a)\"],\"activity\":\"Before class, explore an interactive graphing tool to plot y = x² - 4x + 3 and note the vertex.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Simple Cartesian graph showing the parabola and its vertex.\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"graph\",\"points\":[[0,3],[1,0],[2,-1],[3,0],[4,3]],\"x_label\":\"x\",\"y_label\":\"y\",\"title\":\"y = x² - 4x + 3\"},\"presenter_notes\":\"Explain how the graph confirms the solutions found by factoring (roots at x = 1 and x = 3) and introduces the vertex at (2, -1) as the minimum point.\"},{\"title\":\"Finding Maximum and Minimum from the Graph\",\"key_points\":[\"Identify the vertex on the plotted curve\",\"Determine if it is a maximum (a < 0) or minimum (a > 0)\",\"Read the y‑coordinate as the extreme value\"],\"activity\":\"Students plot y = -2x² + 8x + 1 on graph paper, locate the vertex, and state whether it is a maximum or minimum.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Graph with vertex highlighted and axis of symmetry drawn.\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"graph\",\"points\":[[0,1],[1,7],[2,9],[3,7],[4,1]],\"x_label\":\"x\",\"y_label\":\"y\",\"title\":\"y = -2x² + 8x + 1 (Vertex at (2,9))\"},\"presenter_notes\":\"Guide students to calculate the vertex using -b\\/(2a) = -8\\/(2·-2) = 2 and verify it on the graph. Discuss why the parabola opens downward, making the vertex a maximum.\"},{\"title\":\"Real‑Life Optimisation: Maximising Arch Height\",\"key_points\":[\"Translate a practical problem into a quadratic model\",\"Use the vertex formula to find the optimum value\",\"Interpret the result in context\"],\"activity\":\"Group task: design a parabolic bridge arch where the height h (metres) is given by h = -0.5x² + 5x + 2 (x = horizontal distance). Find the maximum height and the distance at which it occurs.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Process diagram outlining steps from problem statement to vertex calculation.\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"process\",\"steps\":[\"Write the quadratic expression for the situation\",\"Identify coefficients a, b, c\",\"Compute x‑coordinate of vertex: x = -b\\/(2a)\",\"Substitute x back into the equation to get maximum height\",\"State the optimum distance and height\",\"Discuss practical implications\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Facilitate the groups as they apply the vertex method. Emphasise checking units and realistic feasibility of the solution.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Slide 7: Quick Recap & Choosing a Method\",\"key_points\":[\"Quadratic equations can be solved by factorisation, formula, or completing the square.\",\"Factorisation is efficient when the quadratic factors into integer binomials.\",\"Select the method that best fits the given equation.\"],\"activity\":\"Students watch a 3‑minute pre‑class video comparing the three solution methods and note which method they would choose for each example.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Comparison chart of solution methods\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"comparison\",\"items\":[\"Factorisation method\",\"Quadratic formula\",\"Completing the square\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Introduce the flipped activity. Emphasise that the video is for preview only; class time will be used for practice. Ask students to justify their method choice in the upcoming group discussion.\"},{\"title\":\"Slide 8: Step‑by‑Step Factorisation (a = 1)\",\"key_points\":[\"Write the quadratic in standard form ax² + bx + c.\",\"Find two numbers whose product is c and sum is b.\",\"Rewrite the middle term using those numbers.\",\"Factor by grouping to obtain (x + m)(x + n).\"],\"activity\":\"In pairs, students list the two numbers for three given quadratics on a shared Google Doc.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Process diagram of the factorisation steps\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"process\",\"steps\":[\"Identify a, b, c in ax² + bx + c\",\"Find m and n such that m·n = c and m + n = b\",\"Rewrite bx as mx + nx\",\"Group terms and factor common factors\",\"Write the final binomial factors\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Walk through each step with a simple example (x² + 5x + 6). Highlight common mistakes, such as mixing up signs. Remind students the video already covered the rationale.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Slide 9: Worked Example – x² + 5x + 6\",\"key_points\":[\"Identify c = 6 and b = 5.\",\"Numbers 2 and 3 satisfy 2·3 = 6 and 2+3 = 5.\",\"Rewrite: x² + 2x + 3x + 6.\",\"Factor: x(x + 2) + 3(x + 2) = (x + 3)(x + 2).\"],\"activity\":\"Students complete a guided worksheet that mirrors the table shown, filling in the missing trial pairs.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Table of trial factor pairs\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"table\",\"headers\":[\"Trial Pair (m,n)\",\"Product (m·n)\",\"Sum (m+n)\"],\"rows\":[[\"(1,6)\",\"6\",\"7\"],[\"(2,3)\",\"6\",\"5\"],[\"(-1,-6)\",\"6\",\"-7\"],[\"(-2,-3)\",\"6\",\"-5\"]]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Explain how the table helps eliminate unsuitable pairs quickly. Encourage students to check sign consistency with b. After the example, ask a quick oral check: what would be the factors if b were -5?\"},{\"title\":\"Slide 10: Factorisation When a ≠ 1\",\"key_points\":[\"Multiply a and c to get the product ac.\",\"Find two numbers that multiply to ac and add to b.\",\"Rewrite the middle term and factor by grouping.\",\"Simplify to obtain the final binomial factors.\"],\"activity\":\"Groups receive a different quadratic (e.g., 2x² + 7x + 3) and create their own trial‑pair table before factoring.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Process diagram for a ≠ 1 case\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"process\",\"steps\":[\"Compute ac = a × c\",\"Find m and n such that m·n = ac and m + n = b\",\"Rewrite bx as mx + nx\",\"Group and factor common terms\",\"Factor out the leading coefficient if needed\",\"Write the final factors\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Model the process with 2x² + 7x + 3. Show that ac = 6, m = 6, n = 1, leading to 2x² + 6x + x + 3 → 2x(x + 3) + 1(x + 3) → (2x + 1)(x + 3). Highlight the importance of checking the product ac.\"},{\"title\":\"Slide 11: Real‑Life Application – Projectile Motion\",\"key_points\":[\"The height of a projectile follows h(t) = -½gt² + v₀t + h₀.\",\"Setting h(t) = 0 gives the times when the projectile hits the ground.\",\"Factorising the quadratic yields the launch and landing times.\"],\"activity\":\"Students use the given formula with g = 9.8 m\\/s², v₀ = 20 m\\/s, h₀ = 1.5 m, factorise the resulting quadratic, and determine the total flight time.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Graph of the projectile trajectory with roots marked\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"graph\",\"title\":\"Illustrative projectile path (h = -4.9t² + 20t + 1.5)\",\"points\":[[0,1.5],[2,18.1],[4,1.9],[4.1,0],[0,0]],\"x_label\":\"Time (s)\",\"y_label\":\"Height (m)\",\"note\":\"Points are illustrative; actual roots obtained by factorisation.\"},\"presenter_notes\":\"Explain that the graph shows a parabola opening downwards. The x‑intercepts correspond to the times when height = 0. After students factorise, confirm the roots match the plotted intercepts.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Slide 12: Consolidation Activity – Practice & Word Problem\",\"key_points\":[\"Apply factorisation to diverse quadratics.\",\"Translate a real‑world scenario into a quadratic equation.\",\"Solve the equation by factorisation to answer the question.\"],\"activity\":\"Each group receives a set of three cards: two pure factorisation problems and one word problem. They must solve, present their factors, and explain the solution steps.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Cards displaying the three problems\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"cards\",\"items\":[\"Problem 1: Factorise 3x² – 14x + 8\",\"Problem 2: Factorise x² – 9\",\"Problem 3: A rectangular garden has length (x + 4) m and width (x – 2) m. Its area is 60 m². Find the possible dimensions.\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Circulate while groups work, checking that they correctly set up the quadratic for the word problem (x² + 2x – 8 = 0) before factorising. Use this time for formative assessment and to address misconceptions.\"},{\"title\":\"Slide 13 – Video Recap: Quadratic Functions Overview\",\"key_points\":[\"Quadratic function: f(x)=ax²+bx+c produces a parabola\",\"Direction of opening depends on sign of a\",\"Key features: vertex, axis of symmetry, roots\"],\"activity\":\"Ask students to note any unclear points from the pre‑class video in their learning journal.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Process diagram showing the three main features of a parabola.\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"process\",\"steps\":[\"Identify coefficients a, b, c\",\"Determine direction of opening (sign of a)\",\"Locate vertex and axis of symmetry\",\"Find roots (where f(x)=0)\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Briefly reaffirm the video content. Emphasise that understanding the shape of the parabola is essential before solving it graphically. Invite a few volunteers to share their journal notes.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Slide 14 – Solving Quadratic Equations Using Graphs\",\"key_points\":[\"Plot the quadratic function on a coordinate plane\",\"The x‑intercepts represent the solutions of the equation f(x)=0\",\"If the parabola does not cross the x‑axis, there are no real solutions\"],\"activity\":\"Students work in pairs to sketch the graph of f(x)=x²‑5x+6 on graph paper and mark the roots.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Comparison chart of two graphs: one intersecting the x‑axis, one not.\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"comparison\",\"items\":[\"Graph A: f(x)=x²‑5x+6 – two real roots at x=2 and x=3\",\"Graph B: f(x)=x²+4x+8 – no real roots (parabola above x‑axis)\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Guide students to locate where the curve meets the x‑axis. Highlight that the graphical method gives an approximate solution, useful for checking algebraic results.\"},{\"title\":\"Slide 15 – Finding Maximum or Minimum (Vertex) from the Graph\",\"key_points\":[\"Vertex is the highest point (maximum) if a<0, lowest point (minimum) if a>0\",\"Vertex coordinates: (h, k) where h = -b\\/(2a) and k = f(h)\",\"The vertex gives the optimum value in real‑life problems\"],\"activity\":\"Using the same graph from Slide 14, students locate the vertex and record its coordinates.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Process steps to locate the vertex on a parabola.\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"process\",\"steps\":[\"Calculate h = -b\\/(2a)\",\"Substitute h into the function to find k\",\"Plot point (h, k) on the graph\",\"Label as maximum (a<0) or minimum (a>0)\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Demonstrate the calculation with the example f(x)=x²‑5x+6 (a=1, b=-5). Show that h=2.5, k= -0.25, giving a minimum point. Connect this to optimisation scenarios.\"},{\"title\":\"Slide 16 – Real‑Life Application: Maximising Rocket Height\",\"key_points\":[\"Model rocket height: h(t)=‑5t²+40t+10 (t in seconds)\",\"The vertex gives the time of maximum height and the height itself\",\"Interpret the result in the context of the problem\"],\"activity\":\"Students calculate the vertex of the given function and write a short explanation of what the values mean for the rocket launch.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Cards showing each step of the application.\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"cards\",\"items\":[\"Identify coefficients: a=‑5, b=40, c=10\",\"Compute h = -b\\/(2a) = -40\\/(‑10) = 4 s\",\"Find k = h(4) = ‑5(4)²+40(4)+10 = 90 m\",\"Interpretation: Maximum height of 90 m reached at 4 s\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Link the mathematical process to the engineering context. Encourage students to think of other scenarios (e.g., profit maximisation) where the vertex is useful.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Slide 17 – Guided Practice: Plot, Solve & Locate Vertex\",\"key_points\":[\"Plot the quadratic, identify roots, and find the vertex\",\"Check graphical solutions against algebraic methods\",\"Reflect on accuracy and usefulness of the graph\"],\"activity\":\"Provide a worked‑example table for students to fill in for f(x)=2x²‑8x+6.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Worked‑example table with columns for x, f(x), graph point, root?, vertex?\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"table\",\"headers\":[\"x\",\"f(x)\",\"Graph Point\",\"Root (Yes\\/No)\",\"Vertex?\"],\"rows\":[[\"0\",\"6\",\"(0,6)\",\"No\",\"\"],[\"1\",\"0\",\"(1,0)\",\"Yes\",\"\"],[\"2\",\"-2\",\"(2,-2)\",\"No\",\"\"],[\"3\",\"0\",\"(3,0)\",\"Yes\",\"\"],[\"4\",\"6\",\"(4,6)\",\"No\",\"Yes (h=2, k=-2)\"]]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Walk through the table, showing how each x‑value is plotted, how roots are identified where f(x)=0, and how the vertex emerges from symmetry. Prompt students to compare with the quadratic formula.\"},{\"title\":\"Slide 18 – Summary & Reflection\",\"key_points\":[\"Graphical method provides visual insight into solutions and optimisation\",\"Vertex gives maximum or minimum values crucial for real‑world problems\",\"Combine graphing with algebraic techniques for verification\"],\"activity\":\"Students complete a quick exit ticket: one thing they learned, one question they still have.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Timeline of the learning steps covered in this session.\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"timeline\",\"steps\":[\"Recall quadratic function basics\",\"Plot and read roots from the graph\",\"Calculate and locate the vertex\",\"Apply to a real‑life scenario\",\"Practice with guided example\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Summarise the key takeaways. Collect exit tickets to gauge understanding and plan follow‑up clarification in the next class.\",\"image\":null}]}',0,'ended','2026-11-02 06:27:14','2026-11-01 18:27:14','2026-11-01 18:33:51','all',0,5,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-11-01 18:33:51','RcKRa2GWlIXuTdISv5nMQHBVfWU7VuOSwBWzHtImS2kcBu61FnoKvHkF9sC4N3RJjwXhd7npAHuE6u+k901y07zpgOkW2GblvmifghL+nT+P2BPNIhqvx+8usFc=',1),
