[1,7],[2,9],[3,7],[4,1]],\"x_label\":\"x\",\"y_label\":\"y\",\"title\":\"y = -2x² + 8x + 1 (Vertex at (2,9))\"},\"presenter_notes\":\"Guide students to calculate the vertex using -b\\/(2a) = -8\\/(2·-2) = 2 and verify it on the graph. Discuss why the parabola opens downward, making the vertex a maximum.\"},{\"title\":\"Real‑Life Optimisation: Maximising Arch Height\",\"key_points\":[\"Translate a practical problem into a quadratic model\",\"Use the vertex formula to find the optimum value\",\"Interpret the result in context\"],\"activity\":\"Group task: design a parabolic bridge arch where the height h (metres) is given by h = -0.5x² + 5x + 2 (x = horizontal distance). Find the maximum height and the distance at which it occurs.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Process diagram outlining steps from problem statement to vertex calculation.\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"process\",\"steps\":[\"Write the quadratic expression for the situation\",\"Identify coefficients a, b, c\",\"Compute x‑coordinate of vertex: x = -b\\/(2a)\",\"Substitute x back into the equation to get maximum height\",\"State the optimum distance and height\",\"Discuss practical implications\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Facilitate the groups as they apply the vertex method. Emphasise checking units and realistic feasibility of the solution.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Slide 7: Quick Recap & Choosing a Method\",\"key_points\":[\"Quadratic equations can be solved by factorisation, formula, or completing the square.\",\"Factorisation is efficient when the quadratic factors into integer binomials.\",\"Select the method that best fits the given equation.\"],\"activity\":\"Students watch a 3‑minute pre‑class video comparing the three solution methods and note which method they would choose for each example.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Comparison chart of solution methods\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"comparison\",\"items\":[\"Factorisation method\",\"Quadratic formula\",\"Completing the square\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Introduce the flipped activity. Emphasise that the video is for preview only; class time will be used for practice. Ask students to justify their method choice in the upcoming group discussion.\"},{\"title\":\"Slide 8: Step‑by‑Step Factorisation (a = 1)\",\"key_points\":[\"Write the quadratic in standard form ax² + bx + c.\",\"Find two numbers whose product is c and sum is b.\",\"Rewrite the middle term using those numbers.\",\"Factor by grouping to obtain (x + m)(x + n).\"],\"activity\":\"In pairs, students list the two numbers for three given quadratics on a shared Google Doc.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Process diagram of the factorisation steps\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"process\",\"steps\":[\"Identify a, b, c in ax² + bx + c\",\"Find m and n such that m·n = c and m + n = b\",\"Rewrite bx as mx + nx\",\"Group terms and factor common factors\",\"Write the final binomial factors\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Walk through each step with a simple example (x² + 5x + 6). Highlight common mistakes, such as mixing up signs. Remind students the video already covered the rationale.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Slide 9: Worked Example – x² + 5x + 6\",\"key_points\":[\"Identify c = 6 and b = 5.\",\"Numbers 2 and 3 satisfy 2·3 = 6 and 2+3 = 5.\",\"Rewrite: x² + 2x + 3x + 6.\",\"Factor: x(x + 2) + 3(x + 2) = (x + 3)(x + 2).\"],\"activity\":\"Students complete a guided worksheet that mirrors the table shown, filling in the missing trial pairs.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Table of trial factor pairs\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"table\",\"headers\":[\"Trial Pair (m,n)\",\"Product (m·n)\",\"Sum (m+n)\"],\"rows\":[[\"(1,6)\",\"6\",\"7\"],[\"(2,3)\",\"6\",\"5\"],[\"(-1,-6)\",\"6\",\"-7\"],[\"(-2,-3)\",\"6\",\"-5\"]]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Explain how the table helps eliminate unsuitable pairs quickly. Encourage students to check sign consistency with b. After the example, ask a quick oral check: what would be the factors if b were -5?\"},{\"title\":\"Slide 10: Factorisation When a ≠ 1\",\"key_points\":[\"Multiply a and c to get the product ac.\",\"Find two numbers that multiply to ac and add to b.\",\"Rewrite the middle term and factor by grouping.\",\"Simplify to obtain the final binomial factors.\"],\"activity\":\"Groups receive a different quadratic (e.g., 2x² + 7x + 3) and create their own trial‑pair table before factoring.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Process diagram for a ≠ 1 case\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"process\",\"steps\":[\"Compute ac = a × c\",\"Find m and n such that m·n = ac and m + n = b\",\"Rewrite bx as mx + nx\",\"Group and factor common terms\",\"Factor out the leading coefficient if needed\",\"Write the final factors\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Model the process with 2x² + 7x + 3. Show that ac = 6, m = 6, n = 1, leading to 2x² + 6x + x + 3 → 2x(x + 3) + 1(x + 3) → (2x + 1)(x + 3). Highlight the importance of checking the product ac.\"},{\"title\":\"Slide 11: Real‑Life Application – Projectile Motion\",\"key_points\":[\"The height of a projectile follows h(t) = -½gt² + v₀t + h₀.\",\"Setting h(t) = 0 gives the times when the projectile hits the ground.\",\"Factorising the quadratic yields the launch and landing times.\"],\"activity\":\"Students use the given formula with g = 9.8 m\\/s², v₀ = 20 m\\/s, h₀ = 1.5 m, factorise the resulting quadratic, and determine the total flight time.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Graph of the projectile trajectory with roots marked\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"graph\",\"title\":\"Illustrative projectile path (h = -4.9t² + 20t + 1.5)\",\"points\":[[0,1.5],[2,18.1],[4,1.9],[4.1,0],[0,0]],\"x_label\":\"Time (s)\",\"y_label\":\"Height (m)\",\"note\":\"Points are illustrative; actual roots obtained by factorisation.\"},\"presenter_notes\":\"Explain that the graph shows a parabola opening downwards. The x‑intercepts correspond to the times when height = 0. After students factorise, confirm the roots match the plotted intercepts.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Slide 12: Consolidation Activity – Practice & Word Problem\",\"key_points\":[\"Apply factorisation to diverse quadratics.\",\"Translate a real‑world scenario into a quadratic equation.\",\"Solve the equation by factorisation to answer the question.\"],\"activity\":\"Each group receives a set of three cards: two pure factorisation problems and one word problem. They must solve, present their factors, and explain the solution steps.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Cards displaying the three problems\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"cards\",\"items\":[\"Problem 1: Factorise 3x² – 14x + 8\",\"Problem 2: Factorise x² – 9\",\"Problem 3: A rectangular garden has length (x + 4) m and width (x – 2) m. Its area is 60 m². Find the possible dimensions.\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Circulate while groups work, checking that they correctly set up the quadratic for the word problem (x² + 2x – 8 = 0) before factorising. Use this time for formative assessment and to address misconceptions.\"},{\"title\":\"Slide 13 – Video Recap: Quadratic Functions Overview\",\"key_points\":[\"Quadratic function: f(x)=ax²+bx+c produces a parabola\",\"Direction of opening depends on sign of a\",\"Key features: vertex, axis of symmetry, roots\"],\"activity\":\"Ask students to note any unclear points from the pre‑class video in their learning journal.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Process diagram showing the three main features of a parabola.\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"process\",\"steps\":[\"Identify coefficients a, b, c\",\"Determine direction of opening (sign of a)\",\"Locate vertex and axis of symmetry\",\"Find roots (where f(x)=0)\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Briefly reaffirm the video content. Emphasise that understanding the shape of the parabola is essential before solving it graphically. Invite a few volunteers to share their journal notes.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Slide 14 – Solving Quadratic Equations Using Graphs\",\"key_points\":[\"Plot the quadratic function on a coordinate plane\",\"The x‑intercepts represent the solutions of the equation f(x)=0\",\"If the parabola does not cross the x‑axis, there are no real solutions\"],\"activity\":\"Students work in pairs to sketch the graph of f(x)=x²‑5x+6 on graph paper and mark the roots.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Comparison chart of two graphs: one intersecting the x‑axis, one not.\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"comparison\",\"items\":[\"Graph A: f(x)=x²‑5x+6 – two real roots at x=2 and x=3\",\"Graph B: f(x)=x²+4x+8 – no real roots (parabola above x‑axis)\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Guide students to locate where the curve meets the x‑axis. Highlight that the graphical method gives an approximate solution, useful for checking algebraic results.\"},{\"title\":\"Slide 15 – Finding Maximum or Minimum (Vertex) from the Graph\",\"key_points\":[\"Vertex is the highest point (maximum) if a<0, lowest point (minimum) if a>0\",\"Vertex coordinates: (h, k) where h = -b\\/(2a) and k = f(h)\",\"The vertex gives the optimum value in real‑life problems\"],\"activity\":\"Using the same graph from Slide 14, students locate the vertex and record its coordinates.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Process steps to locate the vertex on a parabola.\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"process\",\"steps\":[\"Calculate h = -b\\/(2a)\",\"Substitute h into the function to find k\",\"Plot point (h, k) on the graph\",\"Label as maximum (a<0) or minimum (a>0)\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Demonstrate the calculation with the example f(x)=x²‑5x+6 (a=1, b=-5). Show that h=2.5, k= -0.25, giving a minimum point. Connect this to optimisation scenarios.\"},{\"title\":\"Slide 16 – Real‑Life Application: Maximising Rocket Height\",\"key_points\":[\"Model rocket height: h(t)=‑5t²+40t+10 (t in seconds)\",\"The vertex gives the time of maximum height and the height itself\",\"Interpret the result in the context of the problem\"],\"activity\":\"Students calculate the vertex of the given function and write a short explanation of what the values mean for the rocket launch.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Cards showing each step of the application.\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"cards\",\"items\":[\"Identify coefficients: a=‑5, b=40, c=10\",\"Compute h = -b\\/(2a) = -40\\/(‑10) = 4 s\",\"Find k = h(4) = ‑5(4)²+40(4)+10 = 90 m\",\"Interpretation: Maximum height of 90 m reached at 4 s\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Link the mathematical process to the engineering context. Encourage students to think of other scenarios (e.g., profit maximisation) where the vertex is useful.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Slide 17 – Guided Practice: Plot, Solve & Locate Vertex\",\"key_points\":[\"Plot the quadratic, identify roots, and find the vertex\",\"Check graphical solutions against algebraic methods\",\"Reflect on accuracy and usefulness of the graph\"],\"activity\":\"Provide a worked‑example table for students to fill in for f(x)=2x²‑8x+6.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Worked‑example table with columns for x, f(x), graph point, root?, vertex?\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"table\",\"headers\":[\"x\",\"f(x)\",\"Graph Point\",\"Root (Yes\\/No)\",\"Vertex?\"],\"rows\":[[\"0\",\"6\",\"(0,6)\",\"No\",\"\"],[\"1\",\"0\",\"(1,0)\",\"Yes\",\"\"],[\"2\",\"-2\",\"(2,-2)\",\"No\",\"\"],[\"3\",\"0\",\"(3,0)\",\"Yes\",\"\"],[\"4\",\"6\",\"(4,6)\",\"No\",\"Yes (h=2, k=-2)\"]]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Walk through the table, showing how each x‑value is plotted, how roots are identified where f(x)=0, and how the vertex emerges from symmetry. Prompt students to compare with the quadratic formula.\"},{\"title\":\"Slide 18 – Summary & Reflection\",\"key_points\":[\"Graphical method provides visual insight into solutions and optimisation\",\"Vertex gives maximum or minimum values crucial for real‑world problems\",\"Combine graphing with algebraic techniques for verification\"],\"activity\":\"Students complete a quick exit ticket: one thing they learned, one question they still have.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"Timeline of the learning steps covered in this session.\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"timeline\",\"steps\":[\"Recall quadratic function basics\",\"Plot and read roots from the graph\",\"Calculate and locate the vertex\",\"Apply to a real‑life scenario\",\"Practice with guided example\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Summarise the key takeaways. Collect exit tickets to gauge understanding and plan follow‑up clarification in the next class.\",\"image\":null}]}',5,'ended','2026-11-02 06:35:05','2026-11-01 18:35:05','2026-11-01 19:08:45','group',7,5,'[]',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-11-01 19:08:45','5VOJYcEJHbJ53lpe0gMju1yTv7AIuTZWDw7s9udYiL/V5w8GjDcexTduaD2afRrenqr3tyh58+r0hr6oGlqndtpGf0m9KaPZ0YqOsQmuFc0FRSs00aWqryDn/NY=',1),
(10,1,'&OUް���U�\n���\0k�	��nS�=�?�#','Investigating Partial Variation · presentation outline','{\"subject\":\"Mathematics\",\"year\":\"SHS Year 3\",\"template\":\"inquiry\",\"visual_theme\":\"vibrant\",\"slides\":[{\"title\":\"What is Partial Variation?\",\"key_points\":[\"A relationship with a fixed component (c) and a variable component (k·x)\",\"Graph shifts upward; does not pass through the origin\",\"Common in fees that include a base charge plus usage\"],\"activity\":\"Students list everyday costs that combine a base fee with a usage charge.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":null,\"presenter_notes\":\"Introduce the term \'partial variation\' from the curriculum excerpt. Emphasise the constant term c and relate it to real‑life examples like a monthly subscription fee plus per‑unit usage. Prompt students to think of other scenarios.\",\"image\":{\"url\":\"data:image\\/jpeg;base64,\\/9j\\/4AAQSkZJRgABAQAASABIAAD\\/4QBMRXhpZgAATU0AKgAAAAgAAYdpAAQAAAABAAAAGgAAAAAAA6ABAAMAAAABAAEAAKACAAQAAAABAAAC9KADAAQAAAABAAAB+AAAAAD\\/7QA4UGhvdG9zaG9wIDMuMAA4QklNBAQAAAAAAAA4QklNBCUAAAAAABDUHYzZjwCyBOmACZjs+EJ+\\/8AAEQgB+AL0AwEiAAIRAQMRAf\\/EAB8AAAEFAQEBAQEBAAAAAAAAAAABAgMEBQYHCAkKC\\/\\/EALUQAAIBAwMCBAMFBQQEAAABfQECAwAEEQUSITFBBhNRYQcicRQygZGhCCNCscEVUtHwJDNicoIJChYXGBkaJSYnKCkqNDU2Nzg5OkNERUZHSElKU1RVVldYWVpjZGVmZ2hpanN0dXZ3eHl6g4SFhoeIiYqSk5SVlpeYmZqio6Slpqeoqaqys7S1tre4ubrCw8TFxsfIycrS09TV1tfY2drh4uPk5ebn6Onq8fLz9PX29\\/j5+v\\/EAB8BAAMBAQEBAQEBAQEAAAAAAAABAgMEBQYHCAkKC\\/\\/EALURAAIBAgQEAwQHBQQEAAECdwABAgMRBAUhMQYSQVEHYXETIjKBCBRCkaGxwQkjM1LwFWJy0QoWJDThJfEXGBkaJicoKSo1Njc4OTpDREVGR0hJSlNUVVZXWFlaY2RlZmdoaWpzdHV2d3h5eoKDhIWGh4iJipKTlJWWl5iZmqKjpKWmp6ipqrKztLW2t7i5usLDxMXGx8jJytLT1NXW19jZ2uLj5OXm5+jp6vLz9PX29\\/j5+v\\/bAEMACQkJCQkJEAkJEBYQEBAWHhYWFhYeJh4eHh4eJi4mJiYmJiYuLi4uLi4uLjc3Nzc3N0BAQEBASEhISEhISEhISP\\/bAEMBCwwMEhESHxERH0szKjNLS0tLS0tLS0tLS0tLS0tLS0tLS0tLS0tLS0tLS0tLS0tLS0tLS0tLS0tLS0tLS0tLS\\/\\/dAAQAMP\\/aAAwDAQACEQMRAD8A9vooryD4i+J7mGX+wrFzH8oMzKcE7ui\\/TH