35FH9k+Kf+glH\\/35FFo\\/zL8f8hnQUVz\\/APZPin\\/oIx\\/9+RS\\/2T4o\\/wCgjH\\/35FFo\\/wAyA36KwP7J8U\\/9BGL\\/AL8ik\\/snxR\\/0Eo\\/+\\/IotH+Zf18gOgorA\\/snxR\\/0Eo\\/8AvyKP7J8U\\/wDQRj\\/78ii0f5l\\/XyAv6rqMOlWMl5KRlRhF7s5+6o+v8uam8O2EmmaLbWcv30TLD0ZiWI\\/AnFU7Lw2I7pdQ1W4e9uIzlC4ConuqDgHjr+PWumqJyVuVEthS0lLWIj\\/\\/1vb6KKKACiiigAooooAKKKKACiiigAooooAKKKKACiiigAoopaAEooooAKKKKACiiigAooooAKKKKACiiloASilpKACiiigAooooAKKKKACiiigAooooAKKKKACiiigAoopaAEooooAKKKKACiiloASiiigAooooAKKKKACilpKACiiigAooooAKKWkoAKKKKACiiigAooooAKKKKACiiigAooooAKKKKACiiigAooooAKKKKACiiigAooooAKKKKACiiigD\\/9k=\",\"width\":756,\"height\":504,\"alt\":\"7D436F05-65A9-4B37-868A-BB9D06CE4276\",\"source_url\":\"\",\"attribution\":\"\",\"license\":\"Teacher-provided image\"}},{\"title\":\"Direct vs Partial Variation\",\"key_points\":[\"Direct variation: y = kx, passes through (0,0)\",\"Partial variation: y = kx + c, intercept c ≠ 0\",\"Graph of partial variation is a shifted straight line\"],\"activity\":\"Plot two data sets on graph paper and identify the y‑intercept of each.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"comparison\",\"title\":\"Direct vs Partial Variation\",\"items\":[\"Direct Variation\",\"Partial Variation\"],\"descriptions\":[\"y = kx (origin)\",\"y = kx + c (intercept c)\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Use the verified excerpt to explain that partial variation builds on direct variation by adding a constant start value. Highlight how the graph moves up by c units. Ask learners to predict the intercept before plotting.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Investigating Partial Variation with Data\",\"key_points\":[\"Identify fixed cost (c) from the y‑intercept\",\"Determine variable rate (k) from the slope\",\"Form the equation y = kx + c\"],\"activity\":\"Given sample data, students calculate k and c and write the governing equation.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"table\",\"title\":\"Sample Data\",\"headers\":[\"Units (x)\",\"Total Cost (y)\"],\"rows\":[[\"0\",\"50\"],[\"5\",\"100\"],[\"10\",\"150\"]]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Guide pupils through the table: the cost when 0 units are used reveals the fixed component (c = 50). Use two points to compute the slope k = (Δy)\\/(Δx) = (100‑50)\\/(5‑0) = 10. Write the equation y = 10x + 50 and check with the third row.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Solving Real‑Life Problems\",\"key_points\":[\"Translate the word problem into y = kx + c\",\"Insert known values and solve for the unknown\",\"Verify the solution by substitution\"],\"activity\":\"Solve a budgeting problem: a mobile plan costs GH₵30 base plus GH₵5 per GB. Find the cost for 12 GB.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"process\",\"title\":\"Problem‑Solving Steps\",\"steps\":[\"Identify fixed cost (c) and variable rate (k)\",\"Write the equation y = kx + c\",\"Substitute the known quantity (x) and solve for y\",\"Check the answer by recalculating\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Link back to the inquiry approach: students first explore the situation, then formulate the equation, compute, and finally reflect on the answer. Emphasise checking work, as required by the curriculum standard on solving real‑life issues.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Identifying Partial Variation\",\"key_points\":[\"Fixed component added to a variable part\",\"Seen in fees, tariffs, and mixed costs\",\"Differs from direct variation (no fixed start)\"],\"activity\":\"Examine a sample price list and label each charge as fixed or variable.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"cards\",\"title\":\"Components of Partial Variation\",\"items\":[\"Fixed component (e.g., base fee)\",\"Variable component (e.g., per‑unit charge)\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Introduce the concept with everyday examples such as mobile phone plans or bus fares. Emphasise that the relationship starts from a non‑zero intercept. Prompt students to think of other contexts where a constant charge is added to a usage‑based amount.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Representing Partial Variation Algebraically\",\"key_points\":[\"Equation form: y = k + mx\",\"k = fixed part, m = rate of change\",\"Graph is a straight line with y‑intercept k\"],\"activity\":\"From a given data table, write the equation that models the relationship.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"process\",\"title\":\"From Data to Equation\",\"steps\":[\"List pairs (x, y)\",\"Find the constant difference (k)\",\"Calculate the slope (m)\",\"Combine into y = k + mx\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Guide students through each step using a simple example (e.g., cost of printing where there is a setup fee). Highlight how the intercept represents the fixed cost and the slope the variable rate. Encourage questioning: why does the line not pass through the origin?\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Investigating Partial Variation with Data\",\"key_points\":[\"Collect real‑world measurements\",\"Plot points to see linear trend\",\"Determine k and m from the graph\"],\"activity\":\"In groups, plot the supplied data on graph paper and draw the best‑fit line.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"table\",\"title\":\"Sample Data for Partial Variation\",\"headers\":[\"Quantity (x)\",\"Total Cost (y)\"],\"rows\":[[\"1\",\"₵12\"],[\"2\",\"₵22\"],[\"3\",\"₵32\"],[\"4\",\"₵42\"]]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Explain that the data illustrate a fixed charge of ₵2 plus ₵10 per unit. Ask learners to verify the pattern by calculating differences. After plotting, discuss how the line’s intercept and slope correspond to the fixed and variable parts.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Generalising and Solving Real‑Life Problems\",\"key_points\":[\"Use y = k + mx to predict unknown values\",\"Check solutions against context\",\"Reflect on assumptions (linearity, constant rate)\"],\"activity\":\"Solve a budgeting problem: estimate the cost of 7 units given the model.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"comparison\",\"title\":\"Impact of Using the Model\",\"items\":[\"Guesswork only – may miss fixed cost\",\"Model y = k + mx – accurate prediction\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Present a scenario such as hiring a venue with a booking fee plus hourly charge. Walk through the calculation using the derived equation, then ask students to critique the result (e.g., does it make sense?). Highlight the value of proportional reasoning in planning and budgeting.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Exploring Partial Variation\",\"key_points\":[\"Fixed component added to a variable part\",\"Real‑life examples: service fees, base salaries\",\"Builds on direct variation\"],\"activity\":\"Students list everyday situations that include a base charge plus a usage charge.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"cards\",\"title\":\"Partial Variation Basics\",\"items\":[\"Fixed component (c)\",\"Variable part proportional to x (mx)\",\"Total amount = mx + c\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Introduce the concept with a short story (e.g., mobile phone plan). Emphasise that unlike direct variation, the graph does not pass through the origin. Prompt learners to think of other contexts where a constant fee is added. Link to the curriculum indicator on using proportional reasoning for partial variations.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Formulating the Equation\",\"key_points\":[\"Equation form: y = mx + c\",\"m = rate of change, c = fixed amount\",\"Connects to proportional reasoning\"],\"activity\":\"Given a table of costs, students identify m and c and write the equation.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"process\",\"title\":\"Deriving the Equation\",\"steps\":[\"Identify the fixed cost (c) from the intercept\",\"Determine the rate of change (m) from the slope\",\"Combine into y = mx + c\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Guide learners to plot the given points quickly (use graph paper or digital tool). Highlight how the slope represents the variable component per unit change. Ask probing questions: ‘What does the intercept tell us about the situation?’ Reinforce the link between the algebraic form and the real‑world story introduced earlier.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Investigating with Tables\",\"key_points\":[\"Create a value table for x and y\",\"Observe constant increase of y when x increases by 1\",\"Calculate m and c from the table\"],\"activity\":\"In groups, fill in the table and compute the rate and fixed amount.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"table\",\"title\":\"Sample Values Table\",\"headers\":[\"x\",\"y\"],\"rows\":[[\"1\",\"c + m\"],[\"2\",\"c + 2m\"],[\"3\",\"c + 3m\"]]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Explain that each step up in x adds the same amount m to y, confirming proportionality of the variable part. Have students verify the pattern by calculating differences between successive y‑values. Use this to solidify the concept of constant rate and fixed start value before moving to problem solving.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Solving a Real‑Life Problem\",\"key_points\":[\"Apply y = mx + c to a budgeting scenario\",\"Check solution against context\",\"Generalise the method for similar problems\"],\"activity\":\"Solve a word problem: a venue charges GHS 150 booking fee plus GHS 25 per hour; find cost for 6 hours.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"comparison\",\"title\":\"Problem‑Solving Steps\",\"items\":[\"Set up equation (y = 25x + 150)\",\"Insert known value (x = 6)\",\"Calculate y = 300\",\"Interpret: total cost is GHS 300\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Walk through the problem aloud, encouraging students to identify the fixed and variable parts. After solving, ask them to explain why the answer makes sense (e.g., 6 hours × 25 = 150 plus 150 booking = 300). Prompt them to write a short generalisation: ‘For any partial variation, total = fixed + rate × quantity.’ Connect back to the learning indicator and suggest how this reasoning extends to other contexts.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Slide 13 – Investigating Partial Variation\",\"key_points\":[\"Cost = fixed component + variable component\",\"Relationship is linear with a non‑zero intercept\"],\"activity\":\"Collect taxi‑fare data (base fare + per‑km charge) and plot cost vs distance.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"geometry\",\"title\":\"Partial Variation Line\",\"x_min\":-0.7415999999999999,\"x_max\":12.7416,\"y_min\":-8.652,\"y_max\":148.652,\"shapes\":[{\"shape\":\"line\",\"points\":[[0,20],[10,120]],\"label\":\"Cost = 20 + 10·d\"}]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Explain that many real‑world situations start with a base amount (e.g., a taxi’s flag‑fall). The total cost grows proportionally with distance, giving a straight line that does not pass through the origin. Guide students to identify the fixed (20) and variable (10) components from the plotted points.\",\"image\":null},{\"title\":\"Slide 14 – Generalising and Solving Partial‑Variation Problems\",\"key_points\":[\"Form equation y = mx + c\",\"Solve for the unknown variable\"],\"activity\":\"Solve a budgeting problem: a phone plan charges $15 monthly plus $0.05 per minute; find minutes for a $30 bill.\",\"visual_suggestion\":\"\",\"visual\":{\"type\":\"process\",\"title\":\"Problem‑Solving Process\",\"items\":[\"Write the equation y = mx + c\",\"Insert known values\",\"Isolate the unknown (x)\",\"Check the solution in the original context\"]},\"presenter_notes\":\"Model the translation from words to the algebraic form y = 0.05x + 15. Show the algebraic manipulation to find x = (30‑15)\\/0.05 = 300 minutes. Emphasise checking the answer by substituting back into the original situation.\",\"image\":null}]}','{\"html\":\"<article class=\\\"output-card\\\"><div class=\\\"output-heading\\\"><div><h2>Draft preview<\\/h2><p>Teacher-authored template · verify against the official curriculum<\\/p><\\/div><div class=\\\"output-actions\\\"><button class=\\\"small-button\\\" data-action=\\\"print\\\">Print \\/ save as PDF<\\/button><button class=\\\"small-button\\\" data-action=\\\"save\\\">Save draft<\\/button><\\/div><\\/div><div class=\\\"output-body\\\"><div class=\\\"document-title-block\\\"><div class=\\\"eyebrow\\\">POWERPOINT PRESENTATION · TEACHER REVIEW DRAFT<\\/div><h2>Investigating Partial Variation<\\/h2><p>SUSMAMAN SHS<\\/p><\\/div><div class=\\\"document-meta\\\"><div class=\\\"meta-box\\\"><small>Subject<\\/small><strong>Mathematics<\\/strong><\\/div><div class=\\\"meta-box\\\"><small>SHS year<\\/small><strong>SHS Year 3<\\/strong><\\/div><div class=\\\"meta-box\\\"><small>Teaching structure<\\/small><strong>Inquiry &amp; discovery<\\/strong><\\/div><div class=\\\"meta-box\\\"><small>Slide design<\\/small><strong>Vibrant workshop<\\/strong><\\/div><div class=\\\"meta-box\\\"><small>Number of slides<\\/small><strong>14<\\/strong><\\/div><\\/div><div class=\\\"document-section\\\"><h3>Curriculum scope<\\/h3><ul><li>Semester 1 · Week 5: Use proportional reasoning to investigate partial variations, make generalisations and solve · NUMBERS FOR EVERYDAY LIFE · Proportional Reasoning · Content standard: Demonstrate conceptual understanding of proportionality in relation to variation and use it to solve real-life problems. · Variation 2 · VARIATION 2 · Source: Mathematics_Y3_TM_ONLINE-VERSION_FINAL.pdf · MOE Verified · p. 27<\\/li><\\/ul><\\/div><div class=\\\"document-section\\\"><h3>Learning outcomes<\\/h3><ul><\\/ul><\\/div><div class=\\\"document-section\\\"><h3>Learning indicators<\\/h3><ul><\\/ul><p>Source-recorded targets are included where available; any additional suggestions are teacher drafts and are not represented as official curriculum wording.<\\/p><\\/div><div class=\\\"document-section\\\"><h3>Presentation preview · Inquiry &amp; discovery<\\/h3><p>PowerPoint design: Vibrant workshop. Slides can combine editable charts and diagrams with uploaded images. Review the visual on each slide before presenting.<\\/p><div class=\\\"presentation-preview-grid\\\"><article class=\\\"presentation-slide-preview presentation-theme-vibrant presentation-template-inquiry\\\"><header><span>SLIDE 01<\\/span><small>Mathematics · SHS Year 3<\\/small><\\/header><h4>What is Partial Variation?<\\/h4><ul><li>A relationship with a fixed component (c) and a variable component (k·x)<\\/li><li>Graph shifts upward; does not pass through the origin<\\/li><li>Common in fees that include a base charge plus usage<\\/li><\\/ul><div class=\\\"presentation-media-column\\\"><figure class=\\\"presentation-slide-image\\\"><img src=\\\"data:image\\/jpeg;base64,\\/9j\\/4AAQSkZJRgABAQAASABIAAD\\/4QBMRXhpZgAATU0AKgAAAAgAAYdpAAQAAAABAAAAGgAAAAAAA6ABAAMAAAABAAEAAKACAAQAAAABAAAC9KADAAQAAAABAAAB+AAAAAD\\/7QA4UGhvdG9zaG9wIDMuMAA4QklNBAQAAAAAAAA4QklNBCUAAAAAABDUHYzZjwCyBOmACZjs+EJ+\\/8AAEQgB+AL0AwEiAAIRAQMRAf\\/EAB8AAAEFAQEBAQEBAAAAAAAAAAABAgMEBQYHCAkKC\\/\\/EALUQAAIBAwMCBAMFBQQEAAABfQECAwAEEQUSITFBBhNRYQcicRQygZGhCCNCscEVUtHwJDNicoIJChYXGBkaJSYnKCkqNDU2Nzg5OkNERUZHSElKU1RVVldYWVpjZGVmZ2hpanN0dXZ3eHl6g4SFhoeIiYqSk5SVlpeYmZqio6Slpqeoqaqys7S1tre4ubrCw8TFxsfIycrS09TV1tfY2drh4uPk5ebn6Onq8fLz9PX29\\/j5+v\\/EAB8BAAMBAQEBAQEBAQEAAAAAAAABAgMEBQYHCAkKC\\/\\/EALURAAIBAgQEAwQHBQQEAAECdwABAgMRBAUhMQYSQVEHYXETIjKBCBRCkaGxwQkjM1LwFWJy0QoWJDThJfEXGBkaJicoKSo1Njc4OTpDREVGR0hJSlNUVVZXWFlaY2RlZmdoaWpzdHV2d3h5eoKDhIWGh4iJipKTlJWWl5iZmqKjpKWmp6ipqrKztLW2t7i5usLDxMXGx8jJytLT1NXW19jZ2uLj5OXm5+jp6vLz9PX29\\/j5+v\\/bAEMACQkJCQkJEAkJEBYQEBAWHhYWFhYeJh4eHh4eJi4mJiYmJiYuLi4uLi4uLjc3Nzc3N0BAQEBASEhISEhISEhISP\\/bAEMBCwwMEhESHxERH0szKjNLS0tLS0tLS0tLS0tLS0tLS0tLS0tLS0tLS0tLS0tLS0tLS0tLS0tLS0tLS0tLS0tLS\\/\\/dAAQAMP\\/aAAwDAQACEQMRAD8A9vooryD4i+J7mGX+wrFzH8oMzKcE7ui\\/THJ9c4+twg5OyJlJRV2ekz6\\/oltIYp72BHBwVMi5B9xniov+Em8Pf8\\/9v\\/38X\\/Gvlmiur6su5h7Z9j6m\\/wCEm8Pf8\\/8Ab\\/8Afxf8aT\\/hJvD3\\/P8A2\\/8A38X\\/ABr5apKPqy7h7Z9j6m\\/4Sbw9\\/wA\\/9v8A9\\/F\\/xo\\/4Sbw9\\/wA\\/9v8A9\\/F\\/xr5apM0fVl3D2z7H1P8A8JN4e\\/5\\/7f8A7+L\\/AI0n\\/CTeHv8An\\/t\\/+\\/i\\/418s0UfVl3D2z7H1N\\/wk3h7\\/AJ\\/7f\\/v4v+NH\\/CT+Hv8An\\/t\\/+\\/i\\/418s8UtH1Zdw9s+x9S\\/8JP4e\\/wCf+3\\/7+L\\/jR\\/wk3h7\\/AJ\\/7f\\/v4