>SLIDE 02<\\/span><small>Mathematics · SHS Year 3<\\/small><\\/header><h4>Direct vs Partial Variation<\\/h4><ul><li>Direct variation: y = kx, passes through (0,0)<\\/li><li>Partial variation: y = kx + c, intercept c ≠ 0<\\/li><li>Graph of partial variation is a shifted straight line<\\/li><\\/ul><div class=\\\"presentation-visual presentation-visual-cards\\\" role=\\\"img\\\" aria-label=\\\"Direct vs Partial Variation\\\"><strong>Direct vs Partial Variation<\\/strong><div><span><b>01<\\/b>Direct Variation<\\/span><span><b>02<\\/b>Partial Variation<\\/span><\\/div><\\/div><section class=\\\"presentation-slide-activity\\\"><strong><i class=\\\"fa-solid fa-people-group\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\"><\\/i> Classroom activity<\\/strong><p>Plot two data sets on graph paper and identify the y‑intercept of each.<\\/p><\\/section><\\/article><article class=\\\"presentation-slide-preview presentation-theme-vibrant presentation-template-inquiry\\\"><header><span>SLIDE 03<\\/span><small>Mathematics · SHS Year 3<\\/small><\\/header><h4>Investigating Partial Variation with Data<\\/h4><ul><li>Identify fixed cost (c) from the y‑intercept<\\/li><li>Determine variable rate (k) from the slope<\\/li><li>Form the equation y = kx + c<\\/li><\\/ul><div class=\\\"presentation-visual presentation-visual-table\\\" role=\\\"img\\\" aria-label=\\\"Sample Data\\\"><strong>Sample Data<\\/strong><div class=\\\"presentation-visual-table-scroll\\\"><table><thead><tr><th>Units (x)<\\/th><th>Total Cost (y)<\\/th><\\/tr><\\/thead><tbody><tr><td>0<\\/td><td>50<\\/td><\\/tr><tr><td>5<\\/td><td>100<\\/td><\\/tr><tr><td>10<\\/td><td>150<\\/td><\\/tr><\\/tbody><\\/table><\\/div><\\/div><section class=\\\"presentation-slide-activity\\\"><strong><i class=\\\"fa-solid fa-people-group\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\"><\\/i> Classroom activity<\\/strong><p>Given sample data, students calculate k and c and write the governing equation.<\\/p><\\/section><\\/article><article class=\\\"presentation-slide-preview presentation-theme-vibrant presentation-template-inquiry\\\"><header><span>SLIDE 04<\\/span><small>Mathematics · SHS Year 3<\\/small><\\/header><h4>Solving Real‑Life Problems<\\/h4><ul><li>Translate the word problem into y = kx + c<\\/li><li>Insert known values and solve for the unknown<\\/li><li>Verify the solution by substitution<\\/li><\\/ul><div class=\\\"presentation-visual presentation-visual-cards\\\" role=\\\"img\\\" aria-label=\\\"Problem‑Solving Steps\\\"><strong>Problem‑Solving Steps<\\/strong><div><span><b>01<\\/b>Identify fixed cost (c) and variable rate (k)<\\/span><span><b>02<\\/b>Write the equation y = kx + c<\\/span><span><b>03<\\/b>Substitute the known quantity (x) and solve for y<\\/span><span><b>04<\\/b>Check the answer by recalculating<\\/span><\\/div><\\/div><section class=\\\"presentation-slide-activity\\\"><strong><i class=\\\"fa-solid fa-people-group\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\"><\\/i> Classroom activity<\\/strong><p>Solve a budgeting problem: a mobile plan costs GH₵30 base plus GH₵5 per GB. Find the cost for 12 GB.<\\/p><\\/section><\\/article><article class=\\\"presentation-slide-preview presentation-theme-vibrant presentation-template-inquiry\\\"><header><span>SLIDE 05<\\/span><small>Mathematics · SHS Year 3<\\/small><\\/header><h4>Identifying Partial Variation<\\/h4><ul><li>Fixed component added to a variable part<\\/li><li>Seen in fees, tariffs, and mixed costs<\\/li><li>Differs from direct variation (no fixed start)<\\/li><\\/ul><div class=\\\"presentation-visual presentation-visual-cards\\\" role=\\\"img\\\" aria-label=\\\"Components of Partial Variation\\\"><strong>Components of Partial Variation<\\/strong><div><span><b>01<\\/b>Fixed component (e.g., base fee)<\\/span><span><b>02<\\/b>Variable component (e.g., per‑unit charge)<\\/span><\\/div><\\/div><section class=\\\"presentation-slide-activity\\\"><strong><i class=\\\"fa-solid fa-people-group\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\"><\\/i> Classroom activity<\\/strong><p>Examine a sample price list and label each charge as fixed or variable.<\\/p><\\/section><\\/article><article class=\\\"presentation-slide-preview presentation-theme-vibrant presentation-template-inquiry\\\"><header><span>SLIDE 06<\\/span><small>Mathematics · SHS Year 3<\\/small><\\/header><h4>Representing Partial Variation Algebraically<\\/h4><ul><li>Equation form: y = k + mx<\\/li><li>k = fixed part, m = rate of change<\\/li><li>Graph is a straight line with y‑intercept k<\\/li><\\/ul><div class=\\\"presentation-visual presentation-visual-cards\\\" role=\\\"img\\\" aria-label=\\\"From Data to Equation\\\"><strong>From Data to Equation<\\/strong><div><span><b>01<\\/b>List pairs (x, y)<\\/span><span><b>02<\\/b>Find the constant difference (k)<\\/span><span><b>03<\\/b>Calculate the slope (m)<\\/span><span><b>04<\\/b>Combine into y = k + mx<\\/span><\\/div><\\/div><section class=\\\"presentation-slide-activity\\\"><strong><i class=\\\"fa-solid fa-people-group\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\"><\\/i> Classroom activity<\\/strong><p>From a given data table, write the equation that models the relationship.<\\/p><\\/section><\\/article><article class=\\\"presentation-slide-preview presentation-theme-vibrant presentation-template-inquiry\\\"><header><span>SLIDE 07<\\/span><small>Mathematics · SHS Year 3<\\/small><\\/header><h4>Investigating Partial Variation with Data<\\/h4><ul><li>Collect real‑world measurements<\\/li><li>Plot points to see linear trend<\\/li><li>Determine k and m from the graph<\\/li><\\/ul><div class=\\\"presentation-visual presentation-visual-table\\\" role=\\\"img\\\" aria-label=\\\"Sample Data for Partial Variation\\\"><strong>Sample Data for Partial Variation<\\/strong><div class=\\\"presentation-visual-table-scroll\\\"><table><thead><tr><th>Quantity (x)<\\/th><th>Total Cost (y)<\\/th><\\/tr><\\/thead><tbody><tr><td>1<\\/td><td>₵12<\\/td><\\/tr><tr><td>2<\\/td><td>₵22<\\/td><\\/tr><tr><td>3<\\/td><td>₵32<\\/td><\\/tr><\\/tbody><\\/table><\\/div><\\/div><section class=\\\"presentation-slide-activity\\\"><strong><i class=\\\"fa-solid fa-people-group\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\"><\\/i> Classroom activity<\\/strong><p>In groups, plot the supplied data on graph paper and draw the best‑fit line.<\\/p><\\/section><\\/article><article class=\\\"presentation-slide-preview presentation-theme-vibrant presentation-template-inquiry\\\"><header><span>SLIDE 08<\\/span><small>Mathematics · SHS Year 3<\\/small><\\/header><h4>Generalising and Solving Real‑Life Problems<\\/h4><ul><li>Use y = k + mx to predict unknown values<\\/li><li>Check solutions against context<\\/li><li>Reflect on assumptions (linearity, constant rate)<\\/li><\\/ul><div class=\\\"presentation-visual presentation-visual-cards\\\" role=\\\"img\\\" aria-label=\\\"Impact of Using the Model\\\"><strong>Impact of Using the Model<\\/strong><div><span><b>01<\\/b>Guesswork only – may miss fixed cost<\\/span><span><b>02<\\/b>Model y = k + mx – accurate prediction<\\/span><\\/div><\\/div><section class=\\\"presentation-slide-activity\\\"><strong><i class=\\\"fa-solid fa-people-group\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\"><\\/i> Classroom activity<\\/strong><p>Solve a budgeting problem: estimate the cost of 7 units given the model.<\\/p><\\/section><\\/article><article class=\\\"presentation-slide-preview presentation-theme-vibrant presentation-template-inquiry\\\"><header><span>SLIDE 09<\\/span><small>Mathematics · SHS Year 3<\\/small><\\/header><h4>Exploring Partial Variation<\\/h4><ul><li>Fixed component added to a variable part<\\/li><li>Real‑life examples: service fees, base salaries<\\/li><li>Builds on direct variation<\\/li><\\/ul><div class=\\\"presentation-visual presentation-visual-cards\\\" role=\\\"img\\\" aria-label=\\\"Partial Variation Basics\\\"><strong>Partial Variation Basics<\\/strong><div><span><b>01<\\/b>Fixed component (c)<\\/span><span><b>02<\\/b>Variable part proportional to x (mx)<\\/span><span><b>03<\\/b>Total amount = mx + c<\\/span><\\/div><\\/div><section class=\\\"presentation-slide-activity\\\"><strong><i class=\\\"fa-solid fa-people-group\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\"><\\/i> Classroom activity<\\/strong><p>Students list everyday situations that include a base charge plus a usage charge.<\\/p><\\/section><\\/article><article class=\\\"presentation-slide-preview presentation-theme-vibrant presentation-template-inquiry\\\"><header><span>SLIDE 10<\\/span><small>Mathematics · SHS Year 3<\\/small><\\/header><h4>Formulating the Equation<\\/h4><ul><li>Equation form: y = mx + c<\\/li><li>m = rate of change, c = fixed amount<\\/li><li>Connects to proportional reasoning<\\/li><\\/ul><div class=\\\"presentation-visual presentation-visual-cards\\\" role=\\\"img\\\" aria-label=\\\"Deriving the Equation\\\"><strong>Deriving the Equation<\\/strong><div><span><b>01<\\/b>Identify the fixed cost (c) from the intercept<\\/span><span><b>02<\\/b>Determine the rate of change (m) from the slope<\\/span><span><b>03<\\/b>Combine into y = mx + c<\\/span><\\/div><\\/div><section class=\\\"presentation-slide-activity\\\"><strong><i class=\\\"fa-solid fa-people-group\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\"><\\/i> Classroom activity<\\/strong><p>Given a table of costs, students identify m and c and write the equation.<\\/p><\\/section><\\/article><article class=\\\"presentation-slide-preview presentation-theme-vibrant presentation-template-inquiry\\\"><header><span>SLIDE 11<\\/span><small>Mathematics · SHS Year 3<\\/small><\\/header><h4>Investigating with Tables<\\/h4><ul><li>Create a value table for x and y<\\/li><li>Observe constant increase of y when x increases by 1<\\/li><li>Calculate m and c from the table<\\/li><\\/ul><div class=\\\"presentation-visual presentation-visual-table\\\" role=\\\"img\\\" aria-label=\\\"Sample Values Table\\\"><strong>Sample Values Table<\\/strong><div class=\\\"presentation-visual-table-scroll\\\"><table><thead><tr><th>x<\\/th><th>y<\\/th><\\/tr><\\/thead><tbody><tr><td>1<\\/td><td>c + m<\\/td><\\/tr><tr><td>2<\\/td><td>c + 2m<\\/td><\\/tr><tr><td>3<\\/td><td>c + 3m<\\/td><\\/tr><\\/tbody><\\/table><\\/div><\\/div><section class=\\\"presentation-slide-activity\\\"><strong><i class=\\\"fa-solid fa-people-group\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\"><\\/i> Classroom activity<\\/strong><p>In groups, fill in the table and compute the rate and fixed amount.<\\/p><\\/section><\\/article><article class=\\\"presentation-slide-preview presentation-theme-vibrant presentation-template-inquiry\\\"><header><span>SLIDE 12<\\/span><small>Mathematics · SHS Year 3<\\/small><\\/header><h4>Solving a Real‑Life Problem<\\/h4><ul><li>Apply y = mx + c to a budgeting scenario<\\/li><li>Check solution against context<\\/li><li>Generalise the method for similar problems<\\/li><\\/ul><div class=\\\"presentation-visual presentation-visual-cards\\\" role=\\\"img\\\" aria-label=\\\"Problem‑Solving Steps\\\"><strong>Problem‑Solving Steps<\\/strong><div><span><b>01<\\/b>Set up equation (y = 25x + 150)<\\/span><span><b>02<\\/b>Insert known value (x = 6)<\\/span><span><b>03<\\/b>Calculate y = 300<\\/span><span><b>04<\\/b>Interpret: total cost is GHS 300<\\/span><\\/div><\\/div><section class=\\\"presentation-slide-activity\\\"><strong><i class=\\\"fa-solid fa-people-group\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\"><\\/i> Classroom activity<\\/strong><p>Solve a word problem: a venue charges GHS 150 booking fee plus GHS 25 per hour; find cost for 6 hours.<\\/p><\\/section><\\/article><article class=\\\"presentation-slide-preview presentation-theme-vibrant presentation-template-inquiry\\\"><header><span>SLIDE 13<\\/span><small>Mathematics · SHS Year 3<\\/small><\\/header><h4>Slide 13 – Investigating Partial Variation<\\/h4><ul><li>Cost = fixed component + variable component<\\/li><li>Relationship is linear with a non‑zero intercept<\\/li><\\/ul><div class=\\\"presentation-visual presentation-visual-geometry\\\" role=\\\"group\\\" aria-label=\\\"Partial Variation Line\\\"><strong>Partial Variation Line<\\/strong><svg viewBox=\\\"0 0 720 380\\\" role=\\\"img\\\" aria-label=\\\"Partial Variation Line\\\"><defs><clipPath id=\\\"geometry-clip-6cgf7dds3rb\\\"><rect x=\\\"366.54\\\" y=\\\"24\\\" width=\\\"26.91\\\" height=\\\"314\\\"\\/><\\/clipPath><\\/defs><line x1=\\\"366.54\\\" y1=\\\"24\\\" x2=\\\"366.54\\\" y2=\\\"338\\\" class=\\\"presentation-geometry-grid\\\"\\/><line x1=\\\"366.54\\\" y1=\\\"24\\\" x2=\\\"393.46\\\" y2=\\\"24\\\" class=\\\"presentation-geometry-grid\\\"\\/><text x=\\\"366.54\\\" y=\\\"354\\\" text-anchor=\\\"middle\\\" class=\\\"presentation-geometry-label\\\">-0.74<\\/text><text x=\\\"358.54\\\" y=\\\"28\\\" text-anchor=\\\"end\\\" class=\\\"presentation-geometry-label\\\">148.65<\\/text><line x1=\\\"373.27\\\" y1=\\\"24\\\" x2=\\\"373.27\\\" y2=\\\"338\\\" class=\\\"presentation-geometry-grid\\\"\\/><line x1=\\\"366.54\\\" y1=\\\"102.5\\\" x2=\\\"393.46\\\" y2=\\\"102.5\\\" class=\\\"presentation-geometry-grid\\\"\\/><text x=\\\"373.27\\\" y=\\\"354\\\" text-anchor=\\\"middle\\\" class=\\\"presentation-geometry-label\\\">2.63<\\/text><text x=\\\"358.54\\\" y=\\\"106.5\\\" text-anchor=\\\"end\\\" class=\\\"presentation-geometry-label\\\">109.33<\\/text><line x1=\\\"380\\\" y1=\\\"24\\\" x2=\\\"380\\\" y2=\\\"338\\\" class=\\\"presentation-geometry-grid\\\"\\/><line x1=\\\"366.54\\\" y1=\\\"181\\\" x2=\\\"393.46\\\" y2=\\\"181\\\" class=\\\"presentation-geometry-grid\\\"\\/><text x=\\\"380\\\" y=\\\"354\\\" text-anchor=\\\"middle\\\" class=\\\"presentation-geometry-label\\\">6<\\/text><text x=\\\"358.54\\\" y=\\\"185\\\" text-anchor=\\\"end\\\" class=\\\"presentation-geometry-label\\\">70<\\/text><line x1=\\\"386.73\\\" y1=\\\"24\\\" x2=\\\"386.73\\\" y2=\\\"338\\\" class=\\\"presentation-geometry-grid\\\"\\/><line x1=\\\"366.54\\\" y1=\\\"259.5\\\" x2=\\\"393.46\\\" y2=\\\"259.5\\\" class=\\\"presentation-geometry-grid\\\"\\/><text x=\\\"386.73\\\" y=\\\"354\\\" text-anchor=\\\"middle\\\" class=\\\"presentation-geometry-label\\\">9.37<\\/text><text x=\\\"358.54\\\" y=\\\"263.5\\\" text-anchor=\\\"end\\\" class=\\\"presentation-geometry-label\\\">30.67<\\/text><line x1=\\\"393.46\\\" y1=\\\"24\\\" x2=\\\"393.46\\\" y2=\\\"338\\\" class=\\\"presentation-geometry-grid\\\"\\/><line x1=\\\"366.54\\\" y1=\\\"338\\\" x2=\\\"393.46\\\" y2=\\\"338\\\" class=\\\"presentation-geometry-grid\\\"\\/><text x=\\\"393.46\\\" y=\\\"354\\\" text-anchor=\\\"middle\\\" class=\\\"presentation-geometry-label\\\">12.74<\\/text><text x=\\\"358.54\\\" y=\\\"342\\\" text-anchor=\\\"end\\\" class=\\\"presentation-geometry-label\\\">-8.65<\\/text><line x1=\\\"368.02\\\" y1=\\\"24\\\" x2=\\\"368.02\\\" y2=\\\"338\\\" class=\\\"presentation-geometry-axis\\\"\\/><line x1=\\\"366.54\\\" y1=\\\"320.73\\\" x2=\\\"393.46\\\" y2=\\\"320.73\\\" class=\\\"presentation-geometry-axis\\\"\\/><g clip-path=\\\"url(#geometry-clip-6cgf7dds3rb)\\\"><polyline points=\\\"368.02,280.81 387.98,81.19\\\" fill=\\\"none\\\" stroke=\\\"#247456\\\" stroke-width=\\\"2.5\\\"\\/><\\/g><\\/svg><div class=\\\"presentation-geometry-legend\\\"><span><i style=\\\"--shape-color:#247456\\\"><\\/i>Cost = 20 + 10·d<\\/span><\\/div><\\/div><section class=\\\"presentation-slide-activity\\\"><strong><i class=\\\"fa-solid fa-people-group\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\"><\\/i> Classroom activity<\\/strong><p>Collect taxi‑fare data (base fare + per‑km charge) and plot cost vs distance.<\\/p><\\/section><\\/article><article class=\\\"presentation-slide-preview presentation-theme-vibrant presentation-template-inquiry\\\"><header><span>SLIDE 14<\\/span><small>Mathematics · SHS Year 3<\\/small><\\/header><h4>Slide 14 – Generalising and Solving Partial‑Variation Problems<\\/h4><ul><li>Form equation y = mx + c<\\/li><li>Solve for the unknown variable<\\/li><\\/ul><div class=\\\"presentation-visual presentation-visual-cards\\\" role=\\\"img\\\" aria-label=\\\"Problem‑Solving Process\\\"><strong>Problem‑Solving Process<\\/strong><div><span><b>01<\\/b>Write the equation y = mx + c<\\/span><span><b>02<\\/b>Insert known values<\\/span><span><b>03<\\/b>Isolate the unknown (x)<\\/span><span><b>04<\\/b>Check the solution in the original context<\\/span><\\/div><\\/div><section class=\\\"presentation-slide-activity\\\"><strong><i class=\\\"fa-solid fa-people-group\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\"><\\/i> Classroom activity<\\/strong><p>Solve a budgeting problem: a phone plan charges $15 monthly plus $0.05 per minute; find minutes for a $30 bill.<\\/p><\\/section><\\/article><\\/div><\\/div><div class=\\\"document-section\\\"><h3>Teacher lecture notes · private<\\/h3><ol><li><strong>Slide 1:<\\/strong> Introduce the term &#39;partial variation&#39; from the curriculum excerpt. Emphasise the constant term c and relate it to real‑life examples like a monthly subscription fee plus per‑unit usage. Prompt students to think of other scenarios.<\\/li><li><strong>Slide 2:<\\/strong> Use the verified excerpt to explain that partial variation builds on direct variation by adding a constant start value. Highlight how the graph moves up by c units. Ask learners to predict the intercept before plotting.<\\/li><li><strong>Slide 3:<\\/strong> Guide pupils through the table: the cost when 0 units are used reveals the fixed component (c = 50). Use two points to compute the slope k = (Δy)\\/(Δx) = (100‑50)\\/(5‑0) = 10. Write the equation y = 10x + 50 and check with the third row.<\\/li><li><strong>Slide 4:<\\/strong> Link back to the inquiry approach: students first explore the situation, then formulate the equation, compute, and finally reflect on the answer. Emphasise checking work, as required by the curriculum standard on solving real‑life issues.<\\/li><li><strong>Slide 5:<\\/strong> Introduce the concept with everyday examples such as mobile phone plans or bus fares. Emphasise that the relationship starts from a non‑zero intercept. Prompt students to think of other contexts where a constant charge is added to a usage‑based amount.<\\/li><li><strong>Slide 6:<\\/strong> Guide students through each step using a simple example (e.g., cost of printing where there is a setup fee). Highlight how the intercept represents the fixed cost and the slope the variable rate. Encourage questioning: why does the line not pass through the origin?<\\/li><li><strong>Slide 7:<\\/strong> Explain that the data illustrate a fixed charge of ₵2 plus ₵10 per unit. Ask learners to verify the pattern by calculating differences. After plotting, discuss how the line’s intercept and slope correspond to the fixed and variable parts.<\\/li><li><strong>Slide 8:<\\/strong> Present a scenario such as hiring a venue with a booking fee plus hourly charge. Walk through the calculation using the derived equation, then ask students to critique the result (e.g., does it make sense?). Highlight the value of proportional reasoning in planning and budgeting.<\\/li><li><strong>Slide 9:<\\/strong> Introduce the concept with a short story (e.g., mobile phone plan). Emphasise that unlike direct variation, the graph does not pass through the origin. Prompt learners to think of other contexts where a constant fee is added. Link to the curriculum indicator on using proportional reasoning for partial variations.<\\/li><li><strong>Slide 10:<\\/strong> Guide learners to plot the given points quickly (use graph paper or digital tool). Highlight how the slope represents the variable component per unit change. Ask probing questions: ‘What does the intercept tell us about the situation?’ Reinforce the link between the algebraic form and the real‑world story introduced earlier.<\\/li><li><strong>Slide 11:<\\/strong> Explain that each step up in x adds the same amount m to y, confirming proportionality of the variable part. Have students verify the pattern by calculating differences between successive y‑values. Use this to solidify the concept of constant rate and fixed start value before moving to problem solving.<\\/li><li><strong>Slide 12:<\\/strong> Walk through the problem aloud, encouraging students to identify the fixed and variable parts. After solving, ask them to explain why the answer makes sense (e.g., 6 hours × 25 = 150 plus 150 booking = 300). Prompt them to write a short generalisation: ‘For any partial variation, total = fixed + rate × quantity.’ Connect back to the learning indicator and suggest how this reasoning extends to other contexts.<\\/li><li><strong>Slide 13:<\\/strong> Explain that many real‑world situations start with a base amount (e.g., a taxi’s flag‑fall). The total cost grows proportionally with distance, giving a straight line that does not pass through the origin. Guide students to identify the fixed (20) and variable (10) components from the plotted points.<\\/li><li><strong>Slide 14:<\\/strong> Model the translation from words to the algebraic form y = 0.05x + 15. Show the algebraic manipulation to find x = (30‑15)\\/0.05 = 300 minutes. Emphasise checking the answer by substituting back into the original situation.<\\/li><\\/ol><p>These notes are for the teacher and are not shared on student presentation screens.<\\/p><\\/div><div class=\\\"output-warning\\\">Export the editable PowerPoint deck to present or further edit. Review curriculum alignment, data and examples before classroom use.<\\/div><\\/div><\\/article>\"}','2026-11-01 13:16:43','2026-11-01 14:32:00');
